= Chemistry
{wiki}

Chemistry connects the composition and structure of <chemical substances> to their properties and transformations. Its central surprise is that rearranging the same <chemical atoms> can produce a different substance: <molecular hydrogen> and <molecular oxygen> can become <water> without changing which <chemical elements> are present. <Chemical bonds> explain the connections; <chemical reactions> describe the transformations; <stoichiometry> makes quantitative predictions from the bookkeeping.

This gives a useful route from an invisible world to measurable quantities. A <molecule> has a definite composition, such as two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom> in <water>. A sample contains an enormous number of such <molecules>. Counting them through <amount of substance>, measured in <moles>, lets the same small-number ratios describe a macroscopic sample. The beauty is that the bookkeeping survives this huge change of scale.

\Include[chemistry-author]
\Include[chemistry-bibliography]

= Erlenmeyer flask
{c}
{parent=Chemistry}

= Conical flask
{synonym}

A <conical flask> has a narrow neck and a wider body that permits swirling a <chemical solution>, as in a <redox titration>.

= Radical
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Free radical
{synonym}

A <radical (chemistry)> has one or more unpaired <electrons>. In a simple <atomic orbital> or <molecular orbital> occupancy picture, an unpaired <electron> occupies a spatial orbital without an opposite-<spin> partner in that orbital. A dot such as $\mathrm H^{\bullet}$ marks this electronic property; it does not mean a missing <electron> or positive <electric charge>. <Free radicals> can be neutral or charged. Terminology also has a boundary: metal <ions> exhibiting <paramagnetism> are not normally called <free radicals> just because they have unpaired <electrons>.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): R entries by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1994: radical (or free radical), opening definition and metal-ion qualification]{ref}

Compare three species containing the same single <proton>. The <hydrogen atom> $\mathrm H^{\bullet}$ has one <electron> in its $1s$ <atomic orbital>, and is a neutral <radical (chemistry)>. The <hydrogen> <cation> $\mathrm H^+$ has no <electrons>, so no unpaired <electron>. The <hydrogen anion> $\mathrm H^-$ has two <electrons> paired in its $1s$ <atomic orbital>. It is charged but is not a <radical (chemistry)> in its <ground state>. With $N_p$ <protons>, $N_e$ <electrons>, and positive <elementary charge> $e$, the independent <electric charge> count is
$$
q=(N_p-N_e)e;\qquad
\mathrm H^+:q=+e,\quad \mathrm H^{\bullet}:q=0,\quad \mathrm H^-:q=-e.
$$
The <electron pair> and <electric charge> questions require different information. An odd $N_e$ ensures that not all <electrons> can be paired; an even $N_e$ alone does not ensure pairing, as the <molecular orbital> description of <molecular oxygen> illustrates.

\Image[radical-charge-and-orbital-occupancy.png]
{id=image-radical-charge-and-orbital-occupancy}
{title=<Electric charge> and unpaired <electrons> are different properties}
{description=Left: actual <ground state> $1s$ occupancies of $\mathrm H^+$, $\mathrm H^{\bullet}$, and $\mathrm H^-$. Right: stipulated two-orbital models with fixed $N_p=2$ illustrate neutral, positive, and negative <free radicals>; those boxes are not assignments for particular <chemical substances>. Arrows label <spin> occupancy, not particle trajectories.}

The distinction matters whenever a <chemical reaction> mechanism uses dots and charge signs together. <Homolysis> gives one bonding <electron> to each fragment, while <heterolysis> assigns both to one fragment. A <radical chain reaction> can pass an unpaired <electron> from one species to another without generating net <electric charge>. Detection then exploits the unpaired <electron>'s <spin> and <magnetic moment>, rather than merely measuring whether the sample carries an <electric charge>.

= Methyl radical
{parent=Radical (chemistry)}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet}$}

The <methyl radical> is the neutral <radical (chemistry)> $\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet}$, with an unpaired <electron>. It carries the <methane chlorination chain> between its two propagation steps.

= Radical ion
{parent=Radical (chemistry)}

A <radical ion> is both a <radical (chemistry)> and an <ion>. Removing one <electron> from neutral <benzene> can produce $\mathrm{C_6H_6}^{\bullet+}$, a <radical cation>; adding one can produce $\mathrm{C_6H_6}^{\bullet-}$, a <radical anion>. These formulae specify net <electric charge> and an unpaired <electron>, without claiming that both reside on one <chemical atom>.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): R entries by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1994: radical ion, benzene examples and dot/charge placement]{ref}

= Radical cation
{parent=Radical ion}

A <radical cation> has positive net <electric charge> and an unpaired <electron>.

= Radical anion
{parent=Radical ion}

A <radical anion> has negative net <electric charge> and an unpaired <electron>.

= Radical chain reaction
{parent=Radical (chemistry)}

A <radical chain reaction> regenerates a <radical (chemistry)> during propagation. Initiation supplies <free radicals>; propagation consumes one and produces another; termination removes chain-carrying <free radicals>. This explains why one initiating event can lead to many product-forming <chemical reactions>. The <chain-growth polymerization> article develops the corresponding growth of <polymers>.

= Methane chlorination chain
{parent=Radical chain reaction}

Light initiates <methane> chlorination by <photodissociation> of $\mathrm{Cl_2}$ through <homolysis>. The elementary bookkeeping is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{initiation:}\quad &\mathrm{Cl_2}\xrightarrow{h\nu}2\mathrm{Cl}^{\bullet},\\
\text{propagation:}\quad &\mathrm{Cl}^{\bullet}+\mathrm{CH_4}\longrightarrow\mathrm{HCl}+\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet},\\
&\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet}+\mathrm{Cl_2}\longrightarrow\mathrm{CH_3Cl}+\mathrm{Cl}^{\bullet}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $h\nu$ denotes the <photon> <energy>. The first propagation step replaces a <chlorine> <radical (chemistry)> with a <methyl radical>; the second regenerates a <chlorine> <radical (chemistry)>. Adding the two propagation equations cancels both <free radicals> and gives $\mathrm{CH_4+Cl_2\longrightarrow CH_3Cl+HCl}$: <chloromethane> and <hydrogen chloride> are made while the chain carrier is regenerated.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/6.6 Radical Reactions>[Organic Chemistry by John McMurry, §6.6: chlorine photolysis and the two-step methane chlorination cycle]{ref}

\Image[methane-chlorination-radical-bookkeeping.png]
{id=image-methane-chlorination-radical-bookkeeping}
{title=<Methane> chlorination: initiation supplies carriers, propagation replaces them, termination removes them}
{description=The left panel follows the reacting <electron pair> in <homolysis>, then counts chain carriers through two propagation steps. The right panel shows recombination termination. Every <chemical species> in this example is neutral. Electron marks are bookkeeping, not a measured electronic <wavefunction> or a reaction-rate plot.}

For example, recombination $2\mathrm{CH_3}^{\bullet}\longrightarrow\mathrm{C_2H_6}$ of <methyl radicals> makes <ethane> and a <covalent bond> from two unpaired <electrons>, removing two carriers. Recombination of two $\mathrm{Cl}^{\bullet}$ also terminates carriers; in the gas phase, stabilization of the product requires disposal of released <energy>, for example to a third collision partner. The figure omits that partner. Termination competes with propagation, and further chlorination can consume <chloromethane>, so this cycle alone does not predict a pure product or a measured <reaction rate>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/10.2 Preparing Alkyl Halides from Alkanes: Radical Halogenation>[Organic Chemistry by John McMurry, §10.2, Figure 10.2 and following paragraphs: radical combination terminates the chain; further substitution gives product mixtures]{ref}

= Stable radical
{parent=Radical (chemistry)}

An unpaired <electron> does not set a universal <reaction rate>. A <stable radical> can retain radical character during isolation and storage under specified conditions; a persistent <radical (chemistry)> survives long enough under specified conditions to be observed. These descriptions concern lifetime and handling, rather than immunity to every possible <chemical reaction>. <DPPH> is a practical counterexample to the claim that all <free radicals> react immediately.

Two mechanisms help explain persistence. Spreading electronic character over several <chemical atoms>, represented by <chemical resonance>, can lower the <energy> relative to a localized description. Bulky surrounding groups can impede approach to a reactive site and slow recombination. Lower <energy> and slower <chemical kinetics> are different advantages: neither alone guarantees a long lifetime. A primary computational study explicitly separates these effects, using electronic and geometric descriptors; it does not directly measure every predicted lifetime.<A quantitative metric for organic radical stability and persistence using thermodynamic and kinetic features by Shree Sowndarya S. V. and others>[A quantitative metric for organic radical stability and persistence using thermodynamic and kinetic features, Shree Sowndarya S. V., Peter C. St. John and Robert S. Paton, 2021: Abstract and Introduction, thermodynamic stabilization versus kinetic persistence]{ref}

= 2,2-Diphenyl-1-picrylhydrazyl
{parent=Stable radical}
{wiki}

= DPPH
{c}
{synonym}

<DPPH> is a neutral organic <radical (chemistry)> used as a <spin> reference in electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR), also called electron spin resonance (ESR). Its unpaired <electron> remains bound within the <chemical species>: “free radical” does not mean a freely moving <electron>. The <DPPH electron spin resonance experiment> makes its <magnetic moment> observable.

= DPPH electron spin resonance experiment
{c}
{parent=2,2-Diphenyl-1-picrylhydrazyl}

The University of Iowa's <Electron Spin Resonance, University of Iowa laboratory protocol> (revised July 2007, Experiment and Resonance Data Analysis, printed pp. 2–6) specifies <DPPH> on cotton in a tube inside an RF coil, between <Helmholtz coils>. A bridge monitors RF absorption; an <oscilloscope> records this output and the voltage across a $1\,\Omega$ current-monitor resistor. Sweeping the coil current passes through the resonant <magnetic field>. The protocol describes two dips when the sweep revisits resonance; these are repeated crossings, not two distinct spectral lines.

Its calibration replaces the sample with a <Hall effect> probe, maps the <magnetic field> in $2\,\mathrm{mm}$ steps, and accounts for sample dimensions. At least four RF <frequencies> test the resonance relation. Sweep speed is adjusted to obtain comparable passages. Only a local peak-plus-background fit is prescribed, because the full shape is not Gaussian. The protocol also flags unexplained trailing spikes and possible electronics contributions. These controls constrain field and sweep errors; an empty-tube/cotton blank would be an additional background control, not a reported measurement in this protocol.<Electron Spin Resonance, University of Iowa laboratory protocol>[Electron Spin Resonance, revised July 2007: printed pp. 4–7, sweep behavior, field mapping, local fitting and anomalous spikes]{ref}

For an isolated effective <spin one-half> with dimensionless positive factor $g$, the basic resonance model is
$$
\Delta E=g\mu_{\rm B}|B|=h\nu,\qquad
g=\frac{h\nu}{\mu_{\rm B}|B_{\rm res}|}.
$$
Here $\mu_{\rm B}$ is the <Bohr magneton>, $h$ is the <Planck constant>, $\nu$ is the applied <frequency>, and $B_{\rm res}$ is the calibrated resonant <magnetic field>. A change in <frequency> should shift the resonance field proportionally in this model. The factor $g$ describes the electronic <magnetic moment> coupling, not the species' net <electric charge>.

A resonance supports the presence of detectable <paramagnetism>. It does not, by itself, identify an unknown <radical (chemistry)>: other species exhibiting <paramagnetism>, impurities, overlapping responses, and interactions with <atomic nuclei> can complicate interpretation. Conversely, absence of a detectable resonance need not mean absence of <free radicals>; a low <concentration>, a short lifetime, or an unsuitable measurement range can conceal them. The occupancy and chain diagrams are original bookkeeping illustrations; neither is a measured or simulated <DPPH> spectrum.

= Molecule
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A molecule consists of two or more <chemical atoms> joined by <chemical bonds>, with zero net <electric charge>. This follows the terminology of the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/M04002[IUPAC Gold Book, molecule]{ref}. The <continuum approximation> uses bulk fields in place of individual molecular positions and motions.

= Molecular rotation
{parent=Molecule}

<Molecular rotation> changes a <molecule>'s orientation. Its <rotational kinetic energy> depends on the arrangement and <masses> of its <chemical atoms>. A <rigid rotor> freezes internal distances to isolate this motion; <rotational spectroscopy> tests the resulting <energy eigenvalues> through transitions between <quantum states>. The <molecular beam electric resonance> experiment provides a complementary probe of the coupling to an <electric field>.

= Rigid rotor
{parent=Molecular rotation}
{wiki}

A <rigid rotor> models rotational motion with fixed internal separations. Its use in the synthetic <Stark effect> figure omits vibrational motion and additional interactions in real <molecules>.

Rigidity fixes the <molecular geometry>, not the orientation, and does not require a spherical mass distribution. Remove motion of the <centre of mass>, choose <principal axes> of the <inertia tensor>, and write the classical <rotational kinetic energy> as
$$
T_{\rm rot}=\frac12\left(I_a\omega_a^2+I_b\omega_b^2+I_c\omega_c^2\right)
=\frac{L_a^2}{2I_a}+\frac{L_b^2}{2I_b}+\frac{L_c^2}{2I_c}.
$$
Here $I_a,I_b,I_c$ are the principal <moments of inertia>, $\omega_a,\omega_b,\omega_c$ components of <angular velocity>, and $L_a,L_b,L_c$ components of <angular momentum>. Quantization replaces these last components by <angular momentum operators>. Different principal <moments of inertia> produce different spectra: the single-constant <linear rigid rotor> is a special case, not a formula for every <molecule>.<Lecture 24 Supplement: Energy levels of a Rigid Rotor by Dudley Herschbach>[printed p. 1: principal-axis energy and classification; pp. 4–5: asymmetric rotors]{ref}

= Linear rigid rotor
{parent=Rigid rotor}
{title2=$E_J=\hbar^2J(J+1)/(2I)$}

For a <diatomic molecule>, let $m_1,m_2$ be the two <masses> and $r$ their fixed separation. The distances from the <centre of mass> are $r_1=m_2r/(m_1+m_2)$ and $r_2=m_1r/(m_1+m_2)$. A perpendicular axis through the <centre of mass> therefore has <moment of inertia>
$$
I=m_1r_1^2+m_2r_2^2=\mu r^2,\qquad \mu=\frac{m_1m_2}{m_1+m_2}.
$$
The <reduced mass> $\mu$ here is not the <electric dipole moment>, for which we use $\mathbf d$. For a longer <linear molecule>, replace $\mu r^2$ by $\sum_i m_i\rho_i^2$, where $\rho_i$ is each distance along the axis from the <centre of mass>. In the point-mass idealization, the axial <moment of inertia> is zero; turning about that axis changes no nuclear positions, so no independent axial rotational degree of freedom is included.

For the field-free <linear rigid rotor>, assume a fixed internal <quantum state> with no electronic <angular momentum> or electronic <spin>, and omit couplings to nuclear <spin>. The rotational <Hamiltonian> is $\widehat H_{\rm rot}=\widehat{\mathbf L}^{\,2}/(2I)$. Its orientation <wavefunctions> are <spherical harmonics> $Y_{JM}$, and its <angular momentum eigenstates> satisfy
$$
\widehat{\mathbf L}^{\,2}Y_{JM}=\hbar^2J(J+1)Y_{JM},\qquad
\widehat L_zY_{JM}=\hbar M Y_{JM},\qquad
E_J=\frac{\hbar^2}{2I}J(J+1)=hB J(J+1),
$$
where $J=0,1,2,\ldots$ and $M=-J,-J+1,\ldots,J$. Each $E_J$ belongs to $2J+1$ <angular momentum eigenstates> distinguished by the <magnetic quantum number> $M$, an instance of <degenerate energy levels>. The <Planck constant> is $h=2\pi\hbar$; $B$ is the frequency-valued <rotational constant>. Notice that the <energy eigenvalues> scale as $J(J+1)$, not $J^2$.

\Image[linear-rigid-rotor-levels-and-transitions.png]
{id=image-linear-rigid-rotor-levels-and-transitions}
{title=<Linear rigid rotor>: increasing gaps between <energy eigenvalues> and equally spaced transition <frequencies>}
{description=Original dimensionless model: $E_J/(hB)=J(J+1)$. Upward arrows show <photon absorption> with $\Delta J=1$; the sticks show positions only, not measured or predicted intensities. The $2J+1$ states at each <energy eigenvalue> are represented by one horizontal line.}

Subtracting consecutive <energy eigenvalues> gives
$$
\nu_{J\to J+1}=\frac{E_{J+1}-E_J}{h}=2B(J+1).
$$
Thus increasing level gaps produce a regularly spaced series of lines at $2B,4B,6B,\ldots$, with spacing $2B$. Allowed positions do not guarantee that every line is visible: <electric-dipole selection rules>, occupation of the initial <quantum state>, and instrument response also matter.

= Rotational constant
{parent=Linear rigid rotor}
{title2=$B=h/(8\pi^2I)$}

In this article the <rotational constant> is a <frequency>,
$$
B=\frac{h}{8\pi^2I}.
$$
Its dimensions are $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, since the <Planck constant> has dimensions $\mathrm{kg\,m^2\,s^{-1}}$ and a <moment of inertia> has dimensions $\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. Some authors instead call $hB=\hbar^2/(2I)$ the energy-valued <rotational constant>; this is the convention in the earlier synthetic <molecular beam electric resonance> diagram. A third convention uses the <spectroscopic wavenumber> $\widetilde B=B/c$, with $c$ the <speed of light>. Always check units before using $\nu=2B(J+1)$.

If $B$ is known and the two <masses> are specified, the rigid two-atom model gives the <bond length> $r=\sqrt{h/(8\pi^2\mu B)}$. This is why <rotational spectroscopy> can constrain <molecular geometry>. A real fitted constant can depend on <molecular vibration>, so this inference alone does not establish an equilibrium <bond length>.

= Pure rotational electric-dipole selection rules
{parent=Linear rigid rotor}
{title2=$\Delta J=\pm1$}

For the field-free, spin-free <linear rigid rotor> in one fixed internal <quantum state>, the <electric-dipole interaction> is $-\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf E(t)$. A nonzero permanent <electric dipole moment> along the molecular axis allows pure rotational <photon absorption> with $\Delta J=+1$ and <photon emission> with $\Delta J=-1$. The <matrix element> between initial and final <quantum states> must be nonzero: the existence of an <energy> gap alone is insufficient. Relative to a chosen $z$ axis, <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> along $z$ selects $\Delta M=0$; transverse <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> permits $\Delta M=\pm1$.<Lecture 24 Supplement: Energy levels of a Rigid Rotor by Dudley Herschbach>[printed pp. 2–3: dipole transition integrals, polarization and selection rules]{ref}

<Hydrogen chloride> and <carbon monoxide> are examples with a permanent <electric dipole moment>. By symmetry, <molecular hydrogen> and <carbon dioxide> have no permanent <electric dipole moment> in the ideal symmetric internal state. They still rotate and have rotational <energy eigenvalues>, but the simple permanent-<electric-dipole> spectrum described here is absent. This restriction concerns this interaction and these assumptions, not every possible way of detecting <molecular rotation>.

= Isotope shift of a linear rotor
{parent=Linear rigid rotor}

Changing an <isotope> changes a <mass> and therefore the <moment of inertia>. If the <bond length> is held fixed, $B\propto1/\mu$ and every corresponding transition shifts by the same fractional amount. This leading inverse-<reduced-mass> scaling is also used in the more complete diatomic formulation.<Diatomic Spectral Database by National Institute of Standards and Technology/1Σ-Ground State Molecules>[§2a, equations (4)–(5): isotope dependence of rotational constants and reduced mass]{ref}

For a deliberately stipulated <carbon monoxide> example, take $m_{12}=12u$, $m_{13}=13u$, $m_{16}=16u$, $u=1.66053906660\times10^{-27}\,\mathrm{kg}$, $r=1.13\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm m$ for both isotopic forms, and $h=6.62607015\times10^{-34}\,\mathrm{J\,s}$. The integer <masses> are simplifying inputs, not accurate isotopic atomic <masses>; the shared <bond length> is an imposed model assumption. Calculation gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mu_{12,16}&=\frac{48}{7}u,& I_{12,16}&\approx1.45395\times10^{-46}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2},& B_{12,16}&\approx57.7188\,\mathrm{GHz},\\
\mu_{13,16}&=\frac{208}{29}u,& I_{13,16}&\approx1.52080\times10^{-46}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2},& B_{13,16}&\approx55.1817\,\mathrm{GHz}.
\end{aligned}
$$
In particular,
$$
\frac{B_{13,16}}{B_{12,16}}=\frac{\mu_{12,16}}{\mu_{13,16}}=\frac{87}{91}\approx0.956044.
$$
The heavier form's calculated $J=0\to1$ line is $110.363\,\mathrm{GHz}$ rather than $115.438\,\mathrm{GHz}$, a decrease of about $4.396\%$. These are model predictions from the stated inputs, not published measured <frequencies>. An <isotope> changes the spacing as well as the first line; the shift is not a fixed additive offset for the whole spectrum.

\Image[molecular-rotation-isotope-and-distortion-spectra.png]
{id=image-molecular-rotation-isotope-and-distortion-spectra}
{title=Synthetic <rotational spectroscopy> comparison: a heavier <isotope> compresses the rigid spectrum, while <centrifugal distortion> bends the spacing pattern}
{description=Original equal-height sticks for $J=0\to1$ through $4\to5$, using only the stipulated <masses> and <bond length> above. The lower panel additionally stipulates $D/B=0.001$ for the lighter form and compares $2B(J+1)-4D(J+1)^3$ with dashed rigid positions. Neither heights, distortion constant nor line <frequencies> are measured data; no thermal-population or instrument model is included.}

= Centrifugal distortion
{parent=Rigid rotor}
{title2=$E_J/h=BJ(J+1)-D[J(J+1)]^2+\cdots$}

Real <chemical bonds> can stretch as <molecular rotation> increases. The resulting larger <moment of inertia> lowers the <energy eigenvalues> relative to a <rigid rotor>. Within one vibrational <quantum state>, a leading effective correction is
$$
\frac{E_J}{h}=B_vJ(J+1)-D_v[J(J+1)]^2+\cdots,\qquad
\nu_{J\to J+1}=2B_v(J+1)-4D_v(J+1)^3+\cdots.
$$
Both $B_v$ and $D_v$ are in <frequency> units here; $v$ labels the vibrational <quantum state>. For positive $D_v$, the adjacent-line spacing becomes
$$
\nu_{J+1\to J+2}-\nu_{J\to J+1}=2B_v-4D_v(3J^2+9J+7),
$$
so the departures grow with $J$. <Molecular vibration> also changes the effective <rotational constant>: even the lowest vibrational <quantum state> does not have motionless <atomic nuclei>.<Diatomic Spectral Database by National Institute of Standards and Technology/1Σ-Ground State Molecules>[§2a, equations (7)–(8): distortion and vibration-dependent constants]{ref}

The quartic correction is a limited expansion, not a model to extrapolate to arbitrary $J$. It is small relative to the rigid contribution only while $(D_v/B_v)J(J+1)\ll1$; eventually higher terms, changed internal motion, or <chemical bond> breaking matter. In the synthetic plot, the largest upper-state label is $J=5$, giving a stipulated correction ratio of $0.03$. No universal maximum valid $J$ follows without a particular <molecule> and precision target.

The <quantum numbers> also have limits of meaning. Here $J$ describes spin-free rotation. In a <molecule> with electronic <spin>, rotation alone is commonly labelled $N$, while $J$ includes electronic <angular momentum>; including nuclear <spin> introduces additional total-<angular-momentum> labels and resolved splittings. An applied <electric field> can mix different field-free $J$ states through the <Stark effect>. For identical <atomic nuclei>, the <Pauli exclusion principle> can restrict combinations of rotational and nuclear-<spin> states. The simple $2J+1$ degeneracy and every-integer-$J$ ladder therefore describe the stated idealization, not all observed sublevels.<Diatomic Spectral Database by National Institute of Standards and Technology/1Σ-Ground State Molecules>[§2a: hyperfine discussion; §2b: distinct definitions of J, N and F]{ref}

= Rotational spectroscopy
{parent=Molecular rotation}
{wiki}

<Rotational spectroscopy> probes transitions between <molecular rotation> <quantum states>. In the simple <linear rigid rotor>, line spacing determines a <moment of inertia>, while an <isotope shift of a linear rotor> tests the mass dependence. More general <molecular geometry> requires several principal <moments of inertia> and more complicated assignments. The combination makes spectra sensitive fingerprints of both composition and shape.

= Fourier transform microwave spectroscopy
{parent=Rotational spectroscopy}

= FTMW spectroscopy
{c}
{synonym}

<Fourier transform microwave spectroscopy> excites gaseous <molecules> with a pulse of <electromagnetic radiation>, then records the emitted time-dependent <electric field>. A <Fourier transform> extracts transition <frequencies> from the decaying signal. In a cavity implementation, two facing mirrors form a resonator; tuned excitation, receiver electronics and digitization replace the molecule-counting readout of <molecular beam electric resonance>. The pulsed radiation prepares <quantum coherence> between rotational <quantum states>; it does not set the whole gas spinning at one classical <angular velocity>.

For documented laboratory context, <Evolution of Microwave Spectroscopy at the National Bureau of Standards (NBS) and the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) by F. J. Lovas and others> (§5, printed pp. 280–282) describes the NIST cavity instrument, phase-locked sources, mirror tuning and detector-mixer modifications. That account explains one instrument family; it does not establish the electronics of every later commercial <spectrometer>.

= Rotational monitoring of deuterium exchange
{parent=Fourier transform microwave spectroscopy}

A distinct experiment in <An Automated, Highly Selective Reaction Monitoring Instrument Using Molecular Rotational Resonance Spectroscopy by Ann Adele Byars and others> (Figure 1 and Figure 3) tracked exchange of <hydrogen> for <deuterium> in <ethyl acetoacetate> mixed with deuterated <methanol>. A pump circulated <chemical solution> through a valve; a calibrated loop injected aliquots into a temperature-controlled inlet, held at room <temperature> for this exchange experiment. <Neon> carried the vaporized sample to a cavity <spectrometer>. Separately recorded broadband spectra and fitted rotational <Hamiltonians> identified transitions for the four isotopic forms before targeted monitoring.<An Automated, Highly Selective Reaction Monitoring Instrument Using Molecular Rotational Resonance Spectroscopy by Ann Adele Byars and others>[apparatus and assignment paragraphs around Figure 1; deuterium-exchange method and Figure 3]{ref}

The readout was each assigned line's signal over reaction time, normalized by the sum of the four signals. Transitions with the same <quantum numbers> reduced response-factor differences. A solvent wash addressed observed sample carryover. The signals distinguished the unsubstituted, two singly substituted, and doubly substituted forms. Only one gas-phase structural form was detected; the authors explicitly caution that their readout does not recover the structural <chemical equilibrium> in <chemical solution>.<An Automated, Highly Selective Reaction Monitoring Instrument Using Molecular Rotational Resonance Spectroscopy by Ann Adele Byars and others>[deuterium-exchange paragraphs surrounding Figure 3; sample carryover and wash paragraph]{ref}

This example uses nonlinear <molecules>, so neither the single-constant <linear rigid rotor> nor the synthetic comb above is a fit to its spectrum. Its connection to the model is the sensitivity of <moments of inertia> to <isotope> placement. Line position helps assign a species; signal size additionally depends on sample transfer, initial <quantum state> populations and instrument response. Matching <quantum numbers> is a useful control, not a proof of identical response factors. These distinctions explain why resolving species and determining their <concentrations> are separate inference steps.

= Linear molecule
{parent=Molecule}

A <linear molecule> has collinear <atomic nuclei> in its idealized <molecular geometry>. Both a <diatomic molecule> and <carbon dioxide> can supply <linear rigid rotor> examples, although their <electric-dipole selection rules> need not give an observable permanent-<electric-dipole> spectrum.

= Diatomic molecule
{parent=Linear molecule}
{wiki}

A <diatomic molecule> contains two <chemical atoms>; <hydrogen chloride> and <carbon monoxide> are examples. Their fixed-separation <moment of inertia> is $I=\mu r^2$ in the <linear rigid rotor> model.

= Molecular vibration
{parent=Molecule}
{wiki}

<Molecular vibration> changes internal separations and <bond angles>, unlike the fixed internal <molecular geometry> of a <rigid rotor>. Its coupling to <molecular rotation> makes measured <rotational constants> depend on the vibrational <quantum state>.

= Molecular geometry
{parent=Molecule}
{wiki}

Why do <methane>, <ammonia>, and <water> have different shapes when each central <chemical atom> is surrounded by eight <valence electrons>? The answer separates the positions of <atomic nuclei> from the <electron domains> used in a <VSEPR model>.

<Molecular geometry> describes the three-dimensional arrangement of the <atomic nuclei> in a <molecule>, including <bond lengths> and <bond angles>. For a larger <molecule>, a shape name often describes just the neighbours of one central <chemical atom>. A <Lewis structure> records connections and electron bookkeeping; its flat layout does not fix <molecular geometry>.

In the <VSEPR model>, <electron pair geometry> includes both bonding <electron domains> and <lone pairs>, whereas <molecular geometry> counts only the <chemical atoms>. In <methane>, four <bond pairs> and no <lone pairs> give <tetrahedral molecular geometry>. In <ammonia>, three <bond pairs> and one <lone pair> give <trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry>. In <water>, two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs> give <bent molecular geometry>. All three use the same ideal tetrahedral <electron pair geometry>; this does not make their actual <bond angles> equal. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Flowers, Theopold, Langley and Robinson, Chemistry 2e, §7.6: electron-pair and molecular geometries].

\Image[molecular-geometry-domains-and-atoms.png]
{id=image-molecular-geometry-domains-and-atoms}
{title=Four <electron domains>, three arrangements of <atomic nuclei>: <methane>, <ammonia>, and <water>}
{description=Original geometric constructions. The upper row is an ideal <VSEPR model> counting scaffold: orange LP markers identify <lone pairs>, not additional <chemical atoms> or measured <electron density>. The lower row shows only <chemical atoms>; <ammonia> and <water> use the sourced approximate <bond angles> discussed below. Sizes and <bond lengths> are arbitrary. Three-dimensional projections can change the apparent <bond angles> on the page. Dotted outlines are geometric guides, not extra <chemical bonds>.}

A static drawing also needs a physical interpretation. The <atomic nuclei> undergo <oscillations>; an arrangement at a minimum of <potential energy> and an experimentally averaged arrangement need not have identical <bond lengths> or <bond angles>. This distinction matters when comparing measurements with <quantum mechanics> calculations; see the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/BT07003[IUPAC Gold Book: experimental bond-length conventions]. <Gas electron diffraction> gives a concrete route from a laboratory signal to <molecular geometry> rather than inferring shape from a drawing alone.

= Bond angle
{parent=Molecular geometry}
{title2=$\theta$}

A <bond angle> is the <angle> between two <chemical bonds> sharing a central <chemical atom>. In H–O–H, the middle symbol identifies the vertex: the <angle> is at <oxygen>. Let $u$ and $v$ be the nonzero displacement <vectors> from that <atomic nucleus> to the two neighbours. The actual three-dimensional <bond angle> satisfies
$$
\cos\theta=\frac{u\cdot v}{\|u\|\|v\|},\qquad 0\leq\theta\leq\pi,
$$
using the <dot product> and <Euclidean norm>. Rotating the whole <molecule> preserves this expression; projecting it onto a page generally does not.

There is another useful way to find a <bond angle>. If the two <bond lengths> are $a,b$ and the distance between the outer <atomic nuclei> is $d$, the <law of cosines> gives
$$
d^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\cos\theta.
$$
For equal <bond lengths> $a=b$, this becomes $d=2b\sin(\theta/2)$. Thus a measured outer-atom separation contains information about a <bond angle> even though those outer <chemical atoms> are not directly joined by a <chemical bond>.

For a stipulated rigid, equal-<bond-length> model using <water>'s $104.4776^\circ$ equilibrium <bond angle>, $d/b\approx1.5811$, whereas a straight H–O–H arrangement would give $d/b=2$. These are calculated geometric ratios, not new measured distances. <Gas electron diffraction> illustrates how pair-distance information can constrain <molecular geometry>. Substituting independently averaged distances into the rigid <triangle> formula does not automatically recover an equilibrium <bond angle>, because averaging and this nonlinear calculation need not commute.

= Tetrahedral molecular geometry
{parent=Molecular geometry}
{wiki}

In <tetrahedral molecular geometry>, four neighbouring <chemical atoms> surround a central <chemical atom> in the directions of the vertices of a <tetrahedron>. In the regular idealization, the central <atomic nucleus> lies at the centre of a <regular tetrahedron>, and every pair of <chemical bonds> makes the same <bond angle>. <Methane>, $\mathrm{CH}_4$, provides the standard example: its <carbon> has four bonding <electron domains> and no <lone pair>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Chemistry 2e, §7.6: tetrahedral methane].

The exact ideal <bond angle> follows from four directions proportional to $(1,1,1)$, $(1,-1,-1)$, $(-1,1,-1)$, and $(-1,-1,1)$. Each has squared <Euclidean norm> $3$, and each distinct pair has <dot product> $-1$. Hence
$$
\theta=\arccos(-1/3)\approx109.4712^\circ.
$$
This is a statement about a <regular tetrahedron>, rather than a universal exact value for every <molecule> labelled tetrahedral. It also explains why a cross-shaped flat drawing with $90^\circ$ gaps is misleading: four equal directions can spread out more evenly in three dimensions.

The same <vectors> sum to zero. Consequently, four equal bond-dipole contributions along those directions cancel. The symmetric <methane> <molecule> is <nonpolar>, but a tetrahedral shape alone does not imply a <nonpolar molecule>: replacing one neighbour can make the electronic distribution and bond contributions unequal. <Chemical polarity> depends on both <molecular geometry> and <electron density>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/2-2-polar-covalent-bonds-and-dipole-moments[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §2.2: molecular dipoles and methane].

= Trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry
{parent=Molecular geometry}
{wiki}

In <trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry>, three neighbouring <chemical atoms> form a triangular base and the central <chemical atom> lies outside its plane. The pyramid has four <atomic nuclei>; its central <chemical atom> is the apex, not the centre of the base. <Ammonia>, $\mathrm{NH}_3$, has this arrangement. The https://cccbdb.nist.gov/exp2x.asp?casno=7664417&charge=0[NIST CCCBDB experimental ammonia entry, geometric data, citing Herzberg (1966)]{ref} lists an H–N–H <bond angle> of $106.67^\circ$, commonly rounded to $106.7^\circ$ or about $107^\circ$.

The <Lewis structure> of <ammonia> allocates eight <valence electrons> as three <bond pairs> and one <lone pair>. Four <electron domains> therefore suggest tetrahedral <electron pair geometry>; removing the <lone pair> marker leaves three <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> below <nitrogen>. The <VSEPR model> treats the <lone pair> as more space-demanding and predicts compression relative to $109.4712^\circ$, but it does not calculate the precise $106.67^\circ$ value. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Chemistry 2e, §7.6: lone-pair effects].

Three equal bond-dipole contributions in this pyramid cancel sideways, but retain a component along its axis. By contrast, three equal contributions lying in one plane at $120^\circ$ would cancel completely. This shows how a small change in <molecular geometry> can permit a permanent <electric dipole moment>. <Ammonia> is a <polar molecule>; NIST lists a measured magnitude of about $1.476$ debye in the same https://cccbdb.nist.gov/exp2x.asp?casno=7664417&charge=0[experimental entry, electric dipole moment, citing Shimizu (1970)]{ref}. The vector argument predicts allowed direction and cancellation, not the full measured magnitude.

= Bent molecular geometry
{parent=Molecular geometry}
{wiki}

In <bent molecular geometry>, two neighbours and their central <chemical atom> form a noncollinear <triangle>, with an interior <bond angle> strictly between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$. <Water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, is the standard example. The https://cccbdb.nist.gov/exp2x.asp?casno=7732185&charge=0[NIST CCCBDB experimental water entry, internal coordinates, citing Hoy and Bunker (1979)] gives the equilibrium H–O–H <bond angle> as $104.4776^\circ$ with quoted uncertainty $0.0019^\circ$, usually rounded to $104.5^\circ$.

The <oxygen> in <water> has two bonding <electron domains> and two <lone pairs>. Its ideal <electron pair geometry> is tetrahedral, while the three <atomic nuclei> give <bent molecular geometry>. Three points always lie in a plane; this does not make the four-domain scaffold planar. Nor does the word bent by itself determine an <electron domain> count: the same atomic shape can arise from other domain arrangements.

In an illustrative model with two equal bond-dipole contributions of magnitude $p$ separated by <bond angle> $\theta$, their sum of <vectors> points along the bisector and has magnitude $2p\cos(\theta/2)$. At $180^\circ$ it vanishes; at $104.4776^\circ$ it is approximately $1.2247p$. This ratio is a geometric calculation, not a prediction of <water>'s measured <electric dipole moment>. The entire <electron density>, including contributions represented by <lone pairs>, matters. <Water> is a <polar molecule>; <chemical polarity> helps explain its interactions with other <polar molecules>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/2-2-polar-covalent-bonds-and-dipole-moments[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §2.2: water, lone-pair contributions, and polarity].

= Gas electron diffraction
{parent=Molecular geometry}
{wiki}

= GED
{c}
{synonym}

<Gas electron diffraction> infers <molecular geometry> from the <diffraction> of <electrons> by a gaseous sample. It makes a useful experimental test of shape: distances between both bonded and nonbonded <atomic nuclei> affect the signal, so a proposed straight arrangement and a bent arrangement can be distinguished through their pair separations.

A concrete modern apparatus is the Bielefeld instrument described by https://doi.org/10.1515/znb-2015-0186[Reuter, Vishnevskiy, Blomeyer and Mitzel (2016), Gas electron diffraction of increased performance, §§1, 3 and 12]{ref}. A hot cathode supplies <electrons>; apertures and magnetic lenses shape the beam. A temperature-controlled nozzle injects sample vapour across it inside a <vacuum> chamber. Pumps and a cold trap reduce unwanted residual gas. A central beam stop catches the strong unscattered beam; off-axis scattered <electrons> expose imaging plates. A rotating sector can attenuate the intense small-angle region to accommodate detector range. The authors checked both the spatial scale of the plate scanner and its response to electron dose. These checks matter because an error in detector position or intensity calibration can masquerade as a structural difference.

\Image[molecular-geometry-electron-diffraction.png]
{id=image-molecular-geometry-electron-diffraction}
{title=<Gas electron diffraction>: apparatus, pair-distance inference, and a <bond angle>}
{description=Original schematic, not an engineering drawing or a recorded <diffraction> pattern. Rays identify possible incident and scattered paths; the detector is deliberately shown without invented rings or intensity traces. The lower <triangle> is a normalized rigid-model construction using $104.4776^\circ$; it illustrates a geometric inference, not distances measured in this apparatus.}

What is measured is detector signal as a function of position, converted to scattering <angle> using the sample-to-detector distance. Randomly oriented <molecules> give radial information. After extracting the molecular intensity, compare it with predictions from a candidate arrangement and adjust the <bond lengths> and <bond angles> to improve the fit. The https://www.york.ac.uk/chemistry/people/dwann/wanngroup/time-averagedged/[Wann Electron Diffraction Group's account of time-averaged GED, experiment and theory] describes radial averaging and this refinement. The apparatus does not photograph one <molecule> with labelled <atomic nuclei>.

Why can distances survive random orientation? In a simplified rigid pair model with separation $r$, write $s$ for the magnitude of the difference between incident and scattered <wave vectors>. Averaging the interference factor over uniformly distributed directions gives
$$
\frac12\int_{-1}^{1}\cos(srt)\,dt=\frac{\sin(sr)}{sr},
$$
with limiting value one at $sr=0$. This follows by integrating the <cosine>; $t$ is the <cosine> of the angle between the pair and the scattering direction. The <oscillations> depend on $r$, although the orientation has disappeared. A real calculation weights many pairs by their <scattering amplitudes> and includes nuclear motion. The <wave interference> explanation and superposed pair-distance contributions are discussed in the same https://www.york.ac.uk/chemistry/people/dwann/wanngroup/time-averagedged/[Wann group theory account]{ref}.

Once a consistent structural model supplies two equal <bond lengths> $b$ and an outer-atom separation $d$, the <law of cosines> converts these to a <bond angle>. For example, the illustrative $d/b=1.5811$ corresponds to approximately $104.5^\circ$, while $d/b=2$ is straight. This geometric step explains what a shape measurement is trying to establish; it is not a new <water> measurement.

There are two essential limits. First, recovering labelled three-dimensional positions from a summed radial signal is an <inverse problem>: composition, candidate connectivity, and other structural information can be needed to distinguish competing fits. Karle's authored review https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC432024/?page=-1[Structure determination of gaseous and amorphous substances by diffraction methods (1977), abstract] explains why too few independent observations make supplementary structural information indispensable. A good fit alone does not prove that the proposed arrangement is unique. Second, <atomic nuclei> are moving. Distances inferred by <gas electron diffraction> are motion-dependent averages; corrections are required before identifying them with a minimum-<potential-energy> arrangement. For experimental distance conventions, see the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/BT07003[IUPAC Gold Book: bond length]. These limitations explain why model assumptions and the averaging convention must accompany a precise <bond angle>.

= Nucleoside
{parent=Molecule}
{wiki}

A <nucleoside> combines a <nucleobase> with a <sugar>, without the <phosphate> groups of a <nucleotide>. <Adenosine> is the <nucleoside> built from <adenine> and <ribose>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: nucleosides and nucleotides].

= Adenosine
{parent=Nucleoside}
{wiki}

<Adenosine> consists of <adenine> joined to <ribose>. Adding one, two, or three <phosphate> groups at the <ribose>'s $5'$ position gives <AMP>, <ADP>, or <ATP>, respectively. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the components of nucleotides].

= Nucleotide
{parent=Molecule}

A <nucleotide> contains a <nucleobase>, a <sugar>, and one or more <phosphate> groups. In <DNA> and <RNA>, linked <nucleotides> form a <polymer> whose <nucleotide sequence> carries information. The <sugar> is <deoxyribose> in <DNA> and <ribose> in <RNA>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nucleotide[NHGRI: nucleotide].

= Adenosine triphosphate
{parent=Nucleotide}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{ATP}$}

= ATP
{c}
{synonym}

<ATP> is a <nucleotide> consisting of <adenosine>, itself <adenine> joined to <ribose>, carrying three <phosphate> groups. From the <ribose> outward, these groups are labelled $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$. <ATP> connects <energy>-supplying processes to <energy coupling> for synthesis and <active transport>; the same carrier can be regenerated and reused. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.4: ATP composition and recycling]{ref}.

\Image[atp-adp-phosphate-cycle.png]
{title=<ATP> has three <phosphate> groups; <ADP> has two, and regeneration requires <energy> input}
{description=Original component schematic, rather than a molecular structure or a <reaction mechanism>. Lines indicate component order and omit connecting <oxygen> <chemical atoms>, <electric charges>, and three-dimensional geometry. Both sides retain <adenine> and <ribose>; <hydrolysis> consumes <water>, whereas the reverse net <chemical reaction> forms <water>. The <energy> labels describe transfers, not extra material <reactants> or <reaction products>.}

The net <hydrolysis> commonly used in <energy coupling> is
$$
\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}\longrightarrow\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i.
$$
Here $\mathrm P_i$ denotes <inorganic phosphate>. At buffered <pH> $7$, this biochemical shorthand groups the appropriate <protonation> states into pools and leaves <proton> exchange with the <buffer solution> implicit. An explicit, charge-balanced choice is $\mathrm{ATP}^{4-}+\mathrm{H_2O}\longrightarrow\mathrm{ADP}^{3-}+\mathrm{HPO_4}^{2-}+\mathrm H^+$, without implying these are the only <chemical species> present. See the https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/thermod2/[IUBMB recommendations for biochemical thermodynamics].

The quoted textbook biochemical <standard state> value is approximately
$$
\Delta G^{\circ\prime}=-30.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
This convention uses fixed <pH> $7$, <temperature> $298.15\,\mathrm K$ ($25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$), reference <pressure> $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$ ($1\,\mathrm{bar}$), unit reference <thermodynamic activities> for dissolved <reactants> and <reaction products>, and <water> <thermodynamic activity> approximated by one. The prime indicates the fixed-<pH> biochemical convention. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.4: standard ATP hydrolysis value] and https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy[Biology 2e, 6.2: biochemical standard conditions]. This is a reference value, rather than a universal value inside a <biological cell>; precise tables must also specify <chemical solution> conditions and any <magnesium> <ion> binding convention. Different reference conventions and data treatments give different tabulated values, as discussed in https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3299200/[Li et al., 2010: a database of thermodynamic quantities for biochemical reactions].

An <exergonic reaction> has a negative change in <Gibbs free energy>, so the overall <ATP> <hydrolysis> is favourable under those reference conditions. Breaking a <chemical bond> by itself costs <energy>; forming new <chemical bonds> can release <energy>. The net change compares all <reactants> with all <reaction products>, including their interactions with <water> and the <entropy> contribution. Calling the phosphate connection a "high-energy bond" describes a large favourable change in <Gibbs free energy> on <hydrolysis>, rather than <energy> released by bond breaking alone. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.5 Strengths of Ionic and Covalent Bonds> and https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/[Cooper, The Cell: ATP and metabolic energy].

Composition changes the driving force. At the same fixed <pH>, <temperature>, <pressure>, and binding convention, write the molar reaction change as
$$
\Delta G=\Delta G^{\circ\prime}+RT\ln Q,\qquad
Q=\frac{a_{\mathrm{ADP}}a_{\mathrm P_i}}{a_{\mathrm{ATP}}}.
$$
Here $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, $T$ the absolute <temperature>, and $a$ a <dimensionless> <thermodynamic activity> for a biochemical pool. Constant <water> <thermodynamic activity> is absorbed into the reference term; fixed <pH> accounts for buffered <protons>. The shorthand $\Delta G$ means transformed molar reaction <Gibbs free energy> change, $\Delta_rG'$, rather than a finite sample's total change. The <natural logarithm> acts on a <dimensionless> <reaction quotient>, never on <concentrations> with units. See the https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/thermod2/[IUBMB biochemical thermodynamics recommendations] and the general <equilibrium constant> relation.

For a stipulated ideal dilute <chemical solution>, let $a_i=[i]/c^\circ$, with $c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. Choose $[\mathrm{ATP}]=5\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$, $[\mathrm{ADP}]=0.5\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$, and $[\mathrm P_i]=1\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$, ignoring <activity coefficient> corrections and <magnesium> binding. These are illustrative inputs, not a claim about a particular <biological cell>. Then
$$
Q=\frac{(0.0005)(0.001)}{0.005}=10^{-4},\qquad
RT=(0.008314462618)(298.15)=2.47895703\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},
$$
and
$$
\Delta G=-30.5+2.47895703\ln(10^{-4})
\approx-53.332\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
Increasing $Q$ tenfold raises $\Delta G$ by $RT\ln10\approx5.708\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at this <temperature>. Thus the <ATP> to <ADP> ratio and available <inorganic phosphate> matter, even with the same <standard state> value. These are local molar predictions at the stipulated composition, rather than a constant total yield as an isolated sample reacts.

= Adenosine diphosphate
{parent=Nucleotide}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{ADP}$}

= ADP
{c}
{synonym}

<ADP> contains the same <adenosine> unit as <ATP>, with two <phosphate> groups instead of three. One <ATP> converted by the stated <hydrolysis> gives one <ADP> and one <inorganic phosphate>: $3=2+1$ accounts for the <phosphate> groups while <adenine> and <ribose> are retained. <ADP> is therefore a reusable material carrier, rather than <ATP> with its entire structure discarded.

Regeneration reverses the net <chemical reaction>:
$$
\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i\longrightarrow\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}.
$$
At the same stipulated reference conditions its $\Delta G^{\circ\prime}$ is $+30.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, the negative of the <hydrolysis> value. It is an <endergonic reaction> and requires <energy> input through <energy coupling>; a <catalyst> alone cannot supply that input. Recycling the material carrier does not recycle the expended driving <energy>. <Cellular respiration> and <light-dependent reactions> supply routes for regeneration. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/[Cooper, The Cell: ATP generation and energy coupling].

<ADP> is a <nucleotide> with two <phosphate> groups. Formation of <ATP> from <ADP> and <phosphate> couples to <energy> supply in <light-dependent reactions>; the <Calvin cycle> returns <ADP> after using <ATP>. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: recycling ATP and ADP]{ref}.

= Adenosine monophosphate
{parent=Nucleotide}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{AMP}$}

= AMP
{c}
{synonym}

<AMP> is <adenosine> carrying one <phosphate> group at the <ribose>'s $5'$ position. The count distinguishes <AMP> from <ADP> with two and <ATP> with three; <adenosine> itself has none. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/[Cooper, The Cell: ATP, ADP, and AMP].

= Nucleotide base
{parent=Molecule}
{wiki}

= Nucleobase
{synonym}

= Adenine
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm A$}
{wiki}

= Guanine
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm G$}
{wiki}

= Cytosine
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Thymine
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm T$}
{wiki}

= Uracil
{parent=Nucleotide base}
{title2=$\mathrm U$}
{wiki}

= Quantum chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Electron density
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{title2=$n(\mathbf r)$}

The electron density describes the expected number of <electrons> per unit <volume> at position $\mathbf r$. For a system with a fixed number $N$ of <electrons>, $\int n(\mathbf r)\,d^3\mathbf r=N$. A <Lewis structure> allocates electron counts without specifying this spatial density.

= Electron configuration
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}

An <electron configuration> records the occupation of <electron shells> and <electron subshells> by <electrons>. For neutral <sodium> in its <ground state>, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6\,3s^1$ gives $2+2+6+1=11$ <electrons>; adding the occupations within each <electron shell> gives $(2,8,1)$. A superscript counts <electrons>, rather than multiplying the preceding labels. Removing the outer <electron> gives the <sodium> <ion> $\mathrm{Na}^{+}$ with $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6$, or $(2,8)$. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, electron configurations of atoms and ions].

= Electron shell
{parent=Electron configuration}
{title2=$n$}
{wiki}

An <electron shell> collects the allowed electronic <quantum states> sharing a <principal quantum number> $n$. Its occupancy is the number of <electrons> assigned to those states in an <electron configuration>. An <electron shell> is not a material surface or a prescribed orbit.

= Electron subshell
{parent=Electron shell}

An <electron subshell> groups the allowed electronic <quantum states> within an <electron shell> by their <orbital angular momentum> quantum number $\ell$. Labels $s$ and $p$ mean $\ell=0$ and $\ell=1$, respectively; their capacities are two and six <electrons>. The leading number in $2p$ identifies the <electron shell>, while $p$ identifies the <electron subshell>.

= Atomic orbital
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\phi_{n\ell m}(\mathbf r)$}

An atomic orbital is a one-<electron> spatial <wavefunction> used to describe an <electron>'s state in a <chemical atom>. For a one-electron <hydrogen atom>, orbitals can be exact <energy eigenstates>; descriptions of many-electron atoms instead use an orbital approximation. A normalized atomic orbital has $\int|\phi|^2\,d^3\mathbf r=1$, and the <Born rule> gives the position <probability density> $|\phi|^2$. It is a function extending through space, not a prescribed classical orbit. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-3-development-of-quantum-theory[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 6.3: atomic orbitals and the wavefunction description]{ref}

The <linear combination of atomic orbitals> combines these single-centre functions to approximate a <molecular orbital>. The <orbital overlap> measures their <inner product>, and their relative <quantum phase> controls the <interference> shown in <molecular orbital theory>.

= Molecular orbital theory
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}

<Molecular orbital theory> describes the electronic arrangement of a <molecule> using <molecular orbitals>: one-<electron> spatial <wavefunctions> extending over the combined <atomic nuclei>. Filling <bonding molecular orbitals> can stabilize a <chemical bond>; filling <antibonding molecular orbitals> opposes that stabilization. A <molecular orbital> is a building block of a many-<electron> description, rather than the entire many-<electron> <wavefunction>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/ef9da1132ff6233d732a1d6115099467_MIT5_61F17_lec24.pdf[Robert W. Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 24, pp. 1–4: molecular orbitals and the LCAO approximation].

First make the <interference> visible with an exactly normalized one-dimensional toy. Replace the three-dimensional $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom> by two <Gaussian functions> centred at $x=\pm a$, with <dimensionless> $x$ and $a>0$:
$$
\phi_A(x)=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-(x+a)^2/2},\qquad
\phi_B(x)=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-(x-a)^2/2},\qquad
S=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\phi_A\phi_B\,dx=e^{-a^2}.
$$
The normalized sum and difference are $\psi_\pm=(\phi_A\pm\phi_B)/\sqrt{2(1\pm S)}$, following the same <linear combination of atomic orbitals> algebra. At $a=1$, the <orbital overlap> is $S=e^{-1}\approx0.367879$. At the midpoint, $\phi_A(0)=\phi_B(0)\approx0.455581$, giving
$$
\psi_+(0)\approx0.550879,\qquad |\psi_+(0)|^2\approx0.303468,\qquad
\psi_-(0)=|\psi_-(0)|^2=0.
$$
Both <probability densities> have total <integral> one: <interference> redistributes <probability>, rather than creating an extra <electron>. These are toy <wavefunctions>, not actual <hydrogen atom> orbitals or predictions of <molecular hydrogen> <energies>.

\Image[molecular-orbital-interference.png]
{title=Normalized one-dimensional <Gaussian function> toy: signed <probability amplitudes> add or cancel before their squares give <probability densities>}
{description=Centres at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ give <orbital overlap> $S=e^{-1}$. The difference has a zero at the midpoint; its negative lobe does not carry positive <electric charge>. All density curves shown are normalized on the line, rather than in three-dimensional space.}

Now fill the two $1s$-derived <molecular orbitals> in the minimal model. The lower <bonding molecular orbital> is labelled $\sigma_g$; the upper <antibonding molecular orbital> is $\sigma_u^*$. The <Pauli exclusion principle> permits at most two <electrons> in each spatial <molecular orbital>, with opposite <spin> projections along a chosen axis. For the lowest <electron configuration> in this model, fill $\sigma_g$ first. The subscripts $g$ and $u$ denote <even> and <odd> behaviour under inversion through the midpoint; the star marks an <antibonding molecular orbital>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 8.4: occupancy and bond order] and https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–3: bonding, antibonding, and symmetry labels].

With $N_b$ <electrons> in <bonding molecular orbitals> and $N_a$ in <antibonding molecular orbitals>, the model's <bond order> is $b=(N_b-N_a)/2$. The three elementary counts are:
* <Molecular hydrogen>, $\mathrm H_2$: two <electrons> give $(\sigma_g)^2$ and $b=(2-0)/2=1$.
* The <hydrogen> molecular <ion> $\mathrm H_2^+$: one <electron> gives $(\sigma_g)^1$ and $b=(1-0)/2=1/2$.
* Two neutral <helium> <chemical atoms>, $\mathrm{He}_2$: four <electrons> give $(\sigma_g)^2(\sigma_u^*)^2$ and $b=(2-2)/2=0$ in this minimal $1s$ model.

\Image[molecular-orbital-occupancy.png]
{title=Schematic <molecular orbital> occupancy for $\mathrm H_2$, $\mathrm H_2^+$, and $\mathrm{He}_2$: <bond orders> one, one half, and zero}
{description=Each horizontal line is one spatial <molecular orbital>; paired arrows represent opposite <spin> projections. Vertical positions show ordering only, with arbitrary gaps rather than measured <energies> or a common scale across species.}

The zero <bond order> says this minimal model gives no net <covalent bond> for $\mathrm{He}_2$. It does not rule out every bound pair of <helium> <chemical atoms>: a very weak <van der Waals force> can bind a neutral <helium> dimer, which has been observed experimentally. See https://www.atom.uni-frankfurt.de/publications/files/ZellerPNAS-2016.pdf[Zeller et al., Imaging the He2 quantum halo state using a free electron laser, PNAS 113, 14651–14655 (2016), p. 14651: van der Waals binding] and https://scholarworks.gvsu.edu/chm_articles/6/[Luo et al., The weakest bond: Experimental observation of helium dimer, Journal of Chemical Physics 98, 3564–3567 (1993)]. The <bond order> count does not include that weak binding mechanism.

The useful surprise is that sharing alone does not determine a <chemical bond>: the relative <quantum phase> and the occupancy matter together. The same two shapes explain why adding an <electron> to a <bonding molecular orbital> increases the model's <bond order>, while adding one to an <antibonding molecular orbital> decreases it. <Molecular orbital theory> also accounts for the unpaired <electrons> and <paramagnetism> of <molecular oxygen>, which its all-paired <Lewis structure> misses. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Chemistry 2e, 8.4: the molecular orbital description of oxygen].

= Molecular orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{title2=$\psi(\mathbf r)$}
{wiki}

A <molecular orbital> is a one-<electron> spatial <wavefunction> for the combined <atomic nuclei> of a <molecule>. For a normalized <molecular orbital>, the <Born rule> gives the position <probability density> $|\psi(\mathbf r)|^2$, with $\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\psi|^2\,d^3\mathbf r=1$. The <molecular orbital> is the amplitude, rather than its squared <modulus> or a classical trajectory.

In the independent-orbital approximation, a doubly occupied <molecular orbital> contributes $2|\psi|^2$ to the <electron> number density, whose <integral> is two. An empty <molecular orbital> contributes zero, even though its shape is still part of the model. The <molecular hydrogen> example therefore uses one occupied spatial <molecular orbital> for two <electrons>, distinguished by their <spins>. A negative real <probability amplitude> means a relative <quantum phase> of $\pi$, not positive <electric charge>; multiplying the whole <wavefunction> by $-1$ changes only its <global phase>.

= Bonding orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital}
{title2=$\sigma_g$}
{wiki}

= Bonding molecular orbital
{synonym}

Occupying a <bonding molecular orbital> favours a <chemical bond>. For the two equivalent $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom>, the sum $\sigma_g=\sigma_+$ has constructive <interference> between the <protons>. The resulting <electron> density in that region helps stabilize the combined system. Its lower <energy> in this model follows from the <quantum Hamiltonian>, not from the plus sign alone; see https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–2].

For example, $(\sigma_g)^2$ in <molecular hydrogen> supplies $N_b=2$, hence <bond order> one when $N_a=0$. The sum in the <molecular orbital theory> toy has midpoint <probability density> $0.303468$, whereas the difference has zero there. The normalization factors must be included in this comparison.

= Antibonding orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital}
{title2=$\sigma_u^*$}
{wiki}

= Antibonding molecular orbital
{synonym}

Occupying an <antibonding molecular orbital> opposes a <chemical bond>. For equivalent $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom>, the difference $\sigma_u^*=\sigma_-$ vanishes on the plane halfway between the <protons>: every point there is equally far from both centres, so the amplitudes cancel. This nodal plane removes the contribution of this <molecular orbital> to <electron> density on that plane; it does not make an entire finite region empty. The $1s$-derived <antibonding molecular orbital> lies above its <bonding molecular orbital> partner in <energy>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–2].

In the $\mathrm{He}_2$ minimal model, $(\sigma_u^*)^2$ adds $N_a=2$ and cancels the <bond order> contribution of $(\sigma_g)^2$. Opposite signs on the two lobes describe <probability amplitudes>, while both lobes have nonnegative <probability density> after squaring. The star is an orbital label, not an extra <electron> or an <electric charge>.

= Linear combination of atomic orbitals
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{wiki}

= LCAO
{c}
{synonym}

The <LCAO> approximation builds a <molecular orbital> as a <linear combination> of single-centre <atomic orbitals>. For two equivalent normalized real $1s$ functions $\phi_A$ and $\phi_B$, choose their signs positive and define their <orbital overlap> $S$. At nonzero separation, $0<S<1$, and the normalized combinations are
$$
\sigma_\pm(\mathbf r)=\frac{\phi_A(\mathbf r)\pm\phi_B(\mathbf r)}{\sqrt{2(1\pm S)}},\qquad
S=\int_{\mathbb R^3}\phi_A(\mathbf r)\phi_B(\mathbf r)\,d^3\mathbf r.
$$
Here $\sigma_+=\sigma_g$ and $\sigma_-=\sigma_u^*$. Equal coefficients use the equivalence of the two centres; a general <LCAO> need not have equal coefficients. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/ef9da1132ff6233d732a1d6115099467_MIT5_61F17_lec24.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 24, pp. 3–6: LCAO coefficients and overlap integrals].

For the <Gaussian function> toy's $a=1$, the two denominators are $\sqrt{2(1+S)}\approx1.654013$ and $\sqrt{2(1-S)}\approx1.124385$. To check <wavefunction normalization>, expand the square: $\int(\phi_A\pm\phi_B)^2=1+1\pm2S$. Dividing by $2(1\pm S)$ gives one. The local <probability density> is
$$
|\sigma_\pm|^2=\frac{\phi_A^2+\phi_B^2\pm2\phi_A\phi_B}{2(1\pm S)}.
$$
The cross term is the <interference>; adding the separate densities first would lose it. These formulas normalize the chosen <linear combination> exactly, while the restricted choice of <atomic orbitals> remains an approximation to an actual <molecular orbital>.

= Orbital overlap
{parent=Linear combination of atomic orbitals}
{title2=$S$}

The <orbital overlap> of normalized real <atomic orbitals> is their <inner product> $S=\int\phi_A\phi_B$. It is a <dimensionless> measure of their common amplitude, not an <electron> count. For the <Gaussian function> toy, increasing $a$ from $1$ to $2$ reduces $S$ from $e^{-1}\approx0.367879$ to $e^{-4}\approx0.018316$. As $S$ tends to zero, both <LCAO> normalization denominators tend to $\sqrt2$.

= Bond order
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{title2=$b=(N_b-N_a)/2$}
{wiki}

In the elementary <molecular orbital theory> count, <bond order> is half the excess of <electrons> in <bonding molecular orbitals> over those in <antibonding molecular orbitals>. Thus adding one <electron> to a <bonding molecular orbital> raises $b$ by $1/2$, while adding one to an <antibonding molecular orbital> lowers it by $1/2$. The configurations drawn above give $1$, $1/2$, and $0$. This count summarizes net <covalent bond> occupancy; it is neither a measured <energy> nor a universal test for every kind of binding. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Chemistry 2e, 8.4: molecular orbital bond order].

= Chemical bond
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <chemical bond> associates <chemical atoms> or groups of <chemical atoms> into a sufficiently stable structure to treat it as a distinct entity. The concept covers connections within <molecules> and in extended solids; see the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/CT07009[IUPAC Gold Book, chemical bond]. It is useful because composition alone does not specify how the <chemical atoms> are connected, and those connections help determine a <chemical substance>'s properties and possible <chemical reactions>.

In a <covalent bond>, <electrons> are shared between <chemical atoms>. Two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> can form a <molecular hydrogen> <molecule>, with its <electrons> interacting with both <atomic nuclei>. In the usual description of this diatomic <molecule>, the <potential energy> as a function of nuclear separation has a minimum: moving the nuclei too close or too far raises the <energy>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.2 Covalent Bonding>.

Separating bonded <chemical atoms> requires <energy>; forming the corresponding <chemical bonds> releases <energy>. Whether an overall <chemical reaction> releases <energy> depends on both the <chemical bonds> broken and those formed. The lines in the <stoichiometry> diagram indicate connections schematically, rather than literal rods or a sequence of microscopic events.

= Bond-dissociation energy
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$D$}

A <bond-dissociation energy> specifies the <energy> needed for a stated dissociation into separated fragments. An <energy> from the lowest vibrational state, an <energy> from the bottom of a <potential energy> well, and a finite-temperature molar dissociation <enthalpy> require different conventions; do not silently mix them in an <electronegativity> formula.

Which fragments, in which <quantum states>, and at which <temperature>? For a neutral gaseous <molecule> with a specified two-electron <covalent bond>, the usual thermochemical convention is <homolysis>,
$$
\mathrm{A{-}B}(g)\longrightarrow\mathrm A^{\bullet}(g)+\mathrm B^{\bullet}(g).
$$
Each fragment receives one of the two bonding <electrons>; the dots denote unpaired <electrons>. The final fragments are isolated from each other, with their own relaxed <molecular geometry> and specified electronic <quantum states>. The <energy> cost includes this relaxation, rather than merely stretching the initial <chemical bond> while freezing everything else. For a neutral parent, <heterolysis> instead produces $\mathrm A^+(g)+\mathrm B^-(g)$, or the opposite assignment of <electric charges>; those are different final states and different <energies>. A value for gaseous neutral fragments cannot be transferred to <ions>, a <chemical solution>, or a <solid> without accounting for those changed conditions.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/bond-dissociation energy>[definition of bond-dissociation energy: molar enthalpy for homolysis of a specified molecular entity]{ref}

The distinction is useful beyond terminology. <Pauling electronegativity> compares consistently defined <bond-dissociation energies>; <photodissociation> can reveal a specified ground-state threshold; a thermal <chemical reaction> balance requires compatible <enthalpies>. A threshold is also different from an <activation energy>: it compares initial and final <quantum states>, whereas a barrier can obstruct the path between them.

= Bond potential-well depth
{parent=Bond-dissociation energy}
{title2=$D_e$}

For a <diatomic molecule>, let $V(r)$ be the electronic <potential energy> at <bond length> $r$, with the <atomic nuclei> treated as fixed while finding the electronic <energy>. Choose zero at infinitely separated <chemical atoms> in the specified electronic <quantum states>. If the minimum occurs at $r_e$, the <bond potential-well depth> is
$$
D_e=V(\infty)-V(r_e)=-V(r_e).
$$
The bottom of this curve describes an equilibrium <bond length>, not an attainable motionless vibrational <quantum state>. Changing the zero of <potential energy> shifts both endpoints equally and leaves $D_e$ unchanged. A <quantum harmonic oscillator> approximates the curve near its minimum but rises without limit, so that local approximation alone cannot supply a dissociation asymptote. A <Morse potential> supplies both a minimum and a finite separated-fragment limit.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/dissociation energy>[definition: subscripts e and 0 distinguish the potential-energy minimum and ground state]{ref}

= Ground-state dissociation energy
{parent=Bond-dissociation energy}
{title2=$D_0$}

The <ground-state dissociation energy> starts at the lowest vibrational <quantum state>, with the lowest allowed rotational <quantum state> and the fragment states explicitly specified. For a <diatomic molecule> whose fragments are ground-state <chemical atoms>, omitting any additional rotational correction,
$$
D_0=D_e-E_{\rm ZP},
$$
where $E_{\rm ZP}$ is the vibrational <zero-point energy> measured upward from the well minimum. The initial <molecule> already has this <energy>, so less additional <energy> is needed to reach the same final limit.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/bond-dissociation energy in theoretical chemistry>[definition of bond-dissociation energy in theoretical chemistry: D0 = De minus zero-point vibrational energy]{ref}

For fragments that are themselves <molecules>, include their <zero-point energies> too. Define $D_e$ as the electronic <energy> difference between separately relaxed products and reactant; then, with consistent rotational conventions,
$$
D_0=D_e+\sum_{\rm fragments}E_{\rm ZP}-E_{\rm ZP,parent}.
$$
Thus “subtract the parent's <zero-point energy>” is sufficient for atomic products but generally incomplete for molecular products. An excited final <quantum state> raises its channel threshold by its excitation <energy>; that threshold does not become the ground-product $D_0$ just because a detector selects it.

= Bond-dissociation enthalpy
{parent=Bond-dissociation energy}
{title2=$\Delta_{\rm diss}H^{\circ}(T)$}

A <bond-dissociation enthalpy> is the molar <enthalpy> change for the specified <homolysis> at <temperature> $T$, with reactant and products in specified <standard states>. Its usual units are $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The superscript $\circ$ specifies <standard states>; it does not fix $T$ to room <temperature>.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/bond-dissociation enthalpy>[definition and symbols: standard molar enthalpy for homolysis, Δdiss H°]{ref}

Write $\delta h_i(T)=H_{m,i}^{\circ}(T)-H_{m,i}^{\circ}(0)$ for each species' molar thermal <enthalpy> increment. With $D_0$ an <energy> per parent <molecule>,
$$
\Delta_{\rm diss}H^{\circ}(T)=N_{\rm A}D_0+\delta h_A(T)+\delta h_B(T)-\delta h_{AB}(T).
$$
Here $N_{\rm A}$ is the <Avogadro constant>. The thermal increments include translational <kinetic energy>, <molecular rotation>, thermally excited <molecular vibration> and electronic states when populated, and the $pV$ contribution to <enthalpy>. They are additional to the <zero-point energies> already included in $D_0$. If values of <standard enthalpy of formation> at the same $T$ are available, <Hess's law> gives the same quantity as $\Delta_fH^{\circ}(A)+\Delta_fH^{\circ}(B)-\Delta_fH^{\circ}(AB)$.

Units alone do not identify the convention. Multiplying either $D_e$ or $D_0$ by the <Avogadro constant> gives molar <energy>, but does not add thermal <enthalpy>. One <electronvolt> per dissociation corresponds to $96.485332\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. A <spectroscopic wavenumber> $\widetilde\nu$ represents <energy> $hc\widetilde\nu$, using the <Planck constant> $h$ and <speed of light> $c$; $1\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ corresponds to $0.011962657\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.<Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>[SI defining constants h, c, e and Avogadro constant; conversions calculated from them]{ref}

= Stipulated bond dissociation model
{parent=Bond-dissociation energy}

Take an invented <diatomic molecule> with a <Morse potential>, setting the separated-atom <energy> to zero:
$$
V(r)=D_e\left[\left(1-e^{-a(r-r_e)}\right)^2-1\right],\qquad
D_e=2.0000\,\mathrm{eV},\qquad A=\hbar\omega_e=0.2000\,\mathrm{eV}.
$$
Here $a$ is an inverse length, $r_e$ the equilibrium <bond length>, and $\omega_e=a\sqrt{2D_e/\mu}$, with <reduced mass> $\mu$. These stipulated parameters describe a simple model, not a measured <chemical substance>. For the one-dimensional <Morse oscillator>, <energy eigenvalues> above the minimum are
$$
\epsilon_v=A\left(v+\tfrac12\right)-\frac{A^2}{4D_e}\left(v+\tfrac12\right)^2,
\qquad v=0,1,\ldots,\quad v+\tfrac12<\frac{2D_e}{A}.
$$
Consequently,
$$
E_{\rm ZP}=\frac A2-\frac{A^2}{16D_e}=0.09875\,\mathrm{eV},\qquad
D_0=2.0000-0.09875=1.90125\,\mathrm{eV}.
$$
The harmonic estimate $A/2=0.1000\,\mathrm{eV}$ is slightly larger than this model's exact <zero-point energy>. The physical bound-level restriction matters: the quadratic expression is not an infinite ladder of vibrational <quantum states>.

\Image[bond-dissociation-well-and-zero-point.png]
{id=image-bond-dissociation-well-and-zero-point}
{title=<Bond potential-well depth> and <ground-state dissociation energy> reach the same separated-atom limit from different starting <energies>}
{description=Original stipulated <Morse potential> with $D_e=2\,\mathrm{eV}$ and $A=0.2\,\mathrm{eV}$. The horizontal coordinate is $a(r-r_e)$; the horizontal $v=0$ line is an <energy eigenvalue>, not a classical trajectory or a measured spectrum.}

For a separate thermal illustration using this same stipulated $D_0$, assume an <ideal gas>, fully active <molecular rotation> of the parent, negligible thermal <molecular vibration>, and negligible electronic excitation for all species. Each monatomic product contributes $\delta h=5RT/2$; the <diatomic molecule> contributes $7RT/2$. Thus the net increment is $3RT/2$, with <molar gas constant> $R$. At $T=300\,\mathrm K$,
$$
N_{\rm A}D_e\approx192.971\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\qquad
N_{\rm A}D_0\approx183.443\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\qquad
\Delta_{\rm diss}H^{\circ}\approx187.184\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
This last result follows from the stated thermal model, not a universal correction for every <chemical bond>. Frozen rotations, accessible excited states, different fragment composition, or a condensed <phase> change the thermal increments.

= Sequential bond-dissociation energies
{parent=Bond-dissociation energy}
{title2=$D_1,D_2,\ldots$}

Equivalent <chemical bonds> in an intact <molecule> need not have equal successive <bond-dissociation enthalpies>. Once one <chemical bond> is removed, the next reactant has a different electronic <quantum state> and may relax to a different <molecular geometry>. The successive steps compare different species. This distinction also explains why a tabulated average <chemical bond> strength is not necessarily the cost of the first cleavage.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.5 Strengths of Ionic and Covalent Bonds>[“Bond Strength: Covalent Bonds”, methane paragraph: equivalent initial bonds and unequal sequential breaking costs]{ref}

For an invented species $AB_2$, stipulate the following gas-phase steps, with all <enthalpies> at the same <temperature> and the same <standard states>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
AB_2(g)&\longrightarrow AB(g)+B(g),&D_1&=300\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\\
AB(g)&\longrightarrow A(g)+B(g),&D_2&=400\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The symbols stand for hypothetical species with consistently chosen electronic states. They are not values for <water> or another measured <molecule>. By <Hess's law>, the total <enthalpy of atomization> for $AB_2(g)\to A(g)+2B(g)$ is $700\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$; the <arithmetic mean> per broken <chemical bond> is $350\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Neither individual step equals that mean. Nor must the second step be cheaper: here it is deliberately more expensive. An exact sequence sums exactly; substituting averages taken from other <molecules> generally gives only an estimate.

\Image[bond-energy-conventions-and-sequential-cleavage.png]
{id=image-bond-energy-conventions-and-sequential-cleavage}
{title=Two stipulated examples: changing the <energy> convention for one dissociation, and changing the reactant during sequential cleavage}
{description=Left: $N_{\rm A}D_e$, $N_{\rm A}D_0$, and the model <bond-dissociation enthalpy> at $300\,\mathrm K$. Right: cumulative <enthalpy> levels $0,300,700\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ for two successive steps, with costs $300$ and $400$ and mean $350$. The panels are separate hypothetical systems; neither contains molecular measurements.}

= Homolysis
{parent=Chemical bond}

<Homolysis> splits a <covalent bond> with one bonding <electron> assigned to each fragment. A neutral closed-shell <molecule> thereby produces fragments with unpaired <electrons>, each a <radical (chemistry)>. The usual <bond-dissociation enthalpy> uses this product convention.

= Heterolysis
{parent=Chemical bond}

<Heterolysis> assigns both bonding <electrons> to one fragment. For an initially neutral <molecule>, the resulting <cation> and <anion> define a different dissociation channel from <homolysis>; their isolated gas-phase <energy> differs from that of solvated <ions>.

= Photodissociation
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

<Photodissociation> separates a <molecule> into fragments after <photon absorption>. For an isolated parent with a single absorbed <photon>, <conservation of energy> gives
$$
h\nu+E_{\rm initial,int}=D_0+E_{\rm products,int}+E_{\rm recoil}.
$$
Internal excitation <energies> here are relative to the parent and product ground states used to define $D_0$; $E_{\rm recoil}$ is the total product translational <kinetic energy> in the parent's <centre of mass> frame. Measuring one product's <kinetic energy> is insufficient by itself. For two fragments of <masses> $m_A,m_B$, <conservation of momentum> gives $E_{\rm recoil}=E_A(m_A+m_B)/m_B$ when $A$ is detected. Unknown product excitation can conceal part of the available <energy>.

= Photofragment-ion imaging
{parent=Photodissociation}
{wiki}

<Photofragment-ion imaging> detects fragment <velocities> by ionizing neutral products and mapping their motion onto a position-sensitive detector using an <electric field>. In a velocity-mapping implementation, the image radius measures transverse speed under fixed lens settings. Selecting an arrival-time slice helps extract the central plane of the three-dimensional distribution. Species identification and electronic <quantum state> selection are essential for turning a radius into a particular dissociation threshold.

= Nitrogen dissociation threshold from fragment images
{parent=Photofragment-ion imaging}

A <molecular nitrogen> experiment used a $0.5\,\mathrm{mm}$ pulsed nozzle and two skimmers. A $5\,\mathrm{cm}$ tube raised the rotational <temperature>. Opposed vacuum-ultraviolet <laser> pulses excited selected molecular levels and, about $10\,\mathrm{ns}$ later, selectively ionized excited <nitrogen> fragments. A <microchannel plate detector>, phosphor screen and camera recorded radii.<Accurate measurements of the bond dissociation energies of 14N2, 14N15N and 15N2 by Liya Lu and others>[Lu, Jiang and Gao, §2, printed manuscript pp. 2–3: nozzle, tube, pump/probe and imaging readout]{ref}

The measured squared radii were fitted against molecular excitation <energies>, referenced to the parent <ground state>:
$$
E_j=D_{0,\rm channel}+\kappa R_j^2.
$$
The intercept at zero radius gave the excited-product threshold; the fitted slope absorbed the detector scale. A wavemeter monitored the excitation source; scanning the probe covered the recoil <Doppler shift>. Subtracting the atomic excitation <energies> converted the intercept to ground-product $D_0$.<Accurate measurements of the bond dissociation energies of 14N2, 14N15N and 15N2 by Liya Lu and others>[§1: image-radius equation; §2: wavemeter and probe scan; §3 and Figure 4: fits; §4: atomic excitation correction]{ref}

An apertured grounded plate and detector timing rejected faster competing channels. Selecting fragment electronic states distinguished nearby thresholds. Agreement of $D_e$ across <isotopes> after <zero-point energy> correction tested consistency. The method still requires correctly assigned levels, adequate near-threshold signal and stable imaging; a signal drop alone can reflect another channel or a barrier.<Accurate measurements of the bond dissociation energies of 14N2, 14N15N and 15N2 by Liya Lu and others>[§2: shielding plate and timing; §3: state-selected channels; §4 and Table 2: isotope consistency and limitations]{ref}

This measurement determines a state-resolved <ground-state dissociation energy>. Obtaining a finite-<temperature> <bond-dissociation enthalpy> requires the thermal increments above; obtaining a <bond potential-well depth> requires the <zero-point energy> correction. The three quantities describe different starting conditions for the same specified separation.

= Electronegativity
{parent=Chemical bond}
{title2=$\chi$}
{wiki}

= Electronegative
{synonym}

<Electronegativity> describes a <chemical atom>'s tendency to attract <electron density> towards itself in a <chemical bond>. It helps answer a useful question: when two different <chemical atoms> share <electrons>, which end of the <chemical bond> tends to be richer in <electron density>? For <hydrogen chloride>, the answer is <chlorine>: the <covalent bond> is a <polar covalent bond>, conventionally written $\mathrm H^{\delta+}-\mathrm{Cl}^{\delta-}$. The symbols indicate <partial atomic charges>, not isolated <ions> carrying whole elementary <electric charges>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.2, “Pure vs. Polar Covalent Bonds”, HCl example]{ref}

<Electronegativity> is a comparison scheme, not the number of <electrons> on a <chemical atom>. A <partial atomic charge> depends on how the <electron density> is partitioned; <formal charge> instead follows an integer allocation in a <Lewis structure>. Neither is numerically equal to <electronegativity>. Nor is <electronegativity> identical to <electron affinity>, which concerns adding an <electron> to an isolated species. Different <electronegativity> scales use different measured inputs and conventions; compare numbers only after specifying the scale.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/electronegativity>{ref}

For a concrete comparison, the Royal Society of Chemistry lists <Pauling electronegativities> $\chi_{\rm P}(\mathrm H)=2.20$ and $\chi_{\rm P}(\mathrm{Cl})=3.16$. Their difference is $0.96$, so the <electronegativity> rule predicts <bond polarization> towards <chlorine> in <hydrogen chloride>. An isolated <molecular hydrogen> <molecule> has identical ends and zero <electronegativity> difference. These examples explain a direction of unequal sharing, not a quantitative <partial atomic charge>.<Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry/Hydrogen — Element information, properties and uses>{ref}<Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry/Chlorine — Element information, properties and uses>{ref}

\Image[electronegativity-scales-hydrogen-chlorine.png]
{id=electronegativity-scales-hydrogen-chlorine}
{title=<Pauling electronegativity> and <Mulliken electronegativity> for <hydrogen> and <chlorine>: separate scales, separate units; the <Mulliken electronegativity> values are calculated from the stated ground-state inputs}

A larger <electronegativity> difference often suggests stronger <bond polarization>, but there is no universal numerical boundary where a <covalent bond> suddenly becomes <ionic bonding>. Tables of cutoffs are classification aids with exceptions. <Hydrogen fluoride>, for example, is described by a <polar covalent bond> even when its <electronegativity> difference exceeds a textbook's nominal ionic threshold. The actual <electron density>, physical setting, and <chemical substance>'s structure matter.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.2, “Electronegativity and Bond Type”, general-guide warning and HF exception]{ref}

The distinction from <ionization energy> is equally useful: an <ionization energy> measures the cost of removing an <electron> from a specified isolated species, whereas <electronegativity> compares attraction for <electron density> in a bonded environment. Neither the removal cost alone nor the attachment release alone supplies a unique bonded-atom <electronegativity>. A scale combines selected measured inputs with a model and a convention.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/electronegativity>[E01990: multiple definitions; Mulliken average versus Pauling bond-energy differences]{ref}

= Pauling electronegativity
{c}
{parent=Electronegativity}
{title2=$\chi_{\rm P}$}

= Pauling scale
{c}
{synonym}

<Pauling electronegativity> is a relative, <dimensionless> scale motivated by <bond-dissociation energies>. The interesting observation is that a <chemical bond> between unlike <chemical atoms> can be stronger than a simple baseline inferred from the corresponding like-atom <chemical bonds>. An empirical excess <energy> can then be associated with an <electronegativity> difference. The 1932 paper <The Nature of the Chemical Bond. IV. The Energy of Single Bonds and the Relative Electronegativity of Atoms> built such a map; its original coordinates used <hydrogen> as zero, so those coordinates should not be mistaken for the modern tabulated <Pauling electronegativities>.<The Nature of the Chemical Bond. IV. The Energy of Single Bonds and the Relative Electronegativity of Atoms by Linus Pauling>[pp. 3576–3578, equation (1), Table III: H 0.00, Cl 0.94]{ref}

One arithmetic-baseline version of the <Pauling scale> relation is
$$
\Delta_D=D_{AB}-\frac{D_{AA}+D_{BB}}{2},\qquad
\left|\chi_{\rm P}(A)-\chi_{\rm P}(B)\right|\approx\sqrt{\frac{\Delta_D}{1\,\mathrm{eV}}}.
$$
Here $D_{AB}$, $D_{AA}$ and $D_{BB}$ are consistently defined <bond-dissociation energies> per <molecule>, and the <arithmetic mean> is the chosen baseline. With molar <energies> instead, write $\sqrt{\Delta_D/(96.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}})}$, approximately $0.102\sqrt{\Delta_D/(1\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}})}$. The denominator makes the result <dimensionless>; the factor $0.102$ is not a unit-independent magic constant. This is an empirical relation with a specified baseline, not a definition valid for every <chemical bond>.<Energy Electronegativity and Chemical Bonding by Stepan S. Batsanov>[§1 equation (1), coefficients for eV, kcal/mol and kJ/mol; §2.2, dependence on other bond properties]{ref}

For an explicitly synthetic example, suppose $D_{AA}=D_{BB}=300\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ and $D_{AB}=396.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ with the same temperature and dissociation convention. Then $\Delta_D=96.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ and the model gives an <electronegativity> difference of $1$. Replacing $D_{AB}$ by $300\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ gives zero; a negative excess would make this real square-root model inapplicable, not produce a negative <electronegativity> difference. These invented inputs illustrate the formula and are not measurements of named <chemical substances>.

Only differences appear, so adding the same constant to every <Pauling electronegativity> leaves this relation unchanged. A reference value must fix the origin. The <bond-dissociation energies> alone also do not tell which endpoint attracts <electron density> more strongly, because interchanging $A$ and $B$ leaves the formula unchanged. Chemical comparisons fix that direction. Thus <Pauling electronegativity> is useful as a compact ordering, while the underlying measured <energies> and approximations remain essential.

= Pauling bond-energy excess
{c}
{parent=Pauling electronegativity}
{title2=$\Delta_D$}

A <Pauling bond-energy excess> subtracts a chosen like-atom baseline from the unlike-atom <bond-dissociation energy>. For an unequal-input arithmetic example, stipulate $D_{AA}=200$, $D_{BB}=450$ and $D_{AB}=421.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, all with one dissociation convention. The <arithmetic mean> baseline and excess are
$$
D_{\rm base}=\frac{200+450}{2}=325\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\qquad
\Delta_D=421.485-325=96.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
The arithmetic-baseline <Pauling scale> model therefore gives $|\chi_{\rm P}(A)-\chi_{\rm P}(B)|=\sqrt{96.485/96.485}=1$. The <bond-dissociation energy> $421.485$ is not itself the excess: subtracting the baseline is essential. All three numbers are stipulated, not experimental values for named <chemical elements>.

\Image[pauling-bond-energy-excess-construction.png]
{id=image-pauling-bond-energy-excess-construction}
{title=Constructing a <Pauling bond-energy excess> from stipulated <bond-dissociation energies> and converting it to a <dimensionless> <electronegativity> difference}

Changing the baseline changes the model. For the same stipulated inputs, a <geometric mean> baseline would be $\sqrt{200\times450}=300\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, yielding an excess of $121.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ and a difference of approximately $1.122$. It is a different construction, even though both start from the same <bond-dissociation energies>. State which baseline is used before comparing <electronegativity> differences.

The arithmetic-baseline <Pauling scale> also has a useful inverse: a difference of $0.5$ corresponds to an excess of $24.12125\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, and a difference of $2$ corresponds to $385.94\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Doubling the <electronegativity> difference quadruples the modeled excess. Interchanging the endpoint labels changes neither quantity; the <square root> alone cannot choose the direction of <bond polarization>.

In <The Nature of the Chemical Bond. IV. The Energy of Single Bonds and the Relative Electronegativity of Atoms> (printed pp. 3576–3578, Table II and equation (1)), <Linus Pauling> organized excess <energies> for <chemical bonds> into an approximately additive map of their <square roots>. He also identified a limitation: his H–F map separation of $2$ predicted an excess of $4.00\,\mathrm{eV}$, exceeding his table's value by $1.23\,\mathrm{eV}$. Those are historical values and a historical model discrepancy, not modern recommended inputs.<The Nature of the Chemical Bond. IV. The Energy of Single Bonds and the Relative Electronegativity of Atoms by Linus Pauling>[printed pp. 3576–3578, Table II, equation (1), and paragraph beginning “These coördinates”]{ref}

= Mulliken electronegativity
{c}
{parent=Electronegativity}
{title2=$\chi_{\rm M}$}

= Mulliken scale
{c}
{synonym}

<Mulliken electronegativity> uses both the cost of removing an <electron> and the <energy> released by accepting an <electron>. For specified isolated-atom states, the common ground-state convention is
$$
\chi_{\rm M}=\frac{I+A_{\rm ea}}{2},\qquad
I=E(X^+)-E(X),\qquad A_{\rm ea}=E(X)-E(X^-).
$$
The free <electron>'s <energy> is the zero reference. Here $I$ is the <ionization energy> and $A_{\rm ea}$ is the <electron affinity>, positive for exothermic attachment. If attachment is instead tabulated as the signed <energy> change $\Delta E_{\rm attach}=E(X^-)-E(X)$, the same expression is $(I-\Delta E_{\rm attach})/2$. Stating the sign convention prevents adding the wrong quantities. The 1934 original treated suitable valence states, which are not automatically the ground states used in this example.<A New Electroaffinity Scale; Together with Data on Valence States and on Valence Ionization Potentials and Electron Affinities by Robert S. Mulliken>{ref}

<Mulliken electronegativity> has <energy> units before any additional rescaling. With $I$ and $A_{\rm ea}$ in <electronvolts> per <chemical atom>, $\chi_{\rm M}$ is in <electronvolts>; with both in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, it is a molar <energy>. For <hydrogen>, the RSC ground-state inputs are $I=1312.05$ and $A_{\rm ea}=72.769\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, giving $\chi_{\rm M}=692.4095\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\approx7.176\,\mathrm{eV}$ per <chemical atom>. For <chlorine>, $I=1251.186$ and $A_{\rm ea}=348.575\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ give $799.8805\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\approx8.290\,\mathrm{eV}$. These are calculated ground-state <Mulliken electronegativities>, not the RSC's tabulated <Pauling electronegativities>.<Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry/Hydrogen — Element information, properties and uses>{ref}<Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry/Chlorine — Element information, properties and uses>{ref}

Both schemes place <chlorine> above <hydrogen> here, but their numerical differences cannot be subtracted from one another or equated. Dividing a molar <Mulliken electronegativity> by an <energy> conversion factor changes units; it does not transform it into a <Pauling electronegativity>. A further empirical mapping would require its own calibration and stated domain.

= Electron affinity
{parent=Mulliken electronegativity}
{wiki}
{title2=$A_{\rm ea}$}

<Electron affinity> measures how strongly an isolated neutral species binds an additional <electron>. In the spectroscopic convention used here, it is the positive <energy> required to detach that <electron> from a bound <anion>. With the free <electron> at rest assigned zero <energy>,
$$
A_{\rm ea}=E(X)+E(e^-)-E(X^-),\qquad
\Delta E_{\rm attach}=-A_{\rm ea}.
$$
The <quantum states> of $X$ and $X^-$ must be specified, usually their <ground states>. Positive $A_{\rm ea}$ means that attachment lowers the <energy>; a signed attachment <energy> is then negative. This convention agrees with the IUPAC definition and with the atomic tables used for <Mulliken electronegativity>.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/electron affinity>{ref}

For <chlorine>, a published <photodetachment threshold> is $A_{\rm ea}/(hc)=29138.59(22)\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$, approximately $3.612725\,\mathrm{eV}$ per <chemical atom>. Here $h$ is the <Planck constant>, $c$ the <speed of light>, and the inverse-length unit expresses an <energy> through a spectroscopic <wavenumber>. The parentheses give the reported uncertainty in the last digits, not a range of possible sign conventions.<Isotope shift in the electron affinity of chlorine by U. Berzinsh and others>[§2.3, printed pp. 6–7: reported threshold 29138.59(22) inverse centimetres]{ref}

\Image[electron-affinity-attachment-detachment-levels.png]
{id=image-electron-affinity-attachment-detachment-levels}
{title=<Electron affinity> of <chlorine>: positive detachment <energy> and negative attachment <energy> connect the same isolated-species levels; a finite-temperature <electron attachment enthalpy> needs additional corrections}

An <electron affinity> is neither an <electronegativity> nor a <lattice energy>. The first describes a specified isolated-species <energy> difference, <electronegativity> compares attraction for <electron density> in a <chemical bond>, and <lattice energy> refers to an entire <ionic crystal> and separated gaseous <ions>. A stable <anion> can therefore help explain <ionic bonding> without describing the full <energy> balance for making a <salt>.

= Electron attachment enthalpy
{parent=Electron affinity}
{title2=$\Delta H_{\rm attach}^{\circ}(T)$}

An <electron attachment enthalpy> is the signed molar <enthalpy> change for a stated attachment reaction,
$$
X(g)+e^-(g)\longrightarrow X^-(g).
$$
For favourable attachment such as <chlorine>'s first attachment, the <electron attachment enthalpy> is negative. Its leading isolated-ground-state <energy> contribution is $-N_{\rm A}A_{\rm ea}$, where $N_{\rm A}$ is the <Avogadro constant>. At finite <temperature>, consistent thermal populations, translational contributions and the $pV$ term in <enthalpy> must also be included, together with the same gaseous-<electron> reference used in the corresponding <ionization energy> term. Consequently a measured spectroscopic <electron affinity> is not automatically an exact $298.15\,\mathrm K$ attachment <enthalpy> with a minus sign. A <Born-Haber cycle> must use compatible <enthalpies> throughout.

= Photodetachment
{parent=Electron affinity}

<Photodetachment> removes an <electron> from an <anion> using a <photon>. For <chlorine>, the reaction is $\mathrm{Cl}^-(g)+\gamma\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cl}(g)+e^-$. Measuring the minimum <photon> <energy> for the specified final <quantum state> gives a <photodetachment threshold> and hence an <electron affinity>.

= Photodetachment threshold
{parent=Photodetachment}
{title2=$E_{\rm th}$}

In the published <chlorine> experiment, negative <ions> were accelerated and mass-selected, then overlapped with a tunable <laser> beam. A downstream <electric field> deflected charged <ions>; neutral <chemical atoms> continued to a plate, whose secondary <electrons> were counted. Scanning the <laser> <frequency> produced an onset in this neutral-atom signal. The apparatus therefore inferred an <electron affinity> from a threshold, rather than measuring the <heat> of attachment.<Isotope shift in the electron affinity of chlorine by U. Berzinsh and others>[§2, printed pp. 2–4, Figure 1: ion preparation, deflectors, neutral-atom secondary-emission detector and threshold fitting]{ref}

Reversing the <laser> direction and averaging the two thresholds corrected the first-order <Doppler shift>. A <Fabry–Pérot interferometer> and reference spectral lines calibrated the <frequency>. Finite <laser> bandwidth, unresolved <quantum state> splitting and shifts of the calibration lines limited the inference; the authors included these effects in fitting and the error estimate. The level diagram shows only the extracted gap, not a measured signal curve.<Isotope shift in the electron affinity of chlorine by U. Berzinsh and others>[§§2.1 and 2.3, printed pp. 3–7, equation (2), Figures 2 and 5: direction reversal, calibration, bandwidth and reference-line systematic shifts]{ref}

= Electronvolt
{parent=Mulliken electronegativity}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{eV}$}

An <electronvolt> is $1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm J$. One <electronvolt> per particle corresponds to approximately $96.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.<Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>{ref}

= Experimental inputs to electronegativity scales
{parent=Electronegativity}

The contrast between <Pauling electronegativity> and <Mulliken electronegativity> becomes clearer by following the measurements into the formulas. A <Pauling bond-energy excess> compares three compatible <bond-dissociation energies>; <Mulliken electronegativity> takes the <arithmetic mean> of an <ionization energy> and an <electron affinity>. The former asks how much stronger an unlike-atom <chemical bond> is than a stipulated baseline; the latter combines two different isolated-species <energy> gaps.

Actual readouts precede those model choices. In the <nitrogen dissociation threshold from fragment images>, fitted squared image radii versus assigned excitation <energies> reveal a threshold after accounting for the product <quantum states>. In the <chlorine> <photodetachment threshold> experiment, mass-selected <anions> overlap a tunable <laser>, and neutral-atom counts reveal the onset of <electron> detachment. Opposite <laser> directions control the <Doppler shift>. That second threshold supplies an <electron affinity>, not an <electronegativity>; the <Mulliken scale> still needs an independent <ionization energy>.<Accurate measurements of the bond dissociation energies of 14N2, 14N15N and 15N2 by Liya Lu and others>[§1, recoil-energy/image-radius equation; §3.2, Figure 5, squared image sizes versus term energies; §3.2, threshold channel assignment]{ref}<Isotope shift in the electron affinity of chlorine by U. Berzinsh and others>[§2, printed pp. 2–4, Figure 1: mass selection and neutral counting; §2.1, direction reversal]{ref}

For an original arithmetic comparison, stipulate isolated-species $I=(8,7,6)\,\mathrm{eV}$ and positive attachment-release $A_{\rm ea}=(0.5,3,3)\,\mathrm{eV}$ for fictitious species $A,B,C$. Their <Mulliken electronegativities> are $(4.25,5,4.5)\,\mathrm{eV}$. The largest <ionization energy> belongs to $A$, while the largest <Mulliken electronegativity> belongs to $B$: accepting an <electron> matters as well as retaining one. Using signed attachment <energies> $(-0.5,-3,-3)\,\mathrm{eV}$ in $(I-\Delta E_{\rm attach})/2$ gives exactly the same results.

\Image[electronegativity-scale-inputs-and-orderings.png]
{id=image-electronegativity-scale-inputs-and-orderings}
{title=Stipulated inputs to <Mulliken electronegativity> and arithmetic-baseline <Pauling electronegativity>: different <energies>, units and orderings}

Independently stipulate $D_{RR}=300$ and $D_{XX}=(200,300,450)\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ for $X=A,B,C$, with unlike-atom values
$$
D_{RX}=\frac{D_{RR}+D_{XX}}{2}+96.485\,s_X^2\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\qquad
(s_A,s_B,s_C)=(0.5,1,1.5).
$$
Thus $D_{RX}=(274.12125,396.485,592.09125)\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, and the <Pauling scale> differences from $R$ are $(0.5,1,1.5)$. If all three fictitious species are placed above $R$ on that scale, their ordering is $A<B<C$, whereas the stipulated <Mulliken scale> gives $A<C<B$. The <bond-dissociation energies> and isolated-species <energies> were invented independently: this demonstrates what the formulas do, not a measured disagreement for actual <chemical elements>. A unit conversion cannot force these two input models to agree.

= Chemical environment dependence of electronegativity
{parent=Electronegativity}

A tabulated <electronegativity> is useful because it compresses many comparisons, but one number per <chemical element> suppresses chemical context. Changing the bonded partners, <bond length> or <electron configuration> changes the conditions under which a <chemical atom> attracts <electron density>. The <bond-dissociation energy> can change for reasons beyond <bond polarization>, so attributing every extra unit of <energy> solely to an <electronegativity> difference is an approximation.<Energy Electronegativity and Chemical Bonding by Stepan S. Batsanov>[§2.2, opening paragraph: bond distance and atomic valence affect bond energy]{ref}

An <oxidation state> labels a formal allocation of <electrons>; it is not a measured <partial atomic charge>. State-sensitive <electronegativity> models may give different values for different <oxidation states> or <electron configurations>, while a simple <chemical element> table intentionally provides a common comparison convention. Likewise, replacing an isolated <chemical atom> by a site in an <ionic crystal> introduces a surrounding <electric field> and many neighbours. Applying the same tabulated <electronegativity> without adjustment becomes an additional assumption.<Energy Electronegativity and Chemical Bonding by Stepan S. Batsanov>[§2.1, Table 1 and valence footnotes; §2.2, Table 2; §2.3, opening paragraph on cooperative effects in solids]{ref}

For the ground-state <Mulliken scale>, the state dependence is visible directly in the formula. Replacing $X$ by $X^+$ replaces $E(X^+)-E(X)$ with $E(X^{2+})-E(X^+)$ as the removal cost, and replaces $E(X)-E(X^-)$ with $E(X^+)-E(X)$ as the attachment release. These are different <ionization energies> and <electron affinities>. This isolated-<ion> arithmetic does not claim that a bonded <chemical atom> with the same formal <oxidation state> is an isolated <ion>. In a <molecule>, the actual <electron density> and <dipole evidence for bond polarization> supply complementary information about that environment.

= Dipole evidence for bond polarization
{parent=Electronegativity}

A permanent <electric dipole moment> gives evidence of separated positive and negative <electric charge> in an overall neutral <molecule>. This is a more direct probe of <chemical polarity> than an <electronegativity> table. For <hydrogen chloride>, NIST lists about $1.109$ <debye> for the lowest vibrational state and $1.093$ <debye> for the equilibrium value. They describe different averaging conventions, not two contradictory measurements. The NIST WebBook traces the vibrational value $1.1085$ <debye> to molecular-beam electric-resonance measurements in 1970.<HCl (Hydrogen chloride): experimental data by National Institute of Standards and Technology>{ref}<Hydrogen chloride: Constants of diatomic molecules by Klaus P. Huber and others>{ref}

How does <molecular beam electric resonance> reveal an <electric dipole moment>? A source and apertures form a beam of <molecules> in <vacuum>. A first nonuniform <electric field>, A, selects <quantum states> through their different <Stark effects>. In a central region, C, electrodes supply a nearly uniform <electric field> and applied radiation drives transitions at selected <frequencies>. A second nonuniform <electric field>, B, analyzes the outgoing <quantum states>; a detector measures the rate of arriving <molecules>. In a transmission-loss arrangement, resonant radiation transfers some <molecules> to a <quantum state> rejected by B, so the measured arrival rate dips. The observable is a change in detected <molecules> as the radiation <frequency> is scanned, rather than light absorption itself. This general apparatus description is documented in a later experimental thesis; it is not a reconstruction of the exact 1970 HCl instrument.<High Resolution Molecular Beam Study of Alkali Cyanides by Johannes Jacobus van Vaals>[§2, printed pp. 11–14: fields A, C and B, detected flux, parallel plates, radiation and detector]{ref}

\Image[bond-dipole-beam-resonance.png]
{id=bond-dipole-beam-resonance}
{title=<Molecular beam electric resonance>: schematic state selection and a synthetic <Stark effect> signal from a <rigid rotor> model; neither apparatus dimensions nor signal curves are measured HCl data}

The inference comes from how transition <frequencies> change with the known <electric field>. The interaction $-\boldsymbol\mu\cdot\mathbf E$ couples the <electric dipole moment> to the <electric field>; fitting the <quantum mechanics> of the relevant rotational states extracts its magnitude. A free rotating <molecule> need not show a first-order shift proportional to $|\mathbf E|$: the weak-field <Stark effect> can be quadratic. The diagram uses a simplified <rigid rotor>, with $B$ an <energy> constant, to illustrate this behavior. It omits the fine details of actual spectra.

Useful controls include scans with the radiation off to measure the baseline arrival rate, repeated scans at several calibrated <electric fields>, and agreement between independently assigned transitions. These are methodological checks, not claims about undocumented steps of the 1970 experiment. Field calibration and state assignment limit the inferred <electric dipole moment>. The published 1970 abstract confirms analysis of HCl and DCl beam-resonance spectra and combination with <infrared spectroscopy> intensities to obtain a distance-dependent <electric dipole moment>; its full apparatus and calibration record were unavailable here.<Dipole Moment and Hyperfine Parameters of H35Cl and D35Cl by E. W. Kaiser>[pp. 1686–1703, authored abstract; full text unavailable]{ref}

Even an accurately measured <electric dipole moment> is not an absolute <partial atomic charge>. Replacing a <hydrogen chloride> <molecule> by two point <electric charges> $\pm q$ separated by <bond length> $r$ gives $|\mu|=|q|r$. Using $1.109$ <debye> and $r=1.27455\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm m$ gives $|q|\approx0.181$ times the elementary <electric charge>. This is an effective two-point model, combining a vibrationally averaged <electric dipole moment> with an equilibrium <bond length>, not a unique partition of the actual <electron density> or a measured “18.1 percent ionic bond”.<Hydrogen chloride: Constants of diatomic molecules by Klaus P. Huber and others>{ref}

For a <molecule> with several <chemical bonds>, <molecular geometry> can cancel opposing contributions to the total <electric dipole moment>. <Carbon dioxide> illustrates why an overall <nonpolar molecule> need not have unpolarized individual <chemical bonds>. An <electronegativity> comparison helps anticipate local <bond polarization>; the measured molecular <electric dipole moment> tests the entire <electric charge> distribution.

= Debye
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Dipole evidence for bond polarization}
{wiki=Debye}
{title2=$\mathrm D$}

= Debye
{synonym}

The <debye> is a unit of <electric dipole moment>: $1\,\mathrm D\approx3.33564\times10^{-30}\,\mathrm{C\,m}$.<Triatomic Spectral Database by National Institute of Standards and Technology/5. Special Units, Fundamental Constants, and Useful Conversion Factors>{ref}

= Molecular beam electric resonance
{parent=Dipole evidence for bond polarization}

The apparatus and dipole inference are described under <dipole evidence for bond polarization>. The <rigid rotor> supplies a general starting model for rotational <quantum states>; <rotational spectroscopy> measures their transition <frequencies>, while <molecular beam electric resonance> can select and analyze states through their <Stark effects>.

= Bond polarization
{parent=Electronegativity}

<Bond polarization> is unequal sharing of <electron density> across a <chemical bond>. An <electronegativity> difference is a heuristic for its direction and tendency, not a unique <partial atomic charge>.

= Polar covalent bond
{parent=Bond polarization}

A <polar covalent bond> shares <electrons> unequally, as in <hydrogen chloride>.

= Hydrogen chloride
{parent=Polar covalent bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{HCl}$}

= Hydrogen fluoride
{parent=Polar covalent bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{HF}$}

= Ionic bonding
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

<Ionic bonding> binds oppositely charged <ions> through attraction governed by <electrostatics>. In ordinary solid <sodium chloride>, each <cation> interacts with many <anions>, and also with other <cations>: attraction and repulsion together contribute to the binding of an extended <ionic crystal>. The <chemical formula> $\mathrm{NaCl}$ specifies equal numbers of the two kinds of <ions>; it does not divide the <solid> into isolated two-atom <molecules>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.1, “The Formation of Ionic Compounds”, Figure 7.3]{ref}

This distinction explains a striking combination of properties: ordinary dry <sodium chloride> conducts <electric current> poorly, while its molten form or a sufficiently concentrated <chemical solution> in <water> conducts well. The <ions> already exist in the <solid>; their ability to move through the sample changes. A <conductivity experiment on salt solutions> tests this transport picture, rather than simply asserting that charged constituents must make every material conductive.

\Image[ionic-crystal-pair-and-binding-energy.png]
{id=image-ionic-crystal-pair-and-binding-energy}
{title=An isolated <ion pair>, a plane of an extended <ionic crystal>, and a synthetic <potential energy> model with a stable spacing}
{description=Original schematic geometry and calculated model curves, not a measured <electron density>, <diffraction> pattern, or experimental <lattice energy>. Four nearest neighbours are visible in the plane of the <rock salt structure>; two more lie perpendicular to it. Disk sizes are arbitrary. The right-hand curves use the explicitly stipulated <dimensionless> model below.}

= Salt
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Ionic bonding}
{wiki}

= Salt
{synonym}

= Ionic compound
{synonym}

A <salt> is an assembly of <cations> and <anions> with zero total <electric charge>. Its constituents can be single <chemical atoms> or groups joined internally by <covalent bonds>. Thus <ionic bonding> and <covalent bonds> can occur in the same <chemical substance>, at different structural levels.<Chemistry 2e>[§2.6, polyatomic ions and ionic compounds]{ref}

= Potassium permanganate
{parent=Salt (chemistry)}
{title2=$\mathrm{KMnO}_4$}

A <salt> of <potassium> <cations> and <permanganate> <anions>, supplying <permanganate> in a <redox titration>.

= Stoichiometry of ionic compounds
{parent=Salt (chemistry)}
{title2=$\sum_i\nu_i z_i=0$}

For a neutral <formula unit>, let $\nu_i$ count <ions> of type $i$ with <electric charge> $z_i e$, where $e$ is the <elementary charge>. The <stoichiometry> must satisfy
$$
\sum_i\nu_i z_i=0.
$$
For $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^-$, the smallest positive counts are $1:1$. For $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^-$, they are $1:2$, giving <calcium chloride> $\mathrm{CaCl_2}$: $1(2)+2(-1)=0$. The formula counts <ions>, not the number of nearest neighbours. A <counterion> is the oppositely charged partner needed to balance a charged species; its identity can change <solubility> and other properties without changing the identity of that species.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.1, neutral ionic formulas and charge balance; §2.6, ionic compound examples]{ref}

= Calcium chloride
{parent=Stoichiometry of ionic compounds}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CaCl_2}$}

= Ionic crystal
{parent=Ionic bonding}
{wiki}

An <ionic crystal> repeats an arrangement of <ions> in three dimensions. A <unit cell> describes that repetition, whereas a <formula unit> describes composition. The two accounting devices answer different questions. <Coulomb's law> is not restricted to an arbitrarily drawn line connecting one <cation> to one <anion>: every <ion> contributes to the <electric field> experienced by the others.

= Rock salt structure
{parent=Ionic crystal}

The <rock salt structure> of ordinary <sodium chloride> has a <coordination number> of six for each kind of <ion>: six nearest neighbours of the opposite sign surround each <ion> along three perpendicular directions. Its conventional cubic <unit cell> contains four <formula units>; sharing corner, face, and edge sites between adjacent cells must be included in that count. Sixfold local coordination is compatible with $1:1$ overall <stoichiometry>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.1, Figure 7.3; §10.6, “The Structures of Ionic Crystals”, NaCl unit-cell accounting]{ref}

The checkerboard plane in the diagram is a slice through this three-dimensional arrangement. It leaves out the two nearest neighbours above and below the plane. Repeating a complete even-by-even patch gives equal numbers of signs; the displayed odd-by-odd crop has a boundary imbalance and is not a complete neutral <ionic crystal> or a <unit cell>. Other <salts> need not have the <rock salt structure> or the same <coordination number>.

= Coordination number
{parent=Ionic crystal}
{title2=$\mathrm{CN}$}

In this structural use, the <coordination number> counts nearest neighbouring constituents. State which neighbours are counted and which <ionic crystal> structure is being described; it is not an <ion>'s <electric charge> number.

= Ion pair
{parent=Ionic bonding}

An <ion pair> is an associated <cation> and <anion>. An isolated <ion pair> lacks the extended neighbourhood of an <ionic crystal>. In a <chemical solution>, <ion pairs> can coexist with more widely separated <ions>; their abundance depends on conditions. A neutral gas-phase <sodium chloride> <molecule> can be treated as a discrete entity, with its own <quantum mechanics> and dissociation products. That use of <molecule> does not describe the constituents of ordinary solid <sodium chloride>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[§8–3, distinction between vaporized NaCl pairs and separated gaseous ions]{ref}<Conductivity of Solutions by Harvard Natural Sciences Lecture Demonstrations>{ref}

= Electron transfer and ionic character
{parent=Ionic bonding}

The bookkeeping steps $\mathrm{Na}\to\mathrm{Na}^++e^-$ and $\mathrm{Cl}+e^-\to\mathrm{Cl}^-$ conserve <electric charge> and describe the <electron> inventories of separated species. They do not establish a chronological mechanism in which a labeled <electron> jumps between two pre-existing <chemical atoms> and becomes a classical object owned exclusively by one of them. In a bound material, <quantum mechanics> determines the <electron density> and the total <energy>.

<Ionic bonding> and a <covalent bond> are useful limiting descriptions with a continuum between them. <Electronegativity> helps anticipate unequal sharing of <electron density>, but a numerical cutoff does not measure a universal fraction of <ionic bonding>. A <partial atomic charge> depends on how the <electron density> is partitioned; integer <formal charges> are a bookkeeping convention. Using $\pm e$ in a point-charge model therefore does not claim that every partition of the actual <electron density> assigns exactly those values.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[§30–2, “Chemical bonds in crystals”: gradations between covalent and ionic bonding]{ref}<Chemistry 2e>[§7.2, “Electronegativity and Bond Type”: general guide and exceptions]{ref}

= Electrostatic model of ionic binding
{parent=Ionic bonding}
{title2=$U_{ij}=z_i z_j e^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0r_{ij})$}

For two point <electric charges> in <vacuum>, <Coulomb's law> gives the interaction <potential energy>
$$
U_{ij}(r_{ij})=\frac{z_i z_j e^2}{4\pi\epsilon_0 r_{ij}},
$$
with zero at infinite separation. Here $r_{ij}$ is the distance between them and $\epsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>. Opposite signs give negative <potential energy> and attraction; equal signs give positive <potential energy> and repulsion. For a finite collection of distinct point <electric charges>, sum over $i<j$ to count each pair once. A bulk <ionic crystal> requires the extended lattice sum, including both signs, rather than multiplying one attractive pair by the <coordination number>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[§8–1, equations (8.1)–(8.3); §8–3, many-neighbour lattice sum]{ref}

At short distances, overlapping <electron density> makes the simple point-charge picture inadequate. <Quantum mechanics>, including the <Pauli exclusion principle>, matters to the steep repulsive contribution. Pure attraction proportional to $-1/r$ would collapse as $r\to0$; it cannot set an equilibrium spacing on its own. A useful illustrative model is
$$
U(r)=-\frac{A}{r}+\frac{B}{r^n},\qquad A>0,\quad B>0,\quad n>1.
$$
Here $r$ scales an assumed fixed arrangement, $A$ packages its attractive contribution, and $B/r^n$ represents short-range repulsion. The constants have units <energy> times length and <energy> times length to the power $n$, respectively. This is a model, not a fitted description of a named <salt>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[§8–3, close-distance repulsion and limits of a purely electrostatic lattice calculation]{ref}

Differentiating gives $r_0=(nB/A)^{1/(n-1)}$ and $U''(r_0)=(n-1)A/r_0^3>0$, so the stationary spacing is a stable minimum. For the diagram's stipulated $n=9$, put $x=r/r_0$ and $E_0=A(1-1/n)/r_0>0$. Then
$$
\frac{U(r)}{E_0}=\frac{-9/x+1/x^9}{8},\qquad U(r_0)=-E_0.
$$
The <dimensionless> curves show how competing interactions select a spacing. Their <energy> scale and exponent are synthetic; neither is a measured <lattice energy> or a <melting> prediction.

= Lattice energy
{parent=Ionic bonding}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta H_{\rm latt,diss}$}

A <lattice energy> needs a reaction, a sign convention, and a stated amount. Using the lattice-dissociation <enthalpy> convention for <sodium chloride>,
$$
\mathrm{NaCl}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Na}^+(g)+\mathrm{Cl}^-(g),\qquad
\Delta H_{\rm latt,diss}>0.
$$
The reverse lattice-formation <enthalpy> is negative with the same magnitude when initial and final states match. <Chemistry 2e> gives $769\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ for this separation, per <mole> of <sodium chloride> <formula units>. This is about $7.97$ <electronvolts> per <formula unit>, not per individual <ion>. It concerns separated gaseous <ions>, rather than neutral <chemical atoms>, molten <salt>, or hydrated <ions> in <water>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.5, “Ionic Bond Strength and Lattice Energy”, positive convention, reverse convention and NaCl value]{ref}

Also distinguish an <internal energy> change from an <enthalpy> change: $H=U+pV$. A static point-charge calculation omits motion and thermal contributions. Reversing a reaction reverses its <energy> change; replacing <internal energy> with <enthalpy> additionally changes the defined quantity. The <Born-Haber cycle> infers a lattice-formation <enthalpy> by combining other <enthalpies>, <ionization energies>, and <electron affinities>. These imagined stages are a thermodynamic accounting path, not a claim about the actual sequence of a <chemical reaction>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.5, “The Born-Haber Cycle”, Figure 7.13 and Table 7.4]{ref}

= Born-Haber cycle
{c}
{parent=Lattice energy}

A <Born-Haber cycle> uses <Hess's law> to infer a <lattice energy> from measurable <enthalpy> differences and isolated-species data. The motivating difficulty is that separating a <mole> of an <ionic crystal> into mutually separated gaseous <ions> is not a convenient direct <calorimetry> experiment. Instead, compare two accounting routes between the same elemental reference states and the same <solid>. Because <enthalpy> is a <state function>, the signed differences close exactly when all inputs describe compatible states. These routes do not assert a <reaction mechanism> or a temporal sequence of <reaction intermediates>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.5 Strengths of Ionic and Covalent Bonds>[“The Born-Haber Cycle”, Figure 7.13 and Table 7.4]{ref}

For one <mole> of <sodium chloride> <formula units>, the overall formation reaction is
$$
\mathrm{Na}(s)+\tfrac12\mathrm{Cl}_2(g)\longrightarrow\mathrm{NaCl}(s),\qquad F=\Delta H_{\rm f}^{\circ}.
$$
The <stoichiometry> requires half a <mole> of diatomic <chlorine> <molecules>, supplying one <mole> of <chlorine> <chemical atoms>. A half coefficient counts an <amount of substance>; it does not describe half of an individual <molecule>. Write each auxiliary <chemical reaction> with its own sign:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Na}(s)&\longrightarrow\mathrm{Na}(g) && S>0,\\
\tfrac12\mathrm{Cl}_2(g)&\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cl}(g) && B>0,\\
\mathrm{Na}(g)&\longrightarrow\mathrm{Na}^+(g)+e^-(g) && I>0,\\
\mathrm{Cl}(g)+e^-(g)&\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cl}^-(g) && A=\Delta H_{\rm attach}^{\circ}<0,\\
\mathrm{Na}^+(g)+\mathrm{Cl}^-(g)&\longrightarrow\mathrm{NaCl}(s) && L_{\rm form}<0.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $S$ is the <sublimation> <enthalpy>, $B$ the <enthalpy of atomization> of <chlorine> per <mole> of <chemical atoms>, and $I$ the molar ionization <enthalpy> represented by the <ionization energy> input in elementary treatments. If the <bond-dissociation energy> table gives the compatible molar <enthalpy> for $\mathrm{Cl}_2(g)\to2\mathrm{Cl}(g)$, use half that number for $B$. Both <ions> in the last step are gaseous, and no aqueous <hydration of ions> belongs in that step.

Adding these equations cancels the gaseous <chemical atoms>, the gaseous <ions> and the free <electron>, leaving precisely the overall formation reaction. Thus
$$
F=S+B+I+A+L_{\rm form},\qquad
L_{\rm diss}=-L_{\rm form}=S+B+I+A-F.
$$
The closed traversal returns from the <solid> to the elemental reference states, adding $-F$; therefore $S+B+I+A+L_{\rm form}-F=0$. Notice that the signed attachment term $A$ is negative. Substituting a positive spectroscopic <electron affinity> for $A$ would reverse the attachment contribution.

\Image[sodium-chloride-born-haber-enthalpy-cycle.png]
{id=image-sodium-chloride-born-haber-enthalpy-cycle}
{title=A closed <Born-Haber cycle> for <sodium chloride> using explicitly synthetic molar <enthalpies>: levels represent complete material inventories, and horizontal position is bookkeeping rather than a <reaction coordinate>}

The diagram deliberately uses invented, internally consistent inputs in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$: $S=100$, $B=120$, $I=500$, $A=-350$ and $F=-400$. Preparation of separated gaseous <ions> raises the <enthalpy> by $370$, so $L_{\rm form}=-770$ and $L_{\rm diss}=+770$. The closure check is $100+120+500-350-770+400=0$. These numbers are not measured <sodium chloride> data and do not replace the source's $769\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ example in <lattice energy>. Every level retains one <sodium> nucleus, one <chlorine> nucleus and zero total <electric charge>; the ionization level explicitly retains the free <electron>.

= Lattice enthalpy from solution calorimetry
{parent=Born-Haber cycle}

A second indirect route makes the experimental distinction especially clear: a <calorimeter> can measure dissolution of a weighed <salt> into a known amount of <water>, but its final <ions> are hydrated, not separated gaseous <ions>. At compatible <standard states> and the same <temperature>,
$$
\Delta H_{\rm sol}^{\circ}=L_{\rm diss}+\Delta H_{\rm hyd,pair}^{\circ},\qquad
L_{\rm diss}=\Delta H_{\rm sol}^{\circ}-\Delta H_{\rm hyd,pair}^{\circ}.
$$
Here the combined <hydration of ions> term takes one <mole> each of gaseous $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ to the specified aqueous reference state. Its sum suffices: dissolution <calorimetry> alone cannot independently assign single-ion <hydration of ions> <enthalpies>, or determine $L_{\rm diss}$ without another input. A finite-<concentration> measurement also requires dilution corrections before it can supply $\Delta H_{\rm sol}^{\circ}$.

In a simple constant-<pressure> solution <calorimeter>, an insulated vessel, stirrer and <thermometer> follow the <temperature> change after mixing. A calibrated effective <heat capacity> $C_{\rm eff}$ converts the corrected <temperature> change to <heat>. With no applied heating, negligible external <heat transfer>, and only pressure-volume <work>, the approximate molar result is
$$
\Delta H_{\rm sol}\simeq-\frac{C_{\rm eff}\Delta T}{n}.
$$
Here $n$ is the <amount of substance> dissolved and $C_{\rm eff}$ includes the <chemical solution> and the vessel. Cooling means positive dissolution <enthalpy>, because the dissolving sample takes <heat> from its surroundings.<Chemistry 2e>[§5.2, Figures 5.11–5.13 and discussion of including solution and calorimeter heat capacities]{ref}

An actual reference measurement used <potassium chloride>, not <sodium chloride>: a platinum-lined <calorimeter> with an adiabatic shield and a quartz-oscillator <thermometer>. Because dissolution absorbed <heat>, measured electrical heating prevented loss of shield control; the readouts were electrical input and the vessel/shield <temperature> record. Stirring was checked visually in a dummy vessel. The <thermometer> was compared with a calibrated resistance <thermometer>, and electrical calibration bracketed the reaction. The reported result was $+(17.584\pm0.017)\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at $298.15\,\mathrm K$, for one <mole> of <potassium chloride> in $500$ <moles> of <water>.<The Enthalpy of Solution of SRM 1655 (KCl) in H2O by Marthada V. Kilday>[§3 and §4: apparatus, calibration, readouts and specified dilution; inspected through indexed article text]{ref}

Checks on residual <water>, particle size and final <concentration>, and agreement with a second <calorimeter>, constrained systematic errors. Imperfect shield tracking required <heat transfer> corrections. This experiment measures an <enthalpy of solution>, not a direct <lattice energy>; the hydration and dilution bookkeeping still has to be supplied. The full article could not be opened here; the inspected indexed text supports the stated apparatus and checks, without reconstructing its complete uncertainty budget.<The Enthalpy of Solution of SRM 1655 (KCl) in H2O by Marthada V. Kilday>[§§2–4 and conclusion: moisture, particle-size and dilution studies; confirmation with an isoperibol calorimeter; full-page access blocked]{ref}

= Potassium chloride
{parent=Lattice enthalpy from solution calorimetry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{KCl}$}

= Physical properties of salts
{parent=Ionic bonding}

Many <ionic crystals> have high <melting> <temperatures> compared with small molecular <solids>, because reorganizing their strongly interacting <ions> costs <energy>. But <melting> leaves a dense <liquid> with interactions; it does not perform the complete separation defining a <lattice energy>. At fixed pressure, the transition occurs when the <Gibbs free energies> of the <solid> and <liquid> match. The relevant <enthalpy> and <entropy> differences both matter, so <lattice energy> alone is not a universal ordering of <melting> <temperatures>. Strong <covalent bonds> in extended networks can also give high <melting> <temperatures>.<Chemistry 2e>[§10.5, “Ionic Solids” and “Covalent Network Solid”; §16.4, equations defining Gibbs free energy and its change]{ref}

= Solubility
{parent=Physical properties of salts}
{wiki}

<Solubility> specifies the amount that can remain dissolved at <chemical equilibrium> under stated conditions; it is different from how quickly a sample dissolves. Separating <ions> from an <ionic crystal> costs <energy>, but <solvation> establishes new interactions with the <solvent>. In <water>, <hydration of ions> couples charged <ions> to polar <water> <molecules>. The net <enthalpy> of dissolution can have either sign; <entropy> and <concentration> also enter the <Gibbs free energy> balance. Consequently, being a <salt> does not guarantee high <solubility> in <water> or any other <solvent>.<Chemistry 2e>[§11.1, “The Formation of Solutions”, Figure 11.4; §11.3, definition of solubility; §16.4, free energy]{ref}

Low <solubility> must also be distinguished from weak <electrolyte> behavior. A small amount of an <ionic compound> can dissolve essentially completely as <ions>, giving few charge carriers because little material dissolved. Calling that a <weak electrolyte> solely because its <chemical solution> conducts poorly would conflate two different questions.<Chemistry 2e>[§11.2, “Ionic Electrolytes”: sparingly soluble compounds can be strong electrolytes]{ref}

= Solvation
{parent=Solubility}
{wiki}

<Solvation> is stabilization through interactions between a <solute> and its <solvent>. It involves both the <solute>'s surroundings and reorganization of the <solvent>: neighbors move, orientations change, and existing interactions compete with new ones. <Electric forces>, <van der Waals forces>, and specific interactions such as <hydrogen bonds> can contribute. <Solvation> can also occur at groups on an insoluble material, so it does not require complete <dissolution>.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/solvation>{ref}

<Solvation> by <water> is called hydration. <Hydration of ions> is the charged special case, but neutral <molecules> such as <sucrose> can also interact with <water> without becoming <ions>. A hydrated <cation> retains its positive net <electric charge>; orienting neutral <water> <molecules> around it does not supply an extra <electron>. <Dissolution>, <desolvation>, and <ionization> describe different changes, as the examples below show.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.1 The Dissolution Process>[Opening sucrose example]{ref}<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.2 Electrolytes>[“Ionic Electrolytes” and “Covalent Electrolytes”]{ref}

The useful question is therefore not just whether a <solute> attracts a <solvent>, but how much the whole system gains after paying for the rearrangements. <Solvation free energy> describes a specified transfer into a <solvent>; <solvation thermodynamic balance> explains why even cooling during <dissolution> can accompany favorable mixing. The existing <solubility> discussion concerns how much remains dissolved at <chemical equilibrium>.

= Solvation shell
{parent=Solvation}

A <solvation shell> is the region of <solvent> constituents surrounding a <solute>, with local arrangements influenced by their interactions. The first <solvation shell> contains the closest neighbors under a stated distance or interaction criterion. A <coordination number> counts those neighbors; it requires such a criterion and can fluctuate. Neither a drawn ring nor a few illustrated <molecules> determines a measured <coordination number>.

In a <liquid>, a <solvation shell> is an evolving neighborhood: <solvent> <molecules> reorient and exchange with the surroundings. Different <ions> can produce different local dynamics. <Spectroscopic measurement of hydration dynamics> provides an experimental way to distinguish some of those environments without pretending that a shell is a fixed cage.

= Hydration shell
{parent=Solvation shell}

A <hydration shell> is a <solvation shell> in <water>. The existing <hydration of ions> discussion describes preferential orientations around <cations> and <anions>.

\Image[hydration-shell-charge-orientation.png]
{id=image-hydration-shell-charge-orientation}
{title=Qualitative <hydration shells>: opposite orientations of polar <water> <molecules> around a <cation> and an <anion>, with each <ion>'s net <electric charge> retained}
{description=Original schematic, not measured molecular positions. Atom spacings and the dashed shell boundary are illustrative. Five selected <water> glyphs in each panel do not assert a <coordination number>. Each <water> <molecule> is neutral despite its <partial atomic charges>.}

= Ion-dipole attraction
{parent=Solvation shell}
{title2=$U=-\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf E$}

An <ion-dipole attraction> couples an <ion>'s <electric field> to a <molecule>'s <electric dipole moment>. In the elementary point-<electric dipole> approximation, the interaction <potential energy> is $U=-\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf E$. An orientation with the <electric dipole moment> along the <electric field> lowers this <energy>. For <water>, the <oxygen> end preferentially faces a <cation>, and the <hydrogen> end an <anion>. At nonzero <temperature>, fluctuations and competing <hydrogen bonds> prevent perfect alignment of every <molecule>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.2 Electrolytes>[“Ionic Electrolytes”, Figure 11.7 and its explanatory paragraph]{ref}

= Spectroscopic measurement of hydration dynamics
{parent=Solvation shell}

What separates a <hydration shell> from the surrounding <water> experimentally? <Electrical conductivity> addresses a different question. The documented lamp demonstration immerses metal <electrodes> in a <chemical solution>, rinses them between samples, and records brightness; adding <sodium chloride> to distilled <water> makes it conductive. A <water> blank and dissolved <sucrose> comparison test whether dissolved material alone explains the signal. Fixed geometry, <temperature>, and stated <concentrations> are additional proposed controls. This reveals mobile <electric charge>, with limits from contamination, electrode interfaces and detection threshold; it supplies no shell positions. <Conductivity experiment on salt solutions> explains the apparatus and controls in detail.<Conductivity of Solutions by Harvard Natural Sciences Lecture Demonstrations>[“Setting it Up” and “Comments”]{ref}

In the authored study <Influence of ions on the hydrogen-bond structure in liquid water by Anne Willem Omta and others>, <pump-probe spectroscopy> uses tunable mid-<infrared> pulses traversing a sample. The probe's <wave polarization> is set at $45^{\circ}$ to the pump; after the sample, splitting and <polarizers> select parallel and perpendicular transmission. Measuring each transmission with and without the pump gives $D_{\parallel}$ and $D_{\perp}$, the respective logarithms of the transmission ratios. Their <dimensionless> <anisotropy> is
$$
R(t)=\frac{D_{\parallel}(t)-D_{\perp}(t)}{D_{\parallel}(t)+2D_{\perp}(t)}.
$$
Its decay probes reorientation rather than merely loss of vibrational excitation. Dilute <semiheavy water> in <heavy water> separates the O–H and O–D bands. Varying the <semiheavy water> <concentration> checked that transfer of excitation between nearby O–H groups did not determine the decay. These apparatus and control details are from the inspected author-uploaded article, not a new experiment.<Influence of ions on the hydrogen-bond structure in liquid water by Anne Willem Omta and others>[§II, printed p. 12458, Figure 1 and equations (2)–(3); author-uploaded article text inspected]{ref}

The related thesis chapter documents two O–H absorption bands in a <sodium perchlorate> <chemical solution>: approximately $3400\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for groups forming <hydrogen bonds> to <water>, and $3575\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for groups forming <hydrogen bonds> to the <anion>. Selecting pump and probe bands, then a later delay window, separated their responses. The anion-associated <anisotropy> decayed more slowly; the water-bonded component showed no resolved change in decay time across the tested <sodium perchlorate> <concentrations>. The chapter also used O–D excitation to extend observation beyond the shorter O–H vibrational lifetime. These results support distinct local dynamics rather than a permanently fixed <hydration shell>. Spectral overlap, finite observation windows, and the selected <salts> limit the inference: this does not prove that every <ion> leaves all distant <water> properties unchanged.<Ion solvation in water: femtosecond spectroscopy of hydrogen-bond dynamics by Michel F. Kropman/8 The influence of ions on the hydrogen-bond structure of water>[§8.2 and §8.3, printed pp. 82–89, Figures 8.1–8.7; PDF text inspected, numerical decay-time OCR unreliable and not reproduced]{ref}

= Pump-probe spectroscopy
{parent=Spectroscopic measurement of hydration dynamics}

<Pump-probe spectroscopy> measures a sample's response to a probe pulse at a controlled delay after an exciting pump pulse. Varying delay follows dynamics; selecting <frequency> and <wave polarization> can distinguish different contributions. <Spectroscopic measurement of hydration dynamics> is an application to <water> surrounding <ions>.

= Heavy water
{parent=Spectroscopic measurement of hydration dynamics}
{title2=$\mathrm D_2\mathrm O$}

<Heavy water> replaces both ordinary <hydrogen> nuclei of <water> with <deuterium>.

= Semiheavy water
{parent=Spectroscopic measurement of hydration dynamics}
{title2=$\mathrm{HDO}$}

<Semiheavy water> contains one ordinary <hydrogen> nucleus and one <deuterium> nucleus. Dilute <semiheavy water> in <heavy water> provides relatively isolated O–H groups for <infrared spectroscopy>.

= Dissolution
{parent=Solvation}

<Dissolution> transfers material into a <chemical solution>. It can include separation from an <ionic crystal> and <solvation>, or dispersal of neutral <molecules>. For <sucrose> and <sodium chloride>, respectively,
$$
\mathrm{C}_{12}\mathrm{H}_{22}\mathrm{O}_{11}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{C}_{12}\mathrm{H}_{22}\mathrm{O}_{11}(aq),\qquad
\mathrm{NaCl}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Na}^{+}(aq)+\mathrm{Cl}^{-}(aq).
$$
Here $(aq)$ specifies <water> as the <solvent>; it does not specify a fixed number of attached <water> <molecules>. The neutral <sucrose> remains molecular. <Dissociation of an ionic compound> separates pre-existing <ions> in the second example; <solvation> stabilizes their new surroundings.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.1 The Dissolution Process>[Opening molecular and ionic dissolution examples]{ref}<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.2 Electrolytes>[“Ionic Electrolytes”]{ref}

<Ionization> instead creates <ions> from neutral species. <Hydrogen chloride> reacting with <water> illustrates <protonation> of <water> rather than mere rearrangement of a <hydration shell>:
$$
\mathrm{HCl}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm O\longrightarrow\mathrm{H}_3\mathrm O^{+}+\mathrm{Cl}^{-}.
$$
The <hydronium> and negative <ion> together preserve total <electric charge>. <Solvation> can influence this <chemical reaction>, but an <ion>'s <solvation> does not itself change its net <electric charge>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.2 Electrolytes>[“Covalent Electrolytes”, HCl proton-transfer example]{ref}

= Dissociation of an ionic compound
{parent=Dissolution}

<Dissociation of an ionic compound> separates its constituent <ions>. It differs from creating charged species by <ionization> of neutral <molecules>. Dissolved <ions> can also associate into <ion pairs>, so a dissociated description does not exclude all subsequent association.

= Sodium perchlorate
{parent=Dissociation of an ionic compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaClO}_4$}

<Sodium perchlorate> is the <salt> used in the spectrally selected example of <spectroscopic measurement of hydration dynamics>.

= Desolvation
{parent=Solvation}

<Desolvation> removes some or all of the <solvent> environment of a species. Removing a <hydration shell> does not remove an <ion>'s <electric charge>. Complete reversal of a specified <solvation> transfer reverses its <Gibbs free energy> and <enthalpy> changes when the same states and conventions are used. Partial <desolvation>, exchange of one <solvent> for another, and creation of a gaseous species have different endpoints; their costs need not match.

= Solvation free energy
{parent=Solvation}
{title2=$\Delta G_{\mathrm{solv}}^{\circ}$}

For transfer of the same species $X$ from a gaseous reference state into a <solvent> $S$, define the molar <solvation free energy> by
$$
X(g)\longrightarrow X(S),\qquad
\Delta G_{\mathrm{solv}}^{\circ}(S)=\mu_X^{\circ}(S)-\mu_X^{\circ}(g).
$$
Here $\mu^{\circ}$ is a standard <chemical potential>. Negative <solvation free energy> means that transfer into the stated <solvent> lowers the <Gibbs free energy> relative to the stated gaseous reference. Include <solute>-<solvent> interactions, making room in the <solvent>, and its reorganization; counting only a favorable pair interaction omits part of the transfer.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/solvation energy>{ref}

State the <temperature>, gaseous reference <pressure>, dissolved reference <concentration> or other <standard state> convention, and species identity. Reference-state changes alter the quoted <solvation free energy>. For example, with an ideal dilute <thermodynamic activity> $a=c/c^{\circ}$, replacing $c^{\circ}$ by $\alpha c^{\circ}$ while preserving the physical <chemical potential> requires $\mu^{\circ}$ to increase by $RT\ln\alpha$. Thus the corresponding <solvation free energy> increases by $RT\ln\alpha$ if the gaseous reference stays fixed. This follows directly from $\mu=\mu^{\circ}+RT\ln a$, with $R$ the <molar gas constant>.

= Solvent-specific stabilization
{parent=Solvation free energy}

<Solvent-specific stabilization> means that the same species need not have the same <solvation free energy> in different <solvents>. <Chemical polarity>, <hydrogen bonds>, and the cost of reorganizing each <solvent> all matter. <Water> can stabilize <ions> by <ion-dipole attractions>; a favorable isolated attraction alone still cannot decide the total <dissolution> balance.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/solvation energy>{ref}

With a common gaseous reference and compatible dissolved <standard states>, subtraction removes that reference:
$$
\Delta G_{\mathrm{transfer}}^{\circ}(A\to B)
=\mu_X^{\circ}(B)-\mu_X^{\circ}(A)
=\Delta G_{\mathrm{solv}}^{\circ}(B)-\Delta G_{\mathrm{solv}}^{\circ}(A).
$$
A negative result favors <solvent> B under those reference conventions. This comparison assumes the same species $X$: if <protonation>, <ionization>, or formation of <ion pairs> changes its identity, include a <chemical equilibrium> for each corresponding <chemical reaction>.

= Solvation thermodynamic balance
{parent=Solvation}
{title2=$\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S$}

At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, compare fully specified initial and final states using
$$
\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S.
$$
The <entropy> change is for the whole selected system, including both <solute> and <solvent>. Dispersing a <solute> can increase accessible arrangements while orienting nearby <solvent> <molecules> restricts others. One picture of an ordered <hydration shell> cannot determine the sign of the total <entropy> change. A negative <Gibbs free energy> change favors the specified process; it does not determine its <reaction rate>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/16.4 Free Energy>[Opening definition and Table 16.3]{ref}

An illustrative <enthalpy> accounting path separates <solute> constituents, reorganizes the <solvent>, and establishes their interactions:
$$
\Delta H=\Delta H_{\mathrm{separate}}+\Delta H_{\mathrm{reorganize}}+\Delta H_{\mathrm{interact}}.
$$
This is a chosen decomposition between compatible states, not a measured sequence. If a gaseous-ion-to-<chemical solution> <solvation> term already includes <solvent> reorganization, do not add that reorganization a second time. <Lattice enthalpy from solution calorimetry> gives the alternative <Hess's law> route that combines lattice separation with the complete <hydration of ions> term.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.1 The Dissolution Process>[“The Formation of Solutions”, Figure 11.4]{ref}

\Image[solvation-enthalpy-entropy-balance.png]
{id=image-solvation-enthalpy-entropy-balance}
{title=Synthetic <dissolution> bookkeeping: <solvent> interactions change the <enthalpy> balance, while total <entropy> also enters the <Gibbs free energy>}
{description=Original stipulated model for one <mole> of a hypothetical 1:1 <salt> at $300\,\mathrm K$, with two unspecified <solvents>. No plotted value is a measurement or a claim about a named <chemical substance>. The accounting path is not a <reaction coordinate> or an <activation energy>.}

The invented inputs are $+100$ for separation and $+20$ for <solvent> reorganization, in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Interaction terms of $-150$ in <solvent> A and $-90$ in <solvent> B give $\Delta H_A=-30$ and $\Delta H_B=+30$. Stipulated total <entropy> changes of $-50$ and $+150\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ give $-T\Delta S_A=+15$ and $-T\Delta S_B=-45\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at $300\,\mathrm K$. Both selected processes therefore have $\Delta G=-15\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. This deliberately constructed comparison shows why the signs of the <enthalpy> and <Gibbs free energy> changes need not agree; it does not predict either <solvent>'s <solubility>.

For incremental <dissolution> at a particular composition, the relevant <chemical potential> difference also depends on <thermodynamic activities>. At <chemical equilibrium> that driving difference vanishes. A quoted standard <Gibbs free energy> change and the finite change between arbitrary mixtures answer different questions; use compatible <standard states> and the <reaction quotient> when discussing <solubility>.

The motivating <calorimetry> experiment is especially instructive. A weighed <potassium chloride> sample dissolves in a known amount of <water> in a platinum-lined, shielded <calorimeter>; a quartz-oscillator <thermometer> and measured electrical heating provide the readouts. Electrical calibration before and after the reaction, comparison with a resistance <thermometer>, and checks on residual <water> and particle size constrain errors. The inspected authored article reports positive <enthalpy of solution> at $298.15\,\mathrm K$ for the specified dilution, despite complete <dissolution>. This is a combined <enthalpy> balance, not an isolated <solvation free energy> or direct single-ion <hydration of ions> measurement. Shield tracking and final <concentration> matter; the existing <lattice enthalpy from solution calorimetry> discussion gives the numerical result and the conversion limits.<The Enthalpy of Solution of SRM 1655 (KCl) in H2O by Marthada V. Kilday>[Abstract, §§3–4 and conclusion; inspected indexed authored text, direct PMC access challenged]{ref}

= Hydration of ions
{parent=Solvation}

<Hydration of ions> is <solvation> by <water>. The polar <water> <molecules> orient preferentially around <cations> and <anions>, though the surrounding arrangement fluctuates rather than forming a permanently fixed cage.<Chemistry 2e>[§11.2, Figure 11.7 and “Ionic Electrolytes”]{ref}

= Conductivity experiment on salt solutions
{parent=Physical properties of salts}

How can a clear <chemical solution> reveal otherwise invisible mobile <ions>? The documented <Conductivity of Solutions by Harvard Natural Sciences Lecture Demonstrations> demonstration uses metal <electrodes> enclosed in tubing, an electrical supply, and a lamp in the circuit. The protocol specifies about $150\,\mathrm{mL}$ samples in labeled $250\,\mathrm{mL}$ beakers and a separate deionized-<water> rinse station. The recorded readout is lamp brightness after the <electrodes> enter each sample; the simplified variant tests <water> before and after adding <sodium chloride>. These are apparatus and procedure specifications, not new measurements reported here.<Conductivity of Solutions by Harvard Natural Sciences Lecture Demonstrations>{ref}

\Image[salt-solution-conductivity-controls.png]
{id=image-salt-solution-conductivity-controls}
{title=<Electrical conductivity> comparisons with a lamp tester, and the opposite motions of <cations> and <anions> in an <electric field>}
{description=Original circuit schematic and qualitative model outcomes, not photographs or recorded brightness data. A dark lamp means a signal below this tester's visible threshold, not identically zero <electrical conductivity>. Arrows show <drift velocities> during one half-cycle of an alternating supply; both signs contribute to the same <conventional current>. <Water> <molecules> and <hydration of ions> are omitted from the microscopic panel.}

The useful comparison is <water>, a <sucrose> <chemical solution>, and a <sodium chloride> <chemical solution> with the same probe geometry. A documented university account reports no visible light with <water>, a glowing lamp with dissolved <sodium chloride>, and a weak filament glow with its <sucrose> sample. The diagram summarizes the latter as little or no visible light; the precise detection threshold depends on the tester. <Sucrose> is a control for the idea that any dissolved material must conduct: its dispersed neutral <molecules> do not provide the abundant mobile <ions> supplied by the <salt>. This contrast is a documented teaching observation, not a numerical calibration of lamp brightness.<Chemistry 2e>[§11.1, sucrose dissolves as molecules; §11.2, Figure 11.6 and ionic versus nonionic charge carriers]{ref}<Conductivity of solutions by the migration of ions>{ref}

Rinsing the <electrodes> between samples controls carryover of dissolved <ions>, and the <water> blank checks the solvent background. Repeating the blank after the <salt> sample would test whether that rinse was effective. Additional methodological controls are fixed immersed area, electrode separation, supply setting, <temperature>, and comparison at stated <concentrations>. These additional controls are proposed checks, not undocumented claims about the source demonstration.

The inference is that mobile <electric charge> carriers complete the circuit. In the <chemical solution>, an <electric field> drives positive <ions> along the field and negative <ions> against it; multiplying each <drift velocity> by its <electric charge> makes both contributions to <conventional current> point the same way. With an alternating supply the direction reverses each half-cycle. The external wires instead carry <electric current> through <electrons>. The lamp alone identifies neither the species nor an exact <electrical conductivity>.

Important limits remain. <Water> has a small intrinsic population of <hydronium> and <hydroxide>, so a dark lamp does not prove absence of <ions>. Changes in probe geometry affect measured <electrical conductance>; <electrolysis>, electrode interfaces, contamination, and heating can also affect a real circuit. The comparison supports a transport model but does not independently measure a <coordination number>, a partitioned <partial atomic charge>, or a <lattice energy>.

Nor does poor conduction by ordinary dry <sodium chloride> establish that all <ionic crystals> are electrical insulators. <Crystallographic defects> can allow transport, and <solid electrolytes> can have substantial ionic <electrical conductivity> without <melting>. The authored abstract of <A lithium superionic conductor by Noriaki Kamaya and others> reports room-<temperature> ionic conduction in a <solid electrolyte>; the full experimental article was not accessible here.<A lithium superionic conductor by Noriaki Kamaya and others>{ref}

= Crystallographic defect
{parent=Physical properties of salts}
{wiki}

A <crystallographic defect> is a departure from ideal repeated structure. In an <ionic crystal>, a missing <ion> can provide an available site for another <ion> to move into; movement still requires overcoming an <activation energy>. This is why an ideal immobile-site model is a limit rather than a universal law for <solid> <electrical conductivity>.<Chemistry 2e>[§10.5, “Crystal Defects”: vacancies and other imperfections]{ref}

= Solid electrolyte
{parent=Physical properties of salts}

A <solid electrolyte> carries <electric current> through mobile <ions> while remaining a <solid>. The distinction between ionic and electronic <electrical conductivity> matters: different charge carriers serve different roles in <electrochemistry>.

= Bond length
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$r$}

A <bond length> describes the separation of the <atomic nuclei> of two bonded <chemical atoms>. Distinguish an equilibrium geometry from an instantaneous or experimentally averaged separation. Methods of determining <molecular geometry> can use different averaging conventions, so reported <bond lengths> need their measurement context. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/BT07003[IUPAC Gold Book: bond length]. Changing a <bond length> is one possible <reaction coordinate>.

= Partial atomic charge
{parent=Chemical bond}

A partial atomic charge assigns an <electric charge> to a <chemical atom> by partitioning the actual <electron density>. The result depends on the partitioning convention and need not be an integer. It differs from <formal charge>, which divides bonding <electrons> equally in a specified <Lewis structure>.

= Electron pair
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

An <electron pair> consists of two <electrons> with opposite <spins> sharing the same spatial part of their <quantum state>. The <Lewis structure> distinction between a <bond pair> and a <lone pair> records whether that pair is assigned to a connection between <chemical atoms> or to one <chemical atom>.

= Hydrogen bond
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$X-\mathrm H\cdots Y$}

A <hydrogen bond> is an attractive association involving a <hydrogen> attached to another <chemical atom>, and an accepting atom or group. In the IUPAC 2011 definition, the atom $X$ in $X-\mathrm H$ is more <electronegative> than <hydrogen>, and evidence of bond formation is required. The familiar strong examples use O–H or N–H groups, but restricting the concept to only nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine misses weaker cases. A short distance alone is not the entire definition.https://publications.iupac.org/pac/pdf/2011/pdf/8308x1637.pdf[Arunan et al., Definition of the hydrogen bond, IUPAC Recommendations 2011]{ref}

\Image[water-hydrogen-bond-roles.png]
{title=Two water molecules play different hydrogen-bond roles while retaining their own covalent O–H bonds}

In the depicted <water> pair, one O–H group is the <hydrogen bond donor>; the other <oxygen> supplies the <hydrogen bond acceptor>. The solid O–H line denotes a <covalent bond> within a <water> <molecule>; the dotted H···O line denotes the <hydrogen bond> between them. One <water> <molecule> can play both roles in a larger arrangement because it has O–H groups and <lone pairs>.

This makes <hydrogen bonds> useful for understanding organization beyond a single <molecular formula>. They connect <water> <molecules> and contribute to <protein> folding and recognition between <molecules>. The same types of <chemical atoms> can support different associations depending on which donor and acceptor sites are exposed and how they are oriented. Alberts and colleagues discuss these noncovalent interactions in https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Molecular Biology of the Cell, the chemical components of a cell].

<Hydrogen bonds> are often directional, with an approximately straight donor–H–acceptor arrangement favourable in familiar cases; strength and preferred geometry depend on the system. The IUPAC definition uses converging experimental or theoretical evidence rather than one universal distance threshold. The author-hosted https://ipc.iisc.ac.in/~ea/iupac/iupac/IUPAC_finalreport_Nov_2009TG.pdf[2009 task-group draft, §§3.1–3.3] explains the definition and typical criteria leading to the recommendation.

= Hydrogen bond donor
{parent=Hydrogen bond}
{title2=$X-\mathrm H$}

A <hydrogen bond donor> is the $X-\mathrm H$ group supplying the attached <hydrogen> in a <hydrogen bond> $X-\mathrm H\cdots Y$. The donor atom $X$, or the whole <molecule> containing it, may also be called the donor. The terminology does not imply transfer of a free <proton>: in the ordinary <water> pair, the O–H <covalent bond> remains. Familiar donors include O–H groups in <water> and N–H groups in <ammonia>. See https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Andrew Glasfeld, Intermolecular Attraction, pp. 12–13: donor and acceptor roles].

\Image[hydrogen-bond-donor-acceptor-site-map.png]
{title=<Hydrogen bond donor> and <hydrogen bond acceptor> roles depend on attached <hydrogen>, <protonation>, and <chemical resonance>}
{description=Toy site map for ordinary O–H and N–H associations, not measured geometry or an exhaustive list of <hydrogen bonds>. $R$ and $R'$ denote groups attached through <carbon>. Coloured labels identify possible sites, rather than count the number of simultaneous contacts.}

The attached <hydrogen> is essential to this role. At the <nitrogen> site, an unprotonated <amine> $R_3\mathrm N$ can be a <hydrogen bond acceptor>, but has no N–H <hydrogen bond donor>. <Protonation> gives $R_3\mathrm{NH}^+$: the new N–H group can donate, while that <nitrogen> no longer has the accepting <lone pair>. This is a change of chemical form, not a permanent label attached to a <chemical element>. Here $R$ denotes a group attached through <carbon>; the statement concerns the <nitrogen> site and does not rule out weaker C–H associations elsewhere. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-3-basicity-of-amines[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 24.3: amine protonation] and https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld: protonated amines as donors].

An <amide> with an N–H <covalent bond> can donate even though its <nitrogen> is ordinarily not an accepting site. For example, in $R-\mathrm C(=\mathrm O)-\mathrm{NH}-R'$, N–H is a <hydrogen bond donor> and the <carbonyl group>'s <oxygen> is a <hydrogen bond acceptor>. Replacing the remaining N–H by another group attached through <carbon> removes this N–H donor without removing the accepting <oxygen>. The analogous division of roles in a <peptide bond> helps explain <protein> structure. See https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld: amide roles and biological interactions]. The useful question is therefore which site supplies the <hydrogen>, rather than whether the whole substance is simply “hydrogen bonding”.

= Hydrogen bond acceptor
{parent=Hydrogen bond}

A <hydrogen bond acceptor> supplies the electron-rich atom or group $Y$ interacting with the attached <hydrogen> of a <hydrogen bond donor> in $X-\mathrm H\cdots Y$. An <oxygen> <lone pair> in <water> is the familiar example. Acceptance describes the association, not necessarily acquisition of a <proton>. A single <molecule>, such as <water> or <ammonia>, can offer both <hydrogen bond donor> and <hydrogen bond acceptor> sites. See https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld: water and nitrogen-containing accepting sites].

A <lone pair> drawn in a <Lewis structure> is a useful clue, not a universal test. At an ordinary neutral <amide> <nitrogen>, the nominal <lone pair> participates in <chemical resonance> with the <carbonyl group>, so this <nitrogen> is ordinarily excluded from lists of <hydrogen bond acceptors>; the <carbonyl group>'s <oxygen> remains an accepting site. <Protonation> changes the test again: $\mathrm{NH}_4^+$ has four N–H <covalent bonds> and no <nitrogen> <lone pair>, whereas <ammonia> has three and one. These distinctions follow the actual chemical form, and accepting ability also depends on the partner and whether it can approach the site. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-3-basicity-of-amines[McMurry: amide resonance and protonation] and https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld: limitations of nitrogen accepting sites].

Conversely, an accepting group need not supply an atom-centred <lone pair>. The following <phenol>–<benzene> experiment probes an O–H donor interacting with the ring's distributed $\pi$ <electron density>.

\Image[hydrogen-bond-phenol-infrared-controls.png]
{title=<Infrared spectroscopy> distinguishes free and associated <phenol>: schematic experimental logic, no plotted or simulated spectra}
{description=Original toy layout of the comparisons reported by Saggu, Levinson and Boxer (2011). The connector identifies an O–H donor and a ring accepting region; it does not specify a measured <bond length> or the calculated contact geometry.}

Saggu, Levinson and Boxer (2011) used <infrared spectroscopy> to compare $200\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$ <phenol> in different <solvents>. The O–H stretching peak was at $3611\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in $\mathrm{CCl}_4$ and $3555.9\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in <benzene>. To distinguish association from a general <solvent> effect, they diluted the ring-containing <chemical solutions> with $\mathrm{CCl}_4$: two peaks appeared, with the free peak nearly fixed while the associated peak depended on the partner. Replacing the O–H <hydrogen> with <deuterium> reproduced the ordering of shifts for different partners. Thus the perturbed O–H group identifies the <hydrogen bond donor>; the ring supplies the <hydrogen bond acceptor>. A peak shift alone does not specify a unique <molecular geometry>. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3202663/[Saggu, Levinson and Boxer, Direct measurements of electric fields in weak OH···π hydrogen bonds, JACS 133 (2011), Figure 1, Table 1 and dilution controls]{ref}

The roles make recognition between <molecules> intelligible: exposed <hydrogen bond donors> need compatible <hydrogen bond acceptors> in reachable positions. A count of sites is a starting description, not a prediction of binding strength. In <water>, forming a contact between two partners can also replace their existing <hydrogen bonds> to <water>; the <Gibbs free energy> of association concerns the whole change. See https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld, pp. 15–16: competition with solvent interactions].

= Carbonyl group
{parent=Chemical bond}
{title2=$\mathrm{C=O}$}

A <carbonyl group> contains a <carbon>–<oxygen> double <covalent bond>. It occurs in <aldehydes> and <amides>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/12-7-interpreting-infrared-spectra[McMurry: carbonyl-containing groups]{ref}

= Covalent bond
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

A <covalent bond> joins <chemical atoms> through shared <electrons>. In the familiar two-centre description, one <bond pair> contributes to a connection between two <atomic nuclei>. Sharing means that the <electrons> belong to the combined arrangement and interact with both <atomic nuclei>; it does not mean that identifiable dots shuttle along drawn paths. The https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01384[IUPAC Gold Book, covalent bond]{ref} emphasizes the concentration of <electrons> between <atomic nuclei> and the resulting attraction and characteristic separation. The pair picture is especially useful for simple <molecules>, but is not a universal description of every <covalent bond>.

A <Lewis structure> turns this picture into practical bookkeeping. Count the <valence electrons>, indicate which <chemical atoms> are connected, and allocate two <electrons> to each line. A <bond pair> is shared; a <lone pair> is assigned to one <chemical atom> without connecting it to another. A double line represents two <bond pairs>. Dots and lines record allocations, rather than positions, trajectories, or a measured three-dimensional arrangement. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, Lewis symbols and structures]{ref}

For <molecular hydrogen>, $\mathrm H_2$, each neutral <hydrogen> <chemical atom> supplies one <valence electron>. Thus $1+1=2$ <valence electrons> make one <bond pair>, represented by $\mathrm{H-H}$, with no <lone pairs>. Each <hydrogen> counts the same shared pair when checking its two-electron arrangement. The <molecule> still contains two <electrons>, not four: counting a pair around both endpoints does not create extra <electrons>.

For <water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, neutral <oxygen> supplies six <valence electrons> and the two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> supply one each: $6+1+1=8$. Two O–H connections consume four <electrons> as two <bond pairs>. The remaining $8-4=4$ <electrons> form two <lone pairs> on <oxygen>. Around <oxygen>, the two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs> count as eight <electrons>; each <hydrogen> counts two. The whole <water> <molecule> has ten <electrons>, because two additional <oxygen> <electrons> are omitted from this <valence electron> drawing.

For <ammonia>, $\mathrm{NH}_3$, the total is $5+3\times1=8$ <valence electrons>: five from <nitrogen> and three from <hydrogen>. Three N–H connections use six <electrons> as three <bond pairs>, leaving two <electrons> as one <lone pair> on <nitrogen>. Thus <water> and <ammonia> have the same total of eight <valence electrons> but different allocations: two plus two pairs versus three plus one. This small calculation already distinguishes their connectivity without a detailed <quantum mechanics> calculation.

\Image[lewis-electron-pair-counts.png]
{title=<Lewis structures> count <bond pairs> and <lone pairs> in <molecular hydrogen>, <water>, <ammonia>, and <molecular oxygen>; schematic layouts do not specify molecular shapes}

For <molecular oxygen>, $\mathrm O_2$, $6+6=12$ <valence electrons> give the conventional $\mathrm{O=O}$ <Lewis structure>. Its two <bond pairs> use four <electrons>, leaving eight as four <lone pairs>, two on each <oxygen>. Each <oxygen> consequently counts eight <electrons> locally. However, actual <molecular oxygen> has two unpaired <electrons> and exhibits <paramagnetism>: it is attracted into a stronger <magnetic field>. The all-paired <Lewis structure> cannot capture this observation. <Molecular orbital theory> describes the unpaired <electrons>; the successful counting diagram alone is insufficient. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, molecular orbital theory]{ref}

Why does sharing produce a stable separation? For <molecular hydrogen>, the electronic arrangement responds as the <atomic nuclei> approach. Their interactions can lower the <energy>, but pushing them too close raises it. A curve of <potential energy> against separation therefore has a <local minimum>, rather than falling forever. Attraction and repulsion balance there. This is a statement about the combined system, not a claim that stationary <electrons> sit between otherwise motionless <atomic nuclei>. <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.2 Covalent Bonding>{ref}

The following <Morse potential> makes those features calculable. Set $x=r/r_e$, where $r$ is separation and $r_e$ its equilibrium value, and let $D$ be the well depth. With the self-chosen parameter $a=2$, define the <dimensionless quantities>
$$
u(x)=\frac{V(r)}{D}=\left(1-e^{-2(x-1)}\right)^2-1,
\qquad f(x)=\frac{F(r)r_e}{D}=-\frac{du}{dx}=4\left(e^{-4(x-1)}-e^{-2(x-1)}\right).
$$
Here $F=-dV/dr$ is the net separating <force>, obtained from the <derivative> of the <potential energy>. Positive $f$ pushes the <chemical atoms> apart; negative $f$ draws them together. These are illustrative curves with $r_e=D=1$ in chosen units, not measured <molecular hydrogen> data or fitted parameters.

\Image[covalent-bond-energy-model.png]
{title=Illustrative <dimensionless> <Morse potential> and its <force>: a stable <local minimum>, short-distance repulsion, and separation toward zero <potential energy>}

At $x=1$, $u=-1$ and $f=0$. The <second derivative> is $u''(1)=8>0$, confirming a <local minimum>. At $x=0.5$, $u=(1-e)^2-1\approx1.9525$; at $x=1.5$, $u=(1-e^{-1})^2-1\approx-0.6004$. As $x$ increases without bound, $u$ approaches zero from below. Thus moving from the well bottom to separated <chemical atoms> requires an increase of $D$ in this model's <potential energy>. An actual <quantum mechanics> dissociation threshold also depends on the initial motion of the <atomic nuclei>, so the well depth alone is not every measured dissociation <energy>.

The <force> panel separates $f$ into a positive term $4e^{-4(x-1)}$ and a negative term $-4e^{-2(x-1)}$. At $x=1$ they are $+4$ and $-4$, cancelling exactly; nearer separations favour the positive term. These are algebraic components of the chosen <Morse potential>, not separately measured microscopic <forces>. Their usefulness is to show how a balance arises from the slope of one <energy> curve.

The beauty of the pair picture is its economy: a few marks summarize connectivity and constrain what a proposed <molecule> can contain. Its historical anchor is Gilbert N. Lewis's 1916 paper https://doi.org/10.1021/ja02261a002[The Atom and the Molecule, Journal of the American Chemical Society 38, 762–785]{ref}, also available as an https://zenodo.org/records/1429068[archived original paper]{ref}. <Lewis structures> remain useful because they answer counting questions quickly; <quantum mechanics> supplies the richer account when <energy>, shape, or unpaired <electrons> matter.

= Phosphodiester bond
{parent=Covalent bond}
{wiki}

A <phosphodiester bond> connects two groups through a <phosphate> diester. In a <nucleic acid> backbone, the bridging <phosphate> is connected through <oxygen> to the $3'$ <carbon> position of one <sugar> and the $5'$ <carbon> position of another. These <covalent bonds> connect successive <nucleotides> along a strand; they are distinct from the <hydrogen bonds> between paired <nucleobases>.

= Disulfide bond
{parent=Covalent bond}
{title2=$\mathrm{S-S}$}

A <disulfide bond> is the single <covalent bond> between the two adjacent <sulfur> <chemical atoms> in a <disulfide>, conventionally written $\mathrm{R-S-S-R'}$. Here $R$ and $R'$ stand for the attached organic groups; they may belong to the same <molecule> or to initially separate <molecules>. A <disulfide bond> is a chemical bridge, rather than a <hydrogen bond> or a mere contact between nearby groups. Two <thiols>, $\mathrm{R-SH}$ and $\mathrm{R'-SH}$, can supply its two <sulfur> endpoints. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/18-7-thiols-and-sulfides[John McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 18.7: thiol oxidation and disulfides]{ref}

The <stoichiometry> of forming a <disulfide bond> is especially revealing. Written as an <oxidation> <half-reaction>, it is
$$
\mathrm{R-SH+R'-SH\longrightarrow R-S-S-R'+2H^++2e^-}.
$$
The reverse <reduction> <half-reaction> consumes two <electrons> and two <hydrogen ions> to restore two <thiols>. Both sides contain two <sulfur> <chemical atoms>; the two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> have moved into the two <hydrogen ions>, and the net <electric charge> remains zero because $+2-2=0$. Thus one <disulfide bond> corresponds to two <thiol> groups and two transferred <electrons>. These <half-reactions> describe <redox> bookkeeping, not a complete standalone <chemical reaction>: an accompanying <reactant> must accept or supply the <electrons>, and <pH> affects the actual <protonation> of the groups. <Mercaptoethanol> can promote <reduction> through exchange of <disulfide bonds>, but adequate excess and appropriate conditions are needed; adding it does not by itself prove complete <reduction>. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3766385/[Jakob R. Winther and Colin Thorpe, Quantification of Thiols and Disulfides (2014), section 3: reducing reagents and completeness]{ref}

In a <protein disulfide bond>, the endpoints are normally the <amino acid side chains> of two <cysteine> <amino acid residues>. An intrachain <protein disulfide bond> closes an intramolecular bridge within one <polypeptide chain>; an interchain <protein disulfide bond> connects different <polypeptide chains>, and can arise by intermolecular crosslinking of initially separate <molecules>. Forming or reducing these bridges changes <covalent bond> connectivity and can change <protein structure>, while preserving the order of <amino acid residues> and the <peptide bonds> in the <protein backbone>. A complete chemical account of <protein primary structure> includes crosslinks as well as sequence, so preservation of sequence does not imply preservation of every <covalent bond>. Mature <insulin> has two interchain <protein disulfide bonds> and one intrachain <protein disulfide bond>. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14744022/[Seung-Gu Chang, Ki-Doo Choi, Seung-Hwan Jang and Hang-Cheol Shin, Role of disulfide bonds in the structure and activity of human insulin (2003)]{ref}

\Image[disulfide-bonds-redox-and-bridges.png]
{title=<Disulfide bond> <redox> bookkeeping and schematic intrachain and interchain <protein disulfide bonds>}
{description=Original connectivity cartoons, not measured <protein structures>. Blue and green traces preserve their numbered <amino acid residues>; amber S-S links connect <amino acid side chains> without replacing a <peptide bond>. The cartoons are invented examples, not <insulin> coordinates.}

A bridge is useful because it constrains which parts of a <polypeptide chain> can move apart without breaking a <covalent bond>. Its placement therefore matters as well as its presence. The <ribonuclease A refolding experiment> provides the existing experimental example: breaking and reforming four <protein disulfide bonds> can accompany loss and recovery of function. Counting four bridges alone cannot tell whether their endpoints have the native pairing. This is an attractive connection between simple chemical counting and the richer problem of <protein folding>.

How can an experiment reveal an initially hidden <disulfide bond>? <Ellman's reagent>, also called <DTNB>, measures free <thiols> by producing a yellow absorbing species. Its reactive <disulfide bond> undergoes exchange with the <conjugate base> $\mathrm{R-S^-}$ of a <thiol>, producing a mixed <disulfide> attached to $R$ and one released 2-nitro-5-thiobenzoate species, abbreviated TNB. At the assay <pH>, the strongly colored form is the dianion $\mathrm{TNB^{2-}}$: one negative charge is on <sulfur> and the other is on the <carboxylic acid>-derived $\mathrm{COO^-}$ group. With excess <DTNB> and complete reaction, one released TNB reports one reacted <thiol>. George L. Ellman's 1959 paper introduced the tissue assay; it was subsequently reexamined quantitatively. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13650640/[George L. Ellman, Tissue sulfhydryl groups (1959)]{ref} https://assets.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/LSG/manuals/MAN0011216_Ellmans_Reag_UG.pdf[Pierce Biotechnology, Ellman's Reagent instructions, introduction and reaction diagram]{ref}

The modern apparatus can be simple: tubes, pipettes, a <buffer solution>, <cysteine> standards, <DTNB>, and an instrument that measures <absorbance> through a cuvette at <wavelength> $412\,\mathrm{nm}$. The Pierce protocol uses $50\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ of a $4\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}$ <DTNB> stock, $2.50\,\mathrm{mL}$ of <buffer solution>, and $250\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ of sample. Its <buffer solution> is $0.1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ sodium <phosphate> at <pH> 8.0 with a metal-binding additive. After 15 minutes at room <temperature>, measure <absorbance> against a <buffer solution> plus <DTNB> blank; run known <cysteine> <concentrations> in parallel. The observable is increased yellow color and increased blank-corrected <absorbance> as more accessible <thiols> react, rather than direct imaging of <disulfide bonds>. These are published protocol conditions, not measurements performed for this article. https://assets.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/LSG/manuals/MAN0011216_Ellmans_Reag_UG.pdf[Pierce Biotechnology, Ellman's Reagent instructions, page 2: standard and cuvette procedures]{ref}

For a before/after test, split a purified <protein> sample into matched aliquots. Measure accessible free <thiols> in one aliquot; subject the other to complete <reduction> of its <disulfide bonds>, remove the reducing <reagent>, then measure the resulting <thiols> with fresh <DTNB>. Process the unreduced aliquot through a matching cleanup. Normalize to recovered <protein> and known <dilution>, since cleanup can change both. To estimate all reducible <protein disulfide bonds>, expose buried groups using matching <protein denaturation> conditions in both aliquots and calibrate in that same <chemical solution>; a native-accessibility measurement answers a different question. A larger corrected reading after <reduction> supports conversion of previously unreactive groups into detectable <thiols>. Attribution to <disulfide bonds> requires excluding other reducible <sulfur> modifications. This is a proposed comparison based on the detection principles, not a reconstruction of Ellman's original tissue measurements. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3766385/[Winther and Thorpe (2014), sections 1.2 and 4.1: indirect disulfide detection and assay limitations]{ref}

\Image[disulfide-bonds-ellman-workflow.png]
{title=Matched <Ellman's reagent> assay workflow and an explicitly hypothetical <disulfide bond> count}
{description=Original apparatus and workflow schematic. The displayed <absorbances> are calculated illustrations for two free <thiols> and two <disulfide bonds> per model <protein> at equal final <protein> <concentration>; they are not measured data or a calibration curve. Reducing <reagent> removal and matched accessibility are prerequisites for interpreting the difference.}

Under the <Beer-Lambert law>, the blank-corrected <absorbance> is $A=\varepsilon lc$, where $l$ is the cuvette path length, $c$ is the released TNB <concentration>, and $\varepsilon$ is its molar absorption coefficient. Riddles, Blakeley and Zerner measured $\varepsilon_{412}=14150\,\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,cm^{-1}}$ in dilute aqueous salt <chemical solutions>, confirming the value independently using <cysteine>. It must not be applied indiscriminately to other <solvents> or <protein denaturation> mixtures. https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0003269779907929[Peter W. Riddles, Robert L. Blakeley and Burt Zerner, Ellman's reagent: a reexamination (1979)]{ref} For aliquots compared on the same final volume and <protein> <concentration> basis, the ideal estimate is
$$
c_{\mathrm{S-S}}=\frac{c_{\mathrm{SH,after}}-c_{\mathrm{SH,before}}}{2}
=\frac{A_{\mathrm{after}}-A_{\mathrm{before}}}{2\varepsilon l},
\qquad
\bar n_{\mathrm{S-S}}=\frac{c_{\mathrm{S-S}}}{c_{\mathrm{protein}}}.
$$
The factor of two follows from the <reduction> <stoichiometry>; $\bar n_{\mathrm{S-S}}$ is the number of <disulfide bonds> per <protein> <molecule>, or an average for a mixed population. If the aliquots have different <dilutions> or recoveries, convert each reading to a common sample basis before subtracting.

As a hypothetical calculation, choose $c_{\mathrm{protein}}=10\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ in each final cuvette, two free <thiols> and two <disulfide bonds> per <protein> <molecule>, and $l=1\,\mathrm{cm}$. Before <reduction>, $c_{\mathrm{SH}}=20\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ gives $A=0.283$; complete <reduction> adds four <thiols> per <protein> <molecule>, so $c_{\mathrm{SH}}=60\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ gives $A=0.849$. The difference $0.566$ gives $c_{\mathrm{S-S}}=20\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ and $\bar n_{\mathrm{S-S}}=2$. The published tube volumes imply a <dilution> factor $2.80/0.250=11.2$; reversing that <dilution> multiplies both <thiol> and <protein> <concentrations> by 11.2 and leaves the bridge count per <protein> <molecule> unchanged.

Controls determine whether that arithmetic has chemical meaning. Use fresh <cysteine> standards and a processed blank without <protein> to detect color generated by residual reducing <reagent>. Even a reducing <reagent> containing no <thiols> can interfere: Han and Han showed that tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine, abbreviated TCEP, reduces <DTNB> to two TNB equivalents per TCEP. https://doi.org/10.1006/abio.1994.1290[J. C. Han and G. Y. Han, A Procedure for Quantitative Determination of Tris(2-Carboxyethyl)phosphine (1994)]{ref} In the hypothetical model, $5\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ residual TCEP in only the reduced cuvette would add $A=0.1415$ and falsely add $0.5$ bridge per <protein> <molecule>. A stable endpoint and agreement after a further <reduction> treatment help check completion. A sample without <DTNB> checks its own color; cloudy samples can also attenuate light without producing TNB. Match <pH>, <temperature>, accessibility and calibration <chemical solution>, and minimize delay that permits reoxidation. If two already-free but buried <thiols> become accessible only in the reduced aliquot, they mimic one new <disulfide bond> per <protein> <molecule>. Finally, bulk <absorbance> supplies a count under these assumptions: it does not locate the <cysteine> endpoints, distinguish intrachain from interchain bridges, or establish the native pairing of a <protein disulfide bond>.

= Lewis structure
{parent=Covalent bond}
{c}
{wiki}

= Lewis formula
{c}
{synonym}

= Lewis dot structure
{c}
{synonym}

Before calculating electronic <energy>, ask a simpler question: where can the available <valence electrons> go? A <Lewis structure> makes that budget visible, and <nitrate> shows why a correct budget can still require several <resonance structures>.

A <Lewis structure> records which <chemical atoms> are connected in a <molecule> or <ion> and how its <valence electrons> are allocated. One line represents one <bond pair>, two lines two <bond pairs>, and three lines three <bond pairs>; two dots beside a <chemical atom> represent one <lone pair>. The marks are a counting language, rather than measured <electron> positions. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/L03513[IUPAC Gold Book, Lewis formula].

For <water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, the budget is $6+2\times1=8$ <valence electrons>. Two O–H lines use four <electrons>; the other four form two <lone pairs> on <oxygen>. For <ammonia>, $\mathrm{NH}_3$, $5+3\times1=8$ <valence electrons> give three N–H lines and one <lone pair> on <nitrogen>. Both <molecules> contain four <electron pairs>, but their allocations differ. Each shared <bond pair> is counted once in the total budget, even though both endpoints count it when checking the <octet rule>.

\Image[lewis-water-ammonia.png]
{title=Eight <valence electrons> in each <molecule>: <water> has two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs>; <ammonia> has three <bond pairs> and one <lone pair>}
{description=Each blue line represents two <electrons>; each orange dot represents one <electron>. The <oxygen> and <nitrogen> each count eight <electrons> locally, while each <hydrogen> counts two. These are schematic <Lewis structures>, not predictions of molecular shape or <electron> trajectories.}

A practical construction method is:
* Add the neutral <chemical atoms>' <valence electron> counts. Add one <electron> for each unit of negative <electric charge>, or subtract one for each unit of positive <electric charge>.
* Choose candidate connections and put one <bond pair> on each. <Hydrogen> normally has one connection in these simple <Lewis structures>.
* Place remaining <electrons> as <lone pairs>, checking the <octet rule>. If an endpoint has eight <electrons> but the central <chemical atom> lacks them, try converting an endpoint <lone pair> into an additional <bond pair>, keeping the total budget fixed.
* Compute every <formal charge> and check its sum against the net <electric charge>. Compare reasonable candidates, including <chemical resonance> when several allocations share the same connections.

For <carbon dioxide>, $4+2\times6=16$ <valence electrons>. Starting with two single C–O connections leaves twelve <electrons>, enough for three <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>, but only four <electrons> around <carbon>. Converting one <lone pair> from each <oxygen> into a second <bond pair> gives $\mathrm{O=C=O}$. Eight <electrons> are in the four <bond pairs>, and eight are in four <lone pairs>, two on each <oxygen>. <Carbon> has no <lone pair>; all three <chemical atoms> satisfy the <octet rule>, and all three <formal charges> vanish.

For the <nitrate ion>, $\mathrm{NO}_3^-$, the <valence electron> budget is
$$
5+3\times6+1=24.
$$
<Nitrogen> contributes five, the three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> contribute eighteen, and the negative <electric charge> adds one <electron>. Start with <nitrogen> connected to each <oxygen> by one <bond pair>: those three lines use six <electrons>. Put the remaining eighteen <electrons> into three <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>. Each <oxygen> now satisfies the <octet rule>, but <nitrogen> counts only six <electrons>. Reassign one <oxygen> <lone pair> as a second <bond pair> on its N–O connection. This changes the allocation without adding an <electron>; it applies the construction procedure in https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.3: drawing Lewis structures].

The resulting <nitrate> <Lewis structure> has one N=O connection and two N–O connections. Its four <bond pairs> use eight <electrons>, and its eight <lone pairs> use sixteen: $8+16=24$. The doubly connected <oxygen> has two <lone pairs>; each singly connected <oxygen> has three; <nitrogen> has none. All four <chemical atoms> satisfy the <octet rule>. Any of the three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> can supply the additional <bond pair>, giving the three equivalent <resonance structures> below.

\Image[nitrate-resonance-contributors.png]
{title=Three equivalent <nitrate> <resonance structures>, each with 24 <valence electrons>}
{description=The same labelled <chemical atoms> occupy the same positions in all three <Lewis structures>. Each line counts two <electrons>; each dot counts one. Each drawing has four <bond pairs>, eight <lone pairs>, <formal charge> $+1$ on <nitrogen>, $-1$ on each singly connected <oxygen>, and zero on the doubly connected <oxygen>. The brackets give the net <electric charge>; the arrows indicate <chemical resonance>, not a <chemical reaction> or elapsed time.}

Why not draw three N=O connections to make the <ion> look symmetric? With two <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>, that drawing still spends $12+12=24$ <electrons> and has the right total <formal charge>, but places twelve <electrons> around <nitrogen>. It fails the <octet rule> for this second-period <chemical element>. Three equivalent <resonance structures> express the symmetry without that invalid local count.

The economy is beautiful: a handful of dots and lines rules out many impossible counts and identifies <lone pairs> that can participate in <acid-base chemistry>. A <Lewis structure> helps choose rational candidate connections before a detailed <quantum chemistry> calculation. It does not specify a complete many-<electron> <wavefunction>, actual <electron density>, or molecular geometry. Adding the separate <VSEPR model>, described in https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Chemistry 2e, 7.6: molecular structure and polarity], predicts bent <water> and pyramidal <ammonia>; the layouts above alone make no such prediction. <Molecular orbital theory> supplies a richer electronic description, including the unpaired <electrons> of <molecular oxygen> that an all-paired <Lewis structure> misses.

In 1916 Gilbert N. Lewis proposed two-dot notation for the shared <electrons> of a <chemical bond> and fuller dot formulae for the outer <electrons>, explicitly relating a shared pair to the existing single-line notation. See https://doi.org/10.1021/ja02261a002[Lewis, The Atom and the Molecule, JACS 38, 762–785 (1916), pp. 777–778], available as an https://zenodo.org/records/1429068[archived original paper]{ref}; the relevant passage is also readable in a https://www.chemteam.info/Chem-History/Lewis-1916/Lewis-1916.html[transcription of the original paper]{ref}. The modern <quantum mechanics> interpretation should be distinguished from his historical model of fixed outer <electrons>.

= Valence shell electron pair repulsion theory
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki=VSEPR_theory}

= VSEPR model
{c}
{synonym}

= VSEPR theory
{c}
{synonym}

The <VSEPR model> predicts approximate <molecular geometry> by arranging the <electron domains> around a central <chemical atom> to reduce their mutual repulsion. Start with a <Lewis structure>, count the domains, and then distinguish the resulting <electron pair geometry> from the positions of the <chemical atoms> themselves. A <lone pair> counts as a domain even though it is not another <chemical atom>.https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-091-introduction-to-solid-state-chemistry-fall-2018/g9v8zj6VObw_transcript.pdf[MIT 3.091, lecture 11: Lewis structures, domains and molecular shapes]{ref}

Three worked counts have the same total of eight <valence electrons>. In <methane>, <carbon>'s four plus the contributions of four <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> give $4+4=8$: four <bond pairs>, no <lone pairs>, and <tetrahedral molecular geometry>. In <ammonia>, <nitrogen>'s five plus three from <hydrogen> give $5+3=8$: three <bond pairs> and one <lone pair>, producing <trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry>. In <water>, <oxygen>'s six plus two from <hydrogen> give $6+2=8$: two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs>, producing <bent molecular geometry>. All three have four <electron domains>; their <molecular geometries> differ.

\Image[vsepr-four-domain-shapes.png]
{title=Four electron domains give three molecular shapes as lone pairs replace bonded neighbours}

The regular tetrahedral directions can be represented by the <unit vectors>
$$
\frac1{\sqrt3}(1,1,1),\quad
\frac1{\sqrt3}(1,-1,-1),\quad
\frac1{\sqrt3}(-1,1,-1),\quad
\frac1{\sqrt3}(-1,-1,1).
$$
Each pair has <dot product> $-1/3$, so the ideal <bond angle> is $\theta=\arccos(-1/3)\approx109.47^\circ$. This is a geometric calculation, rather than a prediction that every four-domain <molecule> has that exact <bond angle>. <Lone pairs> and different kinds of <chemical bonds> distort the ideal arrangement; <water>'s H–O–H angle is about $104.5^\circ$.https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Chemistry 2e, 7.6: ideal geometry and water's bond angle]{ref}

The model is attractive because small-number bookkeeping produces a spatial prediction without first solving for every <molecular orbital>. Shape then helps explain <chemical polarity> and which <hydrogen bond donor> or <hydrogen bond acceptor> sites are accessible. It is a qualitative model, not a full calculation of the electronic <energy> or a universal solution for every <chemical compound>. Gillespie and Nyholm's 1957 review https://doi.org/10.1039/QR9571100339[Inorganic stereochemistry, Quarterly Reviews 11, 339–380]{ref} is a historical anchor for its development.

= Electron domain
{parent=Valence shell electron pair repulsion theory}

An <electron domain> is a region of <electron density> counted around a central <chemical atom> in the <VSEPR model>. One <lone pair> counts as one domain; a bond to one neighbour counts as one domain even when its <bond order> is two or three. Domain count is therefore different from simply counting <electron pairs>.

= Electron pair geometry
{parent=Valence shell electron pair repulsion theory}

<Electron pair geometry> describes the arrangement of all <electron domains> in the <VSEPR model>, including <lone pairs>. <Molecular geometry> instead describes the arrangement of <chemical atoms>. Four domains have a tetrahedral ideal arrangement, but keeping only the bonded neighbours gives the different <molecular geometries> of <methane>, <ammonia> and <water>.

= Bond pair
{parent=Lewis structure}

= Bonding pair
{synonym}

A <bond pair> is an <electron pair> allocated to a connection between two <chemical atoms> in a <Lewis structure>. A single line stands for one <bond pair>, or two <electrons>. A double connection contains two <bond pairs>, or four <electrons>; a triple connection contains three <bond pairs>, or six <electrons>. A <bond pair> is shared bookkeeping for the combined arrangement, rather than two distinguishable <electrons> each permanently owned by one endpoint.

In <water>, two <bond pairs> use $2\times2=4$ of the eight <valence electrons>. In <ammonia>, three <bond pairs> use $3\times2=6$ of the eight <valence electrons>. In $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, each C=O connection contains two <bond pairs>, so there are four <bond pairs> in the entire <carbon dioxide> <molecule>. Counting connections and counting <bond pairs> therefore give different answers for multiple connections.

The same <bond pair> is treated differently for different questions: both endpoints count its two <electrons> for the <octet rule>, whereas each endpoint receives just one <electron> for <formal charge>. Neither convention asserts equal physical <electron density> at the endpoints. For electronic shapes, occupancy, and <energy>, see <molecular orbital theory> rather than treating a <bond pair> line as an entire <wavefunction>.

= Lone pair
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki}

A <lone pair> is an <electron pair> assigned to one <chemical atom> without a connecting line to another <chemical atom> in a <Lewis structure>. It is usually shown as two dots. <Water> has two <lone pairs> on <oxygen>, accounting for four <valence electrons>; <ammonia> has one <lone pair> on <nitrogen>, accounting for two. In $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, each <oxygen> has two <lone pairs>, and <carbon> has none.

A <lone pair> can be reactive. In <protonation> of <ammonia>, the <nitrogen> <lone pair> becomes a new <bond pair> to a <proton>:
$$
\mathrm{NH}_3+\mathrm H^+\longrightarrow\mathrm{NH}_4^+.
$$
The <proton> supplies no <electron>, so the eight <valence electrons> are retained: three <bond pairs> plus one <lone pair> become four <bond pairs>. The resulting <nitrogen> has <formal charge> $5-0-8/2=+1$; each <hydrogen> has <formal charge> zero. This is why identifying an available <lone pair> helps explain the <chemical base> behaviour of <ammonia>, rather than merely decorating its formula.

Lewis already used the <ammonia> to $\mathrm{NH}_4^+$ example in https://zenodo.org/records/1429068[The Atom and the Molecule (1916), pp. 777–778]. In modern terms the dots identify an allocation of <electrons>, not two stationary particles or an isolated patch unaffected by the rest of the <molecule>. A <molecular orbital> description can spread electronic amplitude over several <chemical atoms> while a localized <Lewis structure> remains useful for <acid-base chemistry>.

= Formal charge
{parent=Lewis structure}
{title2=$\mathrm{FC}=v-n-b/2$}
{wiki}

The <formal charge> of a <chemical atom> in a <Lewis structure> assigns it all of its nonbonding <electrons> and half of the <electrons> in its connections:
$$
\mathrm{FC}=v-n-\frac b2.
$$
Here $v$ is the neutral <chemical atom>'s <valence electron> count, $n$ is the number of nonbonding <electrons> assigned to it, and $b$ is the number of bonding <electrons> incident on it. Count <electrons>, not <electron pairs>: two <lone pairs> mean $n=4$, and two double connections mean $b=8$. The number $\mathrm{FC}$ gives <electric charge> in units of the positive <elementary charge>. Equal splitting is a bookkeeping convention, regardless of the actual <electron density>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/08169[IUPAC Gold Book, formal charge].

\Image[lewis-carbon-dioxide-formal-charge.png]
{title=Sixteen <valence electrons> in two <carbon dioxide> <Lewis structures>: $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ has zero <formal charges>, while $\mathrm{O^-{-}C\equiv O^+}$ has separated <formal charges>}
{description=The first <Lewis structure> has two <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>. The second has three <lone pairs> on the singly connected <oxygen>, one on the triply connected <oxygen>, and none on <carbon>. Both use four <bond pairs> and four <lone pairs>; both satisfy the <octet rule>. Their contributions to <chemical resonance> are unequal, and the drawn <formal charges> are not <partial atomic charges>.}

For $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, each <oxygen> has $v=6$, $n=4$, $b=4$, giving <formal charge> $6-4-4/2=0$. <Carbon> has $v=4$, $n=0$, $b=8$, giving <formal charge> $4-0-8/2=0$. For $\mathrm{O^-{-}C\equiv O^+}$, the left <oxygen> has <formal charge> $6-6-2/2=-1$, <carbon> still has <formal charge> zero, and the right <oxygen> has <formal charge> $6-2-6/2=+1$. The net <electric charge> is zero in either drawing.

The <Lewis structure> with no separated <formal charges> is the dominant contributor in this elementary comparison; the charge-separated drawing is a less important contributor, rather than an equally dominant alternative. This is a qualitative selection rule, not a calculation of <energy> or numerical <chemical resonance> weights. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.4: comparing carbon dioxide structures]. A zero <formal charge> does not imply zero <partial atomic charge>: the latter depends on partitioning the actual <electron density>, which a <Lewis structure> does not supply.

As a short consistency derivation, summing $n+b/2$ over all <chemical atoms> counts every drawn <electron> once: a nonbonding <electron> is assigned once, and a bonding <electron> contributes half at each of its two endpoints. Thus
$$
\sum_i\mathrm{FC}_i=\sum_i v_i-N_{\mathrm{valence}},
$$
where $N_{\mathrm{valence}}$ is the total drawn <valence electron> count. The right side is the net <electric charge> in units of <elementary charge>. This catches a wrong budget even when individual <octet rule> checks look plausible.

= Resonance
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki}

= Chemical resonance
{synonym}

A single <nitrate> <Lewis structure> singles out one N=O connection, yet the three N–O <chemical bonds> of the isolated symmetric <ion> are equivalent. <Chemical resonance> reconciles that symmetry with the integer <electron pair> allocations of a <Lewis structure>. The equal <chemical bond> lengths in <nitrate> are discussed in https://www.open.edu/openlearn/mod/oucontent/view.php?id=87848&section=_unit5.2[The Open University, Discovering chemistry: resonance structures].

<Chemical resonance> uses several <Lewis structures> with the same <chemical atom> connections and total <electric charge> to describe electronic structure that one allocation cannot fully express. The <chemical atoms> stay in place; the positions of <bond pairs>, <lone pairs>, and <formal charges> differ between drawings. A two-headed arrow $\leftrightarrow$ connects the drawings and is distinct from a <chemical equilibrium> arrow $\rightleftharpoons$. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/08205[IUPAC Gold Book, resonance form].

In each <nitrate> <resonance structure>, use the existing <formal charge> convention $\mathrm{FC}=v-n-b/2$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{FC}(\mathrm N)&=5-0-8/2=+1,\\
\mathrm{FC}(\text{singly connected O})&=6-6-2/2=-1,\\
\mathrm{FC}(\text{doubly connected O})&=6-4-4/2=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $v$ counts neutral-<chemical atom> <valence electrons>, $n$ counts nonbonding <electrons>, and $b$ counts incident bonding <electrons>. The sum is $+1-1-1+0=-1$, agreeing with the <nitrate ion>'s net <electric charge>. The <formal charge> rules and their distinction from actual atomic <electric charge> are given in https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.4: formal charges and resonance].

Keep one labelled <oxygen> fixed while reading the three <nitrate> <resonance structures>. Its connection is double in one drawing and single in two. Equal weighting within this three-contributor <Lewis structure> model gives the average <bond order>
$$
\overline b_{\mathrm{N-O}}=\frac{2+1+1}{3}=\frac43.
$$
The same calculation applies to all three N–O connections. Averaging the drawn <formal charges> similarly gives $-2/3$ per <oxygen>, while <nitrogen> retains $+1$; $+1+3(-2/3)=-1$. These fractions summarize the chosen contributors. They are not a measurement of <partial atomic charge>, a literal fraction of an <electron> sitting on an <oxygen>, or time spent in one drawing.

\Image[nitrate-delocalized-bonding.png]
{title=Equivalent N–O <chemical bonds> in symmetric <nitrate>: the equal-contributor <bond order> count is $4/3$}
{description=All three connections have the same graphical treatment. A solid line with a dashed companion is a schematic indication of shared multiple-<chemical bond> character, not a plot of <electron density> or a physical fractional line. The labels $4/3$ record an average within three octet <Lewis structures>; no universal, directly measured <bond order> of exactly $1.333\ldots$ is asserted.}

The <resonance hybrid> has one spatially continuous <electron density> extending over the <ion>. None of its N–O <chemical bonds> is permanently selected as the double connection of one contributor. The dashed marks above are symbols; <electrons> do not travel along them or make the marks float between <chemical atoms>. For isolated symmetric <nitrate>, symmetry makes the three N–O <chemical bonds> equivalent; an environment that distinguishes the <oxygen> sites can remove that equivalence. The fraction $4/3$ alone predicts neither a <chemical bond> length nor a dissociation <energy>. The general relation between multiple <chemical bonds>, lengths, and strengths is discussed in <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.5 Strengths of Ionic and Covalent Bonds>; its tabulated averages are not a nitrate-specific calibration of $4/3$.

<Ozone> provides a useful two-contributor comparison. Its $3\times6=18$ <valence electrons> give three <bond pairs> and six <lone pairs>. One <resonance structure> has a double O–O connection on the left and a single one on the right; the other reverses these allocations while retaining the same <chemical atom> positions and connections. The central <oxygen> has one <lone pair> and <formal charge> $+1$; the singly connected terminal <oxygen> has three <lone pairs> and <formal charge> $-1$; the doubly connected terminal <oxygen> has two <lone pairs> and <formal charge> zero. Thus $6+12=18$ <electrons> and zero net <electric charge> are conserved. The equal-contributor <bond order> count is $(2+1)/2=3/2$ for either connection. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-111sc-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2014/3549685d450773a066a716832634203f_MIT5_111F14_Lec10.pdf[MIT 5.111, lecture 10, pp. 4–5: ozone resonance structures].

For <carbon dioxide>, $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ and $\mathrm{O^-{-}C\equiv O^+}$ share sixteen <valence electrons> and the same O–C–O connections. The mirror drawing $\mathrm{O^+\equiv C{-}O^-}$ is also possible. The two charge-separated drawings are equivalent to one another for an isolated symmetric <carbon dioxide> <molecule>, but neither is equally dominant with $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ in the elementary <formal charge> comparison.

<Chemical resonance> does not mean that a <molecule> rapidly flips between drawings, nor that separate <molecules> at <chemical equilibrium> carry the individual drawings. The actual <electron density> belongs to one combined electronic description. In a <quantum mechanics> treatment, contributors enter a <linear combination> of many-<electron> <wavefunctions>; a <molecular orbital> treatment can describe the extended electronic arrangement through <molecular orbitals>. The drawings alone provide neither a measured density nor numerical weights. Their usefulness is to expose the limitations of one localized allocation without pretending that every algebraically allowed <Lewis structure> contributes equally.

= Resonance structure
{parent=Resonance (chemistry)}

= Resonance form
{synonym}

= Resonance contributor
{synonym}

A <resonance structure> is one <Lewis structure> contributing to a <chemical resonance> description. The contributors retain the same <atomic nuclei> in the same positions, the same connections, and the same total <valence electron> count and <electric charge>; allocations of <bond pairs>, <lone pairs>, and <formal charges> differ. They are alternative representations of one electronic structure, not distinct substances with different atomic arrangements. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance[Chemistry 2e, 7.4: resonance forms].

= Resonance hybrid
{parent=Resonance (chemistry)}

A <resonance hybrid> is the combined electronic description expressed using several <resonance structures>. The word does not describe a mixture of separate <molecules> or <ions>: a <nitrate ion> has one <electron density>, rather than three species in <chemical equilibrium>. Equal weighting is appropriate for the equivalent <nitrate> contributors in the elementary model; the unequal <carbon dioxide> contributors illustrate why it is not a general rule. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance[Chemistry 2e, 7.4: resonance hybrids do not fluctuate between forms].

= Octet rule
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki}

The <octet rule> is a useful empirical guide: many <Lewis structures> of <chemical compounds> containing <carbon>, <nitrogen>, and <oxygen> give each of those <chemical atoms> eight <electrons> when nonbonding <electrons> and all incident bonding <electrons> are counted. <Hydrogen> instead normally counts two <electrons>. These are local counts, not independent budgets: a shared <bond pair> counts at both endpoints but only once in the <molecule>'s <valence electron> total.

In <water>, <oxygen> counts $4+4=8$ <electrons>; in <ammonia>, <nitrogen> counts $2+6=8$; in $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, <carbon> counts $0+8=8$ and each <oxygen> counts $4+4=8$. The charge-separated <carbon dioxide> drawing also satisfies the <octet rule>, so the <octet rule> alone cannot select the dominant contributor. <Formal charge> provides another check.

The <octet rule> is a heuristic rather than a theorem about every <chemical bond>. Odd <valence electron> totals cannot be partitioned entirely into <electron pairs>, and some valid <Lewis structures> have fewer or more than eight <electrons> around a <chemical atom>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures[Chemistry 2e, 7.3: exceptions to the octet rule]. The capacity of the $s$ and $p$ <electron subshells>, $2+6=8$, helps motivate the guide for these <chemical elements>, but does not prove universal compliance. Lewis discussed a stable outer group of eight, together with a two-<electron> arrangement for <hydrogen>, in https://zenodo.org/records/1429068[The Atom and the Molecule (1916), pp. 768 and 774].

= Valence electron
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

A <valence electron> belongs to the electronic arrangement used to describe a <chemical atom>'s <chemical bonds> and ordinary <chemical reactions>. For the neutral <hydrogen>, <carbon>, <nitrogen>, and <oxygen> <chemical atoms> used here, the <valence electrons> occupy the outermost occupied <electron shell>. Their respective <electron configurations> are $1s^1$, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^2$, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^3$, and $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^4$, giving one, four, five, and six <valence electrons>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations[Chemistry 2e, 6.4: electron configurations and valence electrons].

<Lewis structures> show these <valence electrons> while omitting the inner <electrons>. Consequently, eight drawn <valence electrons> in <water> do not mean eight total <electrons>: neutral <water> has ten, with two inner <oxygen> <electrons> omitted. Neutral <ammonia> likewise has ten total <electrons> but eight <valence electrons>; neutral <carbon dioxide> has twenty-two total <electrons> but sixteen <valence electrons>.

This distinction makes the small counts useful: connections and possible <lone pairs> can be organized without representing every <electron> or solving the entire many-<electron> <wavefunction>. The outermost-<electron shell> shortcut applies to the examples above, rather than serving as a universal definition for all <chemical elements>; the electronic arrangement relevant to <chemical bonds> can also involve other <electron subshells>.

= Molar mass
{parent=Chemistry}
{title2=$M,\ M_n$}

<Molar mass> is <mass> per <amount of substance>, commonly expressed in $\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. For chain counts $n_i$ and chain <molar masses> $M_i$, the number average is $M_n=\sum_i n_iM_i/\sum_i n_i$. Relating it to a <degree of polymerization> requires a defined unit-mass convention and end-group accounting.

= Polymer
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <polymer> is a large molecular chain or network built from many chemically linked repeating units. Statistical chain models idealize its microscopic structure by segment vectors and describe its conformation through distributions, correlations and scattering. A <freely jointed chain> fixes each segment length while allowing independent orientations; a <Gaussian chain> replaces the segment or coarse-grained displacement distribution by a <Gaussian distribution>. These ideal models neglect excluded-volume and other segment interactions.

= Flory approximation
{c}
{parent=Polymer}
{title2=$\mathcal F/(k_BT)\sim R^2/(Nb^2)+vN^2/R^d$}

The <Flory approximation> balances the entropic cost of expanding an <ideal chain> against a mean-field <excluded volume> interaction. For $N$ segments of length $b$ in $d$ spatial dimensions, minimizing $R^2/(Nb^2)+vN^2/R^d$ gives $R^{d+2}\sim vN^3b^2$. This yields the scaling exponents $3/5$ in three dimensions and $3/4$ in two dimensions. It is a scaling approximation, not an exact numerical free energy or proof of critical exponents; confinement replaces $R^d$ by the accessible coil volume.

= Polymerization
{parent=Polymer}

<Polymerization> converts a <monomer>, or a mixture of <monomers>, into a <polymer>: repeated <chemical reactions> establish a connected molecular chain or network. The central question is how connections form, not merely whether a material becomes solid. Joining small <molecules> can produce a thread that can be handled, while the individual <molecules> remain invisible. See https://old.iupac.org/publications/pac/1974/pdf/4003x0477.pdf[IUPAC, Basic Definitions of Terms Relating to Polymers, definition 2.2].

\Image[polymerization-monomers-interface-units.png]
{id=polymerization-monomers-interface-units}
{title=From two liquid layers to a <nylon 6,10> thread: <monomers>, a <constitutional repeating unit>, and statistical segments}
{description=Original schematic of the authored UCSB <polymerization> demonstration described below. Chemical formulas identify the actual reactants and interior repeat; colored blocks identify their contributions. The lower coarse-graining sketch deliberately groups three repeats per model segment. Shapes, sizes, and that grouping are illustrative, not measured molecular geometry or a fitted <Kuhn length>.}

In <step-growth polymerization>, complementary reactive groups on <monomers> and on shorter or longer chains can react with one another. A two-unit chain can join a three-unit chain to make a five-unit chain; growth does not require a specially active end to add only free <monomers>. An example is the <nylon 6,10> reaction of $\mathrm{H_2N(CH_2)_6NH_2}$ with $\mathrm{ClC(=O)(CH_2)_8C(=O)Cl}$: each <amine> end can form an <amide> connection with an acid-chloride end, with formal elimination of $\mathrm{HCl}$. Both reactants have two suitable ends, allowing repeated joining. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/5474dc576b0669a8a09e9b70e39b1239_lec02_09082006.pdf[Paula Hammond, MIT 10.569 lecture 2: step-growth joining] and https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/d9f9ac7021b0d0f418d3d3c90dbfb94b_lec05_09152006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 5, p. 4: acid chloride–amine reaction].

In <chain-growth polymerization>, a <monomer> reacts with an active site on a growing chain, and the growth step regenerates an active site. Schematically, $P_j^*+M\longrightarrow P_{j+1}^*$, where $j$ counts incorporated <monomeric units> and the star denotes reactivity rather than a particular <electric charge>. <Ethene> forming <polyethylene> is an example: the double <chemical bond> is converted into single <chemical bonds> as the <carbon> backbone grows. Initiation creates the active chains; subsequent reactions can deactivate them or transfer reactivity to another <molecule>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00958/plain[IUPAC: chain polymerization] and https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/31-1-chain-growth-polymers[John McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 31.1: chain growth]. Whether growth is stepwise or carried by an active site is a different question from whether small byproducts are released: <chain-growth polymerization> can also release them.

\b[A motivating experiment: drawing nylon from a liquid interface.] Feldwinn's UCSB demonstration places $25\,\mathrm{mL}$ of an aqueous <chemical solution> of hexamethylenediamine, $\mathrm{H_2N(CH_2)_6NH_2}$, in a $100\,\mathrm{mL}$ beaker, then gently adds $25\,\mathrm{mL}$ of sebacoyl chloride, $\mathrm{ClC(=O)(CH_2)_8C(=O)Cl}$, in an organic <solvent>. The procedure specifies hexane; other parts of that handout list cyclohexane. The layers remain separate. The instructor catches material at their interface with tweezers and winds it slowly onto a glass rod; the handout reports continued spooling of <nylon 6,10>. It specifies freshly prepared acid-chloride <chemical solution> and washing the thread before touching it. These are observations and conditions of a supervised laboratory demonstration, not measurements of chain lengths. See https://people.chem.ucsb.edu/feldwinn/darby/DemoLibrary/DemoPDFs/Demo044.pdf[Feldwinn, UCSB Demonstration Library, Demo 044, pp. 1–3].

The mechanistic interpretation is that the <monomers> encounter one another near the interface, where <amide> connections produce the film; withdrawing it exposes a region where more material can form. An authored research demonstration independently reports a rapidly formed interfacial film collapsing into a thread on withdrawal, using cyclohexane and an aqueous <chemical solution> containing potassium hydroxide. See https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jchemed.6b00767[Polymer Day: Outreach Experiments for High School Students, Journal of Chemical Education (2017), nylon demonstration]. This is the striking observation: a thread much longer than the beaker can emerge because material is continually supplied to the formation region, rather than because a pre-existing thread was hidden in the liquid.

Useful \i[proposed controls], not results reported in those demonstrations, omit one <monomer> at a time while retaining the same <solvents>, liquid amounts, and pulling procedure. The prediction is loss of a renewable drawable <nylon 6,10> film; merely finding cloudiness would not establish <polymerization>, since other <chemical reactions>, including acid-chloride <hydrolysis>, can occur. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/21-4-chemistry-of-acid-halides[McMurry, 21.4: competing acid-chloride hydrolysis]. Stopping and restarting withdrawal would probe renewal at the interface. Even successful drawing does not measure the <degree of polymerization>, establish a chain-length <probability distribution>, or prove the entire growth mechanism. Chemical identification and a molecular-size measurement would be needed for those stronger conclusions. These controls and limits are inferences from the setup, not invented experimental data.

\b[Why almost complete reaction matters in step growth.] For ideal linear <step-growth polymerization>, assume exactly two reactive groups per starting <molecule>, equal numbers of complementary groups, only joining of different chains, no rings, no branching, and no loss of chain material. Let $N_0$ count starting <monomer molecules>, including both reactant species, and let $p$ be the fraction of either type of reactive group consumed. There are initially $N_0$ groups of each type, so $pN_0$ connections reduce the number of chain-bearing <molecules> to $N=N_0(1-p)$; the count excludes small byproducts but includes unreacted <monomers>. The number-average <degree of polymerization>, measured in <monomeric units>, is therefore
$$
X_n=\frac{N_0}{N}=\frac{1}{1-p}.
$$
Thus $p=0.90$, $0.99$, and $0.999$ give $X_n=10$, $100$, and $1000$. Losing the last fraction of unreacted groups matters enormously. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/c7b7a987f7d5b66e0832be42fbf52b7d_lec03_09112006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 3: balanced step-growth conversion]. This counts residues of both feed species; a <nylon 6,10> interior <constitutional repeating unit> contains two such <monomeric units>.

For an $AA+BB$ feed with $A_0\leq B_0$ initial reactive-group counts, put $r=A_0/B_0$ and now let $p$ denote the fraction of the \i[limiting $A$ groups] consumed. Under the same linear, no-ring assumptions, $N_0=(A_0+B_0)/2$ and $N=N_0-pA_0$, giving
$$
X_n=\frac{1+r}{1+r-2rp}.
$$
For $r=0.98$, even complete consumption of $A$ gives $X_n=(1.98)/(0.02)=99$. <Stoichiometry> controls chain length as well as yield. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/5474dc576b0669a8a09e9b70e39b1239_lec02_09082006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 2, pp. 2–3: stoichiometric imbalance]. The liquid-interface demonstration is not a homogeneous, balanced reactor, so its thread length cannot be inserted into this formula as a molecular chain length.

\Image[polymerization-monomers-conversion-models.png]
{id=polymerization-monomers-conversion-models}
{title=Two ideal <degree of polymerization> counts: late growth in <step-growth polymerization> and fixed-chain growth in ideal <living polymerization>}
{description=Toy analytic curves, not measured data. Left: $X_n=(1+r)/(1+r-2rp)$, with $p$ the limiting-group conversion and $r$ the initial reactive-group ratio. Right: $X_n=qR$, with $q$ the fraction of feed <monomer molecules> incorporated and $R$ their initial number per initiated chain. The two horizontal axes count different entities; the right plot represents a special ideal model, not all <chain-growth polymerization>.}

\b[A contrasting chain count.] In ideal <living polymerization>, assume no chain termination or irreversible chain transfer, all chains initiated before the counted growth, one chain per effective initiator, a fixed chain count $C$, and every consumed <monomer molecule> incorporated into those chains. If $M_0$ is the initial number of feed <monomer molecules> and $q$ their incorporated fraction, conservation gives $X_n=qM_0/C=qR$ in <monomeric units>, excluding initiator fragments. For $R=200$, incorporating half the feed gives $X_n=100$. If initiation consumes one <monomer molecule> per chain, the fixed-chain interval starts at $q=1/R$, rather than $q=0$. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/42d2e82b585136f5b6849919a0ee4449_lec21_11012006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 21, p. 1: ideal living-chain count]. The mean does not imply identical chain lengths. <Living polymerization> does not require reversible loss of <monomers>; the defining absence of chain termination and irreversible chain transfer is explained in https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/IUPAC_PolymEdu_EduWorkshop_PPT_Shipp2.pdf[Devon Shipp, IUPAC Polymer Education workshop, Radical Polymerizations II, slide 50]. Ordinary <chain-growth polymerization> with new chains, termination, or transfer need not follow the straight line.

The useful connection is between local chemistry and a macroscopic material: selecting reactive ends and their proportions controls connectivity, while the resulting <polymer>'s conformation is a separate problem described by models such as the <freely jointed chain> or <Gaussian chain>. Formation of <chemical bonds>, the count of <monomeric units>, and the count of statistical segments must be kept distinct.

= Step-growth polymerization
{parent=Polymerization}
{wiki}

In <step-growth polymerization>, suitable reactive groups on <molecules> of different chain lengths can join. The linear count and its assumptions are given in <polymerization>.

A one-unit <monomer>, a two-unit chain, and a twenty-unit chain can all be joining partners if their <functional groups> are compatible and accessible. Schematically, $P_j+P_k\longrightarrow P_{j+k}$: the subscripts count <monomeric units>, and end-group chemistry is suppressed. In <chain-growth propagation>, by contrast, $P_j^*+M\longrightarrow P_{j+1}^*$ requires a free <monomer> and a <polymerization-active site>. An arbitrary pair of existing chains is not a propagation pair in that mechanism. The practical consequence of <step-growth polymerization> is that most <monomers> can have joined something while the average chain is still short.

Whether a joining step releases a small <molecule> is a separate question. <Polycondensation> releases small products during the joining chemistry, as in the <nylon 6,10> demonstration in <polymerization>. <Polyaddition> joins suitable chains without that elimination: reacting a <diol> with a molecule bearing two <isocyanate> groups can build a <polyurethane>. Thus <step-growth polymerization> and <polycondensation> are not synonyms.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[polyaddition, P04720, definition and note 1; polycondensation, P04722, definition and growth-step equation; source recommendation p. 2307]{ref}

= Linear step-growth molecule count
{parent=Step-growth polymerization}
{title2=$N=N_0-J$}

Use an ideal feed in which every starting <molecule> has exactly two suitable <functional groups>: either one $A$ and one $B$, or two $A$ groups mixed with two $B$ groups. Only an $A$–$B$ pairing can form a joining <chemical bond>. Assume no loss of feed-derived material, no splitting of chains, no competing consumption of the counted <functional groups>, no branching, and no <cyclization in step-growth polymerization>. Linear here describes connectivity; a chain can bend in space. Small byproducts of <polycondensation> do not enter the chain-bearing <molecule> count.

Let $N_0$ count all initial feed <molecules>, $J$ the number of intermolecular joins, and $N$ all remaining chain-bearing <molecules>, including unreacted <monomers>. Each join turns two <molecules> into one, so $N=N_0-J$ exactly. If chain $i$ contains $j_i$ feed-derived <monomeric units>, conservation gives $\sum_{i=1}^{N}j_i=N_0$. Consequently the number-average <degree of polymerization> is
$$
X_n=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}j_i}{N}=\frac{N_0}{N_0-J}.
$$
This is a count of both feed species' <monomeric units>, rather than a count of interior <constitutional repeating units>. For $AA+BB$ chemistry an interior repeat often contains two feed-derived units, as explained in <monomer>. Equal <functional group> reactivities are not needed for this bookkeeping at a specified $J$; predicting a time course or a chain-length <probability distribution> requires additional assumptions.

\Image[step-growth-molecular-joining-count.png]
{id=image-step-growth-molecular-joining-count}
{title=Exact <step-growth polymerization> count: joining a two-unit chain to a three-unit chain consumes one pair of <functional groups>}
{description=Original stipulated eight-$AB$-molecule example. Circles are feed-derived <monomeric units>, lines joining <chemical bonds>, and letters the surviving <functional groups>. Internal $A$ and $B$ groups have already reacted and are not drawn. The depicted layouts are schematic connectivity, not molecular geometry or sampled experimental chains.}

For the figure's eight $AB$ feed <molecules>, three joins can give lengths $(2,3,1,1,1)$, so $N=5$ and $X_n=8/5$. Joining the first two chains gives $(5,1,1,1)$: the incorporated total stays eight, $N$ becomes four, and $X_n=2$. A <graph> makes the bookkeeping visible: each starting <monomer molecule> is a <vertex of a graph>, and each intermolecular joining <chemical bond> connects two previously distinct <connected components of a graph>. The connected pieces are the counted <molecules>.

= Functional-group conversion in step growth
{parent=Linear step-growth molecule count}
{title2=$p$}

In a balanced bifunctional feed there are initially $N_0$ $A$ groups and $N_0$ $B$ groups. Define the <functional-group conversion in step growth> by $p=J/N_0$: the reacted fraction of either group type. Each join consumes one of each. Therefore $N=N_0(1-p)$. In the eight-$AB$ example, three joins give $p=3/8$ and four give $p=1/2$. In the latter state only three of eight starting <monomer molecules> remain isolated: their disappearance fraction is $5/8$, different from $p=1/2$. Neither this group-based $p$ nor disappearance of isolated <monomers> is the incorporated-feed fraction in the ideal <living polymerization> count.

With unequal initial counts $A_0\leq B_0$, this article always uses $p=J/A_0$, the conversion of the limiting $A$ groups. The reacted fraction of $B$ groups is $J/B_0=rp$, where $r=A_0/B_0$. The fraction of all initial <functional groups> consumed is $2J/(A_0+B_0)=2rp/(1+r)$. At nonzero conversion these three fractions coincide only at $r=1$. Naming the measured group type prevents an apparently correct equation from using the wrong conversion.

= Carothers equation
{c}
{parent=Step-growth polymerization}
{title2=$X_n=1/(1-p)$}

The balanced <Carothers equation> follows immediately from <linear step-growth molecule count> and <functional-group conversion in step growth>:
$$
X_n=\frac{1}{1-p}.
$$
The same <Carothers equation> extends to unequal group counts once the limiting-group convention is specified.<Generalization of the Carothers equation for linear step growth polymers by Emilio Meaurio>[Introduction, equations (1)–(6): Carothers equation, end-group count and limiting-$A$ conversion]{ref}

For an original balanced feed of $2000$ <monomer molecules>, $1900$, $1960$, and $1990$ intermolecular joins give $p=0.95$, $0.98$, and $0.995$. The remaining counts are $100$, $40$, and $10$, hence number-average <degree of polymerization> values $20$, $50$, and $200$. The last thirty joins quadruple the average even though they consume only another $1.5\%$ of the initial groups of either type. These are exact synthetic counts, not experimental measurements.

The <derivative> $dX_n/dp=(1-p)^{-2}$ explains the sensitivity: near $p=0.99$, an absolute conversion uncertainty of $0.001$ changes the predicted <degree of polymerization> substantially. The exact balanced values at $p=0.989$ and $0.991$ are approximately $90.9$ and $111.1$, instead of $100$. An instrument reporting rounded “complete conversion” cannot distinguish those chain lengths. In a finite no-ring system, even one chain retains two ends; the maximum $p$ is $1-1/N_0$ and $X_n=N_0$. The formal divergence at $p=1$ is a large-system limit, not an infinite chain in a finite flask.

= Stoichiometric imbalance in step growth
{parent=Carothers equation}
{title2=$r=A_0/B_0$}

For a strictly bifunctional $AA+BB$ feed, label the limiting <functional groups> $A$, set $0<r=A_0/B_0\leq1$, and define $p=J/A_0$ as in <functional-group conversion in step growth>. The initial <molecule> count is $N_0=(A_0+B_0)/2$, and each intermolecular join consumes one $A$ and one $B$. Thus
$$
N=\frac{A_0+B_0}{2}-pA_0,\qquad
X_n=\frac{1+r}{1+r-2rp}.
$$
This version of the <Carothers equation> uses limiting-group conversion, not the fraction of all groups reacted.<Generalization of the Carothers equation for linear step growth polymers by Emilio Meaurio>[Introduction, equations (2)–(6), especially the paragraph before equation (3): A is the limiting reactant used to define p]{ref}

As an original finite example, mix $95$ $AA$ <monomer molecules> with $100$ $BB$ <monomer molecules>. Then $(N_0,A_0,B_0)=(195,190,200)$ and $r=0.95$. At $p=0.90$, $171$ joins leave $24$ <molecules>, giving $X_n=195/24=8.125$. At $p=1$, $190$ joins leave five chains and ten unreacted $B$ groups; every chain has two $B$ ends, and $X_n=39$. Continuing to wait cannot remove the missing-$A$ constraint within this model.

For $r<1$, complete limiting-group conversion gives the ceiling $X_{n,\max}=(1+r)/(1-r)$. Reaching a target $X_n=K>1$ therefore requires $r\geq(K-1)/(K+1)$, even before considering incomplete conversion. For $K=200$, $r\geq199/201\approx0.99005$. Equality requires $p=1$; any lower conversion demands closer balance. The imbalance $1-r$ is measured relative to the initial number of $B$ groups, not relative to the total number of groups. A monofunctional impurity changes the initial groups-per-<molecule> count, so it cannot be inserted into this strictly bifunctional formula by retaining an unexamined $r$.

\Image[step-growth-conversion-stoichiometry-sensitivity.png]
{id=image-step-growth-conversion-stoichiometry-sensitivity}
{title=<Carothers equation> sensitivity to limiting-group conversion and initial <stoichiometric imbalance in step growth>}
{description=Original analytic model curves with logarithmic vertical axes. Left: $X_n=(1+r)/(1+r-2rp)$ and the balanced example points. Right: $X_{n,\max}=(1+r)/(1-r)$ versus $100(1-r)$, the initial excess-$B$ fraction relative to $B_0$. These are ideal linear no-ring calculations, not fits to the <polyurethane> study.}

= Cyclization in step-growth polymerization
{parent=Step-growth polymerization}

In <cyclization in step-growth polymerization>, compatible <functional groups> on the same chain react and close it into a ring. They consume groups without merging two <molecules>. If $R$ ring-closing events and $J$ intermolecular joins occur in an otherwise balanced bifunctional feed, the group conversion is $p=(J+R)/N_0$, while $N=N_0-J=N_0(1-p)+R$. Thus
$$
X_n=\frac{N_0}{N_0(1-p)+R}.
$$
For example, an $AB$ dimer can close its remaining $A$ and $B$ ends: two group pairings have occurred among two starting <monomer molecules>, giving $p=1$, but the resulting single cyclic <molecule> has only two <monomeric units>. This counterexample shows exactly where the linear <Carothers equation> fails. Rings have no chain ends, so counting every chain-bearing <molecule> as two ends also fails.

= Polyaddition
{parent=Step-growth polymerization}

<Polyaddition> is <step-growth polymerization> by joining reactions without elimination of small <molecules>. Compatible <functional groups> on different chain lengths remain eligible to join; the word addition alone does not imply <chain-growth polymerization>.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[polyaddition, P04720, definition and notes 1–2]{ref}

= Residual-group monitoring of polyurethane formation
{parent=Polyaddition}

An experimental comparison joined a two-ended <diol> chain, HOPCLOH, with a bifunctional <isocyanate>, at reported <isocyanate>-to-<diol> ratio $1.14:1$. The researchers used oven-dried $25\,\mathrm{mL}$ round-bottom flasks and an IKA RET magnetic hotplate with a metal carrier. They varied <solvent>, <concentration>, reaction <temperature> ($50$ or $61\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$), and duration ($1$ or $8\,\mathrm h$).<Experimental Design (2⁴) to Improve the Reaction Conditions of Non-Segmented Poly(ester-urethanes) (PEUs) Derived from α,ω-Hydroxy Telechelic Poly(ε-caprolactone) (HOPCLOH) by Jaime Maldonado-Estudillo and others>[sections 2.1 and 2.3: flask, heating apparatus and varied conditions]{ref}

A Bruker $500\,\mathrm{MHz}$ <NMR spectroscopy> instrument measured residual <hydroxy group>-adjacent signal relative to an <ester> signal. Independently, <size-exclusion chromatography> used ResiPore columns at $70\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, a refractive-index detector, and polystyrene calibration. Figure 8 reports sharply increasing apparent number-average <molar mass> beyond the reported conversion $0.95$. A deliberately water-exposed <isocyanate> comparison reproduced a $1625\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ <infrared spectroscopy> band assigned to <urea>-containing products. The authors warn that tiny residual <NMR spectroscopy> signals reduce integration precision.<Experimental Design (2⁴) to Improve the Reaction Conditions of Non-Segmented Poly(ester-urethanes) (PEUs) Derived from α,ω-Hydroxy Telechelic Poly(ε-caprolactone) (HOPCLOH) by Jaime Maldonado-Estudillo and others>[sections 3.2–3.3; Figure 3 discussion of the water comparison; section 5.1 and Figure 8: independent conversion and GPC readouts and low-signal limitation]{ref}

The inference is increasing chain size alongside depletion of reactive groups, consistent with <step-growth polymerization>. It is not an exact measurement of $X_n$ in the toy model. The feed contains already extended <diol> chains: counting their constituent repeat units differs from counting each feed <molecule> as one unit. Their varying <molar masses>, calibration against a different <polymer>, residual starting material, and competing <chemical reactions> further obstruct a direct conversion-to-$X_n$ substitution. Report which <functional groups> the conversion readout measures before comparing it with <stoichiometric imbalance in step growth>.

Useful \i[proposed controls], distinct from the reported water comparison, are pre-reaction spectra and matched mixtures omitting each joining partner in turn. Keep <solvent>, heating, and signal normalization matched. Disappearance of an <isocyanate> signal in the omitted-<diol> control would reveal competing consumption rather than establish chain joining. Agreement between residual-group and independently calibrated chain-size readouts is stronger evidence than calculating both quantities from the same <Carothers equation>.

= Polycondensation
{parent=Step-growth polymerization}

<Polycondensation> is <step-growth polymerization> in which joining reactions eliminate small <molecules>. Its chain-bearing <molecule> count excludes those byproducts; otherwise two chains joining while releasing one small product would misleadingly appear to conserve the counted population.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[polycondensation, P04722, definition and growth-step equation]{ref}

= Functionality in polymerization
{parent=Polymerization}
{title2=$f$}

The <functionality in polymerization> is the number of reactive <functional groups> on a <molecule> for the specified joining chemistry. The <linear step-growth molecule count> assumes $f=2$ for every feed <molecule>. A <diol> supplies two <hydroxy groups> in the <polyurethane> example; an impurity supplying only one suitable group can cap a chain. A group count must reflect the selected <chemical reaction>, not simply every chemically recognizable group in the <molecule>.

= Gelation
{parent=Polymerization}

<Gelation> is formation of an extended connected network. In <step-growth polymerization>, feed <molecules> with <functionality in polymerization> above two can introduce branching and permit <gelation>. The <linear step-growth molecule count>'s two-ended-chain interpretation and bifunctional <Carothers equation> must not silently be applied to such networks. Each join between distinct connected pieces still reduces their count by one, but neither that identity nor average size alone specifies the onset of <gelation>.

= Chain-growth polymerization
{parent=Polymerization}
{wiki}

= Chain polymerization
{synonym}

In <chain-growth polymerization>, each growth step joins a <monomer> to a reactive site on a <polymer> chain and regenerates that reactive site. This is the precise mechanistic restriction in IUPAC's definition: growth occurs exclusively through these monomer–chain-site reactions. The word chain refers to the succession of site-regenerating <chemical reactions>. It does not merely mean that the product looks like a long chain. <Chain-growth initiation> starts this succession; <chain-growth propagation> continues it; <chain-growth termination> and <chain transfer in chain polymerization> can change its fate.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[chain polymerization, C00958, definition and notes 1–2; source recommendation p. 2306]{ref}

The contrast with <step-growth polymerization> concerns the permitted joining partners. There, suitable reactive groups can join two existing chains, for example a two-unit chain and a three-unit chain. In <chain-growth propagation>, a free <monomer> adds to a <polymerization-active site>. Release of a small byproduct is a separate classification question: IUPAC explicitly allows condensative <chain polymerization>.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[chain polymerization, C00958, notes 3–6]{ref} The useful consequence is that a few growing chains can incorporate many <monomer molecules> while much of the feed remains unreacted. Their <degree of polymerization> depends on how those units are shared among chains, as <finite chain-growth bookkeeping> makes explicit.

\Image[chain-growth-active-site-bookkeeping.png]
{id=image-chain-growth-active-site-bookkeeping}
{title=<Chain-growth initiation>, <chain-growth propagation>, and two possible <chain-growth termination> counts}
{description=Original schematic for linear chains with one <polymerization-active site> each. Blocks count <monomeric units>; stars identify reactivity, not <electric charge>. $I^*$ is a free initiating species, excluded from the count of polymer-bound sites. The two termination rows are alternative outcomes for two active chains, not consecutive reactions. Joining two chains preserves their incorporated-unit total while reducing the number of <polymer> <molecules>. Neither row specifies a universal <reaction mechanism> or rate.}

= Polymerization-active site
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}

A <polymerization-active site> is the reactive location that enables the next <chain-growth propagation> step. In the schematic
$$
P_j^*+M\longrightarrow P_{j+1}^*,
$$
$M$ is one free <monomer molecule>, $j$ is the chain's <degree of polymerization> in incorporated <monomeric units>, and the star marks the <polymerization-active site>. The site's reactive character survives the growth step, although the <chemical atoms> carrying it can change. The star is not an extra unit, a literal particle, or necessarily a positive or negative <electric charge>. Actual <reaction mechanisms> can involve an unpaired <electron>, a <cation>, an <anion>, or a metal-associated site; the abstract notation does not identify which. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/31.1 Chain-Growth Polymers>[John McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §31.1: successive reactive intermediates and radical, cationic and anionic initiation]{ref}

For bookkeeping below, each linear active chain has exactly one <polymerization-active site>. This is a stipulated model, not a property of every <chain polymerization>: a chain can have several reactive sites, and a reversibly inactive chain can remain capable of future growth. Count currently active sites separately from all chains capable of later reactivation.

= Chain-growth initiation
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}

<Chain-growth initiation> creates a chain capable of <chain-growth propagation>. Write
$$
I^*+M\longrightarrow P_1^*.
$$
This schematic includes the first incorporated <monomeric unit>; any preceding production of $I^*$ is a separate step. If $C$ counts <polymer> <molecules>, $A$ counts polymer-bound <polymerization-active sites>, and $S$ counts incorporated <monomeric units>, this event changes $(C,A,S)$ by $(1,1,1)$. The free $I^*$ was excluded from $A$, so this does not assert creation of reactivity from nothing. An initiator feed amount need not equal the number of successful chains: the efficiency of forming productive chains and the number of initiating species per initiator matter. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/31.1 Chain-Growth Polymers>[McMurry, §31.1, initiating species and the first reactive intermediate]{ref}

= Chain-growth propagation
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}

In the one-site linear model, <chain-growth propagation> changes $(C,A,S)$ by $(0,0,1)$: one <monomer molecule> is consumed, one <monomeric unit> is incorporated, and the <polymerization-active site> is regenerated. Thus longer chains do not imply more active sites. Repeated <chain-growth propagation> can increase $S$ substantially with $A$ unchanged. This is a material count; it supplies no universal time dependence or empirical <chemical kinetics> formula.

= Chain-growth termination
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}

<Chain-growth termination> removes the ability of a reactive chain carrier to continue the succession of growth steps.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[termination, T06274, definition; source recommendation p. 1173]{ref} Two illustrative outcomes have different molecule counts:
$$
\begin{aligned}
P_j^*+P_k^*&\longrightarrow P_{j+k}, &&\Delta(C,A,S)=(-1,-2,0),\\
P_j^*+P_k^*&\longrightarrow P_j+P_k, &&\Delta(C,A,S)=(0,-2,0).
\end{aligned}
$$
The first joins the two chains into one inactive <polymer> <molecule>. The second leaves two inactive <polymer> <molecules>, as can occur when one chain end transfers <hydrogen> to the other instead of their ends joining. End-group chemistry is suppressed in these unit-count symbols; they are not complete <molecular formulas>. Losing two <polymerization-active sites> does not mean losing two chains or any incorporated <monomeric units>. A temporarily inactive, reactivatable site must not be counted as irreversible <chain-growth termination>.

= Chain transfer in chain polymerization
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}

In <chain transfer in chain polymerization>, a growing chain can lose its <polymerization-active site> while another <molecule> acquires reactivity. In a common atom-transfer picture, the old chain becomes inactive and the new carrier can initiate further <chain-growth propagation>.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[chain transfer, C00963, definition and subsequent-polymerization example; source recommendation p. 1094]{ref} The number of reactive carriers can survive even though the identity of the growing chain changes. If the new carrier successfully starts a new chain, $C$ increases; a fixed active-carrier count therefore does not imply a fixed <polymer> <molecule> count. Whether transfer is irreversible, reversible, or involves an already existing chain matters for the final bookkeeping.

= Finite chain-growth bookkeeping
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}
{title2=$X_n=S/C$}

Stipulate a feed of exactly $M_0=40$ <monomer molecules>, four successfully initiated linear chains, one <polymerization-active site> per chain, no <chain transfer in chain polymerization>, no loss of incorporated units, and no further <chain-growth initiation>. Count only initiated chains in $C$; exclude unreacted <monomers>. Assume each consumed <monomer molecule> contributes exactly one <monomeric unit>. With incorporated feed fraction $q=S/M_0$, the number-average <degree of polymerization> is the counting identity
$$
X_n=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{C}j_i}{C}=\frac{S}{C}=\frac{qM_0}{C}.
$$
This is conditional conservation bookkeeping, not a universal empirical law. Immediately after <chain-growth initiation>, the four lengths are $(1,1,1,1)$, so $(C,A,S)=(4,4,4)$ and $q=0.1$. Stipulate twelve subsequent <chain-growth propagation> events, three on each chain: the lengths become $(4,4,4,4)$, giving $(C,A,S)=(4,4,16)$, $q=0.4$ and $X_n=4$. Sixteen units have been incorporated, while twenty-four free <monomer molecules> remain. The chain-length assignment is deliberately chosen, not a prediction of equal growth in an experiment.

= Living versus terminating chain populations
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}

The defining idealization of <living polymerization> excludes <chain-growth termination> and irreversible <chain transfer in chain polymerization>. Fast, effective <chain-growth initiation> is an additional condition behind a nearly fixed growing-chain population, not a guarantee contained in the name alone.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[living polymerization, L03597, definition and initiation/propagation comparison; living polymer, LT07156, definition specifies irreversible transfer]{ref} If all four chains in <finite chain-growth bookkeeping> remain active, completing the feed keeps $C=A=4$ and gives $X_n=10$. Stipulate six further additions to each chain, obtaining $(10,10,10,10)$. Other allocations with the same total also satisfy $X_n=10$: living does not mean identical lengths.

Alternatively, stipulate that the first two length-four chains undergo <chain-growth termination> without joining, while the other two each receive twelve more <monomer molecules>. The final lengths are $(4,4,16,16)$, with $(C,A,S)=(4,2,40)$. The mean <degree of polymerization> is still $10$, but two chains have stopped and two carry the remaining growth. The population <variance> of lengths is $36$, compared with zero in the deliberately equal living allocation. These are synthetic populations, not measured molecular distributions and not a claim that all terminating processes are broader than all living processes. Indeed, assigning all twenty-four remaining additions to one still-active chain gives the living allocation $(28,4,4,4)$, with the same mean but <variance> $108$. Comparable start times and opportunities for <chain-growth propagation> are extra assumptions behind an expectation of more uniform lengths.

\Image[chain-growth-synthetic-chain-populations.png]
{id=image-chain-growth-synthetic-chain-populations}
{title=Same incorporated total and mean <degree of polymerization>, different active-chain populations}
{description=Two explicitly stipulated continuations from four length-four chains and twenty-four remaining <monomer molecules>. Left: all four chains stay active and receive six additions each. Right: two become inactive without joining, and the two survivors receive twelve additions each. Blue identifies active chains and grey inactive chains. Bars are exact chosen integer counts, not sampled data or fits to a molecular distribution. Both final populations contain forty incorporated <monomeric units> in four chains.}

If the two terminated length-four chains instead join, their inactive product has length eight; with the same remaining additions the final lengths are $(8,16,16)$. Now $C=3$, $A=2$, $S=40$ and $X_n=40/3$. Thus <chain-growth termination> can change both which chains grow and which chains enter the denominator. Continuing <chain-growth initiation>, inefficient initiation, <chain transfer in chain polymerization>, and reactivation require their own counts. Predicting an entire length distribution additionally needs a specified <reaction mechanism> and growth statistics. A measured mean alone cannot establish <living polymerization>.

= NMR monitoring of chain polymerization
{c}
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}

<NMR monitoring of chain polymerization> uses nuclear magnetic resonance signals to follow <monomer> and other distinguishable <chemical species>. In a proton experiment, suitable signal <integrals> can track changes in their relative amounts. Disappearance of a <monomer> signal is a consumption readout; it is not a direct count of <polymerization-active sites>. Overlapping signals, baseline errors and competing <chemical reactions> can obstruct the inference. A pre-reaction reference and independent product characterization make the consumption interpretation stronger.

= Size-exclusion characterization of chain polymers
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}

<Size-exclusion characterization of chain polymers> separates dissolved chains using porous columns and records an elution signal. Calibrating that separation against appropriate standards supplies a size-related estimate of molecular masses. Detector response and calibration must accompany any claimed molecular distribution: an elution trace is not automatically a histogram of molecule counts or <degree of polymerization>. Similar signals can conceal chemically different chain populations, while a shift toward larger apparent size alone does not show that every chain retained a <polymerization-active site>.

= Monitoring chain polymerization in a sealed tube
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}

https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlepdf/2015/py/c5py00278h[A simple route to deuterated polystyrene block copolymers by reverse iodine transfer polymerisation by Trevor Gavin Wright and Harald Pasch] (2015, Experimental section, pp. 3237–3238) provides a concrete study of consumption and chain products. The authors used a valve-sealed J Young tube, freeze–pump–thaw preparation and <argon>. <NMR monitoring of chain polymerization> compared a $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ reference with spectra acquired at $70\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ every fifteen minutes for twenty-four hours. Their formulation contained an azo initiating reagent, molecular iodine and a vinyl <monomer>. Companion flask syntheses used stirring and a heated silicone-oil bath.

The authors followed initiating and transfer-agent signals; Figure 3 reports a transfer-agent signal rising during induction and then falling as <polymerization> proceeds. Their <size-exclusion characterization of chain polymers> used porous columns, refractive-index detection and polystyrene calibration. These distinct consumption and size readouts concern transfer-mediated chemistry; they do not establish strict <living polymerization>. <A simple route to deuterated polystyrene block copolymers by reverse iodine transfer polymerisation by Trevor Gavin Wright and others>[Wright and Pasch, pp. 3237–3239: Experimental section, Analyses and Figure 3]{ref}

Useful \i[proposed controls], rather than reported measurements, are a matched mixture omitting the initiating reagent, an unheated mixture, and a repeat of the complete formulation. Keep <monomer> amount, <solvent>, tube preparation and acquisition conditions matched where possible. The unheated comparison needs a readout that does not itself initiate <polymerization>; placing it in the hot probe would defeat that control. A mixture omitting iodine asks a different question: it changes <chain transfer in chain polymerization> and induction chemistry, so it is not necessarily a no-<polymerization> control. Record the pre-reaction baseline and compare crude material with isolated product; isolation can selectively remove shorter chains. These comparisons help separate initiation-dependent <monomer> consumption from signal drift and unrelated <chemical reactions>.

The mechanistic inference is deliberately limited. Consumption plus independently characterized larger chain products supports <polymerization>; evidence of an induction stage helps test a proposed <reaction mechanism>. Neither readout directly counts stars in $P_j^*$, proves the absence of <chain-growth termination>, nor recovers every chain length. A chain-extension test would probe retained growth capability, but even a successful extension would not by itself establish that \i[all] original chains extended. No measured molecular distribution is reconstructed here. IUPAC cautions that reversible deactivation can produce living-like behaviour even when some termination occurs.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[reversible-deactivation radical polymerization, 08916, notes 2 and 4: living terminology and remaining active/dormant population]{ref}

= Living polymerization
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}
{wiki}

<Living polymerization> is <chain-growth polymerization> without chain termination or irreversible chain transfer. A linear mean <degree of polymerization> versus feed conversion additionally needs the fixed-chain assumptions in <polymerization>; reversibility of <monomer> addition is not required. See https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/IUPAC_PolymEdu_EduWorkshop_PPT_Shipp2.pdf[Shipp, IUPAC workshop, slide 50].

= Polyurethane
{parent=Polymer}

A <polyurethane> contains repeated connections of the form $\mathrm{{-}O{-}C(=O){-}NH{-}}$. Suitable <hydroxy groups> and <isocyanate> groups can produce those connections by <polyaddition>; exactly two suitable groups per feed <molecule> give the bifunctional linear model only when competing reactions and ring closure are excluded.

= Degree of polymerization
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}
{title2=$X,\ X_n$}

The <degree of polymerization> counts <monomeric units> in a chain. $X_n$ is its number average over a specified collection of chains. State whether unreacted <monomers> are included and distinguish this count from <constitutional repeating units> or statistical segments. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0304[IUPAC: degree of polymerization].

= Repeat unit
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}

= Constitutional repeating unit
{synonym}

A <constitutional repeating unit> is the smallest chemical unit whose repetition describes a regular chain. Its boundaries mark repetition, not necessarily the origin of the <chemical atoms> from a single <monomer>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01286/plain[IUPAC: constitutional repeating unit].

= Nylon 6,10
{c}
{parent=Polymer}

<Nylon 6,10> has an <amide>-linked chain whose <constitutional repeating unit> contains a six-<carbon> diamine contribution and a ten-<carbon> diacid-derived contribution. See <monomer> for its <stoichiometry> and <polymerization> for the authored demonstration.

= Polyethylene
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}

In ideal linear <polyethylene>, incorporated <ethene> contributes $\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}CH_2{-}}$ <monomeric units>; the smallest <constitutional repeating unit> is $\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}}$. See <monomer> for the counting distinction.

= Monomer
{parent=Polymer}

A <monomer> is a <chemical substance> whose <molecules> can undergo <polymerization> and contribute units to the essential structure of a <polymer>. A <monomer molecule> is the individual reactant; its incorporated <monomeric unit> is the portion identifiable as derived from that reactant. Incorporation can change <chemical bonds> and release small byproducts, so the unit in the chain need not have the complete <molecular formula> of the feed <molecule>. See https://old.goldbook.iupac.org/html/M/M04019.html[IUPAC: monomer molecule] and https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0304[IUPAC, A brief guide to polymer terminology: monomeric units].

Three different counts answer three different questions. <Monomer molecules> describe the feed; <monomeric units> trace that feed's contributions to the product; a <constitutional repeating unit> describes the smallest chemical pattern repeating along a regular chain. In <nylon 6,10>, two different <monomers> supply one interior <constitutional repeating unit>,
$$
\mathrm{{-}NH{-}(CH_2)_6{-}NH{-}C(=O){-}(CH_2)_8{-}C(=O){-}}.
$$
Its <molecular formula> is $\mathrm{C_{16}H_{30}N_2O_2}$. The diamine feed is $\mathrm{C_6H_{16}N_2}$, and the diacid-chloride feed is $\mathrm{C_{10}H_{16}Cl_2O_2}$; subtracting two $\mathrm{HCl}$ gives the interior-repeat budget. For a finite linear chain made from $n$ of each reactant, there are $2n-1$ joining connections, hence $2n-1$ formal $\mathrm{HCl}$ eliminations, rather than $2n$. One <amine> end and one acid-chloride end remain in that ideal unmodified chain. This is a <stoichiometry> check on the depicted reaction, not a claim that the washed experimental thread retains those ends. The reacting end-group chemistry is documented in https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/d9f9ac7021b0d0f418d3d3c90dbfb94b_lec05_09152006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 5, p. 4]; the specified <nylon 6,10> reactant pair is reported in https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jchemed.6b00767[Polymer Day, nylon demonstration].

<Ethene> shows the reverse counting mismatch. One $\mathrm{CH_2{=}CH_2}$ <monomer molecule> contributes a two-<carbon> $\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}CH_2{-}}$ <monomeric unit> to ideal linear <polyethylene>, without a small byproduct in that growth step. But the smallest <constitutional repeating unit> of its uniform backbone is just $\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}}$: a chain containing $m$ incorporated <ethene> <monomeric units> contains $2m$ such interior one-<carbon> repeats. End groups are additional bookkeeping. The frequently drawn $[\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}CH_2{-}}]_m$ is useful for tracing the feed, although it is not the smallest constitutional repeat. The minimum-pattern definition is given in https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01286/plain[IUPAC: constitutional repeating unit]; the <ethene> growth chemistry is described by <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/31.1 Chain-Growth Polymers>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 31.1]{ref}.

A segment of a <freely jointed chain> or <Gaussian chain> instead belongs to a statistical model. It can represent many <chemical bonds> and several <constitutional repeating units>; its length and the segment count are chosen to describe chain conformation at the intended scale. The three-repeats-per-segment drawing in <polymerization-monomers-interface-units> is a deliberately arbitrary coarse-graining example, not a measured <Kuhn length>. Always state the counting convention before substituting a segment count for a <degree of polymerization>. This separation lets chemistry track which <chemical atoms> entered the material while statistical models track how the material bends and extends.

= Monomeric unit
{parent=Monomer}

An incorporated <monomeric unit> is the largest constitutional unit identifiable as derived from one <monomer molecule>. It need not be a whole unreacted <molecule> or the smallest <constitutional repeating unit>. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0304[IUPAC: monomeric unit].

= Monomer molecule
{parent=Monomer}

An individual <molecule> capable of contributing structural units through <polymerization>. See https://old.goldbook.iupac.org/html/M/M04019.html[IUPAC: monomer molecule].

= Ethylene
{parent=Monomer}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_2{=}CH_2}$}

= Ethene
{synonym}

<Ethene> is the two-<carbon> <monomer> in the <polyethylene> example in <monomer>.

= Nucleic acid
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}

A <nucleic acid> is a <polymer> built from linked <nucleotides>. The two principal kinds are <DNA> and <RNA>. Each <nucleotide> contributes a <sugar>, a <nucleobase>, and <phosphate> to the chain; the <nucleobase> is only the letter-bearing component, not the entire <nucleotide>. <DNA> uses <deoxyribose>, whereas <RNA> uses <ribose>. A chain of six <nucleotides> is therefore not six free <nucleobases> placed beside one another. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nucleic-Acids[NHGRI: nucleic acids].

The chemical scaffold and the variable lettering do different jobs. <Covalent bonds> maintain the <sugar>–<phosphate> backbone, while the order of attached <nucleobases> specifies the <nucleic acid sequence>. A <phosphodiester bond> links the $3'$ position of one <sugar> to the $5'$ position of the next through <phosphate>. These position numbers refer to <carbon> <chemical atoms> in the <sugar>; they define a strand's direction even when it bends or forms a circle. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21134/[Brown, Genomes, 2nd edition: the human genome].

This division is useful: the backbone supplies a connected physical record, and <base pairing> allows its letters to recognize partners. <DNA replication> copies that record, and <transcription> uses a <DNA> template to make <RNA>. <RNA> can also fold into functional shapes through <base pairing> within one chain; a <nucleic acid sequence> specifies chemical order, not a requirement for a straight shape. The beauty is that the same kind of <polymer> can carry a message and form a structure that acts on messages.

= Nucleic acid sequence
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}

= Nucleotide sequence
{synonym}

A <nucleic acid sequence> records the ordered <nucleobases> of a <nucleic acid> strand, conventionally read from $5'$ to $3'$. The standard <DNA> alphabet is <adenine> A, <cytosine> C, <guanine> G, and <thymine> T; the standard <RNA> alphabet uses <uracil> U in place of <thymine> T. Each letter denotes the <nucleobase> attached to one <nucleotide>, so the six-letter <DNA> <nucleic acid sequence> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGTAC}\text{-}3'$ describes six linked <nucleotides>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Base-Pair[NHGRI: representing DNA by a sequence of letters].

Order carries information that composition loses. Both ACGTAC and AACCGT contain two <adenine>, two <cytosine>, one <guanine>, and one <thymine> <nucleobase>, but their <nucleic acid sequences> differ at positions 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. At position 2, for example, the first has <cytosine> and the second <adenine>. Reporting only the four counts cannot reconstruct either ordered record.

To obtain the partner of $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGTAC}\text{-}3'$, apply the <DNA> <base pairing> rules position by position: A selects T, C selects G, G selects C, T selects A, A selects T, and C selects G. Alignment gives $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TGCATG}\text{-}5'$. Its opposite end labels matter because the two are <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>. Reading the partner from its own $5'$ end reverses the aligned lettering and yields the <reverse complement>, $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GTACGT}\text{-}3'$.

\Image[dna-reverse-complement.png]
{title=Complement, then reverse: <DNA> ACGTAC has aligned partner TGCATG and <reverse complement> GTACGT}

Reversal alone gives CATGCA; complementation alone gives aligned TGCATG. Neither operation alone gives the partner written in the usual $5'$-to-$3'$ convention. Applying the <reverse complement> operation twice returns ACGTAC: GTACGT complements to CATGCA, which reverses to ACGTAC. This convention makes a single reported strand sufficient to specify an ideally complementary <DNA> duplex, provided its direction is known.

= Reverse complement
{parent=Nucleic acid sequence}

A <nucleotide sequence> obtained by reversing the order and complementing each <nucleobase> according to the appropriate <base pairing> rules.

= Base pair
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=bp}

A <base pair> is an association of two <nucleobases> through <hydrogen bonds> in a compatible arrangement. It commonly joins opposite strands of a <nucleic acid double helix>, but can also join two regions of one folded <RNA> strand. Canonical Watson–Crick <DNA> <base pairs> match <adenine> with <thymine> and <guanine> with <cytosine>; canonical <RNA> <base pairs> use <adenine> with <uracil> instead. The <nucleobases> belong to complete <nucleotides>, including their attached <sugars> and backbone <phosphate>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Base-Pair[NHGRI: base pair].

The abbreviation bp also measures the length of a paired region. Our ACGTAC/TGCATG duplex has six <base pairs>, containing twelve <nucleotides> altogether. Its upper strand contains two <adenine>, two <cytosine>, one <guanine>, and one <thymine> <nucleobase>; the lower contains one, one, two, and two respectively. Combined, the counts are three of each <nucleobase>. Equality of total <adenine> and <thymine>, or of total <guanine> and <cytosine>, need not hold within either individual strand.

= Base pairing
{parent=Base pair}

<Base pairing> is the recognition and association of compatible <nucleobases>. In canonical Watson–Crick geometry, an <adenine>–<thymine> <base pair> has two <hydrogen bonds>, while a <guanine>–<cytosine> <base pair> has three. These counts refer to those specific geometries and usual chemical forms; they are not a definition of every possible <base pair>. <RNA> can also contain other pairings, and canonical <adenine>–<uracil> pairing replaces <adenine>–<thymine> pairing. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-2-dna-structure-and-sequencing[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: DNA structure].

\Image[dna-antiparallel-base-pairs.png]
{title=Six canonical Watson–Crick <DNA> <base pairs>: opposite backbone directions and two or three <hydrogen bonds> per pair}

For this illustrative duplex, the six <base pairs> are A–T, C–G, G–C, T–A, A–T, and C–G. Thus three contribute two <hydrogen bonds> each and three contribute three each:
$$
3\times2+3\times3=15.
$$
Each dotted connector in the diagram counts one <hydrogen bond>; coloured boxes denote whole <nucleobases>, not individual <chemical atoms>. The solid rails denote the <covalent bond> scaffold. Separating the two strands disrupts <base pairing> and <base stacking> without requiring the backbone to be cut.

The count of fifteen <hydrogen bonds> is not fifteen identical contributions to a duplex's <Gibbs free energy>. <Base stacking>, interactions with <water> and <ions>, <temperature>, and the order of neighbouring <base pairs> also matter. Knowing the fraction of <guanine>–<cytosine> <base pairs> alone does not fix a <nucleic acid double helix>'s shape or stability. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21134/[Brown, Genomes: pairing and stacking interactions]. What makes <base pairing> especially useful is its selectivity: an exposed <DNA> strand constrains the partner's <nucleic acid sequence>, giving <DNA replication> a chemical recognition rule.

= Nucleic acid double helix
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}

A <nucleic acid double helix> consists of two <nucleic acid> strands winding around a common axis. In the familiar <DNA> arrangement, <sugar>–<phosphate> backbones lie outside and paired <nucleobases> lie inside, stacked along the axis. The strands are <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>. The flattened <base pairing> ladder above displays partners and direction; winding that ladder into a <helix> adds spatial organization. <RNA> can also form double-helical paired regions, so the geometry is not exclusive to <DNA>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26821/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: DNA structure].

The useful surprise is that a structure explains a copying principle. Complementary <base pairs> allow each <DNA> strand to specify the other's <nucleic acid sequence>; separate strands can each act as templates in <DNA replication>. This is a structural constraint, while the <enzymes> and stages of <DNA replication> supply the actual process. A <nucleic acid sequence> specifies letter order without, by itself, giving every detail of the three-dimensional <helix>.

On 25 April 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick published a model with two oppositely directed chains, outside <phosphate> groups, and specific <base pairs>. Their short paper explicitly connected this pairing to a possible copying mechanism, while describing the model as requiring further experimental tests. See the original https://doi.org/10.1038/171737a0[Molecular Structure of Nucleic Acids: A Structure for Deoxyribose Nucleic Acid, Nature 171, 737–738]{ref}, with an accessible https://inters.org/files/watsoncrick1953.pdf[reprint of the original paper]{ref}.

Rosalind Franklin and Raymond Gosling's paper in the same issue had a different role: it presented <X-ray> <diffraction> evidence from <DNA> fibres and its structural interpretation, including the helical character of the wetter B form and evidence for <phosphate> groups on the outside. They distinguished forms produced at different water contents and assessed compatibility with the Watson–Crick model. Read their original https://doi.org/10.1038/171740a0[Molecular Configuration in Sodium Thymonucleate, Nature 171, 740–741 (1953)]{ref}. The model and the experimental analysis are distinct contributions; a <diffraction> pattern is evidence constraining a <molecular geometry>, rather than a direct picture of letter-by-letter <base pairing>.

= Antiparallel nucleic acid strands
{parent=Nucleic acid double helix}

Two <nucleic acid> strands with opposite directional alignment: a $5'$-to-$3'$ direction on one is aligned with the $3'$-to-$5'$ direction on the other.

= Base stacking
{parent=Nucleic acid double helix}

<Base stacking> is the close arrangement and interaction of neighbouring <nucleobases> with their approximately planar faces above one another. Along a <nucleic acid double helix>, neighbouring <base pairs> stack successively; <base pairing> instead associates partners across the two strands. Both affect duplex stability, and the interactions depend on the identities and arrangement of neighbouring <nucleobases>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21134/[Brown, Genomes: base pairing and base stacking].

= Stem-loop
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}

= Deoxyribonucleic acid
{parent=Nucleic acid}

= DNA
{c}
{synonym}

<Deoxyribonucleic acid>, or <DNA>, is a <nucleotide> <polymer> that stores biological information in its <nucleotide sequence>. The crucial variable is order: two <DNA> <molecules> can contain the same numbers of each <nucleobase> yet carry different <nucleotide sequences>. This makes a physical <molecule> an inheritable record, rather than merely a mixture of ingredients. <Genes> are functional regions of that record; the whole <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> is not one long <gene>. See https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Deoxyribonucleic-Acid-Fact-Sheet[NHGRI: DNA fact sheet].

Each <DNA> strand has a backbone of alternating <deoxyribose> <sugars> and <phosphate> groups joined through <covalent bonds>. Attached <nucleobases> supply the familiar four-letter alphabet: <adenine> A, <thymine> T, <guanine> G, and <cytosine> C. In ordinary double-stranded <DNA>, <base pairing> matches A with T and G with C. The backbone connects successive <nucleotides> along a strand; <base pairing> connects partners across strands. Keeping these two connections distinct explains how strands can separate while each retains its own <nucleotide sequence>.

The two backbones have directions, conventionally labelled $5'$ and $3'$ after numbered <carbon> <chemical atoms> in <deoxyribose>. They form <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>: following one from $5'$ to $3'$ takes you opposite to the other's $5'$-to-$3'$ direction. For the chosen <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGCC}\text{-}3'$, write its partner directly underneath as $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACGG}\text{-}5'$. Now read that partner from its own $5'$ end: its <reverse complement> is $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GGCAT}\text{-}3'$. Complementing letters and reversing their order are separate operations. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26821/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the structure and function of DNA].

\Image[dna-antiparallel-complement.png]
{title=<DNA> <base pairing> and <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>: an unrolled ladder for ATGCC and its <reverse complement>}

The diagram's rails represent <sugar>–<phosphate> backbones and its coloured blocks represent <nucleobases>, not individual <chemical atoms>. Reading left to right gives five matched positions: A–T, T–A, G–C, C–G, C–G. There are two A–T-type <base pairs> and three G–C-type <base pairs>; the complete two-strand example contains ten <nucleotides>. Reversing the bottom row changes the reading convention, not the physical partners. Real double-stranded <DNA> winds into a <nucleic acid double helix>; the flattened ladder emphasizes <base pairing> and direction without pretending to show its three-dimensional geometry.

The motivating consequence is copying by a template. After strands separate, each existing <DNA> strand specifies which incoming <nucleotides> should accompany it through <base pairing>. <DNA replication> uses <enzymes> to build complementary strands, allowing the record to persist through successive <biological cells>. <Base pairing> supplies a selection rule, while <enzymes> perform the chemistry: a matching diagram alone is not a complete copying mechanism. The connection between structure and copying was already central to the model published by Watson and Crick on 25 April 1953. See https://www.nature.com/articles/171737a0[Watson and Crick, Molecular Structure of Nucleic Acids, Nature 171, 737–738 (1953)].

Stored information needs interpretation. Some <DNA> regions specify <proteins> through <RNA>; others contribute to functional <RNA> or regulate <gene expression>, and some have no known function. <Non-coding DNA> means sequence that does not specify a <protein>'s sequence of <amino acids>, not automatically useless sequence. Nor does a <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> alone determine an <organism>'s fate: <phenotypes> also depend on biological context and environment. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Non-Coding-DNA[NHGRI: non-coding DNA]. Its double-stranded form is often modeled mechanically as a <worm-like chain>, with <elasticity> and effective <excluded volume> depending on solvent conditions.

= Ribonucleic acid
{parent=Nucleic acid}

= RNA
{c}
{synonym}

<Ribonucleic acid>, or <RNA>, is a <nucleotide> <polymer> whose <nucleotide sequence> can both carry information and organize a working molecular shape. Its backbone contains alternating <ribose> <sugars> and <phosphate> groups connected through <covalent bonds>. Its usual <nucleobases> are <adenine> A, <uracil> U, <guanine> G, and <cytosine> C. Thus <RNA> uses U where <DNA> uses T; its <ribose> also differs chemically from <DNA>'s <deoxyribose>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Ribonucleic-Acid-RNA[NHGRI: RNA].

In <transcription (biology)>, an <enzyme> uses one <DNA> strand as a template for <RNA>. For example, the <DNA> template $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACGG}\text{-}5'$ specifies $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUGCC}\text{-}3'$ in <RNA>. The <base pairing> rules now include A–U rather than A–T, while G–C remains. This <RNA> matches the other <DNA> strand's $5'$-to-$3'$ lettering after T is replaced by U. Identifying the template strand matters: replacing T by U in an arbitrarily chosen <DNA> strand is not a general method for deriving the <RNA> product. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: from DNA to RNA].

<RNA> is often a single strand, but single-stranded does not mean straight or entirely unpaired. Complementary regions within one <RNA> <molecule> can undergo <base pairing>, bringing distant positions together. Consider the illustrative twelve-<nucleotide> <nucleotide sequence> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GCAUUUUUAUGC}\text{-}3'$. Pair positions 1 with 12, 2 with 11, 3 with 10, and 4 with 9. The resulting <stem-loop> has four <base pairs>: G–C, C–G, A–U, U–A. Positions 5–8 remain unpaired in the loop. Both arms belong to the same continuous backbone, and the paired arms have the directions of <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>.

\Image[rna-sequence-and-folding.png]
{title=One <RNA> <nucleotide sequence>, two drawings: numbered letters and an illustrative <stem-loop> with four <base pairs>}

The diagram proposes a possible pairing pattern, not a prediction that this short <RNA> must adopt that shape under every condition. Actual <RNA> folding depends on additional interactions, <temperature>, <ions>, and associated <proteins>; the full three-dimensional arrangement contains more information than the drawn <base pairs>. The example's purpose is to show how a linear <nucleotide sequence> can specify contacts within itself.

Different <RNA> roles make this flexibility useful. <Messenger RNA> carries a message that a <ribosome> reads during <translation (biology)> to build a <protein>. <Transfer RNA> helps match that message to <amino acid> building blocks; see https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Transfer-RNA-tRNA[NHGRI: transfer RNA]. <Ribosomal RNA> forms structural and catalytic parts of the <ribosome>. Other <RNA> participates in <gene regulatory networks>, changing how strongly <genes> are expressed. Many <RNA> <molecules> therefore function without being translated into <proteins>; for some <genes>, functional <RNA> is the product itself.

Most strikingly, some folded <RNA> acts as an <enzyme>: these catalytic <RNA> <molecules> are <ribozymes>. The <ribosome>'s bond-forming <active site> is built from <RNA>, although the complete <ribosome> also contains <proteins>. Structural evidence appears in https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10937990/[Nissen et al., The structural basis of ribosome activity in peptide bond synthesis, Science 289, 920–930 (2000)]. <RNA> therefore connects a readable record with physical recognition, regulation, and catalysis: the same kind of <polymer> can be a message, an adaptor, or part of molecular machinery.

= Messenger RNA
{parent=Ribonucleic acid}
{wiki}

= mRNA
{c}
{synonym}

= Transfer RNA
{parent=Ribonucleic acid}
{wiki}

= tRNA
{c}
{synonym}

= Ribosomal RNA
{parent=Ribonucleic acid}
{wiki}

= rRNA
{c}
{synonym}

= Radius of gyration
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}
{title2=$R_g^2=\frac1{2N^2}\sum_{m,n}\langle|\mathbf R_m-\mathbf R_n|^2\rangle$}

For $N$ equally weighted positions, the squared <radius of gyration> is the average squared distance from the <center of mass>: $R_g^2=N^{-1}\sum_n\langle|\mathbf R_n-\mathbf R_{\rm cm}|^2\rangle$, where $\mathbf R_{\rm cm}=N^{-1}\sum_n\mathbf R_n$. Expanding pair differences gives $\sum_{m,n}|\mathbf R_m-\mathbf R_n|^2=2N\sum_n|\mathbf R_n-\mathbf R_{\rm cm}|^2$, and therefore
$$
R_g^2=\frac1{2N^2}\sum_{m,n}\langle|\mathbf R_m-\mathbf R_n|^2\rangle.
$$
For a <Gaussian chain> or <freely jointed chain> with these $N$ positions and mean squared link length $b^2$, each pair <variance> is $b^2|m-n|$. Summing separations yields $R_g^2=b^2(N^2-1)/(6N)\sim Nb^2/6$. Counting all endpoints of $N$ links instead gives $N+1$ positions and the corresponding finite-size formula.

= Ion
{parent=Chemistry}
{title2=$q=ze$}

An <ion> is a <chemical atom> or a group of <chemical atoms> with a nonzero net <electric charge>. A positive <ion> is a <cation>; a negative <ion> is an <anion>. The whole entity carries the <electric charge>: a <hydronium> <ion> is a group of four <chemical atoms> with net charge $+e$. This definition agrees with https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/I03158[IUPAC Gold Book: ion]{ref}.

For an ordinary <ion> containing only <atomic nuclei> and <electrons>, let $N_p$ count all its <protons> and $N_e$ all its <electrons>. Each <proton> contributes the <elementary charge> $+e$, each <electron> contributes $-e$, and <neutrons> contribute zero. Therefore
$$
q=(N_p-N_e)e=ze,\qquad z=N_p-N_e.
$$
The signed integer $z$ is the net <electric charge> number. Removing an <electron> increases $z$ by one; adding an <electron> decreases $z$ by one. Changing the <electron> count leaves the <atomic number>, and therefore the identity of the <chemical element>, unchanged.

For example, a neutral <sodium> <chemical atom> has $11$ <protons> and $11$ <electrons>. Its <cation> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ still has $11$ <protons>, but only $10$ <electrons>, so $q=(11-10)e=+e$. A neutral <chlorine> <chemical atom> has $17$ <protons> and $17$ <electrons>; its <anion> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ has $17$ <protons> and $18$ <electrons>, so $q=(17-18)e=-e$. These are particle inventories, not pictures of <atomic orbitals>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/2.6 Ionic and Molecular Compounds> (§2.6: ions and ionic compounds).

\Image[ion-charge-bookkeeping.png]
{title=<Ion> <electric charge> bookkeeping: neutral <sodium> and <chlorine> <chemical atoms> compared with their <cation> and <anion>}
{description=Every colored dot counts one <proton> or one <electron>. Rows are counting arrays, not spatial models of <atomic nuclei> or <electron shells>. The net <electric charges> are $0,+e,0,-e$.}

One <sodium chloride> <formula unit> accounts for one $\mathrm{Na}^+$ <cation> and one $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ <anion>, whose net <electric charges> sum to zero. Ordinary solid <sodium chloride> is an extended arrangement of <ions>, rather than discrete $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <molecules>. This is a useful distinction: a neutral material can contain charged constituents, and its <chemical formula> tells us their ratio without claiming that each pair is a separate <molecule>.

<Ion> bookkeeping connects <chemistry> to <electric current>: moving <ions> can transport <electric charge>, while stationary <ions> cannot supply a transport <current density>. In <electrochemistry>, both signs of <ions> can contribute to the same <conventional current>. An <ion> opposing a fixed <electric charge> is called a <counterion>.

= Cation
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}

A <cation> is an <ion> with positive net <electric charge>, for example $\mathrm{Na}^+$ or <hydronium>.

= Anion
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}

An <anion> is an <ion> with negative net <electric charge>, for example $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ or <hydroxide>.

= Chloride
{parent=Anion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cl^-}$}

<Chloride> is the <anion> $\mathrm{Cl^-}$, with net <electric charge> $-e$. In the <glycine titration> model, it supplies the negative background <electric charge> introduced with <hydrochloric acid>.

= Permanganate
{parent=Anion}
{title2=$\mathrm{MnO}_4^-$}

A <manganese>-containing <anion> with net <electric charge> $-e$ and <manganese> <oxidation state> $+7$; $e$ is the positive <elementary charge>.

= Nitrate
{parent=Anion}
{title2=$\mathrm{NO}_3^-$}
{wiki}

= Nitrate ion
{synonym}

The <nitrate ion>, $\mathrm{NO}_3^-$, is an <anion> with one <nitrogen> connected to three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> and net <electric charge> $-1$ in units of <elementary charge>. Its 24 <valence electrons> admit three equivalent octet <resonance structures>; see <Lewis structure> for their construction and <chemical resonance> for their interpretation. The N–O connections are <covalent bonds> within the <ion>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.2 Covalent Bonding>.

= Hydrogen ion
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm H^+$}

In <acid-base chemistry>, <hydrogen ion> usually refers to positively charged hydrogen, represented by $\mathrm H^+$. In <water>, this notation describes solvated species, commonly represented by <hydronium>, rather than an isolated bare <proton>. The <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity> defines <pH>.

= Hydronium
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+$}

<Hydronium> is the <ion> formed when a <water> <molecule> accepts a <proton>. It provides the usual aqueous shorthand for the <hydrogen ion> used in <pH> calculations.

= Hydroxide
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{OH}^-$}

= Counterion
{parent=Ion}

A mobile <ion> whose charge has the opposite sign to a charged macromolecule, surface or other fixed species. Its local <concentration> can increase close to that fixed charge; <counterion condensation> distinguishes a bound fraction that remains localized in an infinite-dilution limit.

= Electrochemistry
{parent=Chemistry}

<Electrochemistry> studies how <chemical reactions>, transport, and <chemical equilibrium> couple to <electric charge> and <electric potential>. In a cell, <electrons> travel through the external circuit, while mobile <ions> carry <electric current> through the <electrolyte>. At the <electrodes>, <redox> reactions connect these two forms of <electric charge> transport. This connection makes it possible both to obtain electrical <work> from a <chemical reaction> and to use electrical <work> to drive a <chemical reaction>.

An <anode> is an <electrode> where <oxidation> occurs; a <cathode> is an <electrode> where <reduction> occurs. These definitions refer to the <chemical reactions>, rather than universal positive or negative terminal signs. In a <galvanic cell> delivering electrical <work>, the <anode> is negative and the <cathode> positive. In an <electrolytic cell> driven by an external source, the <anode> is positive and the <cathode> negative. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109[Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), §§3.1 and 3.4] and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-2-galvanic-cells[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §17.2: galvanic cells].

For a worked example, consider an <electrolytic cell> containing molten <sodium chloride>, with the <anode> connected to the positive terminal and the <cathode> to the negative terminal of an external source. At the <cathode>, <sodium> <cations> gain <electrons>; at the <anode>, <chlorine> <anions> lose <electrons>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{cathode, reduction:}\quad&2\mathrm{Na}^++2e^-\longrightarrow2\mathrm{Na},\\
\text{anode, oxidation:}\quad&2\mathrm{Cl}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cl}_2+2e^-,\\
\text{overall:}\quad&2\mathrm{Na}^++2\mathrm{Cl}^-\longrightarrow2\mathrm{Na}+\mathrm{Cl}_2.
\end{aligned}
$$
The first line has net <electric charge> zero on each side; the second has $-2e$ on each side. Adding the lines cancels the transferred <electrons> and preserves both <chemical atom> counts and net <electric charge>. The <sodium> product is neutral, and the <chlorine> product is a diatomic <molecule>. These equations specify molten <sodium chloride>; aqueous <chemical solutions> introduce competing <water> reactions. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.7 Electrolysis> (§17.7: electrolysis of molten sodium chloride).

<Electrochemistry> underlies <battery> charging and the manufacture of chemicals by <electrolysis>. Its particularly useful idea is that an <electron> inventory in a balanced <chemical equation> also accounts for transported <electric charge>. Transport alone is a different question from an <electrode> <chemical reaction>: an <ion> may carry <electric current> through the <electrolyte> without itself being consumed at an <electrode>.

Historically, Michael Faraday introduced the terms <electrode>, <electrolyte>, <anion>, <cation>, and <ion> in the terminology section of his 1834 paper, <Experimental Researches in Electricity, Seventh Series by Michael Faraday> (§§662–667). His §667 explicitly treats the direction assigned to <electric current> as a convention. His <ion> language predates the modern <electron> and <atomic nucleus> picture used in the charge-counting example above.

For an <electrolyte> near <chemical equilibrium>, a <Poisson-Boltzmann equation> can approximate the distribution of <ions> in an <electric potential>. Such an equilibrium model does not by itself describe a cell carrying sustained <electric current>.

= Electrode
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

An <electrode> is an electronic <electrical conductor> in contact with an <electrolyte>. In a cell carrying sustained <redox> <electric current>, <chemical reactions> at this interface exchange <electrons> with <chemical species>. An <electrode> can participate in the <chemical reaction>, as the dissolving <zinc> <anode> of a <Daniell cell> does, or provide a conducting surface without appearing in the net <chemical equation>. This distinction is illustrated by active and inert <electrodes> in https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-2-galvanic-cells[Flowers, Theopold, Langley and Robinson, Chemistry 2e, §17.2].

The interface connects two kinds of <electric charge> transport: <electrons> in the external <electrical circuit> and <ions> in the <electrolyte>. The same <electron> does not travel through the wire, swim through the <salt bridge>, and complete a circuit! The <half-reactions> account for how electronic and ionic transport meet. This is the useful idea behind both obtaining electrical <work> in a <galvanic cell> and supplying electrical <work> in an <electrolytic cell>.

Name an <electrode> from its net <chemical reaction>: <oxidation> means <anode>, and <reduction> means <cathode>. State the operating mode before assigning terminal signs. For a <galvanic cell> supplying a passive load, the <anode> is negative and the <cathode> positive; for an ordinary two-terminal <electrolytic cell> driven by a source, the <anode> is positive and the <cathode> negative. Here positive means higher terminal <electric potential> than the other <electrode>, not a claim that the bulk material has a large positive net <electric charge>. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109[Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), §§3.1 and 3.4].

= Anode
{parent=Electrode}
{wiki}

The <anode> is the <electrode> at which the net <electrode> <chemical reaction> is <oxidation>: the reacting <chemical species> loses <electrons>. This definition is independent of terminal sign. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00370[IUPAC Gold Book: anode].

During <Daniell cell> discharge, a <zinc> <chemical atom> becomes a $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ <cation>, leaving two <electrons> in the <electrode>; those <electrons> leave through the external <electrical circuit>. The <zinc> <anode> is the negative terminal. In an externally driven <electrolytic cell>, <electrons> instead leave the <anode> through the lead to the source's positive terminal. <Conventional current> in that lead points toward the <anode>, opposite to <electron> motion. The <anode> can therefore be positive while still being the site of <oxidation>. See <Daniell cell> for the balanced <half-reaction> and <electrode> for the operating-mode sign conventions.

= Cathode
{parent=Electrode}
{wiki}

The <cathode> is the <electrode> at which the net <electrode> <chemical reaction> is <reduction>: the reacting <chemical species> gains <electrons>. This definition is independent of terminal sign. During <Daniell cell> discharge, $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ <cations> accept <electrons> arriving at the <copper> <cathode> and become neutral <copper>. The <cathode> is then the positive terminal. In an externally driven <electrolytic cell>, the source's negative terminal supplies <electrons> to the negative <cathode>. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109[Pingarrón et al., §3.4: cathode] and <Daniell cell>.

An <ion> carrying <electric current> need not undergo <reduction> at the <cathode>. For example, in the ideal <Daniell cell> charge-balance picture, <potassium> <cations> arriving from a <salt bridge> remain dissolved while $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ <cations> are consumed. Follow the <half-reaction> to identify the reacting species; follow <electric charge conservation> to account for the other moving <ions>.

= Galvanic cell
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

A <galvanic cell> supplies electrical <work> from a spontaneous <redox> <chemical reaction> under the specified conditions. In discharge through a passive load, <oxidation> occurs at its negative <anode> and <reduction> at its positive <cathode>. Separating the reacting compartments makes the <electrons> take an external route, where their transport can deliver useful <work>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-2-galvanic-cells[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §17.2: separated reactions and electrical work].

\Image[galvanic-electrodes-daniell-paths.png]
{title=<Daniell cell> discharge: <electron> flow outside and <ion> transport inside}
{description=Original schematic of a <zinc>/<copper> <galvanic cell> connected to a passive load. A chosen <potassium> <nitrate> <salt bridge> supplies compensating <anions> to the <anode> compartment and <cations> to the <cathode> compartment. The arrows represent charge-balance roles, not measured trajectories or relative fluxes. The bottom inventory is an ideal accounting example for one <mole> of net cell reaction.}

In the metallic external <electrical circuit>, <electrons> move from <anode> to <cathode>, while <conventional current> goes from <cathode> through the load to <anode>. Inside the cell, net ionic <conventional current> closes the loop from the <anode> compartment toward the <cathode> compartment. Oppositely moving <cations> and <anions> can both contribute to that direction; see <electrolyte> for the signed <current density> calculation. A cell can maintain a nonzero terminal <electric potential difference> near open circuit while delivering almost no <power>; the <Daniell cell voltage and load experiment> makes this distinction concrete.

= Daniell cell
{c}
{parent=Galvanic cell}
{wiki}

A <Daniell cell> uses the <zinc>/$\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$/<copper> pairs. In the separated-compartment teaching version, a <zinc> <electrode> contacts an aqueous <chemical solution> containing $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$, and a <copper> <electrode> contacts one containing $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$. A <salt bridge> supplies an ionic connection; a wire and load supply an electronic connection. During discharge,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{anode, oxidation:}\quad&\mathrm{Zn}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-,\\
\text{cathode, reduction:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}(s),\\
\text{net:}\quad&\mathrm{Zn}(s)+\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(aq)+\mathrm{Cu}(s).
\end{aligned}
$$
The <zinc> <electrode> loses material and <copper> is deposited at the <cathode>. Each <half-reaction> conserves <chemical atom> counts and <electric charge>: the first has net charge zero on each side, the second $+2e-2e=0$ on the left and zero on the right. The net reaction has $+2e$ on both sides, with no <electrons> left in its <chemical equation>. The Zn/Cu teaching cell and these <half-reactions> are described in https://www.chem.fsu.edu/chemlab/chm1lab/redox/background.html[Florida State University, Redox Reactions in Voltaic Cells: Construction of a Potential Series, lab background].

Creating one <mole> of $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ adds $2F$ of positive <electric charge> to the <anode> compartment's ionic inventory; consuming one <mole> of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ removes $2F$ of positive <electric charge> from the <cathode> compartment. Here $F$ is the <Faraday constant>. In an ideal bookkeeping example using a <potassium> <nitrate> <salt bridge>, two <moles> of <nitrate> <anions> entering the <anode> compartment compensate the first change, and two <moles> of <potassium> <cations> entering the <cathode> compartment compensate the second. Other <ions> in the cell can share the actual transport. The two compensating inventory changes do not mean that $4F$ flowed through the load: the same series <electric current> transports $2F$ through each cross-section of the circuit per <mole> of net reaction. See <salt bridge>.

Under standard-state conditions near $298\,\mathrm K$, tabulated reduction potentials give a cell <electromotive force> of approximately $1.10\,\mathrm V$. This is a reversible reference value, not a promised loaded reading; actual composition matters through <thermodynamic activity>. The tabulated <copper> and <zinc> values and their subtraction are given in https://chemed.chem.purdue.edu/genchem/topicreview/bp/ch20/electro.php[Purdue University, Electrochemical Reactions: standard-state reduction potentials]. For the net <chemical equation> as written, the molar <Gibbs free energy> change obeys
$$
\Delta_r G=-2F\mathcal E.
$$
Thus $\mathcal E=1.10\,\mathrm V$ corresponds to about $-212\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ of net reaction. At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, $-\Delta_r G$ is the maximum reversible electrical <work> per <mole> for this cell reaction. It is not automatically all the <heat> released by a finite-current cell. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.4 Potential, Free Energy, and Equilibrium> (§17.4: cell potential and free energy).

= Daniell cell voltage and load experiment
{c}
{parent=Daniell cell}

The motivating observation is that a <chemical reaction> can produce a signed electrical reading even when its reacting materials occupy separate vessels. UC San Diego's teaching demonstration places a <copper> strip in $0.1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$ and a <zinc> strip in $0.1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{ZnSO_4}$, using two $400\,\mathrm{mL}$ beakers with about $300\,\mathrm{mL}$ of <chemical solution> in each. The strips connect to a voltage meter. Initially there is no ionic connection; the demonstration specifies a zero reading. A filter-paper <salt bridge> containing aqueous $\mathrm{Na_2SO_4}$ then connects the <chemical solutions>. With the meter's positive lead on <copper> and negative lead on <zinc>, its reported readings are about $+1.1\,\mathrm V$; exchanging the leads reverses the reading. These are observations reported by the university, not data generated for this article. See https://chemistryandbiochemistry.ucsd.edu/undergraduate/teaching-labs/demos/demo9b[UC San Diego, demonstration 9B: apparatus, bridge control and meter polarity].

The controls answer different questions. Exchanging only the meter leads checks the sign convention; it does not reverse the <chemical reaction> or swap the <anode> and <cathode>. Interrupting only the <salt bridge> tests the need for an ionic return path. Its absence prevents sustained cell <electric current>, but a very high-input-resistance meter may still show a transient or unstable reading while interfaces store <electric charge>. The zero reading specified for the university's apparatus is therefore not a universal theorem for every meter. Reconnecting the same <salt bridge> distinguishes a reversible interruption from a disconnected wire or exhausted <chemical solution>. This interpretation follows from <electric charge conservation> in a model allowing interfacial <electric charge> storage; see https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/I03068[IUPAC Gold Book: integral capacitance of an electrode] for the <capacitance> associated with <electrode> charge.

To distinguish terminal <electric potential difference> from delivered <power>, compare the nearly open-circuit reading with readings across a known <resistor> load. In the nearly open-circuit condition, a meter with input <electrical resistance> much larger than the cell's effective internal <electrical resistance> draws little <electric current>. With a load, measure its terminal <electric potential difference> $V$ and infer $I=V/R$ using <Ohm's law>; track the readings over <time> and repeat the nearly open-circuit reading afterwards. Keep the <temperature>, <chemical solution> compositions, immersed <electrode> areas, bridge geometry and electrical contacts comparable. This is a model-based extension of the documented demonstration, not a claim that UC San Diego published a load sweep. A finite-current drop can reflect ionic <electrical resistance>, changes of interfacial <reaction rates>, or depletion near an <electrode>; a single voltage reading cannot identify which dominates. The interfacial and composition contributions are distinguished in https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/O04358[IUPAC Gold Book: overpotential] and https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01230[IUPAC Gold Book: concentration overpotential]{ref}.

For a deliberately simplified calculation, model the cell by a constant <electromotive force> $\mathcal E$ in series with a constant internal <electrical resistance> $r$, and a passive load $R>0$. Take $I$ positive from the <cathode> through the load to the <anode>. Then
$$
I=\frac{\mathcal E}{R+r},\qquad V=IR=\mathcal E-Ir,\qquad P_{\rm load}=IV=I^2R.
$$
The nearly open-circuit limit $R\to\infty$ gives $V\to\mathcal E$ but $I\to0$ and $P_{\rm load}\to0$. A straight $V$-versus-$I$ line is a prediction of constant $r$, not a general law for <electrochemistry>. See https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-1-electromotive-force[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §10.1: internal resistance, terminal voltage and load].

\Image[galvanic-electrodes-load-budget.png]
{title=Toy <Daniell cell> load calculation: terminal <electric potential difference>, <electric charge> and available <work>}
{description=Calculated line and energy bars, not measurements. Chosen inputs are $\mathcal E=1.10\,\mathrm V$, $r=100\,\Omega$, $R=100\,\Omega$ and a $600\,\mathrm s$ interval. The fixed-composition approximation and unit efficiency for the two intended <half-reactions> are assumed. The open-circuit marker is an ideal limit; no fit to experimental data is made.}

For the chosen values, $I=5.50\,\mathrm{mA}$ and $V=0.550\,\mathrm V$. Holding these toy parameters fixed for $600\,\mathrm s$ gives transported <electric charge> $Q=It=3.30\,\mathrm C$ and an <extent of reaction> $\xi=Q/(2F)\simeq17.1\,\mathrm{\mu mol}$, assuming all the <electric current> serves the two specified <half-reactions>. The same $\xi$ counts <zinc> consumed and <copper> deposited, rather than equal <masses>. The energy budget is
$$
\mathcal E Q=3.630\,\mathrm J
=\underbrace{VQ}_{1.815\,\mathrm J\text{ delivered to load}}
+\underbrace{I^2rt}_{1.815\,\mathrm J\text{ internal dissipation}}.
$$
The load's electrical <work> also becomes <Joule heating> when the load is a <resistor>. Here $\mathcal E Q$ accounts for the reaction's available reversible electrical <work>; a full cell thermal budget would also require its thermodynamic <entropy> contribution. Real measurements need uncertainty estimates, a specified meter input <electrical resistance> and calibration, and allowance for changing composition and unwanted <chemical reactions>. The toy value $r=100\,\Omega$ is not a measured property of the university's cell.

= Electrolysis
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

<Electrolysis> uses an external source of electrical <work> to drive <redox> <chemical reactions> at the <electrodes> of an <electrolytic cell>. Its characteristic application is forcing a <chemical reaction> in a direction that is nonspontaneous under the specified conditions: a discharging <galvanic cell> supplies electrical <work>, whereas <electrolysis> consumes electrical <work>. This makes <electrolysis> useful for producing otherwise difficult-to-obtain <chemical substances>, charging a rechargeable <battery>, and depositing a metal coating on a conducting object.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.7 Electrolysis>[§17.7]{ref}

Always identify the <anode> by <oxidation> and the <cathode> by <reduction>. In ordinary driven two-terminal operation, the source withdraws <electrons> from the positive <anode> and delivers <electrons> to the negative <cathode>. In a discharging <galvanic cell>, the <anode> is negative and the <cathode> positive. Thus terminal signs change with operating mode, while the reaction definitions remain valid. Through the <electrolyte>, <ions> transport <electric charge>; they need not all be consumed at an <electrode>. The <electrolyte> and <electrolytic cell> articles explain this transport and its <conventional current> direction.

For a net <chemical equation> transferring $z$ <electrons> per reaction event, the molar reaction <Gibbs free energy> obeys
$$
\Delta_rG=-zF E_{\mathrm{chem}},
$$
where $F$ is the <Faraday constant> and $E_{\mathrm{chem}}$ is the reversible cell <electromotive force> for the reaction in the written direction. A nonspontaneous direction has $\Delta_rG>0$ and $E_{\mathrm{chem}}<0$ at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>. The ideal opposing voltage magnitude is $\Delta_rG/(zF)$; sustaining a finite <electric current> generally needs additional drive because of <electrical resistance> and finite <electrode> <reaction rates>. This thermodynamic voltage concerns the actual composition, rather than necessarily standard conditions.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.4 Potential, Free Energy, and Equilibrium>[§17.4]{ref} Not every use of <electrolysis> increases bulk chemical <Gibbs free energy>: transferring <copper> between equivalent surfaces can have zero bulk $\Delta_rG$ while the source still supplies dissipated electrical <work>. See the existing <electrolytic cell> example.

A particularly revealing experiment measures <copper> transfer instead of merely looking for a coating. Two separated <copper> <electrodes> stand in aqueous $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$ <electrolyte> in a vessel, with insulated leads to a regulated direct-current power supply, a series ammeter and a switch. A balance reads each <electrode>'s <mass> before and after a timed run. The Institute of Physics describes copper cells with ammeters, adjustable current and removable <cathodes>; its comparison uses three similar cells at $1\,\mathrm A$ for ten minutes, $0.5\,\mathrm A$ for ten minutes, and $0.5\,\mathrm A$ for twenty minutes. The specified observation to test is that deposited <copper> <mass> is proportional to <electric current> times <time>. These are documented experimental settings, not measurements generated here.<Electrolysis of copper sulfate solution by Institute of Physics>{ref}

The ideal <half-reactions> are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{anode, oxidation:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-,\\
\text{cathode, reduction:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}(s).
\end{aligned}
$$
Each deposited <copper> <chemical atom> requires two <electrons>, so $z=2$. The <anode> loses <copper> and the <cathode> gains it; ideally their <mass> changes have equal magnitudes, and the bulk <copper> <cation> inventory stays constant. Local <concentrations> near the <electrodes> can nevertheless differ. The sulfate <anions> participate in ionic transport without appearing in these net <half-reactions>; electronic <electric current> in the wires becomes ionic <electric current> in the <electrolyte> through the two interfaces.<Elektrolyse, Bestimmung von e by Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, Faculty of Physics teaching laboratory>{ref}

\Image[electrolysis-copper-process.png]
{title=Driven <copper> transfer: <oxidation> at the positive <anode> and <reduction> at the negative <cathode>}
{description=Original model schematic, not a measured trajectory map. The source withdraws <electrons> on the left and supplies them on the right; bulk ionic <conventional current> closes the circuit. Before/after dry <electrode> <masses> and the integrated <electric current> provide independent readouts. The two <half-reactions> count the same transferred <electric charge>, so their charges must not be added together.}

Clean, rinse, dry and identify the <electrodes> before their initial weighing. After interrupting the <electric current>, rinse away adhering <electrolyte>, dry again and weigh without rubbing off the deposit. KIT's university practical explicitly calls for distilled-water rinsing, careful drying and weighing both <electrodes>; it warns that loose <copper> grains can be lost during handling.<Elektrolyse, Bestimmung von e by Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, Faculty of Physics teaching laboratory>{ref} Useful additional controls are repeated drying and weighing until the reading stabilizes, and a matched unpowered immersion followed by the same rinse/dry cycle. These controls test retained liquid, residual dissolved salts, handling loss and chemical changes unrelated to passed <electric charge>; they are proposed checks, not additional observations reported by KIT. Keep immersed <electrode> area, spacing, <electrolyte> composition and <temperature> comparable across runs, and keep the <electrodes> from touching.

Record the <electric current> as a function of <time>, together with the exact switch-on and switch-off times. Taking $I(t)\geq0$ in the chosen deposition direction, the transported <electric charge> is
$$
Q=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}I(t)\,dt.
$$
For constant <electric current>, $Q=I(t_1-t_0)$; with sampled readings, use <numerical integration> rather than assuming that one ammeter reading remained constant. The two equal-charge Institute of Physics settings each give $600\,\mathrm C$, while its shorter $0.5\,\mathrm A$ setting gives $300\,\mathrm C$. With $M_{\mathrm{Cu}}=63.546\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$, the <mass> per <amount of substance> tabulated by KIT, and $F\approx96485.33212\,\mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$, ideal <Faraday's laws of electrolysis> predict
$$
m_{\mathrm{Cu}}=\frac{M_{\mathrm{Cu}}Q}{2F}
\approx\begin{cases}
0.09879\,\mathrm g,&Q=300\,\mathrm C,\\
0.19758\,\mathrm g,&Q=600\,\mathrm C.
\end{cases}
$$
These are calculated predictions for complete conversion of the measured <electric charge> into retained <copper>, not experimental results. For a stable process, doubling the <electric charge> doubles the deposited <mass>; equal <electric charge> gives equal deposited <mass> even when the <electric current> and duration differ.

\Image[electrolysis-charge-mass.png]
{title=<Faraday's laws of electrolysis> connect transported <electric charge> to deposited <copper> <mass>}
{description=Original calculated model, with no experimental points. Left: assumed constant-current histories with equal areas $Q=600\,\mathrm C$. Right: ideal <copper> <mass> versus <electric charge> and a stipulated 80 percent current-efficiency comparison. The dashed line assumes that 20 percent of the <electric charge> drives other same-direction <electrode> reactions and that all deposited <copper> remains attached.}

The balance reading is a test of the whole model. Competing <reduction> of <water> or other <chemical species> can divert <electric current> from <copper> deposition; transient <electrode> <capacitance> can also consume part of a short pulse without producing <copper>. A fragile deposit can detach even when its formation used the expected <electric charge>. Conversely, retained <electrolyte> or salts can increase an apparent <mass> gain. Comparing <anode> loss with <cathode> gain helps detect such problems but does not identify every cause. A slope below $M_{\mathrm{Cu}}/(2F)$ alone cannot distinguish competing <chemical reactions> from lost deposit. Current efficiency measures the fraction of transported <electric charge> assigned to a specified <electrode> reaction, rather than the fraction of electrical <energy> stored chemically.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/current efficiency>{ref}

The beautiful connection is that an electrical measurement counts chemical events: integrating <electric current> and reading a balance provide two ways of following the same <electron> <stoichiometry>. This makes <electrolysis> useful for controlling coating quantity and for testing a charge-to-<amount of substance> relation. It does not determine coating uniformity, adhesion or the required voltage, which depend on transport and <electrode> <chemical kinetics> as well.

= Faraday's laws of electrolysis
{c}
{parent=Electrolysis}
{wiki}
{title2=$m=MQ/(zF)$}

<Faraday's laws of electrolysis> state that the <mass> of material formed or consumed by a specified <electrode> reaction is proportional to the transported <electric charge>, and that equal <electric charge> produces <masses> proportional to $M/z$ for different reactions. Here $M$ is the product's <mass> per <mole>, and $z$ is the positive number of <electrons> transferred per product entity. For complete use of the <electric charge> in that reaction,
$$
n_{e^-}=\frac QF,\qquad n_{\mathrm{product}}=\frac{Q}{zF},\qquad m=\frac{MQ}{zF}.
$$
The first relation uses the <Faraday constant>; the second uses <half-reaction> <stoichiometry>; the third converts <amount of substance> to <mass>. For equal transported <electric charge> in two ideal cells,
$$
\frac{m_1}{m_2}=\frac{M_1/z_1}{M_2/z_2}.
$$
The number $z$ belongs to the balanced <half-reaction>, rather than to the metal's name alone: <copper> deposited from $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ needs $z=2$. These modern forms of both laws are given in KIT's practical.<Elektrolyse, Bestimmung von e by Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, Faculty of Physics teaching laboratory>{ref}

If other same-direction <electrode> reactions share the measured <electric current>, define an integrated current efficiency $\eta=Q_{\mathrm{product}}/Q$. Provided the product is neither subsequently consumed nor lost, the retained <mass> is
$$
m=\eta\frac{MQ}{zF},\qquad
\eta=\frac{\int I_{\mathrm{product}}(t)\,dt}{\int I(t)\,dt}.
$$
Here $0\leq\eta\leq1$ follows from the specified same-direction partition of <electric current>. It is not a universal bound on every signed partial-current convention: IUPAC notes that simultaneous <oxidation> and <reduction> at one <electrode> can produce a current-efficiency ratio above one.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/current efficiency>{ref} When efficiency varies during a run, use the charge-weighted ratio above, rather than an unweighted average over <time>. If deposition removes material from a multicomponent <chemical solution>, specify which <chemical species> is being counted and balance its <half-reaction> before choosing $z$.

Michael Faraday's accessible 1834 paper, Experimental Researches in Electricity, Seventh Series, §783 states proportionality between chemical action and the quantity of electricity passed. In §§789–791 he describes connecting a molten tin-chloride cell in series with a water-decomposition charge indicator, weighing the receiving <electrode> before and after, and comparing the deposited metal with the indicator's gas readout. The common series <electric current> makes these two readouts comparable. This was a quantitative test of chemical equivalents, rather than merely an observation that a deposit appeared. It is historical evidence, not a suggested classroom procedure; the modern <electron>-counting derivation above uses concepts unavailable to Faraday.<Experimental Researches in Electricity, Seventh Series by Michael Faraday>[§783; §§789–791; pp. 101–103]{ref}

= Electrolytic cell
{parent=Electrolysis}
{wiki}

An <electrolytic cell> uses an external source of electrical <work> to drive <electrode> <chemical reactions>. In the ordinary two-terminal driven mode, its positive <anode> supports <oxidation> and its negative <cathode> supports <reduction>. The source removes <electrons> from the <anode> and supplies <electrons> to the <cathode>; through the <electrolyte>, net <conventional current> goes from <anode> toward <cathode>. Terminal signs refer to the driven cell's terminals relative to each other. The source supplies electrical <power> to the cell. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.7 Electrolysis> (§17.7: electrolysis and electroplating).

For example, transferring <copper> from a soluble <copper> <anode> onto a conducting object at the <cathode> has the ideal <half-reactions>
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{positive anode:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-,\\
\text{negative cathode:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}(s).
\end{aligned}
$$
Their sum transfers <copper> between surfaces. For identical bulk states, this transfer need not have a positive bulk <Gibbs free energy> change; finite <electric current> still needs applied drive to overcome losses. Thus "consumes electrical <work>" does not mean every <electrolytic cell> stores all that <work> as chemical <Gibbs free energy>. <Electrolysis> can instead redistribute material. The charge-to-amount relation remains $\xi=Q/(2F)$ when only these <half-reactions> carry the <electric current>; competing <water> reactions would change the deposited amount. The electroplating principle and charge accounting are described in <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.7 Electrolysis> (§17.7: electroplating and quantitative aspects).

The University of Washington's teaching demonstration makes this material transfer visible with a <copper> <electrode> and a stainless-steel <electrode> in a glass cell containing $0.5\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$. The external source connects its positive lead to <copper> and negative lead to steel; the reported observation is a <copper> coating on the steel. Reversing the source polarity removes the deposited <copper> again: the coated surface now supports <oxidation> and acts as the <anode>. This polarity reversal changes the drive, unlike swapping the measurement leads in the <Daniell cell voltage and load experiment>. Coating appearance alone does not measure charge efficiency or exclude competing <chemical reactions>. See https://chem.washington.edu/lecture-demos/electroplating-copper-stainless-steel[University of Washington, Electroplating Copper on Stainless Steel: apparatus and reversible coating demonstration].

The <copper> <cathode> in this driven example is negative, while the <copper> <cathode> of a discharging <Daniell cell> is positive: both support exactly the same <reduction> <half-reaction>. Likewise, an <anode> need not lose material if its conducting surface is absent from its net <half-reaction>. Neither terminal sign nor a universal mass-change rule defines an <anode> or <cathode>.

= Half-reaction
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

A <half-reaction> is a balanced <chemical equation> for either the <oxidation> or the <reduction> part of a <redox> <chemical reaction>, explicitly including exchanged <electrons>. Match the electron counts before adding the two <half-reactions>; the net equation then contains no transferred <electrons>. A <half-reaction> is bookkeeping, not necessarily an <elementary reaction>. See https://www.chem.fsu.edu/chemlab/chm1lab/redox/background.html[Florida State University, lab background: separate electrode reactions] and <Daniell cell>.

= Salt bridge
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

A <salt bridge> is an ionic connection between <electrolyte> compartments that limits bulk mixing while permitting <electric charge> transport. Its mobile <ions> compensate changes caused by the <electrode> <half-reactions>, keeping the bulk <chemical solutions> approximately electrically neutral during sustained <electric current>. It does not conduct <electrons> like the external wire, and its <ions> need not be consumed at either <electrode>. The ionic return path and compensating ion flows are explained in https://chemed.chem.purdue.edu/genchem/topicreview/bp/ch20/electro.php[Purdue University, Electrochemical Reactions: salt bridge and charge balance].

In a discharging <Daniell cell>, <anions> moving toward the <anode> compartment and <cations> moving toward the <cathode> compartment both contribute to the internal <conventional current> from <anode> toward <cathode>. This charge-balance role is compatible with a negative <anode>: terminal signs do not imply that the whole <anode> <chemical solution> is negatively charged. The simple compensating-inventory example in <Daniell cell> does not fix all individual <ion> fluxes; <diffusion> and the cell's other mobile <ions> can contribute. A real bridge also introduces <electrical resistance> and may introduce a junction <electric potential difference>, so its presence does not guarantee an exact thermodynamic cell reading. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/09080[IUPAC Gold Book: salt bridge and liquid-junction potential].

= Electrolyte
{parent=Electrochemistry}

An <electrolyte> is a conducting medium in which mobile <ions> carry <electric current>, according to https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/09061[IUPAC Gold Book: electrolyte]. In introductory <chemistry>, a substance that yields <ions> when dissolved in <water> is also called an <electrolyte>. Both usages point to the same mechanism: an <electric field> gives mobile <cations> and <anions> directed <drift velocities>.

Mobility matters as much as the presence of <electric charge>. In a simple ideal model of solid <sodium chloride>, <ions> are confined to their sites and do not carry a sustained transport <electric current>. Molten <sodium chloride> has mobile <ions>; dissolved <sodium chloride> gives mobile $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ <ions> in <water>. Real solids can have some ionic transport through imperfections; the immobile-solid picture is an introductory limit, rather than a claim that all solids are perfect insulators.

A <strong electrolyte> yields essentially all <ions> from the dissolved substance, whereas a <weak electrolyte> yields only a fraction under the specified conditions. Dissolved <sodium chloride> is a <strong electrolyte>. <Sucrose> is a <nonelectrolyte>: it dissolves as neutral <molecules>, so dissolving something does not automatically create mobile <ions>. <Water>'s own small population of <hydronium> and <hydroxide> remains possible. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.2 Electrolytes> (§11.2: strong, weak, and nonelectrolytes) and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §11.4, Table 11.3: sucrose and sodium chloride].

<Acetic acid> is a <weak electrolyte> and a <weak acid>. In <water>, some neutral <acetic acid> <molecules> transfer a <proton> to <water>, producing <hydronium> and the negatively charged partner:
$$
\mathrm{CH_3COOH}+\mathrm{H_2O}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}+\mathrm{H_3O^+}.
$$
Both sides have net <electric charge> zero. At <chemical equilibrium>, neutral <acetic acid> coexists with these <ions>; the fraction converted depends on the conditions. <Electrolyte> strength describes this extent of ion production, while <concentration> describes how much substance is present per specified quantity of <chemical solution>. A dilute <strong electrolyte> can contain fewer <ions> per volume than a concentrated <weak electrolyte>. Neither the words strong and weak nor a conductivity comparison alone specifies <concentration>.

Now specify an <electrolytic cell> with a positive <anode> on the left, a negative <cathode> on the right, and an approximately uniform <electric field> pointing right in the bulk <electrolyte>. With a steady external source and neglecting <diffusion> and <convection>, positive <cations> drift right toward the negative <cathode>; negative <anions> drift left toward the positive <anode>. Yet both populations contribute a rightward <conventional current>! For <number densities> $n_+,n_-$ and <drift velocities> $\mathbf v_+,\mathbf v_-$, the transport <current density> is
$$
\mathbf J=n_+(+e)\mathbf v_++n_-(-e)\mathbf v_-.
$$
The product of <electric charge> and <velocity> determines <conventional current>: for the <anion>, both the charge and its rightward velocity component are negative, making its rightward contribution positive. This is the same sign rule used for <electron> <conventional current>. It follows directly from the existing <charge carrier> formula.

\Image[electrolyte-ion-drift-current.png]
{title=Opposite <ion> <drift velocities> contribute to the same <conventional current> in a specified <electrolytic cell>}
{description=The positive <anode> is left and the negative <cathode> right. The upper arrows show <ion> motion; the lower arrows show the signs of $nqv_x$ for <current density>. A uniform bulk <electric field> is assumed; <diffusion>, <convection>, and <electrode> boundary layers are omitted. Arrow lengths are schematic.}

For an independent numerical example, stipulate $n_+=n_-=10^{25}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, $v_{+,x}=2\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, and $v_{-,x}=-3\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. With the exact <elementary charge> $e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$, the contributions are
$$
J_{+,x}=320.4353268\,\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}},\qquad
J_{-,x}=480.6529902\,\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}},\qquad
J_x=801.088317\,\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}}.
$$
Equal <number densities> of singly charged <cations> and <anions> give zero bulk <charge density>, while their motion gives nonzero <current density>. This is why a neutral <electrolyte> can conduct. These numbers are chosen toy inputs, not measurements of <sodium chloride>; maintaining sustained cell <electric current> also requires suitable <electrode> reactions. Blocking <electrodes> can instead lead to charge accumulation and decay of the <electric current>.

An <electrolyte> connects the microscopic motion of <ions> to observable <electrical conductivity>. A counterion-only model keeps just the mobile <counterions> needed to oppose a fixed <electric charge>; <Poisson-Boltzmann equations> use ideal <Boltzmann distributions> to model their equilibrium distributions, rather than the drift example above.

= Strong electrolyte
{parent=Electrolyte}
{wiki}

A <strong electrolyte> yields essentially all <ions> from its dissolved substance in the specified <solvent> and conditions. Strength does not specify <concentration>.

= Weak electrolyte
{parent=Electrolyte}
{wiki}

A <weak electrolyte> yields only a fraction of <ions> from its dissolved substance under the specified conditions, as illustrated by <acetic acid> in <water>.

= Nonelectrolyte
{parent=Electrolyte}

A <nonelectrolyte> supplies neutral <molecules> rather than <ions> on dissolving in the specified <solvent>, as illustrated by <sucrose> in <water>.

= Chemical reaction
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <chemical reaction> interconverts <chemical species>, as defined by the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01033[IUPAC Gold Book, chemical reaction]{ref}. The consumed species are <reactants>; the formed species are <reaction products>. In ordinary <chemical reactions>, <chemical atoms> are rearranged while each <chemical element>'s total atom count is conserved. For a complete <chemical equation>, net <electric charge> is conserved too.

For example, the balanced <chemical equation>
$$
2\mathrm{H}_2+\mathrm{O}_2\longrightarrow2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}
$$
describes conversion of <molecular hydrogen> and <molecular oxygen> into <water>. Both sides contain four <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. Three <reactant> <molecules> become two <reaction product> <molecules>: atom counts are conserved, but molecule counts need not be. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-1-writing-and-balancing-chemical-equations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, balancing chemical equations].

The coefficients describe net consumption and formation, without asserting that three <molecules> collide in one step. A balanced <chemical equation> supplies quantitative constraints; it does not determine how quickly the <chemical reaction> occurs, or whether <chemical equilibrium> prevents complete conversion. Those questions need further information. Keeping these roles distinct makes <stoichiometry> useful even when the microscopic details are complicated.

= Precipitation
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Precipitation (chemistry)> is formation of a solid from a solution. A newly formed solid can create a surface for <heterogeneous catalysis>; observing it does not establish the composition or catalytic role of that surface.

= Redox
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Oxidation-reduction reaction
{synonym}

A <redox> <chemical reaction> couples <oxidation> and <reduction>. In the <electrode> examples here, <oxidation> releases <electrons> and <reduction> accepts them. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-2-classifying-chemical-reactions[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §4.2: oxidation-reduction reactions].

= Oxidation
{parent=Redox}

In the <electrochemistry> examples here, <oxidation> is loss of <electrons> by a <chemical species>; it occurs at the <anode>.

= Oxidation state
{parent=Oxidation}
{title2=$\mathrm{OS}$}
{wiki}

= Oxidation number
{synonym}

An <oxidation state> is an <electron>-bookkeeping assignment to a <chemical atom>: its <electric charge> number after the <ionic approximation of bonds>. It helps identify <oxidation> and <reduction> and determine <stoichiometry>, even for <covalent bonds> without separate charged <ions>. In <water>, each <hydrogen> has <oxidation state> $+1$ and <oxygen> has $-2$; this does not assert that a <water> <molecule> contains two free <protons> and a free oxide <ion>.<Comprehensive definition of oxidation state (IUPAC Recommendations 2016) by Pavel Karen and others>[Karen, McArdle and Takats, Comprehensive definition of oxidation state (IUPAC Recommendations 2016), §2, pp. 832–833: definition and equal splitting of same-element bonds]{ref}

Three conventions answer different questions. <Formal charge> divides each bonding <electron pair> equally in a chosen <Lewis structure>. <Oxidation state> uses the <ionic approximation of bonds>. <Partial atomic charge> instead partitions the actual <electron density> and depends on that partition. In the ordinary <water> <Lewis structure>, every <formal charge> is zero although the <oxidation states> are not. Neither bookkeeping convention supplies numerical <partial atomic charges>.

= Ionic approximation of bonds
{parent=Oxidation state}
{title2=$\mathrm{OS}_i=v_i-N_i^{\mathrm{assigned}}$}

For the routine examples here, assign all bonding <electrons> between different <chemical elements> to the more <electronegative> partner; divide bonding <electrons> between identical <chemical elements> equally. Retain each <lone pair> on its original <chemical atom>. Subtract the assigned <valence electron> count $N_i^{\mathrm{assigned}}$ from the neutral <chemical atom>'s <valence electron> count $v_i$. The IUPAC convention uses Allen <electronegativity>, based on free-<chemical atom> <valence electron> <energies>. Close <electronegativity> comparisons and some reversibly bound <chemical compounds> require care beyond this simple rule.<Comprehensive definition of oxidation state (IUPAC Recommendations 2016) by Pavel Karen and others>[§3 and §4.1, pp. 833–834: Allen electronegativity, electron allegiance and the reversible Lewis-acid-ligand caveat; §5.5, pp. 836–837: ambiguous assignments]{ref}

\Image[oxidation-state-electron-bookkeeping.png]
{id=image-oxidation-state-electron-bookkeeping}
{title=<Oxidation state> assignments for <water>, <hydrogen peroxide>, <molecular oxygen> and <sodium hydride>}
{description=Original bookkeeping diagrams, not <electron density> maps or measured <partial atomic charges>. Arrows indicate allocation of bonding <electrons>; an equal split is used for O–O. The <sodium hydride> panel represents a <formula unit> of an <ionic compound>, not an isolated gas-phase <molecule>.}

The examples explain exceptions to memorized rules:

* <Water>, $\mathrm{H{-}O{-}H}$: <oxygen> receives both O–H <bond pairs> plus its two <lone pairs>, giving eight assigned <valence electrons> and $6-8=-2$. Each <hydrogen> receives none, giving $1-0=+1$.
* <Hydrogen peroxide>, $\mathrm{H{-}O{-}O{-}H}$: each <oxygen> receives its O–H <bond pair>, one <electron> from O–O, and four <electrons> in <lone pairs>. Thus each has $6-7=-1$, while each <hydrogen> remains $+1$. The O–O connection is why the usual $-2$ <oxygen> shortcut fails.
* <Molecular oxygen>, $\mathrm{O}_2$: equal splitting of the O–O bonding <electrons> leaves each <oxygen> assigned six <valence electrons>, so each has <oxidation state> zero.
* <Sodium hydride>, $\mathrm{NaH}$: <hydrogen> is more <electronegative> than <sodium>. The <formula unit> is assigned <sodium> $+1$ and <hydrogen> $-1$, consistent with the <hydrogen anion>. The usual $+1$ <hydrogen> shortcut fails here.

These drawings record allocations, not <molecular geometry> or a physical process of breaking the <chemical bonds>. In particular, the O–O line in the <molecular oxygen> panel only marks the connection; it does not assert a complete <Lewis structure> or <spin> state.

= Oxidation state sum rule
{parent=Oxidation state}
{title2=$\sum_i\mathrm{OS}_i=z$}

Sum <oxidation states> over all <chemical atoms> of the specified <molecule>, <ion> or <formula unit>, including multiplicities:
$$
\sum_i\mathrm{OS}_i=z,\qquad q=ze.
$$
Here $q$ is the net <electric charge> and $e$ the positive <elementary charge>. For a neutral entity the sum is zero; for an <ion> it equals its signed <electric charge> number $z$. The reason is simple: the allocation assigns every <valence electron> exactly once, so the sum is the neutral <electron> budget minus the actual <electron> count.

For <water>, $2(+1)+(-2)=0$; for <hydrogen peroxide>, $2(+1)+2(-1)=0$. In <permanganate>, $\mathrm{MnO}_4^-$, assigning each <oxygen> $-2$ gives $x+4(-2)=-1$, so <manganese> has <oxidation state> $+7$. This is not a free $\mathrm{Mn}^{7+}$ <ion>: the entire <permanganate> <anion> has net <electric charge> $-e$. The sum rule supplies a constraint; without additional assignments it cannot determine every individual <oxidation state>.

= Acidic permanganate-iron balance
{parent=Oxidation state}

An increase in <oxidation state> records <oxidation>; a decrease records <reduction>. Thus $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\to\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ raises the <iron> <oxidation state> by one, while $\mathrm{MnO}_4^-\to\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ lowers the <manganese> <oxidation state> from $+7$ to $+2$. Five <iron> <ions> supply the five-<electron> inventory required per <permanganate> <ion>.

In an acidic <chemical solution>, the complete <half-reactions> are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}&\longrightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+e^-,\\
\mathrm{MnO}_4^-+8\mathrm H^++5e^-&\longrightarrow\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}+4\mathrm H_2\mathrm O.
\end{aligned}
$$
First balance <oxygen> with four <water> <molecules>, then <hydrogen> with eight <protons>, and finally <electric charge> with five <electrons>. Multiply the <iron> <half-reaction> by five and add; the exchanged <electrons> cancel:
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{MnO}_4^-+8\mathrm H^++5\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\longrightarrow\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}+4\mathrm H_2\mathrm O+5\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}.}
$$
Both sides contain one <manganese>, four <oxygen>, eight <hydrogen> and five <iron> <chemical atoms>, with net <electric charge> $+17e$. The $\mathrm H^+$ notation represents the acidic aqueous <proton> inventory; it does not imply bare <protons> moving independently of <water>. These <half-reactions> and their sum are the acidic balance used in the documented <permanganate-iron titration>.<Iron Tablet Determination Analytical Competition (Royal Society of Chemistry resource)>[Iron Tablet Determination Analytical Competition, “Setting the Scene”: both half-reactions and the overall equation]{ref}

\Image[permanganate-iron-electron-balanced-inventory.png]
{id=image-permanganate-iron-electron-balanced-inventory}
{title=<Acidic permanganate-iron balance>: five exchanged <electrons> and conserved <chemical atom> and <electric charge> inventories}
{description=Original exact <stoichiometry> diagram. Matching five formal <electron> slots is a bookkeeping operation, not a picture of free <electrons> in the <chemical solution>. The lower table verifies the net <chemical equation>; its $+17$ is the net <electric charge> number of the written reacting species, not the bulk <chemical solution>.}

The coefficients predict amounts consumed, not a collision of five <ions> or an <elementary reaction>. Bulk <chemical solution> also contains compensating <counterions>, which are omitted from this net <chemical equation>. The <hydrogen> and <oxygen> <oxidation states> are unchanged; balancing their inventories remains essential even though they do not account for the exchanged <electrons>.

= Redox titration
{parent=Redox}

A <redox titration> adds a measured <amount of substance> of one <reactant> to determine another through a known <redox> <chemical equation>. The <burette> reading becomes an amount through <concentration>; the balanced <chemical equation> supplies the amount ratio. A colour endpoint additionally needs a chemical explanation, as in the <permanganate-iron titration>.

= Permanganate-iron titration
{parent=Redox titration}

A proposed modern teaching measurement puts a <pipette>-measured portion of an <iron> $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ <chemical solution> in a <conical flask>, acidifies with excess dilute <sulfuric acid>, and adds standardized <potassium permanganate> from a clamped <burette> while swirling. The documented endpoint principle is that added purple <permanganate> is consumed until the first small excess leaves a pale purple colour; no separate colour indicator is required. Insufficient acidity can produce brown <manganese dioxide>, invalidating the assumed five-<electron> <half-reaction>.<Iron Tablet Determination Analytical Competition (Royal Society of Chemistry resource)>[“Setting the Scene”, final two paragraphs: burette, conical flask, decolourisation, endpoint and excess sulfuric acid]{ref}

Record initial and final <burette> readings; their difference is the delivered <volume (thermodynamics)> $V$. The following controls are proposed additions to test the inference. Run a reagent blank with the same <water>, <sulfuric acid> and endpoint criterion but no sample, obtaining $V_b$. Use a separately established <potassium permanganate> <concentration> $c$, clear the <burette> tip of air, and repeat sample portions. A known added <amount of substance> of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ should increase consumption by one fifth of that amount. A blank estimates reagent demand and the small endpoint excess; it cannot remove an interfering sample <reactant>.

Assuming complete, selective reaction according to the <acidic permanganate-iron balance>, the corrected inference is
$$
n(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+})=5c(V-V_b).
$$
With $c$ in $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, use $V$ and $V_b$ in litres. As an original stipulated calculation, not an experimental result, take $c=0.02000\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, $V=12.60\,\mathrm{mL}$ and $V_b=0.10\,\mathrm{mL}$. Then the <permanganate> consumption is $0.2500\,\mathrm{mmol}$ and the inferred $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ amount is $1.250\,\mathrm{mmol}$. In a $25.00\,\mathrm{mL}$ sample portion this corresponds to $0.05000\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$.

The endpoint detects residual <permanganate>, not the <iron> identity directly. Other sample <reactants> that consume <permanganate> raise the apparent $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ amount; prior <oxidation> of sample $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ lowers it. A recovery check probes response to the added <iron> but does not prove absence of every interference. The readout determines the reacting $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ inventory under the stated conditions, rather than total <iron> in an arbitrary mixture. No measured titres are asserted here.

= Reduction
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Redox}

= Reduction
{synonym}

In the <electrochemistry> examples here, <reduction> is gain of <electrons> by a <chemical species>; it occurs at the <cathode>.

= Hydrolysis
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Hydrolysis> is a <chemical reaction> involving <water> that breaks a <chemical bond>. The <free energy> released by suitably coupled <ATP> <hydrolysis> can drive <active transport>; the available <free energy> depends on the actual composition and conditions.

= Saponification
{parent=Hydrolysis}
{wiki}

<Saponification> is <hydrolysis> of an <ester> under basic conditions. The <conductometric saponification assay> follows <ethyl acetate> consumption by <hydroxide>, producing <ethanol> and an <acetate ion>; its equal-starting-<concentration> case provides a practical <second-order reaction> example.

= Energy coupling
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{title2=$\Delta G_{\mathrm{net}}=\Delta G_1+\Delta G_2$}

<Energy coupling> links an <endergonic reaction> to an <exergonic reaction> through a <reaction mechanism>, so that the combined transformation has a favourable change in <Gibbs free energy>. <ATP> <hydrolysis> is a common driving <chemical reaction>. A shared <reaction intermediate>, produced in one step and consumed in another, can physically connect the transformations; merely placing two independent <chemical reactions> in the same <chemical solution> does not establish <energy coupling>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: coupled reactions and activated intermediates].

\Image[atp-energy-coupling.png]
{title=<Energy coupling>: $+20-30.5=-10.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ under the same stipulated conditions}
{description=Original diagram of cumulative changes in <Gibbs free energy> as two contributions are added. The levels are bookkeeping steps, not a <reaction mechanism>, actual <reaction intermediates>, elapsed time, or <activation energy> barriers. A <reaction mechanism> must enforce the joint transformation for the net favourable value to drive the desired conversion.}

For a computed example, stipulate an <endergonic reaction> $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$ with $\Delta G_1^{\circ\prime}=+20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ under precisely the same biochemical <standard state> conditions as the quoted <ATP> <hydrolysis> value. Assume a <reaction mechanism> couples one <ATP> <hydrolysis> to each A-to-B conversion. The formal sum is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm A&\longrightarrow\mathrm B,
&\Delta G_1^{\circ\prime}&=+20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\\
\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}&\longrightarrow\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i,
&\Delta G_2^{\circ\prime}&=-30.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\\
\mathrm A+\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}&\longrightarrow\mathrm B+\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i,
&\Delta G_{\mathrm{net}}^{\circ\prime}&=-10.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The sum $+20+(-30.5)=-10.5$ gives an <exergonic reaction>. The $+20$ is a chosen teaching value, not a measured property of an identified substance. Outside these reference conditions, add the actual $\Delta G_1$ and $\Delta G_2$, each evaluated using consistent <thermodynamic activities>; mixing a standard value for one <chemical reaction> with an unrelated cellular value for the other gives no justified prediction.

Why do the changes in <Gibbs free energy> add? <Gibbs free energy> is a <state function>. Adding consistent reaction changes cancels any shared <reaction intermediate> and leaves the difference between the combined <reactants> and combined <reaction products>. This explains the thermodynamic accounting; it does not construct the <reaction mechanism>. In many <ATP>-dependent routes, <phosphorylation> transfers a <phosphate> group to a <substrate> or an <enzyme>, and subsequent transformation consumes that modified material. The shared material is what connects the steps. Other <reaction mechanisms> use <ATP> differently. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: ATP-driven biosynthesis].

The useful achievement is that a common regenerated <nucleotide> can connect many different processes in <metabolism>. The net negative change in <Gibbs free energy> permits the coupled conversion, but supplies no <reaction rate>: <enzymes> can lower <activation energy> barriers without changing the endpoint difference. <Energy coupling> changes which overall <chemical reaction> is being considered; <catalysis> accelerates an available route. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.2: free-energy changes and reaction barriers].

A historical landmark is Fritz Lipmann's https://doi.org/10.1002/9780470122464.ch4[1941 essay, Metabolic Generation and Utilization of Phosphate Bond Energy]{ref}. Its sections on the <phosphate> cycle, generation and utilization of phosphate-bond <energy>, and group transfer organized these processes around a reusable chemical carrier. The historical terminology should be read with the modern distinction between net <hydrolysis> and the cost of breaking a <chemical bond>.

= Exergonic reaction
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta G<0$}

An <exergonic reaction> has negative molar reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> under the specified conditions. At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, its forward direction is thermodynamically favourable; its <reaction rate> remains a separate question. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.2: exergonic reactions].

= Endergonic reaction
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta G>0$}

An <endergonic reaction> has positive molar reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> under the specified conditions. Sustained net forward conversion needs a driving process, such as <energy coupling>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.2: endergonic reactions].

= Phosphorylation
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Phosphorylation> attaches a <phosphate> group to a <molecule>. Transfer from <ATP> to a <substrate> or <enzyme> can participate in <energy coupling>; the name alone does not specify the complete <reaction mechanism>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.4: phosphorylation].

= Chemical kinetics
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Chemical kinetics> studies how quickly <chemical reactions> proceed and how their <reaction rates> depend on composition and conditions. It connects a measured change in <concentration> to a <rate law>, then uses a <rate equation> to predict future <concentrations>. This makes it useful for choosing how long to wait for conversion and for testing a proposed <reaction mechanism>. Changing <temperature>, <reactant> <concentrations>, or the presence of a <catalyst> can change <reaction rates>; see https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-2-factors-affecting-reaction-rates[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.2: factors affecting reaction rates].

<Chemical equilibrium> specifies the eventual balance under given constraints; <chemical kinetics> determines how rapidly it is approached. At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, a decrease in <Gibbs free energy> favours conversion but does not guarantee a fast <chemical reaction>: reaching a <transition state> can require crossing a substantial barrier. Thus a mixture can change imperceptibly over an observation period without being at <chemical equilibrium>. The distinction separates which direction is favoured from how long conversion takes. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/12.5 Collision Theory> (§12.5: collision theory and reaction barriers) and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/16-1-spontaneity[Chemistry 2e, §16.1: spontaneity and speed].

= Activation energy
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}
{title2=$E_a$}

= Arrhenius activation energy
{c}
{synonym}

A small increase in <temperature> can produce a large increase in a <reaction rate>. In the simplest <Arrhenius equation> model, warming from $300$ to $310\,\mathrm K$ increases a <rate constant> with $E_a=80\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ by about $2.81$ times, even though the absolute <thermodynamic temperature> rises by only about $3.33\%$. This sensitivity helps explain why cooling can slow unwanted <chemical reactions> and why a hot process can become difficult to control. The comparison assumes the same <rate law> and <reactant> <concentrations>; the <activation energy> characterizes $k(T)$, rather than every change in the measured <reaction rate>.

The physical motivation is the distribution of molecular <energies>. Warming changes the <Boltzmann distribution>, increasing the relative population of energetically costly configurations. A <transition state> can therefore become much more accessible than the modest change in average <kinetic energy> might suggest. The <exponential function> in an <Arrhenius equation> captures such sensitivity in a simple model; it is not a universal formula for the fraction above every barrier in a <Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/12.5 Collision Theory>[§12.5, Activation Energy and the Arrhenius Equation: energy distributions and temperature sensitivity]{ref}

<Activation energy> quantifies how strongly a <rate constant> depends on <thermodynamic temperature>. For a positive <rate constant> $k(T)$, the empirical definition is
$$
E_a(T)=RT^2\frac{d}{dT}\ln\frac{k(T)}{k_{\mathrm{ref}}},
$$
where $R$ is the <molar gas constant> and $k_{\mathrm{ref}}>0$ is a fixed reference with the same units as $k$. The ratio is a <dimensionless quantity>, keeping the <natural logarithm> meaningful without changing its <derivative>. Thus $E_a$ is a molar <energy>, commonly in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$ or $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The <Arrhenius equation> makes $E_a$ constant over its assumed range. This rate-based definition, and the need for care when identifying it with a threshold on a <potential energy surface>, are stated in the <IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/activation energy>.

In a simple barrier picture, reaching a <transition state> costs <energy> relative to the starting <reactants>. The height of that barrier is different from the difference between the <reactants> and <reaction products>. An energetically favourable endpoint can still be reached slowly. An <energy profile (chemistry)> makes the distinction visible, while an <Arrhenius plot> tests temperature sensitivity from <rate constants>. A measured $E_a$ need not equal a barrier read from an arbitrarily chosen diagram.

For a computed example, assume a <first-order reaction> has $k(300\,\mathrm K)=0.100\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, constant $E_a=80\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, and a temperature-independent <pre-exponential factor>. The <Arrhenius equation> gives
$$
\frac{k(310\,\mathrm K)}{k(300\,\mathrm K)}
=\exp\left[\frac{80000}{8.314462618}\left(\frac1{300}-\frac1{310}\right)\right]
\approx2.81398.
$$
Hence $k(310\,\mathrm K)\approx0.281398\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Its <half-life> falls from $\ln2/0.100\approx6.93147\,\mathrm s$ to $\ln2/0.281398\approx2.46322\,\mathrm s$. These are chosen model parameters, not measurements of an identified <chemical reaction>. The exponent uses <kelvin>, not unconverted Celsius temperatures, and $80000\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$ must match $R$ in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$.

\Image[activation-energy-potential-barriers.png]
{id=image-activation-energy-potential-barriers}
{title=Synthetic <potential-energy profiles>: starting levels determine directional <reaction energy barriers>}
{description=Original chosen profiles have <reactant> level $0$, <reaction product> level $-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, and peak levels $80$ and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Forward <reaction energy barriers> are $80$ and $50$; reverse <reaction energy barriers> are $100$ and $70\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The <reaction coordinate> is not time. These curves are illustrative functions, not laboratory measurements or calculated molecular <potential energy surfaces>; their heights do not by themselves specify <Arrhenius activation energies>.}

A microscopic <reaction energy barrier> belongs to a specified path on a <potential energy surface>. An experimental <activation energy> belongs to a specified <rate constant> and its <temperature> dependence. A multi-step <reaction mechanism> can change which step controls the observed conversion, and a temperature-dependent <pre-exponential factor> contributes to the observed <activation energy>. Even the <Gibbs energy of activation> of <transition state theory> is a different quantity: it incorporates <entropy> as well as <enthalpy> and depends on the <standard states>. These distinctions prevent a fitted <Arrhenius plot> from being mistaken for a direct microscopic energy measurement.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/activation energy>{ref}

<Catalysis> illustrates the separation between a path and its endpoints. At the same <temperature> and with the same <reactants>, <reaction products>, and <standard states>, a regenerated <catalyst> changes the available <reaction mechanism> and <rate constants>, while the overall standard change in <Gibbs free energy> and the <equilibrium constant> remain the same:
$$
K=\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta_rG^\circ}{RT}\right).
$$
For a reversible <first-order reaction> pair $\mathrm A\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B$, consistency at <chemical equilibrium> requires $k_f/k_r=K$. A model that multiplies only $k_f$ by a catalytic speedup while holding $k_r$ fixed would change this ratio and misrepresent <catalysis>. This simple ratio applies to that specified <rate law>; a multi-step catalytic <reaction mechanism> needs its own rate equations. A <potential-energy profile> alone cannot determine $K$, because its endpoint difference is not generally the standard change in <Gibbs free energy>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/13.3 Shifting Equilibria: Le Châtelier’s Principle>[§13.3, Effect of a Catalyst: faster equilibration without changing equilibrium composition]{ref}

= Apparent activation energy
{parent=Activation energy}
{title2=$E_{a,\mathrm{app}}(T)=-R\,d\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})/d(1/T)$}

An <apparent activation energy> reports the <temperature> sensitivity of an observed <rate constant>, without assuming that it equals one microscopic <reaction energy barrier>. The <chain rule> converts the empirical <activation energy> definition into the local slope of an <Arrhenius plot>:
$$
E_{a,\mathrm{app}}(T)=-R\frac{d\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})}{d(1/T)}.
$$
The fixed reference $k_{\mathrm{ref}}$ keeps the <natural logarithm> dimensionless. A fitted straight-line slope estimates this quantity over the chosen <temperature> interval; curvature means that one number does not describe every local slope.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/activation energy>{ref}

For the explicit model $k(T)=A(T)\exp[-E_0/(RT)]$ with constant exponent parameter $E_0$, differentiation gives
$$
E_{a,\mathrm{app}}(T)=E_0+RT^2\frac{d}{dT}\ln\frac{A(T)}{A(T_*)},
$$
where $T_*$ is fixed. The second term demonstrates why a microscopic interpretation needs more than a slope. If $E_0$ were itself allowed to depend on <temperature>, differentiation would also contribute $-T\,dE_0/dT$; simply inserting a variable $E_0(T)$ and naming it the measured <activation energy> is generally incorrect.

An instructive <transition state theory> example for an effective <first-order reaction> is
$$
k(T)=\frac{k_{\mathrm B}T}{h}\exp\left(\frac{\Delta S^{\ddagger\circ}}{R}\right)\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta H^{\ddagger\circ}}{RT}\right).
$$
Here $k_{\mathrm B}$ and $h$ are the <Boltzmann constant> and <Planck constant>; $\Delta S^{\ddagger\circ}$ is the <entropy of activation>, and $\Delta H^{\ddagger\circ}$ the <enthalpy of activation>. With those two quantities constant over the chosen range and successful passage assumed, direct differentiation gives $E_{a,\mathrm{app}}=\Delta H^{\ddagger\circ}+RT$, while the <Gibbs energy of activation> is $\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}=\Delta H^{\ddagger\circ}-T\Delta S^{\ddagger\circ}$. These are three distinguishable quantities. The stated <first-order reaction> and fixed <standard states> matter when comparing these formulas with measurements.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): E entries>{ref}

= Arrhenius equation
{c}
{parent=Activation energy}
{wiki}
{title2=$k(T)=A\exp[-E_a/(RT)]$}

The <Arrhenius equation> models the <temperature> dependence of a <rate constant> by
$$
k(T)=A\exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{RT}\right),\qquad A>0,\quad T>0.
$$
Here $A$ is the <pre-exponential factor>, $E_a$ the <activation energy>, $R$ the <molar gas constant>, and $T$ the absolute <thermodynamic temperature>. In its original form, $A$ and $E_a$ are held constant over the range being modeled. It is an empirical relationship, not a universal rule for every <reaction mechanism>. See the <IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/Arrhenius equation>.

Eliminating $A$ between two positive <temperatures> gives the useful prediction
$$
\ln\frac{k(T_2)}{k(T_1)}
=\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac1{T_1}-\frac1{T_2}\right).
$$
For $E_a>0$, warming increases $k$. The logarithmic sensitivity is $d\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})/dT=E_a/(RT^2)$, so there is no universal doubling per ten degrees. The same $10\,\mathrm K$ rise from $300$ to $310\,\mathrm K$ gives a factor $1.67749$ for $E_a=40\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, versus $2.81398$ for $80\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.

\Image[arrhenius-temperature-sensitivity.png]
{title=Temperature sensitivity and straight-line <Arrhenius plots> for two illustrative <activation energies>}
{description=Both models are anchored at $k(300\,\mathrm K)=0.100\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, so they have different <pre-exponential factors>. Left: the higher <activation energy> produces a larger relative change with <temperature>. Right: the <natural logarithm> of the dimensionless ratio $k(T)/k(300\,\mathrm K)$ is linear in inverse <temperature>. These exact model curves are not experimental data.}

The useful simplification is that a strong change in <reaction rate> becomes a straight-line relation after changing variables. Measurements at several <temperatures> can estimate the slope and test whether one pair of constants describes the chosen range. Curvature can indicate a failure of that approximation; fitting a line does not uniquely establish a microscopic <reaction mechanism>.

Svante Arrhenius's 1889 paper on <sucrose> conversion through <catalysis> by a <chemical acid> compared the strong observed <temperature> dependence with the much smaller increase in collision frequency. He discussed van't Hoff's earlier <temperature> relations and proposed a small population of hypothetical active <sucrose>, replenished from ordinary <sucrose>, to interpret the dependence. The inspected translated excerpt derives an inverse-<temperature> relation in its equations (6)–(7) and then applies it to the <reaction rate>. That historical hypothesis is distinct from a modern <transition state> description. See the <On the Reaction Velocity of the Inversion of Cane Sugar by Acids>. The excerpt omits parts of the original paper; it does not provide a complete original apparatus description or dataset.

The <pre-exponential factor> must have the same units as the <rate constant>, since the <exponential function> supplies a <dimensionless quantity>. For a <rate law> $v=k[\mathrm A]^m$, both $A$ and $k$ have units $(\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}})^{1-m}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Thus a <first-order reaction> uses $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, whereas a <second-order reaction> uses $\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. Calling $A$ a frequency factor does not give it frequency units for every <order of reaction>. Its value includes the physical and geometrical factors allowed by the chosen <reaction mechanism>; equal <activation energies> need not mean equal <reaction rates>.

\Image[arrhenius-apparent-energy-and-prefactor.png]
{id=image-arrhenius-apparent-energy-and-prefactor}
{title=Synthetic <Arrhenius plots>: a constant <pre-exponential factor> and a temperature-dependent <pre-exponential factor>}
{description=Both chosen <first-order reaction> models have $k(300\,\mathrm K)=0.100\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and the same exponent parameter $E_0=60\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Blue uses a constant <pre-exponential factor>; orange multiplies it by $(T/300\,\mathrm K)^2$. All markers are synthetic evaluations, not observations. Left: <natural logarithm> of $k/k(300\,\mathrm K)$ against $1000\,\mathrm K/T$. Right: the corresponding <apparent activation energies>, $60$ and $60+2RT/1000$ in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The dashed orange tangent at $300\,\mathrm K$ has numerical slope $-7.81634$ on the displayed horizontal scale.}

To read the blue <Arrhenius plot>, define the <dimensionless quantity> $X=1000\,\mathrm K/T$ and $y=\ln[k/k(300\,\mathrm K)]$. Its numerical slope is $m_X=-60\,000/(1000R)\approx-7.21634$, giving $E_a=-(1000\,\mathrm K)Rm_X=60\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The slope against $1/T$, by contrast, is $-7216.34\,\mathrm K$. The orange tangent at $300\,\mathrm K$ gives $E_{a,\mathrm{app}}\approx64.9887\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$; its finite-interval slope between $280$ and $340\,\mathrm K$ gives about $65.1227\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The distinction between a local <derivative> and a fitted interval matters even with perfectly noise-free synthetic evaluations.

Two measured positive <rate constants> give a two-point estimate
$$
E_{a,\mathrm{fit}}=R\frac{\ln[k(T_2)/k(T_1)]}{1/T_1-1/T_2}.
$$
Several <temperatures> let <linear regression> test the constant-parameter approximation: inspect the <regression residuals>, keep the same <rate law> and composition convention, and account for uncertainty in both $k$ and $T$. A straight line over a short range supports a useful local approximation; it does not uniquely identify a <reaction mechanism>. A fixed change of units or of $k_{\mathrm{ref}}$ shifts the intercept without changing the inferred <activation energy>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/12.5 Collision Theory>[§12.5, Example 12.13 and alternate two-point approach: estimating activation energy from a slope]{ref}

= Pre-exponential factor
{parent=Arrhenius equation}
{wiki}
{title2=$A$}

= Arrhenius prefactor
{c}
{synonym}

= Frequency factor
{synonym}

The <pre-exponential factor> multiplies the <exponential function> in the <Arrhenius equation>. It has the same units as the <rate constant>, so for a <first-order reaction> both have units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Its physical interpretation depends on the <reaction mechanism>; an assumed constant $A$ should not be identified with a universal collision frequency. Comparing <activation energies> alone does not rank absolute <rate constants> when the <pre-exponential factors> differ. See the <IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/pre-exponential factor>.

= Arrhenius plot
{c}
{parent=Arrhenius equation}
{wiki}
{title2=$\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})\ \text{against}\ 1/T$}

An <Arrhenius plot> places $\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})$ against $1/T$, using a fixed reference <rate constant> with the same units as $k$. Under the constant-parameter <Arrhenius equation>,
$$
\ln\frac{k(T)}{k_{\mathrm{ref}}}
=\ln\frac{A}{k_{\mathrm{ref}}}-\frac{E_a}{R}\frac1T.
$$
The slope is $-E_a/R$ and the intercept is $\ln(A/k_{\mathrm{ref}})$. Changing the reference units shifts the intercept but leaves the slope unchanged. If the horizontal coordinate is displayed in $10^{-3}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, its numerical slope must be rescaled before multiplying by $-R$. A temperature-dependent <pre-exponential factor> or <activation energy> can make the plot curved.

= Modified Arrhenius equation
{c}
{parent=Arrhenius equation}
{title2=$k(T)=A_* (T/T_*)^n\exp[-E_0/(RT)]$}

The <modified Arrhenius equation> allows a power of <thermodynamic temperature> in the <pre-exponential factor>:
$$
k(T)=A_*\left(\frac{T}{T_*}\right)^n\exp\left(-\frac{E_0}{RT}\right),
\qquad T_*>0.
$$
Here $A_*$, $E_0$, $n$, and $T_*$ are constant model parameters. Using the <dimensionless quantity> $T/T_*$ leaves $A_*$ with the same units as $k$; in the equivalent convention $BT^n\exp[-E_0/(RT)]$, $B$ instead has units of $k$ multiplied by $\mathrm K^{-n}$. The power exponent $n$ here is not an <order of reaction>. The IUPAC definition uses that equivalent $BT^n$ convention.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/modified Arrhenius equation>{ref}

Applying the <apparent activation energy> definition yields the original short calculation
$$
\frac{d\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})}{dT}=\frac nT+\frac{E_0}{RT^2},
\qquad E_{a,\mathrm{app}}(T)=E_0+nRT.
$$
For the orange synthetic <Arrhenius plot>, $n=2$, $T_*=300\,\mathrm K$, and $E_0=60\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Its <pre-exponential factor> changes by $(310/300)^2\approx1.06778$ from $300$ to $310\,\mathrm K$; combined with the <exponential function>, $k(310\,\mathrm K)\approx0.231991\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, compared with $0.217266\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ for the constant-<pre-exponential-factor> model. The diagram uses those models only to reveal the difference between an exponent parameter and a slope-derived <activation energy>.

= Conductometric determination of activation energy
{parent=Arrhenius equation}

A concrete laboratory route to an <Arrhenius plot> is to infer a <rate constant> at each controlled <temperature> from a changing <electrical conductivity>. The Technical University of Munich's <Laboratory Course in Physical Chemistry for Fundamental Studies: Experiments Manual/Activation Energy of a First-Order Reaction> supplies a teaching protocol, rather than a published set of measured <rate constants>.

The manual follows <tertiary amylbromide> conversion, which produces conducting <ions>. A measuring cell connects to a thermostat; an immersed <electrical conductivity> probe also measures solution <temperature>, and a magnetic stirrer mixes it. Use $80\,\mathrm{mL}$ of $80\%$ by volume <ethanol> in <water>, equilibrate, then add $0.5\,\mathrm{mL}$ of <tertiary amylbromide>. Record the baseline before addition and set time zero at addition. Repeat with fresh mixtures at $25$, $30$, and $35\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, each within $\pm0.5\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$; record for $30$ minutes at the first two <temperatures> and until completion at the last. Clean the cell between runs and keep the added volume fixed. The completed $35\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ mixture supplies endpoint <electrical conductivity> measurements at the other <temperatures>: one endpoint reading cannot be used unchanged at every <temperature>.<Laboratory Course in Physical Chemistry for Fundamental Studies: Experiments Manual/Activation Energy of a First-Order Reaction>[pp. 82–84, §§8.2.5–8.3.2, and p. 85, §8.5.1: reaction, apparatus, temperatures, endpoint and baseline]{ref}

To see what is inferred from the readout, let $\sigma_0$, $\sigma(t)$, and $\sigma_\infty$ be the baseline, current, and completed-mixture <electrical conductivities> at the same <temperature>. Assume their baseline-subtracted changes are proportional to converted <concentration>. Combining that measurement model with a <first-order reaction> gives
$$
f(t)=\frac{\sigma_\infty-\sigma(t)}{\sigma_\infty-\sigma_0}
=\frac{c(t)}{c_0}=e^{-kt},
\qquad -\ln f(t)=kt.
$$
Thus the <electrical conductivity> increases toward an endpoint, while the remaining <reactant> fraction decreases. Fit $-\ln f$ against time to obtain $k$; then fit $\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})$ against $1/T$ in <kelvin> to estimate an <apparent activation energy>. This baseline-aware derivation states the measurement assumptions explicitly; it does not infer a <rate constant> from the steepness of a raw conductivity trace alone.

The controls distinguish a faster <chemical reaction> from a larger instrumental response. Measure the actual solution <temperature>, retain the same <solvent> composition and initial <reactant> <concentration>, and compare endpoint and baseline at that <temperature>. As further diagnostic checks, repeat runs and inspect <regression residuals> for departure from a <first-order reaction>. These checks follow from the model: <electrical conductivity> also depends on how <ions> move, a baseline drift can mimic conversion, and a mixing delay can distort early points. Near completion, a small endpoint error produces a large relative error in $f$ and its <natural logarithm>. A successful fit supports an apparent rate model for this <solvent> and interval; it does not directly image a <transition state> or identify a unique <reaction mechanism>. No measured $k$ or $E_a$ is supplied here.

= Reaction mechanism
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}

A <reaction mechanism> proposes the sequence of <elementary reactions> that produces an overall <chemical reaction>. Its predicted <rate law> must agree with observations; matching one <rate law> need not uniquely identify a <reaction mechanism>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-6-reaction-mechanisms[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.6: reaction mechanisms].

= Reaction intermediate
{parent=Reaction mechanism}
{wiki}

A <reaction intermediate> is formed in one step of a <reaction mechanism> and consumed in a later step, cancelling from the net <chemical equation>. A <reaction intermediate> can connect the transformations in <energy coupling>; it is distinct from a <transition state>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/I03096[IUPAC Gold Book: intermediate].

= Reaction coordinate
{parent=Reaction mechanism}
{wiki}
{title2=$q$}

A <reaction coordinate> parametrizes changes in molecular configuration along a <chemical reaction> path. It can involve a <bond length>, a <bond angle>, or a combination of geometric changes. It is not elapsed time and is not the bulk <extent of reaction>: one describes configurations, while the other accounts for how much material has converted. The precise choice matters when interpreting an <energy profile (chemistry)>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/R05168[IUPAC Gold Book: reaction coordinate].

= Potential energy surface
{parent=Reaction coordinate}
{wiki}
{title2=$V(\mathbf R_1,\ldots,\mathbf R_N)$}

A <potential energy surface> assigns <potential energy> to the configurations of the reacting <chemical atoms>, for a specified electronic description. A <reaction coordinate> selects a path through this many-dimensional configuration space; a <potential-energy profile> then records the <potential energy> along that path. Different paths can share endpoints but cross different barriers. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04780[IUPAC Gold Book: potential-energy reaction surface].

= Energy profile
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Reaction coordinate}
{wiki}

= Reaction energy profile
{synonym}

An <energy profile (chemistry)> plots an explicitly specified <energy> against a <reaction coordinate>. Identify whether the vertical axis is <potential energy>, <enthalpy>, or <Gibbs free energy>; these descriptions are related but not interchangeable. A <potential-energy profile> describes a path through configurations, while a free-energy description also accounts for the relevant thermodynamic conditions.

\Image[reaction-barriers-and-endpoints.png]
{title=Two schematic <potential-energy profiles> share endpoints but have different barriers}
{description=The reactant level is $0$, the product level is $-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, and the two maxima are $80$ and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. These chosen curves illustrate endpoint differences and barrier heights; they are not measured surfaces, trajectories in time, or a complete catalytic cycle.}

For the two chosen profiles, the <reaction energy barriers> from the <reactants> are $80$ and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. From the <reaction products>, the same maxima are $100$ and $70\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ higher. Both paths have endpoint difference $-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The barrier is measured from the relevant starting endpoint, not from an arbitrary zero of <potential energy>.

The appeal of the diagram is that it separates two useful questions: which endpoint is lower, and which path is easier to traverse? <Catalysis> changes the route through <transition states> while retaining the endpoint thermodynamics of the same overall <chemical reaction>. A real catalytic route may have several steps and <reaction intermediates>; the two simple curves do not specify such a <reaction mechanism>. The plotted potential-energy difference alone is not an <equilibrium constant> or a calculated <rate constant>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis[Chemistry 2e, 12.7: alternative reaction routes and catalysis].

= Potential-energy profile
{parent=Energy profile (chemistry)}

A <potential-energy profile> plots the <potential energy> of the reacting system along a specified path from <reactants> through a <transition state> to <reaction products>. It is a particular kind of <energy profile (chemistry)>. For an <elementary reaction>, a suitable <reaction coordinate> parametrizes the path; a multi-step <reaction mechanism> needs the coordinates of its successive steps. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04779[IUPAC Gold Book: potential-energy profile].

= Reaction energy barrier
{parent=Energy profile (chemistry)}
{title2=$\Delta E^\ddagger=E_{\mathrm{peak}}-E_{\mathrm{start}}$}

A <reaction energy barrier> is the height of a specified peak relative to the starting state in an <energy profile (chemistry)>. State which <energy> is used and which direction is being considered. A schematic barrier is not automatically the empirical <activation energy> obtained from the temperature dependence of a <rate constant>.

= Elementary reaction
{parent=Reaction mechanism}
{wiki}

An <elementary reaction> is a single step in a <reaction mechanism>. The <law of mass action> models its directional <reaction rate> using <reactant> <concentrations> raised to the step's stoichiometric multiplicities. Applying that rule requires evidence that the step is elementary; a net <chemical equation> alone supplies no such evidence.

= Molecularity
{parent=Elementary reaction}
{wiki}

<Molecularity> counts the reacting entities in one <elementary reaction>: one for a unimolecular step, two for a bimolecular step. An entity can be a <molecule>, <ion>, or <chemical atom>. <Solvent> entities involved only through <solvation> are excluded. <Molecularity> describes a microscopic event; <order of reaction> describes the <concentration> dependence of a <rate law>. Assigning <molecularity> to a net multi-step <chemical equation> is therefore inappropriate.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): M entries by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1994, entry “molecularity”: elementary event and exclusion of solvation medium]{ref}

= Catalysis
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}

<Catalysis> accelerates a <chemical reaction> by providing an alternative <reaction mechanism> with a more accessible barrier through its <transition states>. A <catalyst> participates in the route and is regenerated. At fixed <temperature>, <catalysis> changes how quickly <chemical equilibrium> is reached without changing the <equilibrium constant>; it does not make an unfavourable overall change in <Gibbs free energy> favourable. <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others>[§12.7, opening definition and Figure 12.19: alternative mechanisms and regeneration]{ref}<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/13.3 Shifting Equilibria: Le Châtelier’s Principle>[§13.3, Effect of a Catalyst: equilibrium composition is unchanged]{ref}

In his 1909 lecture, <Wilhelm Ostwald> recalled the introduction of the term <catalysis> by <Jöns Jacob Berzelius> and emphasized measurable acceleration rather than merely naming an unexplained force. He also discussed <reaction intermediates> and participation of the <catalyst> in the steps whose sum gives the net <chemical reaction>. This is a participant's retrospective account, not evidence that every catalytic <reaction mechanism> is the same.<Nobel Lectures, Chemistry 1901–1921/On Catalysis>[On Catalysis, lecture dated 12 December 1909; paragraphs beginning “The factor which Berzelius regarded” and “It is appropriate here”; editorial footnote says the lecture was reconstructed from notes]{ref}

= Catalytic cycle
{parent=Catalysis}
{wiki}

A <catalytic cycle> follows the changing forms of a <catalyst> through a <reaction mechanism> until its starting form is regenerated. The schematic binding cycle in <catalyst> illustrates the bookkeeping: C is consumed and recovered, whereas AC is a <reaction intermediate> formed and consumed. Cancelling both gives the net A-to-B <chemical equation>. Regeneration concerns the completed cycle; it does not imply that C avoids forming <chemical bonds> or exchanging <electrons>. An alternative <reaction mechanism> may have more <elementary reactions> than the uncatalysed route and still be faster.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others>[§12.7, Figure 12.19 and Homogeneous Catalysts: alternative steps, intermediate formation and catalyst regeneration]{ref}

For that reversible binding cycle, ideal <thermodynamic activities> give step <equilibrium constants> $K_1=a_{\mathrm{AC}}/(a_{\mathrm A}a_{\mathrm C})$ and $K_2=a_{\mathrm B}a_{\mathrm C}/a_{\mathrm{AC}}$. Their <product> is $K_1K_2=a_{\mathrm B}/a_{\mathrm A}=K$: the same cancellations occur in the equilibrium bookkeeping. Choose consistent <standard states> for the species. A proposed cycle with $K_1K_2\ne K$ for the same net conversion at the same <temperature> would contradict its specified <thermodynamics>. This statement concerns free-species <thermodynamic activities>. Appreciable binding in AC can change how the conserved total amount is partitioned among free A, free B and bound AC. An assay counting bound and free material together therefore needs that additional balance; unchanged $a_{\mathrm B}/a_{\mathrm A}$ does not by itself guarantee identical analytical totals when the amount of C changes.

= Catalytic barriers and reaction free energy
{parent=Catalysis}

A directional <Gibbs energy of activation> measures the climb from a starting state to a <transition state>. The net standard reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> measures the difference between endpoints. Changing the climb can strongly change a <rate constant> while leaving that endpoint difference intact. For two illustrative single-barrier routes with common endpoint levels $G_{\mathrm A}^\circ$ and $G_{\mathrm B}^\circ$,
$$
\Delta G_f^{\ddagger\circ}=G_{\mathrm{TS}}^\circ-G_{\mathrm A}^\circ,
\qquad
\Delta G_r^{\ddagger\circ}=G_{\mathrm{TS}}^\circ-G_{\mathrm B}^\circ,
\qquad
\Delta G_f^{\ddagger\circ}-\Delta G_r^{\ddagger\circ}=\Delta_rG^\circ.
$$
Lowering the common <transition state> level lowers both directional <Gibbs energies of activation>; it does not lower just the forward climb. The usual statement that a <catalyst> lowers an <activation energy> describes a kinetic route, not a change in the net <Gibbs free energy>. An <activation energy> inferred from an <Arrhenius plot> is also distinct from a <Gibbs energy of activation> at one <temperature>.

\Image[catalysis-barriers-and-net-free-energy.png]
{id=image-catalysis-barriers-and-net-free-energy}
{title=Illustrative <catalysis> schematic: different barriers, identical endpoint <Gibbs free energy> difference}
{description=Chosen levels in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$: A plus regenerated C at $0$, B plus regenerated C at $-3$, and schematic maxima at $12$ and $7$. Forward barriers are $12$ and $7$; reverse barriers are $15$ and $10$. Neither curve is a measured molecular path. The <reaction coordinate> is not time. These values illustrate the barrier bookkeeping and do not parametrize the separate <reversible catalytic relaxation> example.}

With a common prefactor in <transition state theory>, subtracting the directional barriers yields
$$
\frac{k_f}{k_r}=\exp\!\left(-\frac{\Delta_rG^\circ}{RT}\right)=K,
$$
where $R$ is the <molar gas constant> and $T$ is the absolute <temperature>. This particular <rate-constant> ratio requires the simple compatible directional <rate laws> and consistent <standard states>; a general multistep <reaction mechanism> does not supply two arbitrary measured <rate constants> whose ratio must equal $K$. At fixed <temperature> a <catalyst> preserves $K$ for the same net conversion. The actual reaction change in <Gibbs free energy>, $\Delta_rG=\Delta_rG^\circ+RT\ln Q$, is likewise unchanged at the same <reaction quotient> $Q$. Composition changes during the faster approach to <chemical equilibrium>, so comparing mixtures at the same elapsed time need not compare the same $Q$.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/13.3 Shifting Equilibria: Le Châtelier’s Principle>[§13.3, Effect of a Catalyst: both directional rates accelerate while equilibrium composition is unchanged]{ref}

= Reversible catalytic relaxation
{parent=Catalysis}
{title2=$x(t)=x_{\mathrm{eq}}+(x_0-x_{\mathrm{eq}})e^{-t/\tau}$}

Here is an explicitly stipulated elementary kinetic model, not experimental data. A well-mixed ideal <closed system> has constant <temperature> and <volume>. Alongside an uncatalysed <elementary reaction>, allow an illustrative catalytic encounter that returns C immediately:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm A&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B,
& k_f^u&=0.030\,\mathrm{s^{-1}},
& k_r^u&=0.010\,\mathrm{s^{-1}},\\
\mathrm A+\mathrm C&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B+\mathrm C,
& \alpha_f&=270\,\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}},
& \alpha_r&=90\,\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Both channels obey the <law of mass action>. In the second, the forward <reaction rate> is $\alpha_f[\mathrm A][\mathrm C]$ and the reverse <reaction rate> is $\alpha_r[\mathrm B][\mathrm C]$. C cancels in each encounter, so its free <concentration> $c_C$ stays constant without an external supply. This toy <elementary reaction> encodes regeneration without binding; it is not a claim that real <catalytic cycles> universally consist of one encounter. A binding cycle can accumulate a <reaction intermediate>, in which case constant total C does not imply constant free $c_C$.

Adding the parallel <reaction rates> gives effective <first-order reaction> <rate constants>
$$
k_f=k_f^u+\alpha_f c_C,
\qquad k_r=k_r^u+\alpha_r c_C.
$$
Because $k_f^u/k_r^u=\alpha_f/\alpha_r=3$, each channel balances at the same $[\mathrm B]/[\mathrm A]=3$, and so does their sum. At $c_C=0$ the pair is $(0.030,0.010)\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$; at $c_C=0.001\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ it is $(0.300,0.100)\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Both give $K=3$ with ideal <thermodynamic activities> and a common concentration <standard state>. At $T=300\,\mathrm K$ their compatible standard reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> is $\Delta_rG^\circ=-RT\ln3\approx-2.7403\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Choosing an unrelated reverse <rate constant> would break this consistency.

Let $S=[\mathrm A]+[\mathrm B]$ be the conserved <concentration>, and define the <dimensionless quantity> $x=[\mathrm B]/S$. Then
$$
\frac{dx}{dt}=k_f(1-x)-k_rx
=-(k_f+k_r)(x-x_{\mathrm{eq}}),
\qquad
x_{\mathrm{eq}}=\frac{k_f}{k_f+k_r}=\frac34.
$$
Solving this <first-order linear differential equation> gives
$$
x(t)=\frac34+\left(x_0-\frac34\right)e^{-(k_f+k_r)t},
\qquad
\tau=\frac{1}{k_f+k_r}.
$$
The <chemical relaxation time> is $25\,\mathrm s$ without C and $2.5\,\mathrm s$ with the chosen C. From either $x_0=0$ or $x_0=1$, the <exponential decay> of the deviation leads to the same <chemical equilibrium> $x=3/4$. Reducing that deviation to one percent takes $\ln(100)\tau$: about $115.13\,\mathrm s$ versus $11.51\,\mathrm s$.

\Image[catalysis-reversible-equilibrium-relaxation.png]
{id=image-catalysis-reversible-equilibrium-relaxation}
{title=Two initial compositions and two kinetic speeds approach the same <chemical equilibrium>}
{description=Exact curves from the stipulated <reversible catalytic relaxation> model. Solid curves include $0.001\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ of C; dashed curves omit C. Blue starts entirely as A and orange entirely as B. Both panels use the same equations; the right panel resolves the first $15\,\mathrm s$. The common dotted line is $[\mathrm B]/([\mathrm A]+[\mathrm B])=3/4$, not a fitted plateau or an experimental measurement.}

At this <chemical equilibrium>, conversion continues in both directions. For $S=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, the uncatalysed channel has equal forward and reverse <reaction rates> $0.0075\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. Adding C gives total directional <reaction rates> $0.075\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$ each, yet zero net <reaction rate>. Faster molecular exchange and unchanged composition coexist. This exact calculation explains how <catalysis> can matter enormously within an observation window while leaving the final <equilibrium constant> intact.

= Catalytic selectivity
{parent=Catalysis}

<Catalytic selectivity> concerns which <reaction products> form when competing <reaction mechanisms> are available. Faster conversion is valuable when a process would otherwise be impractically slow; a suitable <catalyst> can also favour a useful product within the available time. In an illustrative pair of irreversible <first-order reactions> A to B and A to D, the initial fraction of converted A going to B is $k_B/(k_B+k_D)$. Multiplying both <rate constants> by the same factor changes speed but leaves this fraction unchanged; changing their relative values changes the kinetic product split. This is a finite-time kinetic illustration, not a new <equilibrium constant>.

A <catalyst> need not accelerate every competing <chemical reaction> equally. <Enzymes>, for example, can distinguish <substrates> through their <active sites>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others>[§12.7, Enzyme Structure and Function, Figure 12.25: substrate binding and specificity]{ref} If every relevant reversible channel eventually equilibrates in a <closed system>, its final <chemical equilibrium> still obeys the endpoint <thermodynamics>. Selective acceleration, product removal, or external driving can make an observed product distribution depend on time and operating conditions. A claim of improved yield must specify those conditions.

= Catalyst deactivation
{parent=Catalysis}

<Catalyst deactivation> is loss of catalytic activity during use. Regeneration in an ideal <catalytic cycle> does not rule out a separate damaging <chemical reaction>, blocking of a surface, or physical loss of <catalyst> from the apparatus. A route can also be slowed by <diffusion> to the surface or by accumulation of a <reaction intermediate>. Such effects limit the usefulness of a low schematic <reaction energy barrier>: measured throughput depends on the whole <reaction mechanism> and on transport. Demonstrating regeneration requires more evidence than observing a faster first run. For a recovered solid <catalyst>, a useful further comparison is a second run with fresh <reactants>, matched recovered amount, and the same mixing and <temperature>, alongside a fresh-solid run and a blank. Loss during recovery or residual <reactants> can otherwise imitate <catalyst deactivation> or apparent reuse. This is a proposed control, not an observation reported for the <catalysed peroxide comparison>.

= Homogeneous catalysis
{parent=Catalysis}
{wiki}

In <homogeneous catalysis>, the <catalyst> and <reactants> occupy the same <thermodynamic phase>.

= Heterogeneous catalysis
{parent=Catalysis}
{wiki}

In <heterogeneous catalysis>, the <catalyst> and <reactants> occupy different <thermodynamic phases>; a solid surface can furnish the catalytic route.

= Catalysed peroxide comparison
{parent=Heterogeneous catalysis}

A simple motivating comparison is visible gas evolution from <hydrogen peroxide>, whose net <chemical equation> is
$$
2\mathrm{H_2O_2}\longrightarrow2\mathrm{H_2O}+\mathrm{O_2}.
$$
The products are <water> and <oxygen>. The Royal Society of Chemistry's teaching resource uses small test tubes and droppers, <hydrogen peroxide> labelled 5–10 volumes, solutions of metal salts including <copper(II) sulfate>, and approximately $1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <sodium hydroxide>. Its qualitative procedure tests additions singly and in pairs to tubes containing about $1\,\mathrm{cm}$ depth of <hydrogen peroxide>. It reports the expected classroom result that the additions individually are not effective under those conditions, whereas the solids produced by mixing metal-salt solution with <sodium hydroxide> give effective <heterogeneous catalysis>. This is the resource's expected observation, not a newly measured ranking.<The best catalyst to decompose hydrogen peroxide by Royal Society of Chemistry>[Resources and Results: small test tubes, droppers, peroxide strength, single versus paired additions, visual readout and expected precipitate effect]{ref}

A focused adaptation compares four matched tubes: no addition, <copper(II) sulfate> alone, <sodium hydroxide> alone, and the pair. Use equal initial <hydrogen peroxide> amounts, identical tubes and the same starting <temperature>. Record stock <concentrations> and added volumes; add <water> to the other tubes to match the total added liquid. Additions can first be checked without <hydrogen peroxide> to distinguish <precipitation (chemistry)> from gas evolution. Start a timer at contact, mix consistently, and observe gas evolution over the same interval. These matching controls are proposed improvements to the qualitative resource, not claimed details of its original trials. Follow its supervised chemical-handling requirements; the stated strengths describe its teaching protocol rather than an unrestricted recipe.

The immediate readout is relative bubbling, together with whether a solid appears. More vigorous bubbling in the paired tube supports faster conversion under the comparison conditions; it does not identify the solid's exact composition or establish a unique <reaction mechanism>. Bubble counts are not a calibrated <reaction rate>: bubble sizes, gas retention and warming can differ. For a quantitative extension, use a freely moving <gas syringe> to record collected <volume> $V(t)$ and compare early slopes $dV/dt$ at matched <temperature> and <pressure>; check for leaks and repeat runs. This extension is a measurement proposal, not source-reported gas traces.

The paired treatment also changes <pH> and produces a surface, so the four tubes alone cannot assign the whole effect to one microscopic catalytic step. A filtered-liquid comparison and a separated, washed-solid comparison would help distinguish dissolved and solid contributions, although washing itself can change the active material. More gas at one time establishes neither a changed <equilibrium constant> nor indefinite <catalyst> reuse. The open gas-evolution experiment is especially unsuitable as a demonstration that two closed mixtures reach the same <chemical equilibrium>: the escaping <oxygen> changes the constraints. The reversible model above supplies that separate explanation. No numerical experimental <rate constants>, barrier heights or gas volumes are asserted here.

= Catalyst
{parent=Catalysis}

A <catalyst> makes a <chemical reaction> proceed faster by participating in an alternative <reaction mechanism> and being regenerated. This makes repeated conversion possible: the same <catalyst> can help many successive <reactants> become <reaction products>. An <enzyme> is a biological <catalyst>. The overall standard change in <Gibbs free energy> is unchanged; see the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00876[IUPAC Gold Book: catalyst].

Regeneration does not mean that the <catalyst> sits unchanged throughout every step. For example, a schematic <reaction mechanism> can temporarily bind A to a <catalyst> C in a <reaction intermediate> AC:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm A+\mathrm C&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{AC},\\
\mathrm{AC}&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B+\mathrm C,\\
\text{net:}\qquad\mathrm A&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B.
\end{aligned}
$$
Adding the steps cancels both the <reaction intermediate> AC and the <catalyst> C from the net <chemical equation>. AC is produced and later consumed; C is used and later recovered. These letters describe possible roles, not a measured <reaction mechanism>. The alternative route is useful only if its <chemical kinetics> permits faster conversion. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.7: catalysts and alternative mechanisms].

For a worked barrier example, consider an idealized reversible A-to-B conversion at fixed <temperature> $T=300\,\mathrm K$. Stipulate standard molar <Gibbs free energy> levels $G_{\mathrm A}^\circ=0$ and $G_{\mathrm B}^\circ=-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, with effective <transition state> levels $80$ without a <catalyst> and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ with a <catalyst>. Use the same <standard states> and the same zero for both routes; the catalytic route includes the regenerated C at both endpoints. Unlike the separate <potential-energy profile> example, this illustration uses <Gibbs free energy> at one specified <temperature>.

\Image[catalyst-alternative-reaction-path.png]
{title=A <catalyst> lowers the stipulated <Gibbs energy of activation> in both directions while retaining the same endpoints}
{description=Original smooth schematic <Gibbs free energy> profiles at $300\,\mathrm K$, with levels in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The horizontal <reaction coordinate> describes progress along each schematic route, not time. These are chosen effective barriers, not measured <potential energy surfaces>, actual molecular paths, or the two-step <reaction mechanism> above. A real catalytic route can have several <transition states> and <reaction intermediates>.}

The <Gibbs energy of activation> is measured from the starting endpoint in the chosen direction. Thus the four barriers are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Delta G_{f,\mathrm{uncat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=80-0=80,&
\Delta G_{f,\mathrm{cat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=50-0=50,\\
\Delta G_{r,\mathrm{uncat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=80-(-20)=100,&
\Delta G_{r,\mathrm{cat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=50-(-20)=70,
\end{aligned}
\qquad\text{all in }\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
Both directional barriers fall by $30\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The endpoint difference stays $\Delta_rG^\circ=-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.

To turn those barriers into <rate constants>, use the Eyring form of <transition state theory> for this effective <first-order reaction> pair, with a common prefactor and unit probability of successful passage:
$$
k=\nu\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}}{RT}\right),
\qquad \nu=\frac{k_{\mathrm B}T}{h}.
$$
Here $k_{\mathrm B}$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, $h$ is the <Planck constant>, and $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. The common prefactor cancels from the comparisons. These <Gibbs energies of activation> are not the empirical <activation energy> $E_a$ of the <Arrhenius equation>. With the stipulated value $R=8.314462618\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, the speedup is
$$
\frac{k_{f,\mathrm{cat}}}{k_{f,\mathrm{uncat}}}
=\frac{k_{r,\mathrm{cat}}}{k_{r,\mathrm{uncat}}}
=\exp\left(\frac{30000}{8.314462618\times300}\right)
\approx1.67248\times10^5.
$$
The exponent is a <dimensionless quantity>: convert $30\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ to $30000\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$ before dividing by $RT$. The <exponential function> explains why a modest barrier reduction can have a dramatic kinetic effect. This number follows from the chosen free-energy barriers and common prefactor; it is not a universal catalytic multiplier or a prediction across <temperatures>.

\Image[catalyst-forward-reverse-rate-ratios.png]
{title=Both directional <rate constants> increase by about $1.67\times10^5$, while their forward-to-reverse ratio stays about $3036$}
{description=Original bar plots at $300\,\mathrm K$. Left: base-ten <logarithms> of the <dimensionless> normalized <rate constants> $k/\nu$ for the four stipulated barriers. A less negative value means faster conversion. Right: the <logarithm> of $k_f/k_r$ is identical for the two routes. Absolute units factor out through the common $\nu=k_{\mathrm B}T/h$; no temperature dependence outside this one model point is asserted.}

Assume ideal <thermodynamic activities> with a common concentration <standard state>, and directional <rate laws> $J_f=k_f[\mathrm A]$ and $J_r=k_r[\mathrm B]$. At <chemical equilibrium>, equal <reaction rates> give $K=[\mathrm B]/[\mathrm A]=k_f/k_r$ for this model. The barriers give
$$
K_{\mathrm{uncat}}=\frac{e^{-80000/(RT)}}{e^{-100000/(RT)}}
=e^{20000/(RT)}
=\frac{e^{-50000/(RT)}}{e^{-70000/(RT)}}=K_{\mathrm{cat}}
\approx3035.58.
$$
The <catalyst> speeds up both directions, so it changes how quickly <chemical equilibrium> is approached without changing this <equilibrium constant>. For a general <chemical reaction>, the thermodynamic relation is $K=\exp[-\Delta_rG^\circ/(RT)]$ with consistent <standard states>; an arbitrary multistep <reaction mechanism> need not permit $K=k_f/k_r$ for two measured <rate constants>. At the same <temperature>, <pressure>, and <thermodynamic activities>, a <catalyst> also leaves the actual overall reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> unchanged. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/13.3 Shifting Equilibria: Le Châtelier’s Principle> (§13.3: a catalyst does not shift equilibrium). To drive an unfavourable overall conversion, a process such as <energy coupling> must change the combined transformation.

= Transition state theory
{parent=Catalyst}
{wiki}
{title2=$k=(k_{\mathrm B}T/h)\exp[-\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}/(RT)]$}

<Transition state theory> relates an <elementary reaction>'s <rate constant> to a <Gibbs energy of activation>. The Eyring expression used in the <catalyst> example assumes every passage counted at the <transition state> succeeds. More generally, a factor accounting for unsuccessful passage multiplies it. Comparing barriers alone gives the quoted speedup only when the remaining prefactors agree. Free-energy profiles can depend on <temperature>, so the example's values at $300\,\mathrm K$ do not determine a temperature curve. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/T06470[IUPAC Gold Book: transition state theory].

= Gibbs energy of activation
{c}
{parent=Transition state theory}
{title2=$\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}$}

= Gibbs energies of activation
{c}
{synonym}

= Activation free energy
{synonym}

The <Gibbs energy of activation> is the standard molar <Gibbs free energy> change associated with going from the starting <reactants> to the <transition state> in <transition state theory>. It specifies the directional barrier, rather than the overall endpoint change in <Gibbs free energy>. Use consistent <standard states> when comparing <Gibbs energies of activation> and <rate constants>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/G02631[IUPAC Gold Book: Gibbs energy of activation].

= Enthalpy of activation
{parent=Gibbs energy of activation}
{title2=$\Delta H^{\ddagger\circ}$}

The standard molar <enthalpy> difference between the <transition state> and the starting <reactants>, at the same <temperature> and <pressure>, in <transition state theory>.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): E entries>{ref}

= Entropy of activation
{parent=Gibbs energy of activation}
{title2=$\Delta S^{\ddagger\circ}$}

The standard molar <entropy> difference between the <transition state> and the starting <reactants>, at the same <temperature> and <pressure>, with specified <standard states>, in <transition state theory>.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): E entries>{ref}

= Transition state
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}

A <transition state> describes configurations at the barrier between <reactants> and <reaction products> in an <elementary reaction>. It is a crossing region, rather than a stable substance that accumulates in a bottle. The IUPAC definition characterizes these configurations by equal probabilities of proceeding toward <reactants> or <reaction products>: https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/T06468[IUPAC Gold Book: transition state]{ref}. Lowering the barrier relative to the starting configuration can increase a <reaction rate>, even when the overall change in <Gibbs free energy> is unchanged.

= Reaction rate
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{title2=$v$}
{wiki}

For one <chemical reaction> in a <closed system> of fixed <volume> $V$, with uniform <concentrations> and no competing reactions or appreciable accumulation of unlisted species, choose the <chemical equation>
$$
a\mathrm A+b\mathrm B\longrightarrow c\mathrm C,\qquad a,b,c>0.
$$
Brackets denote <amount of substance> per <volume>, for example $[\mathrm A]=n_{\mathrm A}/V$. The <stoichiometry> gives one instantaneous <reaction rate>
$$
v=-\frac1a\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}
=-\frac1b\frac{d[\mathrm B]}{dt}
=\frac1c\frac{d[\mathrm C]}{dt}
=\frac1V\frac{d\xi}{dt},
$$
where $\xi$ is the existing <extent of reaction>. The minus signs make forward consumption positive; dividing by the coefficients makes measurements of different <chemical species> agree. Units can be $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/R05156[IUPAC Gold Book: rate of reaction] and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-1-chemical-reaction-rates[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.1: chemical reaction rates].

For the illustrative <chemical equation> $2\mathrm A+\mathrm B\longrightarrow3\mathrm C$, a measured $d[\mathrm C]/dt=0.060\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$ implies $v=0.020\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, $d[\mathrm A]/dt=-0.040\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, and $d[\mathrm B]/dt=-0.020\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. These numbers express <stoichiometry>, without supplying a <rate law>.

Each <derivative> is the slope of a <concentration> curve at one instant. Over a finite interval, $-\Delta[\mathrm A]/(a\Delta t)$ instead gives an average <reaction rate>. For A converting reversibly to B, the net <reaction rate> is $v=d[\mathrm B]/dt=J_f-J_r$; both directional <reaction rates> can remain positive when $v=0$ at <chemical equilibrium>.

= Disappearance rate
{parent=Reaction rate}
{title2=$v_{\mathrm A}=-d[\mathrm A]/dt$}

The <disappearance rate> of a <reactant> A is its positive net loss of <concentration> per unit time, $v_{\mathrm A}=-d[\mathrm A]/dt$. For the fixed-<volume>, single-<chemical-reaction> setting of <reaction rate>, consuming $a$ units of A per conversion gives $v_{\mathrm A}=av$. The factor belongs to the <stoichiometry>; stating it is essential when quoting a <rate constant>. A negative net <disappearance rate> means that A is accumulating.

= Rate law
{parent=Reaction rate}

A <rate law> specifies how a <reaction rate> depends on <concentrations> under stated conditions. A common form is
$$
v=k[\mathrm A]^\alpha[\mathrm B]^\beta,
$$
where $k$ is a <rate constant> and the exponents are partial <orders of reaction>. Determine them experimentally, or derive them from a justified <reaction mechanism>. Do not infer $\alpha=a$ and $\beta=b$ from the net <chemical equation>: its coefficients describe consumption ratios, whereas the exponents describe sensitivity to <concentration>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/R05141[IUPAC Gold Book: rate law] and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-3-rate-laws[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.3: rate laws].

A <rate law> becomes a prediction of time evolution when combined with <stoichiometry> in a <rate equation>, for example $d[\mathrm A]/dt=-av$. Agreement with a measured <rate law> is useful evidence for a <reaction mechanism>, but several routes can give the same dependence.

= Integrated rate law
{parent=Rate law}

An <integrated rate law> relates <concentration> to elapsed time after solving a <rate equation> with an <initial condition>. Its validity retains the assumptions of the underlying <rate law>, including fixed <rate constants> and appropriate <stoichiometry>. The <second-order reaction> reciprocal-<concentration> relation is one example; a different <rate equation> generally requires a different <integrated rate law>.<Chemistry 2e>[§12.4, opening paragraph and “Second-Order Reactions”: concentration as a function of elapsed time]{ref}

= Order of reaction
{parent=Rate law}
{title2=$n$}

= Orders of reaction
{synonym}

= Reaction order
{synonym}

In a power-form <rate law> $v=k[\mathrm A]^\alpha[\mathrm B]^\beta$, the partial <orders of reaction> are $\alpha$ and $\beta$, and the overall <order of reaction> is $n=\alpha+\beta$. For example, doubling $[\mathrm A]$ at fixed $[\mathrm B]$ multiplies $v$ by $2^\alpha$: factors of one, two, and four correspond to partial <orders of reaction> zero, one, and two. These exponents can also be fractional or negative. A single constant <order of reaction> need not describe a <rate law> that is not a power form. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/O04322[IUPAC Gold Book: order of reaction].

For a simple irreversible conversion $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$ with <rate equation> $-d[\mathrm A]/dt=k[\mathrm A]^n$, initial <concentration> $A_0>0$, and fixed $k>0$, compare <zero-order reactions>, <first-order reactions>, and <second-order reactions> using the <dimensionless quantities>
$$
X=\frac{[\mathrm A]}{A_0},\qquad \tau=kA_0^{n-1}t,\qquad \frac{dX}{d\tau}=-X^n.
$$
Their integrated curves, starting at $X(0)=1$, are
$$
\begin{aligned}
n=0:&\quad X=1-\tau\quad(0\leq\tau<1),\\
n=1:&\quad X=e^{-\tau},\\
n=2:&\quad X=\frac1{1+\tau}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For the <zero-order reaction>, the plotted extension $X=\max(1-\tau,0)$ stops conversion at depletion. It does not continue to obey $dX/d\tau=-1$ once $X=0$. A negative <concentration> would be unphysical. The <first-order reaction> and <second-order reaction> approach zero without reaching it at finite time in these models.

\Image[reaction-order-concentration-curves.png]
{title=<Dimensionless> <concentration> decays for three <orders of reaction>, with <zero-order reaction> depletion and the corresponding linearizing plots}

The common $\tau$ axis compares curve shapes after each model's own time rescaling, rather than equal laboratory times for an identical numerical $k$. In the lower panels, $X$, $\ln X$, and $1/X$ respectively give straight lines with slopes $-1$, $-1$, and $+1$ for the matching <order of reaction>. The <natural logarithm> uses a <dimensionless quantity>. These transformed plots help test a proposed <rate law>; the <zero-order reaction> line applies only before depletion. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-4-integrated-rate-laws[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.4: integrated rate laws].

= First-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

A <first-order reaction> has a <reaction rate> proportional to one <reactant> <concentration> in the simple single-species case. For an irreversible $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$ in a <closed system> at fixed <volume>, take $[\mathrm A](0)=A_0$, $[\mathrm B](0)=0$, and constant <rate constant> $k>0$. Its <rate equation> and solution are
$$
\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}=-k[\mathrm A],\qquad
[\mathrm A](t)=A_0e^{-kt},\qquad
[\mathrm B](t)=A_0(1-e^{-kt}),\qquad
v(t)=kA_0e^{-kt}.
$$
A short check is enough: differentiating $A_0e^{-kt}$ gives $-kA_0e^{-kt}$, and the two <concentrations> sum to $A_0$, as required by the one-to-one <stoichiometry>. Thus the same <exponential decay> describes both remaining <reactant> and decreasing <reaction rate>. Fixed $k$ means a fixed fractional loss per unit time, not a fixed absolute consumption rate.

Choose illustrative values $A_0=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and $k=0.2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, with $t$ measured in seconds. Then $[\mathrm A]=e^{-0.2t}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, $[\mathrm B]=(1-e^{-0.2t})\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, and $v=0.2e^{-0.2t}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. The <half-life> is
$$
t_{1/2}=\frac{\ln2}{k}=\frac{\ln2}{0.2}\,\mathrm s\approx3.4657\,\mathrm s.
$$
At each successive <half-life>, half the remaining <reactant> is consumed; $t_{1/2}$ is independent of $A_0$ for this <first-order reaction>. At $t=10\,\mathrm s$, the calculated values are $[\mathrm A]\approx0.135335\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, $[\mathrm B]\approx0.864665\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, and $v\approx0.027067\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. These are model predictions for chosen parameters, rather than experimental measurements.

\Image[chemical-first-order-reaction.png]
{title=Illustrative <first-order reaction>: <reactant> loss, <reaction product> formation, and declining <reaction rate> for $k=0.2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$}

The left panel tracks the two <concentrations>; the right tracks their instantaneous conversion <reaction rate>. Their different vertical units matter: $v=k[\mathrm A]$, so the numerical curves need not have the same height. This gives a practical way to translate a measured <concentration> into a forecast of both conversion and waiting time.

= Zero-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

A <zero-order reaction> has a <reaction rate> independent of the relevant <reactant> <concentration> over the range where its <rate law> applies. For $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$, $[\mathrm A]=A_0-kt$ is valid only before depletion at $t=A_0/k$. The constant-consumption model must stop or change there.

= Second-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

A <second-order reaction> has overall <order of reaction> two: a <rate law> may depend on $[\mathrm A]^2$, or on $[\mathrm A][\mathrm B]$ with partial <orders of reaction> one and one. Doubling all relevant <reactant> <concentrations> multiplies the <reaction rate> by four at fixed conditions. This experimentally determined dependence does not establish <molecularity> or a unique <reaction mechanism>. For an <elementary reaction> obeying the <law of mass action>, <molecularity> and overall <order of reaction> agree; a net <chemical equation> alone cannot establish that the conversion is elementary.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): N and O entries by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1994, entry “order of reaction”, final paragraphs: elementary versus stepwise reactions]{ref}

For the single-<concentration> model, explicitly choose the <disappearance rate> convention
$$
-\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}=k_{\mathrm{obs}}[\mathrm A]^2,\qquad [\mathrm A](0)=[\mathrm A]_0>0,\qquad k_{\mathrm{obs}}>0.
$$
Here $k_{\mathrm{obs}}$ is the coefficient for loss of A, held fixed at the specified <temperature> and other conditions. Its subscript labels the chosen observed <disappearance rate>; it does not imply a <pseudo-first-order reaction>. Assume a uniformly mixed <closed system>, fixed <volume>, and no appreciable reverse or competing <chemical reactions>. The <integrated rate law> is
$$
\boxed{\frac1{[\mathrm A](t)}=\frac1{[\mathrm A]_0}+k_{\mathrm{obs}}t},\qquad
[\mathrm A](t)=\frac{[\mathrm A]_0}{1+k_{\mathrm{obs}}[\mathrm A]_0t}.
$$
The reciprocal-<concentration> plot is a straight line with slope $k_{\mathrm{obs}}$ and intercept $1/[\mathrm A]_0$. A brief derivation uses the <chain rule>: $d(1/[\mathrm A])/dt=-[\mathrm A]^{-2}d[\mathrm A]/dt=k_{\mathrm{obs}}$, then applies the <initial condition>. This is a hyperbolic decay, rather than the <exponential decay> of a <first-order reaction>.<Chemistry 2e>[§12.4, “Second-Order Reactions”, integrated equation and straight-line form; Table 12.2]{ref}

As an original prescribed example, choose $[\mathrm A]_0=0.020\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and $k_{\mathrm{obs}}=0.50\,\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. At $t=100\,\mathrm s$, the reciprocal-<concentration> calculation is $50+50=100\,\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}}$, giving $[\mathrm A]=0.010\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. The initial <disappearance rate> is $0.50(0.020)^2=2.0\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$; after $100\,\mathrm s$ it is $5.0\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. Halving the <concentration> quarters the <disappearance rate>.

<Dimensional analysis> checks every formula: $k_{\mathrm{obs}}[\mathrm A]^2$ has units $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, $k_{\mathrm{obs}}t$ has units $\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}}$, and $k_{\mathrm{obs}}[\mathrm A]_0t$ is a <dimensionless quantity>. Changing <concentration> units changes the numerical <rate constant>: the same example is $[\mathrm A]_0=20\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}$, $k_{\mathrm{obs}}=5.0\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m^3\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, giving $[\mathrm A](100\,\mathrm s)=10\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}$.

\Image[second-order-concentration-and-reciprocal-time.png]
{id=image-second-order-concentration-and-reciprocal-time}
{title=<Second-order reaction> <concentration> decay and reciprocal-<concentration> lines for three prescribed starting <concentrations>}
{description=Original model curves, not measured data. All use the <disappearance rate> coefficient $k_{\mathrm{obs}}=0.50\,\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. The reciprocal lines share a slope while their intercepts differ; marked points give each run's first <half-life>.}

For an <elementary reaction> $2\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm P$, instead define the stoichiometrically normalized <reaction rate> $r=k[\mathrm A]^2$. Two A entities disappear per P formed, so
$$
r=-\frac12\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}=\frac{d[\mathrm P]}{dt}=k[\mathrm A]^2,\qquad
-\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}=2k[\mathrm A]^2,\qquad k_{\mathrm{obs}}=2k.
$$
Consequently the reciprocal-<concentration> slope is $2k$, not $k$. In the prescribed example, the normalized <rate constant> would be $k=0.25\,\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$; initially $r=1.0\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$ while the <disappearance rate> is twice that. With $[\mathrm P](0)=0$, <stoichiometry> gives $[\mathrm P](100\,\mathrm s)=([\mathrm A]_0-[\mathrm A])/2=0.0050\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. Both <rate constants> have the same dimensions, so a units check alone cannot catch their factor-of-two difference. The symbol $r$ denotes the same normalization as $v$ in <reaction rate>.

The <half-life>, meaning the time to reach half a specified starting <concentration>, follows by setting $[\mathrm A]=[\mathrm A]_0/2$:
$$
\boxed{t_{1/2}=\frac1{k_{\mathrm{obs}}[\mathrm A]_0}}.
$$
For $r=k[\mathrm A]^2$ in $2\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm P$, this becomes $t_{1/2}=1/(2k[\mathrm A]_0)$. Its units are seconds because $k_{\mathrm{obs}}[\mathrm A]_0$ has units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Doubling the starting <concentration> halves the first <half-life>; a quoted <half-life> needs its starting <concentration>. This differs from a <first-order reaction>, whose <half-life> is independent of starting <concentration>.<Chemistry 2e>[§12.4, “Half-Life of a Reaction”, second-order derivation and Table 12.2]{ref}

Within the prescribed run, $[\mathrm A]$ reaches $0.010$, $0.0050$, and $0.0025\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ at $100$, $300$, and $700\,\mathrm s$. The successive halving intervals are $100$, $200$, and $400\,\mathrm s$: each begins at a smaller <concentration>. More generally, reaching fraction $f$ of the initial <concentration>, with $0<f<1$, requires $t=(f^{-1}-1)/(k_{\mathrm{obs}}[\mathrm A]_0)$, so $90\%$ consumption takes nine initial <half-lives>, rather than a fixed sequence of equal waiting times.

\Image[second-order-halving-intervals-and-initial-concentration.png]
{id=image-second-order-halving-intervals-and-initial-concentration}
{title=Lengthening <second-order reaction> halving intervals and the dependence of the first <half-life> on initial <concentration>}
{description=Original model predictions, not measurements. Left: the prescribed $0.020\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ run. Right: $t_{1/2}=1/(k_{\mathrm{obs}}[\mathrm A]_0)$ at fixed $k_{\mathrm{obs}}=0.50\,\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, compared with a <first-order reaction> chosen to have $t_{1/2}=100\,\mathrm s$. That comparison uses $k_1=\ln2/(100\,\mathrm s)$, with different units from $k_{\mathrm{obs}}$.}

A straight reciprocal-<concentration> plot tests this particular single-<concentration> <rate equation>. It does not uniquely identify a <reaction mechanism>, nor does curvature rule out every <second-order reaction>: two independently changing <reactant> <concentrations> require their own <integrated rate law>. At low <concentration>, a small <measurement error> $\delta[\mathrm A]$ becomes approximately $-\delta[\mathrm A]/[\mathrm A]^2$ in the reciprocal, magnifying late-time errors. Use the line as a diagnostic; a <nonlinear regression> of the original <concentrations> can retain their measurement scale and explicit error model.

= Conductometric saponification assay
{parent=Second-order reaction}

How can an invisible change in <concentration> produce a readable signal? The <saponification> of <ethyl acetate> by <hydroxide> in <water> gives <ethanol> and an <acetate ion>:
$$
\mathrm{CH_3COOC_2H_5}+\mathrm{OH^-}\longrightarrow\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}+\mathrm{C_2H_5OH}.
$$
The <rate law> used for this assay is $r=k_2[\mathrm{ethyl\ acetate}][\mathrm{OH^-}]$. Each <reactant> has coefficient one, so $r$ equals either <disappearance rate>. If the two initial <concentrations> are equal, they remain equal under this <stoichiometry>, giving $-dc/dt=k_2c^2$ and $k_{\mathrm{obs}}=k_2$, without a factor of two.

<Manual for Advanced Physical Chemistry Laboratory-II by Nasima Arshad> (Unit 7, Experiment 2, printed pp. 66–67) specifies a conductivity cell holding about $60\,\mathrm{mL}$ in constant-<temperature> baths, with $0.040\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <ethyl acetate> and <sodium hydroxide> stocks equilibrated separately. Mixing $30\,\mathrm{mL}$ of each initiates the run. The readout is <electrical resistance>: every minute for ten minutes, every two minutes for ten more, then every five minutes for twenty-five. The cell is rinsed with distilled <water>. A matched <sodium hydroxide>–<water> dilution estimates the initial resistance; a <potassium chloride> standard determines the cell constant. The manual asks for comparison with the extrapolated initial signal.<Manual for Advanced Physical Chemistry Laboratory-II by Nasima Arshad>[Unit 7, Experiment 2, printed pp. 66–67: Procedure and Calculations; title page credits Nasima Arshad as manual author]{ref}

The post-mixing initial <concentration> is $c_0=0.040(30/60)=0.020\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ for each <reactant>, assuming additive <volumes>. This matches our illustrative starting <concentration>, but the prescribed $k_{\mathrm{obs}}=0.50$ is not a measured value from the manual. As <hydroxide> is replaced by the less mobile <acetate ion>, <electrical conductance> decreases. The preceding experiment describes two <platinum> <electrodes> and an alternating-voltage <Wheatstone bridge> to limit polarization at the <electrodes>. For measured <electrical resistance> $R(t)$, <electrical conductance> is $G(t)=1/R(t)$; <electrical conductivity> is $\kappa(t)=K_{\mathrm{cell}}G(t)$. Keep the probe geometry fixed and use one signal convention throughout.<Manual for Advanced Physical Chemistry Laboratory-II by Nasima Arshad>[Unit 7, Experiment 1, printed pp. 62–63: reaction, ionic conductances, inverse resistance, platinum electrodes and alternating-voltage bridge; Experiment 2, p. 66: hydroxide replaced by acetate]{ref}

For the equal-starting-<concentration> model, assume an affine calibration between <electrical conductance> and remaining <hydroxide> <concentration>, with endpoints $G_0$ and $G_\infty$ at the same <temperature>. This gives
$$
\frac{c(t)}{c_0}=\frac{G(t)-G_\infty}{G_0-G_\infty},\qquad
\frac{G_0-G(t)}{G(t)-G_\infty}=\frac{c_0-c(t)}{c(t)}=k_2c_0t.
$$
The last plot has slope $k_2c_0$, with units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, whereas plotting $1/c$ against time has slope $k_2$, with units $\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. These are different slopes. This endpoint transformation also shows why raw resistance must not be substituted for conductance.

Useful additional controls are repeat runs, fixed immersion depth, an independently checked final signal, and comparisons of several starting <concentrations>. Mixing delay obscures early conversion; <temperature> drift changes both the <rate constant> and the signal; contamination adds mobile <ions>. The affine calibration can fail when ionic mobilities change with composition. Near completion, $G-G_\infty$ becomes small, so endpoint error is strongly amplified. Those are methodological limits and proposed checks, not new experimental observations. A conductivity trace supports a calibrated <rate law> over its tested range; it does not independently reveal the full <reaction mechanism>.

For unequal starting <concentrations> $a=[\mathrm A]_0$ and $b=[\mathrm B]_0$ in a one-to-one <chemical reaction>, let $x$ be the consumed <concentration>. Then $[\mathrm A]=a-x$, $[\mathrm B]=b-x$, and
$$
\frac{dx}{dt}=k_2(a-x)(b-x),\qquad
\frac1{b-a}\ln\!\left(\frac{a(b-x)}{b(a-x)}\right)=k_2t\quad(a\ne b).
$$
The <natural logarithm> acts on a <dimensionless quantity>; differentiating the left side with respect to $x$ gives $1/((a-x)(b-x))$, verifying the relation. Mixing $20\,\mathrm{mL}$ of the stated <ethyl acetate> stock with $40\,\mathrm{mL}$ of the <sodium hydroxide> stock would give $a=0.01333\ldots$ and $b=0.02666\ldots\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. The difference $[\mathrm B]-[\mathrm A]=b-a$ stays constant, rather than their ratio. Neither the equal-<concentration> reciprocal formula nor a <pseudo-first-order reaction> approximation is justified solely by that twofold excess.

= Pseudo-first-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

In a <second-order reaction> $\mathrm A+\mathrm B\longrightarrow\mathrm P$ with <rate law> $-d[\mathrm A]/dt=k_2[\mathrm A][\mathrm B]$, a large excess of B can keep $[\mathrm B]\approx[\mathrm B]_0$ during the observation window. Then
$$
-\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}\approx k_1[\mathrm A],\qquad k_1=k_2[\mathrm B]_0,\qquad
[\mathrm A](t)\approx[\mathrm A]_0e^{-k_1t}.
$$
This is a <pseudo-first-order reaction>: the approximate <first-order reaction> describes that window, while changing the excess B <concentration> changes $k_1$. Its units are $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, distinct from the <second-order reaction> <rate constant> $k_2$. For one-to-one <stoichiometry>, the maximum fractional depletion of B is $[\mathrm A]_0/[\mathrm B]_0$; an original $100:1$ initial excess bounds its change by $1\%$. A twofold excess permits $50\%$ depletion and does not provide the same control.<Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994): N and O entries by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry>[Glossary of Terms Used in Physical Organic Chemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1994, entry “order of reaction”: constant B, observed coefficient and pseudo-first-order coefficient]{ref}

= Rate constant
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{title2=$k$}

A <rate constant> is the coefficient in a specified <rate law>, held fixed under specified conditions. It can describe an empirical overall <chemical reaction>, as well as an <elementary reaction>. Fixed <temperature> matters: changing <temperature> can change $k$, even for the same <chemical reaction>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/O04322[IUPAC Gold Book: order of reaction and rate constants] and https://old.goldbook.iupac.org/html/R/R05137.html[IUPAC Gold Book: rate coefficient].

For $v=k[\mathrm A]^n$, dimensional consistency requires
$$
\operatorname{units}(k)=(\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}})^{1-n}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}.
$$
A <zero-order reaction> therefore uses $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$; a <first-order reaction> uses $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$; a <second-order reaction> uses $\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. Comparing bare numerical values of $k$ across different <orders of reaction> is meaningless without their units and <concentrations>. Even for the same <order of reaction>, state whether $k$ multiplies the normalized <reaction rate> $v$ or a particular <reactant> disappearance rate: a stoichiometric coefficient can change that convention.

= Protonation
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Chemical equation
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

A <chemical equation> represents a <chemical reaction> using <chemical formulas>, with <reactants> on the left and <reaction products> on the right. A balanced <chemical equation> has the same number of <chemical atoms> of each <chemical element> on both sides. For <molecular hydrogen> and <molecular oxygen> forming <water>,
$$
2\mathrm{H}_2+\mathrm{O}_2\longrightarrow2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}.
$$
The coefficients $2,1,2$ give the relative numbers of <molecules>; the coefficient $1$ is normally omitted. Count <hydrogen> as $2\times2=4$ on the left and $2\times2=4$ on the right, and <oxygen> as $1\times2=2$ on the left and $2\times1=2$ on the right. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-1-writing-and-balancing-chemical-equations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §4.1: writing and balancing chemical equations].

\Image[chemical-equation-atom-balance.png]
{title=Two <molecular hydrogen> <molecules> and one <molecular oxygen> <molecule> account for two <water> <molecules>}
{description=Exactly four <hydrogen> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> occur on each side. The circles and connecting lines are an invented schematic: they indicate grouping and <chemical bonds>, without representing actual <bond lengths>, <bond angles> or a <reaction mechanism>.}

To balance the initial expression $\mathrm H_2+\mathrm O_2\longrightarrow\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, first put $2$ before <water> to match the two <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. This creates four <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> on the right, so put $2$ before <molecular hydrogen>. Changing a coefficient multiplies the entire <chemical formula>. Changing a subscript changes composition and usually the <chemical substance>: $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ is two <water> <molecules>, whereas $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$ is one <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Never balance a <chemical equation> by changing its subscripts.

The bookkeeping follows from conservation of the <atomic nuclei> in ordinary, non-nuclear <chemical reactions>; <chemical bonds> and the distribution of <electrons> can change. For a <chemical equation> involving <ions>, total <electric charge> must also match. For example, the existing <self-ionization of water> equation has zero total <electric charge> on each side, since the $+1$ of <hydronium> cancels the $-1$ of <hydroxide>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/2.6 Ionic and Molecular Compounds> (§2.6: nuclei and ions).

The same $2:1:2$ ratio relates changes in <amount of substance>: two <moles> of <molecular hydrogen> consumed per <mole> of <molecular oxygen> correspond to two <moles> of <water> formed. A <mole> is a defined unit, not one <molecule>; the little whole-number picture is an abstract counting model, not a claim that a macroscopic sample contains just five <molecules>. See <molecular count and amount of substance> for the exact conversion and https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/mole[BIPM, SI base unit: mole] for the definition.

This is why a balanced <chemical equation> is useful: <stoichiometry> turns the same atom accounting into relations between measurable quantities. It does not determine <reaction rate>, the final conversion set by <chemical equilibrium>, or the actual yield of recovered <reaction products>. The <limiting reagent> gives an upper bound under the specified <chemical reaction>.

= Reactant
{parent=Chemical reaction}

A <chemical species> consumed in a <chemical reaction>.

= Reagent
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Product
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Reaction product
{synonym}

= Substrate
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Reaction substrate
{synonym}

A chemical species on which a reagent or catalyst acts in a <chemical reaction>. An <enzyme> acts on a biochemical <substrate>, a particular instance of this broader reaction role.

= Thermal dissociation
{parent=Chemical reaction}

Thermal dissociation is temperature-driven breaking of a molecule into simpler fragments. The <chemical equilibrium> abundance depends on pressure as well as temperature: reactions increasing particle number are generally favored at low pressure. In an <exoplanet atmosphere>, reduced <water> on a very hot dayside does not by itself establish a low planetary oxygen abundance.

= Stoichiometry
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nu_i$}

<Stoichiometry> relates the quantities consumed and formed in a <chemical reaction>. Its coefficients are ratios of entity counts, or equivalently of <amount of substance>, rather than ratios of <masses>. For the <water> <chemical equation>, consuming two <molecular hydrogen> <molecules> per <molecular oxygen> <molecule> gives the same $2:1$ ratio as consuming two <moles> of <molecular hydrogen> per <mole> of <molecular oxygen>. Multiplying every entity count by the same factor preserves the ratio. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-3-reaction-stoichiometry[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, reaction stoichiometry].

Coefficients and subscripts do different jobs. The coefficient in $2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$ counts two <water> <molecules>; the subscript says that each contains two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>. Changing coefficients scales quantities. Changing a subscript changes the specified <chemical substance>.

\Image[stoichiometry-particle-counts.png]
{title=The same stoichiometric ratio at molecular and molar scales}
{description=The upper schematic counts individual <molecules>; the lower line multiplies each count by the <Avogadro constant> times one <mole>. Circle sizes and positions are illustrative.}

This distinction catches a common counting error: one <mole> of <water> <molecules> contains two <moles> of <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and one <mole> of <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. Always specify what is counted.

<Stoichiometry> records reactant and product multiplicities in a <chemical reaction> or a <chemical reaction network>. The change caused by one reaction is its <stoichiometric vector>; multiplying by the reaction rate gives its contribution to the concentration equations. For two identical reactants the rate-constant convention absorbs any combinatorial factor.

= Molecular count and amount of substance
{parent=Stoichiometry}
{title2=$N=nN_{\mathrm A}$}

The molecular count $N$ is a dimensionless integer; the <amount of substance> $n$ has unit <mole>, symbol $\mathrm{mol}$. They satisfy
$$
N=nN_{\mathrm A},\qquad N_{\mathrm A}=6.02214076\times10^{23}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.
$$
The <Avogadro constant> is exact by the modern definition of the <mole>: see https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/mole[BIPM, SI base unit: mole]. Thus two <water> <molecules> correspond to $2/N_{\mathrm A}$ <moles>, whereas two <moles> of <water> contain $1.204428152\times10^{24}$ <molecules>. A <mole> is a unit for counting specified entities, which may also be <chemical atoms>, <ions> or <electrons>.

= Extent of reaction
{parent=Stoichiometry}
{title2=$\xi$}
{wiki}

For a closed system with one specified <chemical reaction>, the <extent of reaction> $\xi$ records progress from $\xi=0$ through $n_i=n_{i,0}+\nu_i\xi$. Here $n_i$ is the <amount of substance> of species $i$, and the signed <stoichiometric vector> has negative entries for <reactants> and positive entries for <reaction products>. For the <water> <chemical equation> in the order $\mathrm{H}_2,\mathrm{O}_2,\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$, it is $(-2,-1,2)$. The normalization of $\xi$ depends on the chosen coefficients. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E02283[IUPAC Gold Book, extent of reaction].

= Limiting reagent
{parent=Stoichiometry}
{wiki}

= Limiting reactant
{synonym}

The <limiting reagent> is the <reactant> exhausted first if the specified <chemical reaction> proceeds to the maximum conversion permitted by <stoichiometry>. With initial values of <amount of substance> $n_{i,0}$ for the <reactants> and positive consumption coefficients $a_i$, the largest forward <extent of reaction> is $\xi_{\max}=\min_i(n_{i,0}/a_i)$. Compare these quotients, rather than simply choosing the smallest initial quantity. This calculation predicts the maximum <reaction product> quantity and identifies which <reactant> supply must increase to raise it. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-4-reaction-yields[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, limiting reactants].

Start with $3\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen>, $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular oxygen> and no <water>. Assume complete conversion by the displayed <chemical reaction>, without competing reactions. Then
$$
\xi_{\max}=\min\left(\frac{3\,\mathrm{mol}}2,\frac{1\,\mathrm{mol}}1\right)=1\,\mathrm{mol}.
$$
The <molecular oxygen> is the <limiting reagent>. Consumption of $2\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen> and $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular oxygen> forms $2\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <water>, leaving $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen>. Initially there are $1.806642228\times10^{24}$ <molecular hydrogen> <molecules> and $6.02214076\times10^{23}$ <molecular oxygen> <molecules>; after conversion there are $1.204428152\times10^{24}$ <water> <molecules> and $6.02214076\times10^{23}$ leftover <molecular hydrogen> <molecules>.

\Image[limiting-reagent-amounts.png]
{title=Oxygen runs out while one mole of molecular hydrogen remains}
{description=The horizontal axis is <extent of reaction>, rather than time. The endpoint assumes complete conversion by one <chemical reaction>; the curves express <stoichiometry>.}

As a check, the initial inventory has $6\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and $2\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. The final <water> contains $4\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and leftover <molecular hydrogen> supplies the remaining $2\,\mathrm{mol}$. The atom totals balance even though the total <amount of substance> of <molecules> falls from four to three <moles>.

= Acid-base chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}

<Acid-base chemistry> studies how <chemical acids> and <chemical bases> exchange <protons> or <electron pairs>. Here the focus is transfer of <protons> in <water>: a <chemical acid> donates a <proton>, and a <chemical base> accepts it. The resulting <chemical equilibrium> determines <pH>, while a <buffer solution> can resist changes in <pH>. This connects microscopic <chemical reactions> with a compact, measurable description of a <chemical solution>.

The important distinction is between how much <chemical acid> is present and how readily it transfers a <proton>. A <weak acid> and a <strong acid> at the same total <concentration> need not have the same <pH>. Conversely, two <buffer solutions> can have the same <pH> while containing very different total quantities of their <chemical acids> and <chemical bases>.

= Isoelectric point
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{pI}$}

The <isoelectric point> is the <pH> at which a specified <molecule>'s mean net <electric charge> is zero. Different <protonation> states can still carry nonzero <electric charges>; their contributions cancel in the average. For the two-step <glycine speciation model>, this gives $\mathrm{pI}=(\mathrm pK_{a1}+\mathrm pK_{a2})/2$. Additional ionizable <amino acid side chains> require their own <chemical equilibrium> calculations; averaging every <pKa> is not a general rule. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.2, discussion following Figure 26.2: isoelectric points and the dissociations surrounding the neutral form]{ref}

Below its <isoelectric point>, free <glycine> has positive mean <electric charge> and an <electric field> drives it toward the negative <electrode>; above its <isoelectric point>, the mean <electric charge> is negative and the direction reverses. At its <isoelectric point> the charge-driven contribution vanishes. <Diffusion> and bulk movement of the <chemical solution> can still move the sample. <Gel electrophoresis> applies this charge dependence to separation, although <protein> size, shape and interactions also affect migration. The <pKa> values of free <glycine> do not determine a <protein>'s <isoelectric point>: its free <N-terminus>, <C-terminus> and ionizable <amino acid side chains> supply a different inventory. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.2, Figure 26.3 and accompanying electrophoresis discussion]{ref}

= Acid
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki=Acid}

= Chemical acid
{synonym}

A <chemical acid> can donate a <proton>, or accept an <electron pair> to form a <covalent bond>. The <pH> and <buffer solution> examples below use the former meaning. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00071[IUPAC Gold Book, acid].

= Strong acid
{parent=Acid (chemistry)}

In <water>, a <strong acid> transfers its available <proton> essentially completely to <water> under the dilute conditions considered here. For the <pH> example, each initial <chemical acid> unit supplies one <hydronium> <ion>. “Strong” describes this conversion, rather than a high <concentration>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/14.3 Relative Strengths of Acids and Bases>.

= Hydrochloric acid
{parent=Strong acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{HCl}(\mathrm{aq})$}

<Hydrochloric acid> is an aqueous <chemical solution> of <hydrogen chloride>. The <glycine titration> model treats its dissociation into <hydronium> and <chloride> as complete, retaining both <ions> in the <electric charge> balance.

= Weak acid
{parent=Acid (chemistry)}

A <weak acid> establishes a <chemical equilibrium> between its proton-donating form and its <conjugate base> in <water>. For a neutral <monoprotic acid> $\mathrm{HA}$, the aqueous <chemical equation> is
$$
\mathrm{HA(aq)}+\mathrm{H_2O(l)}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{H_3O^+(aq)}+\mathrm{A^-(aq)}.
$$
<Water> accepts a <proton> to become <hydronium>; $\mathrm A^-$ is the <conjugate base>. The double arrow denotes reversible exchange at <chemical equilibrium>, not equal populations. The <acid dissociation constant> and composition determine the <hydronium> <concentration>; substituting total <chemical acid> <concentration> into the <pH> formula is generally incorrect.

“Weak” describes the tendency to transfer a <proton> in a specified <solvent>, rather than how much <chemical acid> was dissolved. A <weak acid> can be concentrated, and a <strong acid> can be dilute. Even the same <weak acid> can have a large <weak acid dissociation fraction> after sufficient <dilution>, while its <acid dissociation constant> stays fixed at the same <temperature> and <standard state> conventions.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/14.3 Relative Strengths of Acids and Bases>[§14.3, acid ionization and concentration-dependent percent ionization]{ref}

= Monoprotic acid
{parent=Weak acid}

A <monoprotic acid> supplies one <proton> per acid unit in the <acid-base chemistry> model under consideration. <Acetic acid> is an example. Several successive proton transfers require several <acid dissociation constants> and a larger set of <chemical species>.

= Weak acid dissociation fraction
{parent=Weak acid}
{title2=$\alpha=[\mathrm A^-]/C_T$}

For a <monoprotic acid> with <analytical concentration> $C_T=[\mathrm{HA}]+[\mathrm A^-]$, define the <dimensionless> <weak acid dissociation fraction>
$$
\alpha=\frac{[\mathrm A^-]}{C_T},\qquad 1-\alpha=\frac{[\mathrm{HA}]}{C_T}.
$$
This counts acid units in the <conjugate base> form. Identifying it with $[\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+]/C_T$ additionally requires negligible <self-ionization of water> and no other sources of <electric charge>. In the ideal dilute <chemical solution> model, with $h=[\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+]/c^\circ$, the <acid dissociation constant> gives $\alpha=K_a/(K_a+h)$. Thus lowering <hydronium> <thermodynamic activity> favors the <conjugate base>.

\Image[weak-acid-dissociation-dilution.png]
{id=weak-acid-dilution-equilibrium}
{title=<Dilution> increases the <weak acid dissociation fraction> while raising <pH> in a synthetic aqueous <chemical equilibrium>}

The diagram uses stipulated <dimensionless> <acid dissociation constant> $K_a=10^{-5}$ and <self-ionization of water> ion product $K_w=10^{-14}$ at fixed <temperature>; these are model inputs, not measurements of a named substance. It solves the <weak acid mass and charge balances>. At $C_T=0.010\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, the calculated <weak acid dissociation fraction> is about $3.11\%$ and <pH> about $3.51$; at $C_T=10^{-4}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, they are about $27.0\%$ and $4.57$. More acid units are dissociated as a fraction of the total, yet fewer <hydronium> <ions> occur per <volume (thermodynamics)>.

At extreme <dilution>, <self-ionization of water> makes $h$ approach $\sqrt{K_w}$, so <pH> approaches $7$ for the chosen model. The limiting <weak acid dissociation fraction> is $K_a/(K_a+\sqrt{K_w})\approx0.9901$, rather than exactly one. The calculation assumes pure added <water>, a neutral <monoprotic acid>, and no competing <chemical reactions>; actual contamination becomes increasingly important as $C_T$ falls.

= Weak acid mass and charge balances
{parent=Weak acid}

Consider only neutral $\mathrm{HA}$ added to pure <water>, at fixed <temperature>, with ideal dilute <thermodynamic activities>, <water> <thermodynamic activity> approximated by one, and no added salts or other <chemical species>. Normalize every <concentration> by $c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$:
$$
c=C_T/c^\circ,\qquad h=[\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+]/c^\circ,\qquad x=[\mathrm A^-]/c^\circ,\qquad y=[\mathrm{HA}]/c^\circ,\qquad o=[\mathrm{OH}^-]/c^\circ.
$$
Conservation of acid units and zero net <electric charge>, respectively, require
$$
x+y=c,\qquad h=x+o.
$$
The second equation balances positive <hydronium> against negative <conjugate base> and <hydroxide>. It would need all additional <ions> if salts or a <chemical base> were added. Together with <acid dissociation constant> $K_a=hx/y$ and the <self-ionization of water> relation $K_w=ho$, these give
$$
x=\frac{K_ac}{K_a+h},\qquad y=\frac{hc}{K_a+h},\qquad o=\frac{K_w}{h},\qquad
h-\frac{K_w}{h}-\frac{K_ac}{K_a+h}=0.
$$
This is exact within the stated ideal model, including depletion and <self-ionization of water>. Multiplication by $h(K_a+h)$ yields the <cubic equation>
$$
h^3+K_ah^2-(K_w+K_ac)h-K_aK_w=0.
$$
For positive $K_a,K_w,c$, the unmultiplied left-hand side increases strictly from negative to positive values as $h$ increases from zero to infinity; its <derivative> is $1+K_w/h^2+K_ac/(K_a+h)^2>0$. There is therefore exactly one positive <root of a polynomial>. A <bisection method> can find it without choosing an unphysical <root of a polynomial>.

If $K_w/h^2\ll1$, neglect <hydroxide>, so $h\approx x$ and the <quadratic equation> retaining acid depletion gives
$$
\frac{x^2}{c-x}=K_a,\qquad
x=\frac{\sqrt{K_a^2+4K_ac}-K_a}{2}
=\frac{2K_ac}{\sqrt{K_a^2+4K_ac}+K_a}.
$$
The last form avoids subtracting nearly equal numbers. If also $x/c\ll1$, neglect depletion to obtain
$$
h\approx x\approx\sqrt{K_ac},\qquad
[\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+]\approx\sqrt{K_a c^\circ C_T}.
$$
Check both conditions after computing: $x/c$ measures the fraction lost from the initial undissociated <weak acid> inventory, while $K_w/h^2$ measures the omitted <hydroxide> contribution to <electric charge> balance. A $5\%$ depletion criterion is a chosen tolerance, not a universal boundary. Keeping depletion does not cure the neglect of <self-ionization of water>. These <weak acid mass and charge balances> also explain why the <square root> estimate cannot be extended to arbitrarily dilute <chemical solutions>.

= Analytical concentration
{parent=Weak acid mass and charge balances}
{title2=$C_T$}

An <analytical concentration> records the total conserved inventory of a specified component per <volume (thermodynamics)> of <chemical solution>, summed over its relevant <chemical species>. Here $C_T=[\mathrm{HA}]+[\mathrm A^-]$; it is not generally the <chemical equilibrium> <concentration> $[\mathrm{HA}]$ of the undissociated <weak acid>.

= Weak acid dissociation measurement
{parent=Weak acid}

A <pH> measurement becomes evidence for a <weak acid> <chemical equilibrium> when paired with a known <analytical concentration> and a calibrated <glass electrode>. For the pure-<weak acid> model, measured <pH> estimates $h=10^{-\mathrm{pH}}$ only in the ideal <thermodynamic activity> approximation. Then $o=K_w/h$, $x=h-o$, $y=c-x$, and $K_a=hx/y$. Require $0<x<c$; values outside that range signal inconsistent measurements or an inadequate model. Repeating this reconstruction across a <dilution> series tests whether one <acid dissociation constant> describes the data, rather than merely fitting one point.

The Analytical Sciences Digital Library's <Analytical Electrochemistry: Potentiometry/Potentiometric Titration of an Unknown Weak Monoprotic Acid> provides a complementary <acid-base titration> experiment. Its apparatus includes a <glass electrode> and meter, a stirring magnet, a <volumetric flask>, <pipettes>, and a <burette> containing standardized <sodium hydroxide>. Prepare an aqueous acid stock of known <analytical concentration>, transfer a measured portion, and stir gently with the <glass electrode> clear of the magnet. Record added <volume (thermodynamics)> and stable <pH> after each addition; use a preliminary run to locate the steep region and smaller additions there on a fresh portion. The readout is the <pH> versus added <volume (thermodynamics)> curve, not a visual count of dissociated <molecules>.

Calibrate with fresh standard <buffer solutions> bracketing the <pH> range being interpreted. Use their assigned values at the actual <temperature>; a pair near <pH> $4$ and $7$ can cover the midpoint of many <weak acid> examples, but does not cover an entire alkaline endpoint region. Rinse between standards and samples to limit carryover, keep the sensing bulb and <reference electrode> junction immersed, and wait for a stable reading. These instrument practices are documented in <HI5221 & HI5222 Instruction Manual: pH and mV and ISE and Temperature Bench Meters by Hanna Instruments> (pp. 45–47, 49–50 and 73–74: calibration, measurement, rinsing and electrode conditioning).

Locate the <acid-base titration> equivalence region from the steepest change of <pH> with added <volume (thermodynamics)>. For one-to-one <neutralization>, standardized <sodium hydroxide> <concentration> times equivalence <volume (thermodynamics)> estimates the initial <amount of substance> of acid. Half that <volume (thermodynamics)> gives approximately equal $[\mathrm{HA}]$ and $[\mathrm A^-]$ only when subsequent <chemical equilibrium> redistribution is small. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> then gives $\mathrm{pH}\approx\mathrm pK_a$. Unequal <activity coefficients> or appreciable <hydronium> or <hydroxide> inventories can shift this estimate. The full <chemical equilibrium> curve supplies a stronger consistency check than its midpoint alone.

Useful controls for this model test are a fresh independently prepared acid portion, a <water> blank, a check standard after measurement to detect instrument drift, and fixed recorded <temperature>. A blank diagnoses contamination but should not be subtracted directly from <pH>, which is a <logarithm>. Keep exposure to atmospheric <carbon dioxide> comparable, especially at high <dilution>, and record any background salt that changes <ionic strength>. These are experimental-design controls suggested by the model's assumptions. The observed curve and its uncertainty determine whether the model is adequate; the diagrams here contain no experimental readouts. An aqueous <acid dissociation constant> cannot be inferred unchanged from a sample requiring a different <solvent> mixture, and a nearly neutral dilute sample makes the difference $h-K_w/h$ particularly sensitive to <pH> error.

= Glass electrode
{parent=Weak acid dissociation measurement}

A <glass electrode> is an <electrode> whose glass membrane responds to <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity>. A meter measures its <electric potential difference> against a <reference electrode>; calibration with standard <buffer solutions> converts this response to operational <pH>.

= Reference electrode
{parent=Weak acid dissociation measurement}

A <reference electrode> supplies the comparison <electric potential> for the <glass electrode> measurement. A combination probe incorporates both <electrodes>; changes at its junction can contribute measurement error.

= Acid-base titration
{parent=Weak acid dissociation measurement}

An <acid-base titration> adds measured quantities of a standardized <chemical acid> or <chemical base> to determine an initially unknown acid or base inventory through <neutralization>. Monitoring <pH> also constrains <chemical equilibrium> parameters such as the <acid dissociation constant>.

= Volumetric flask
{parent=Weak acid dissociation measurement}

A <volumetric flask> prepares a <chemical solution> at a specified total <volume (thermodynamics)>, using a calibration mark.

= Pipette
{parent=Weak acid dissociation measurement}

A <pipette> transfers a measured portion of a <chemical solution>.

= Burette
{parent=Weak acid dissociation measurement}

A <burette> delivers an adjustable measured <volume (thermodynamics)> of a <chemical solution>; the change in its reading gives the delivered <volume (thermodynamics)> in an <acid-base titration>.

= Acid dissociation constant
{parent=Acid (chemistry)}
{wiki}
{title2=$K_a$}

The <acid dissociation constant> quantifies the <chemical equilibrium> for loss of one <proton> from a <chemical acid>. In <water>, write the actual proton transfer as $\mathrm{HA}+\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\rightleftharpoons\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^++\mathrm A^-$. Its full <dimensionless> <equilibrium constant> is
$$
K_{\mathrm{rxn}}=\frac{a_{\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+}a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}a_{\mathrm H_2\mathrm O}}.
$$
The usual aqueous <acid dissociation constant> uses the <solvent> convention with <water> <thermodynamic activity> approximated by one:
$$
K_a=\frac{a_{\mathrm H^+}a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}},\qquad \mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a.
$$
Here hydrated <hydrogen ions> are understood; $\mathrm H^+$ is shorthand, not an isolated bare <proton> in the <chemical solution>. All <thermodynamic activities> and $K_a$ are <dimensionless quantities>, using consistent <standard states>. If <water> <thermodynamic activity> differs appreciably from one, retain it in the full <equilibrium constant> instead of silently mixing conventions.

With <activity coefficients> $\gamma_i$ and reference <concentration> $c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, put $a_i=\gamma_i[i]/c^\circ$ for each <solute>. Then
$$
K_a=\frac{\gamma_{\mathrm H^+}\gamma_{\mathrm A^-}}{\gamma_{\mathrm{HA}}}
\frac{[\mathrm H^+][\mathrm A^-]}{c^\circ[\mathrm{HA}]}
\approx\frac{[\mathrm H^+][\mathrm A^-]}{c^\circ[\mathrm{HA}]}.
$$
The last expression is the dilute ideal <concentration> approximation, not the definition in a nonideal <chemical solution>. Omitting $c^\circ$ instead produces a quotient with units of <concentration>; its numerical value depends on the chosen units. Do not take a <logarithm> of that dimensional quotient. Conditional <concentration> quotients can change with <ionic strength> even at fixed <temperature>, whereas the thermodynamic <acid dissociation constant> retains the <activity coefficient> corrections. The <IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/acid dissociation constant> entry distinguishes these conventions.

At the same <temperature>, <solvent> and <standard states>, a larger <acid dissociation constant>, equivalently a smaller <pKa>, indicates a stronger tendency to donate a <proton>. It does not specify an <analytical concentration>, a <pH>, or a <reaction rate>. Composition and the <weak acid mass and charge balances> are needed to predict <pH>; <chemical kinetics> addresses how quickly the <chemical equilibrium> is approached.

= pKa
{c}
{parent=Acid dissociation constant}
{title2=$\mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a$}

The <pKa> is the negative base-ten <logarithm> of the <dimensionless> <acid dissociation constant>. A lower <pKa> means a larger <acid dissociation constant> and a stronger tendency to donate a <proton> under the same <standard state>, <solvent>, and <temperature> conventions. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> places equal <weak acid> and <conjugate base> <thermodynamic activities> at <pH> equal to <pKa>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation].

= Base
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}

= Chemical base
{synonym}

A <chemical base> can accept a <proton>, using an available <electron pair> to form a <covalent bond>; more generally it can donate an <electron pair> to form a <covalent bond> with another species. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/B00601[IUPAC Gold Book, base].

= Conjugate base
{parent=Base (chemistry)}

A <conjugate base> is what remains after a <chemical acid> donates one <proton>. In the <conjugate acid-base pair> $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm A^-$, $\mathrm{HA}$ is the <conjugate acid> of $\mathrm A^-$; accepting a <proton> reverses the conversion. For example, <acetic acid> $\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$ loses a <proton> to give its <conjugate base> $\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}$. Partners differ by one <proton> and one unit of <electric charge>; they need not always be neutral and negative. <Hydronium>/<water> and <water>/<hydroxide> are also <conjugate acid-base pairs>.

At a prescribed <pH>, the ideal dilute <acid dissociation constant> determines the partition of a <monoprotic acid> inventory between its <chemical species>:
$$
\alpha_{\mathrm A^-}=\frac{K_a}{K_a+10^{-\mathrm{pH}}},\qquad
\alpha_{\mathrm{HA}}=\frac{10^{-\mathrm{pH}}}{K_a+10^{-\mathrm{pH}}}.
$$
These <dimensionless> fractions sum to one. They are the <weak acid dissociation fraction> and its complement when the only acid-bearing <chemical species> are $\mathrm{HA}$ and $\mathrm A^-$. Equal fractions occur at $\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a$ in this ideal approximation. For nonideal <chemical solutions>, equality of <thermodynamic activities> need not mean equality of <concentrations>.

\Image[weak-acid-equilibrium-species.png]
{id=weak-acid-equilibrium-species}
{title=A synthetic <monoprotic acid> partitions between <conjugate acid> and <conjugate base> as imposed <pH> changes}

For stipulated <pKa> $5$, the <conjugate base> fractions at <pH> $4$, $5$ and $6$ are $1/11$, $1/2$ and $10/11$. Here an external reservoir sets <pH>; its <ions> must supply the additional <electric charge> balance. The diagram omits those <ions> and <water> populations. It does not represent three pure $\mathrm{HA}$ samples at identical <analytical concentration> with freely chosen <pH>. In the latter experiment, the <weak acid mass and charge balances> determine <pH> as well as the partition.

= Conjugate acid
{parent=Conjugate base}

The <conjugate acid> of a <chemical base> is the <chemical species> formed when it accepts one <proton>. This is a relationship between partners, rather than a claim that the partner is a <strong acid>.

= Conjugate acid-base pair
{parent=Conjugate base}

A <conjugate acid-base pair> consists of two <chemical species> related by transfer of one <proton>. The <conjugate acid> has one more <proton> and one more positive unit of <electric charge> than the <conjugate base>.

= Base dissociation constant
{parent=Conjugate base}
{title2=$K_b$}

For the <conjugate base> $\mathrm A^-$ accepting a <proton> from <water>,
$$
\mathrm A^-+\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{HA}+\mathrm{OH}^-,\qquad
K_b=\frac{a_{\mathrm{HA}}a_{\mathrm{OH}^-}}{a_{\mathrm A^-}}.
$$
This <base dissociation constant> uses the same <water> <thermodynamic activity> approximation and <standard state> conventions as the <acid dissociation constant>. Multiplying the two relations cancels the partner <thermodynamic activities>:
$$
K_aK_b=a_{\mathrm H^+}a_{\mathrm{OH}^-}=K_w,\qquad
\mathrm pK_a+\mathrm pK_b=\mathrm pK_w,\qquad
\mathrm pK_b=-\log_{10}K_b,\quad \mathrm pK_w=-\log_{10}K_w.
$$
Here $K_w$ is the aqueous ion product associated with <self-ionization of water>. At fixed <temperature>, a larger <acid dissociation constant> gives a smaller <base dissociation constant> for its own <conjugate base>; this reciprocal rule compares matching <conjugate acid-base pairs>. A <weak acid> does not necessarily have a <conjugate base> that reacts almost completely with <water>. For example, stipulated $K_a=10^{-5}$ and $K_w=10^{-14}$ give $K_b=10^{-9}$.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/14.3 Relative Strengths of Acids and Bases>[§14.3, Relative Strengths of Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs, cancellation giving Ka Kb = Kw]{ref}

The value of $K_w$ changes with <temperature>; $10^{-14}$ is the familiar approximate value near $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, not a universal constant. If <water> is retained explicitly in both full <equilibrium constants>, their product instead equals $a_{\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+}a_{\mathrm{OH}^-}/a_{\mathrm H_2\mathrm O}^2$, the full <equilibrium constant> for $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\rightleftharpoons\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^++\mathrm{OH}^-$. The <acid dissociation constant>, <base dissociation constant> and <self-ionization of water> convention must agree.

= pH
{c}
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{pH}=-\log_{10}a_{\mathrm H^+}$}

The <pH> of a <chemical solution> is the negative base-ten <logarithm> of its <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity>:
$$
\mathrm{pH}=-\log_{10}a_{\mathrm H^+},\qquad a_{\mathrm H^+}=10^{-\mathrm{pH}}.
$$
The <thermodynamic activity> is a <dimensionless quantity>, so the <logarithm> has a <dimensionless> argument. The definition uses <thermodynamic activity>, rather than total <chemical acid> <concentration>. Single-<ion> <thermodynamic activities> require a convention; practical <pH> measurements are tied to calibrated standards. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04524[IUPAC Gold Book, pH].

In a dilute ideal <chemical solution> in <water>, a useful approximation is
$$
a_{\mathrm H^+}\approx\frac{[\mathrm H^+]}{c^\circ},\qquad c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}},\qquad
\mathrm{pH}\approx-\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{[\mathrm H^+]}{1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}}\right).
$$
Square brackets denote <concentration> in <moles> per litre. In <water>, $[\mathrm H^+]$ is shorthand for the <hydronium> <concentration>, often written $[\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+]$; it does not describe isolated bare <protons>. Departures from ideal behavior need <activity coefficients>. The <concentration> formula approximates the <thermodynamic activity> definition.

For an ideal <strong acid> supplying one <proton> per initial unit, stipulate a total <concentration> of $0.001\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and negligible <self-ionization of water>. Then $[\mathrm H^+]\approx10^{-3}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, so
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx-\log_{10}(10^{-3})=3.
$$
Reducing that <concentration> tenfold by <dilution> gives $10^{-4}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and <pH> $4$, under the same assumptions. More generally, dividing the <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity> by ten raises <pH> by one. This is why the scale compresses an enormous range: equal steps of <pH> represent multiplicative changes, rather than equal differences in <concentration>. These are model calculations, rather than measured samples.

\Image[ph-logarithmic-scale.png]
{title=One step of <pH> represents a tenfold change in <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity>}
{description=The <logarithm> makes equal spacing on the <pH> axis correspond to factors of ten on the <thermodynamic activity> axis. The marked <strong acid> example uses the dilute ideal <concentration> approximation and negligible <self-ionization of water>. The plotted interval is illustrative; it does not bound possible <pH> values.}

Neutrality depends on <temperature>. In the dilute ideal <water> model, define the <dimensionless> ion product $K_w=([\mathrm H^+]/c^\circ)([\mathrm{OH}^-]/c^\circ)$ and $\mathrm pK_w=-\log_{10}K_w$. Equal <hydronium> and <hydroxide> <concentrations> imply
$$
\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{neutral}}=\frac{\mathrm pK_w}{2}.
$$
At $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, the textbook approximation $K_w\approx10^{-14}$ gives neutral <pH> $7$. Since $K_w$ varies with <temperature>, neutrality need not mean <pH> $7$. Nor is <pH> restricted to $0$–$14$: the activity definition permits negative <pH> when $a_{\mathrm H^+}>1$, and <pH> above $14$ when $a_{\mathrm H^+}<10^{-14}$. High <concentrations> can invalidate the dilute ideal approximation. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-2-ph-and-poh[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, pH and pOH].

<S. P. L. Sørensen> introduced <pH> at the Carlsberg Laboratory in 1909, as recorded in the https://carlsbergfondet.dk/en/about-the-foundation/our-history/timeline-of-the-foundation-s-history/[Carlsberg Foundation historical timeline].

= Buffer solution
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}

A <buffer solution> contains appreciable quantities of a <weak acid> and its <conjugate base>, and resists changes in <pH> caused by small quantities of added <chemical acid> or <chemical base>. The <conjugate base> accepts added <protons>; the <weak acid> supplies <protons> when a <chemical base> consumes them. Consuming most of either partner undermines the buffering action. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, buffers].

For a separate toy calculation, stipulate <pKa> $5$ for a <weak acid> $\mathrm{HA}$; this is an input, not measured material data. Initially $1\,\mathrm L$ of <buffer solution> contains $0.10\,\mathrm{mol}$ of $\mathrm{HA}$ and $0.10\,\mathrm{mol}$ of its <conjugate base> $\mathrm A^-$. In the ideal <concentration> approximation, the <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> gives initial <pH> $5$. Add $0.010\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydroxide> with negligible volume change. The dominant <neutralization> has one-to-one <stoichiometry>:
$$
\mathrm{HA}+\mathrm{OH}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm A^-+\mathrm H_2\mathrm O.
$$
Each <mole> of consumed <hydroxide> removes one <mole> of <weak acid> and forms one <mole> of <conjugate base> and <water>. Thus the inventories after <neutralization> are
$$
n_{\mathrm{HA}}\approx(0.10-0.01)\,\mathrm{mol}=0.09\,\mathrm{mol},\qquad
n_{\mathrm A^-}\approx(0.10+0.01)\,\mathrm{mol}=0.11\,\mathrm{mol}.
$$
The shared volume cancels from the <concentration> ratio. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> therefore predicts
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx5+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{0.11}{0.09}\right)=5+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{11}{9}\right)=5.0871501757\ldots.
$$
This small increase illustrates how conversion between abundant partners absorbs added <chemical base>. The calculation neglects the tiny free <hydronium> and <hydroxide> inventories, changes during re-establishment of <chemical equilibrium>, <self-ionization of water>, and <activity coefficient> corrections. The displayed inventories approximate the final <chemical species> distribution; they are not an exact solution of all <chemical equilibrium> conditions.

\Image[buffer-neutralization-inventory.png]
{title=Added <hydroxide> transfers a finite inventory from <weak acid> to <conjugate base>}
{description=Toy <pKa> $5$, initial volume $1\,\mathrm L$, and negligible added volume. Bars show the dominant <neutralization> bookkeeping before and after adding $0.010\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydroxide>; the <pH> values use the ideal <concentration> approximation. The acid-plus-base inventory stays $0.20\,\mathrm{mol}$. Neither the bars nor the <pH> values are measured material data or exact speciation.}

A <buffer solution> has a finite <buffer capacity>: its <weak acid> inventory supplies protection against added <chemical base>, and its <conjugate base> inventory supplies protection against added <chemical acid>. Here an addition approaching $0.10\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydroxide> uses up the available $\mathrm{HA}$ in the dominant <stoichiometry> calculation. Close to that limit, the neglected <chemical equilibrium> contributions matter; once $\mathrm{HA}$ is exhausted, inserting a zero denominator into the <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> cannot predict the actual <pH>. Excess <hydroxide> and the remaining <chemical species> require a fresh <chemical equilibrium> calculation. This is why a <buffer solution> stabilizes conditions for <chemical reactions> over a finite range of additions. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, buffer capacity and added base].

For the pair $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm A^-$, rearranging the <acid dissociation constant> gives the activity relation
$$
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}}\right).
$$
With <thermodynamic activity> of <water> approximated by one and ideal dilute <thermodynamic activities> for both partners, this yields the familiar <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation>:
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{[\mathrm A^-]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}\right).
$$
The <concentrations> are at <chemical equilibrium>; using initial values requires negligible equilibration changes. Below, also neglect <self-ionization of water> and competing <chemical reactions>.

Stipulate $\mathrm pK_a=4.76$ for an illustrative <weak acid>, without identifying it with a measured substance. A <conjugate base> to <weak acid> <concentration> ratio of two gives
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx4.76+\log_{10}2=5.061029995\ldots\approx5.06103.
$$
Equal <concentrations> give a ratio of one, whose <logarithm> is zero, hence <pH> $4.76$. Multiplying the ratio by ten raises <pH> by one: ratios $0.1$, $1$, and $10$ give <pH> values $3.76$, $4.76$, and $5.76$. The ratio controls the ideal prediction, while the absolute quantities control how much added <chemical acid> or <chemical base> the <buffer solution> can accommodate.

\Image[buffer-ratio-ph.png]
{title=The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> turns a tenfold ratio change into one unit of <pH>}
{description=The stipulated <weak acid> has $\mathrm pK_a=4.76$. Points mark <conjugate base> to <weak acid> <concentration> ratios $0.1$, $1$, $2$, and $10$. The curve uses ideal dilute <thermodynamic activities>, <chemical equilibrium>, and negligible <self-ionization of water>; it is a calculation, rather than measured data.}

= Henderson-Hasselbalch equation
{c}
{parent=Buffer solution}
{wiki=Henderson–Hasselbalch_equation}
{title2=$\mathrm{pH}\approx\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}([\mathrm A^-]/[\mathrm{HA}])$}

The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> estimates <buffer solution> <pH> using a <weak acid>'s <acid dissociation constant> and the <conjugate base> to <weak acid> <concentration> ratio.

Its activity form is an exact rearrangement of the chosen <chemical equilibrium> relation. Write the acid dissociation step as $\mathrm{HA}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm H^++\mathrm A^-$, with hydrated <hydrogen ions> understood in <water> and the <solvent> convention incorporated into the <acid dissociation constant>. For consistent <standard states>,
$$
K_a=\frac{a_{\mathrm H^+}a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}},\qquad
\mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a,\qquad
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}}\right).
$$
Here each $a_i$ is a <dimensionless> <thermodynamic activity> at <chemical equilibrium>. Exactness refers to this specified <acid dissociation constant> relation and the chosen single-<ion> <thermodynamic activity> convention, not to identifying the ratio with quantities initially mixed. If <water> is instead written explicitly as a <reactant>, its <thermodynamic activity> must be treated consistently. See https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf[Hippler and Metcalfe, Using activities to correct the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation (2020), equations 3–4].

For a toy <weak acid> with stipulated <pKa> $5$, use the ideal dilute <concentration> approximation and put $r=[\mathrm A^-]/[\mathrm{HA}]$. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> becomes $\mathrm{pH}\approx5+\log_{10}r$:
$$
r=0.1\ \Rightarrow\ \mathrm{pH}\approx4,\qquad
r=1\ \Rightarrow\ \mathrm{pH}\approx5,\qquad
r=10\ \Rightarrow\ \mathrm{pH}\approx6.
$$
More <conjugate base> relative to <weak acid> means higher <pH>. Each tenfold increase in the ratio contributes exactly one to the calculated <pH>. The stipulated <pKa> is not a tabulated value for an identified substance.

\Image[buffer-ph-versus-species-ratio.png]
{title=Toy <pKa> $5$: ratios $0.1$, $1$, and $10$ give calculated <pH> $4$, $5$, and $6$}
{description=Both panels show the same <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> in the ideal <concentration> approximation, with linear and <logarithmic scales> for the <conjugate base> to <weak acid> ratio. The <logarithmic scale> turns the curve into a straight line. The plotted range is illustrative, rather than a guarantee of useful <buffer capacity>.}

The distinction between <thermodynamic activity> and <concentration> is explicit when $a_i=\gamma_i[i]/c^\circ$, where $\gamma_i$ is an <activity coefficient> and $c^\circ$ is the reference <concentration>:
$$
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{[\mathrm A^-]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}\right)+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{\gamma_{\mathrm A^-}}{\gamma_{\mathrm{HA}}}\right).
$$
Neglecting the final term gives the usual approximate <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation>. It is justified when the ratio of <activity coefficients> is sufficiently close to one, as in a suitable dilute ideal approximation. <Ionic strength>, including contributions from other dissolved <ions>, affects <activity coefficients>; a nominally dilute <weak acid> alone does not establish that all corrections are small. The ratio must use <chemical equilibrium> <concentrations>. Replacing these by prepared amounts or post-<neutralization> inventories is a further approximation, suitable only when subsequent redistribution is small. See https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf[Hippler and Metcalfe, concentration, activity, and analytical-concentration approximations].

The short derivation is to solve the <acid dissociation constant> relation for $a_{\mathrm H^+}$, take a negative base-ten <logarithm>, and use the definitions of <pH> and <pKa>. Its useful beauty is that a <chemical equilibrium> becomes a simple rule about a ratio: select a <weak acid> with <pKa> near the desired <pH>, then adjust the partner ratio. Scaling both partner <concentrations> equally leaves the ideal <pH> prediction unchanged, while changing <buffer capacity>. In practical <buffer solutions>, <temperature>, <ionic strength>, and the available inventories still matter.

= Buffer capacity
{parent=Buffer solution}
{title2=$\beta$}

<Buffer capacity> measures resistance to a change in <pH>: how much additional <chemical acid> or <chemical base> a <chemical solution> accommodates per small change in <pH>. Its differential form is a local <derivative>, rather than the total inventory available before a <buffer solution> is exhausted. Two <buffer solutions> can have identical <pH> and very different <buffer capacities>.

Choose a constant <volume (thermodynamics)> $V$ and fixed <temperature>. Let $n_B$ count added <moles> of fully dissociated, monovalent <chemical base> supplying <hydroxide>, and $n_A$ added <moles> of fully dissociated, monovalent <strong acid> supplying <hydrogen ions>. Neither addition supplies the <weak acid> component itself. The signed addition is $b=(n_B-n_A)/V$, with units $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, and
$$
\beta=\frac{\mathrm db}{\mathrm d\mathrm{pH}}=\frac1V\frac{\mathrm dn_B}{\mathrm d\mathrm{pH}}=-\frac1V\frac{\mathrm dn_A}{\mathrm d\mathrm{pH}}.
$$
The last two forms describe separate base-only and acid-only additions. The <buffer capacity> is positive for the stable <chemical equilibrium> model below and is reported in $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ per unit of <pH>. Since <pH> is <dimensionless>, the dimensional unit is just $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$; stating “per <pH>” makes the measurement convention clear. Here constant <volume (thermodynamics)> is an idealization, realized approximately by small additions of concentrated reagent.

For a sufficiently small addition, $\Delta\mathrm{pH}\approx\Delta b/\beta$. A large <buffer capacity> means a small local change of <pH> for a given addition. Over a finite interval, use the changing <buffer capacity> instead:
$$
\Delta b=\int_{\mathrm{pH}_0}^{\mathrm{pH}_1}\beta(p)\,\mathrm dp.
$$
This <integral> gives a finite dose in $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$; it need not equal the initial <buffer capacity> times the eventual change in <pH>.

= Buffer capacity of a monoprotic acid
{parent=Buffer capacity}

Assume an ideal dilute aqueous <chemical solution> with just one <monoprotic acid> pair $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm A^-$, <self-ionization of water>, and other <ions> that balance <electric charge> without undergoing further <chemical reactions>. Hold the <analytical concentration> $C=[\mathrm{HA}]+[\mathrm A^-]$ constant. Keep the <acid dissociation constant> $K_a$ and the <self-ionization of water> constant $K_w$ <dimensionless>, consistently with <pH>:
$$
c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}},\qquad H=10^{-\mathrm{pH}}=\frac{[\mathrm H^+]}{c^\circ},\qquad K_w=\frac{[\mathrm H^+][\mathrm{OH}^-]}{(c^\circ)^2}.
$$
Thus $H$ denotes normalized <hydrogen ion> <concentration>, not a dimensional <concentration>. The <chemical equilibrium> and conserved <analytical concentration> give
$$
[\mathrm A^-]=\frac{C K_a}{K_a+H},\qquad [\mathrm{OH}^-]=\frac{c^\circ K_w}{H},\qquad [\mathrm H^+]=c^\circ H.
$$
Adding <chemical base> converts <weak acid> to <conjugate base>, increases free <hydroxide>, and decreases free <hydrogen ion> <concentration>. Balancing <electric charge>, including the added reagent's accompanying <ions>, gives the signed dose relative to any initial <chemical equilibrium> at $p_0$:
$$
b(p)-b(p_0)=Q(p)-Q(p_0),\qquad Q(p)=[\mathrm A^-]+[\mathrm{OH}^-]-[\mathrm H^+].
$$
Differentiate with respect to <pH>, using $\mathrm dH/\mathrm dp=-(\ln10)H$. The result is
$$
\boxed{\beta=(\ln10)\left(\frac{C K_a H}{(K_a+H)^2}+c^\circ H+\frac{c^\circ K_w}{H}\right).}
$$
The first term is the <monoprotic acid> pair's contribution, $\beta_{\mathrm{pair}}$; the remaining terms are the free <hydrogen ion> and <hydroxide> contributions, $\beta_{\mathrm{water}}$. Each has units $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ per <pH>. Equivalently, with dimensional $H_c=[\mathrm H^+]$, $K_{a,c}=c^\circ K_a$ and $K_{w,c}=(c^\circ)^2K_w$, the expression is $(\ln10)(C K_{a,c}H_c/(K_{a,c}+H_c)^2+H_c+K_{w,c}/H_c)$. Mixing the dimensional and <dimensionless> conventions would give incorrect units.

For $r=K_a/H=10^{\mathrm{pH}-\mathrm pK_a}$,
$$
\beta_{\mathrm{pair}}=(\ln10)C\frac{r}{(1+r)^2}\leq\frac{\ln10}{4}C,
$$
because $(1+r)^2-4r=(1-r)^2\geq0$. Equality occurs at $r=1$: equal <weak acid> and <conjugate base> <concentrations>, or <pH> equal to <pKa>. This is the exact maximum of the pair contribution in this model. The full <buffer capacity> need not have its maximum there: free <hydrogen ions> and <hydroxide> make it grow toward the acidic and alkaline extremes. When these contributions are small, the central peak lies close to <pKa>.

= Buffer capacity and concentration
{parent=Buffer capacity}

Stipulate <pKa> $5$ and $K_w=10^{-14}$; these are model inputs, not measurements of an identified substance. At <pH> $5$ the pair contribution is $0.575646\ldots C$. For <analytical concentrations> $C=0.010$ and $0.100\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, respectively, it is
$$
\beta_{\mathrm{pair}}=0.00575646\ldots\quad\text{and}\quad0.0575646\ldots\;\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\ per\ pH}.
$$
The free <hydrogen ion> and <hydroxide> contribution is the same at this <pH>, $\beta_{\mathrm{water}}=(\ln10)(10^{-5}+10^{-9})\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}=0.0000230282\ldots\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ per <pH>. The full <buffer capacities> are therefore approximately $0.00577949$ and $0.0575877\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ per <pH>. Identical <pH> fixes the free <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity>; it does not fix the inventory of <weak acid> and <conjugate base> available for <neutralization>.

\Image[monoprotic-buffer-capacity-concentration.png]
{id=monoprotic-buffer-capacity-concentration}
{title=At fixed <pKa>, the pair contribution to <buffer capacity> scales with <analytical concentration>}
{description=Ideal <monoprotic acid> model with stipulated <pKa> $5$ and $K_w=10^{-14}$. Left: the pair contribution for two <analytical concentrations>, with its maximum at <pH> equal to <pKa>. Right: pair, free <hydrogen ion> and <hydroxide>, and total contributions for $C=0.010\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, on a <logarithmic scale>. Curves are calculated, not measured; the constant ideal <activity coefficient> assumption limits their interpretation in real samples.}

At one unit of <pH> on either side of <pKa>, $r=10$ or $0.1$, giving $\beta_{\mathrm{pair}}/\beta_{\mathrm{pair,max}}=40/121\approx0.331$. The partners still form a <buffer solution>, but its local resistance is only about one third of the maximum for that <analytical concentration>. At fixed <pH>, the pair contribution scales exactly with $C$; the full <buffer capacity> contains the additional $C$-independent <water> contribution. Consequently, extreme <dilution> does not justify dropping <self-ionization of water>.

= Finite titrant response of a buffer
{parent=Buffer capacity}

Prepare the same ideal <monoprotic acid> model at initial <pH> $5$, with appropriate accompanying <ions> to balance <electric charge>. To predict the new <pH> after a finite signed addition $\Delta b$, solve $Q(\mathrm{pH}_1)-Q(5)=\Delta b$ using the <chemical equilibrium> expression above. For $\Delta b=0.002\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ of added <chemical base>, the calculated final <pH> is $5.366798$ at $C=0.010\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, but $5.034749$ at $C=0.100\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. The initial local approximation gives changes of about $0.34605$ and $0.034730$, respectively; its accuracy decreases as the partner balance changes appreciably.

\Image[buffer-finite-titrant-ph-response.png]
{id=buffer-finite-titrant-ph-response}
{title=Equal finite additions produce different changes of <pH> in dilute and concentrated <buffer solutions>}
{description=Calculated constant-<volume (thermodynamics)> <chemical equilibrium> responses, initially at <pH> $5$, with stipulated <pKa> $5$ and $K_w=10^{-14}$. Positive dose means added <chemical base>, negative dose added <strong acid>. The left panel includes exhaustion of the dilute sample's useful pair inventory; the right panel compares its finite response with the initial local <derivative> approximation. Free <hydrogen ions> and <hydroxide> are included throughout.}

For the dilute example, additions beyond approximately $0.005\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ in either direction exceed the initial inventory of one partner. The full <chemical equilibrium> calculation still produces a finite <pH>; an inventory-only <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> calculation would attempt to use a negative partner quantity. At $+0.006\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ the dilute example reaches <pH> $10.99564$, whereas the concentrated example reaches only $5.104698$. This demonstrates a finite operating range, not an abrupt cessation of all <buffer capacity>.

= Buffer capacity measurement
{parent=Buffer capacity}

A proposed modern measurement extends the <weak acid dissociation measurement> by estimating the local slope of an <acid-base titration>. Transfer a measured sample with a <pipette> into a stirred vessel; clamp a combination <glass electrode>/<reference electrode> clear of the stirring magnet and place a <temperature> probe nearby. A calibrated <burette> delivers a <chemical base> of independently standardized <concentration> $c_T$, such as <sodium hydroxide>. Record the initial sample <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_0$, cumulative delivered <volume (thermodynamics)> $v$, stable <pH>, and <temperature> after each small addition. Use a separate fresh portion for an acidward series. These are proposed measurement conditions, not reported experimental results.

For the instrument calibration, use fresh standard <buffer solutions> bracketing the measured <pH> range, with values appropriate to the actual <temperature>. The Hanna manual specifies separate rinse and calibration portions, gentle stirring, and approximately $4\,\mathrm{cm}$ immersion of the probe tip. It calls for stable readings; the sensing bulb and <reference electrode> junction must remain immersed. Rinse between standards and samples and avoid carrying rinse <water> into the measured portion.<HI5221 & HI5222 Instruction Manual: pH and mV and ISE and Temperature Bench Meters by Hanna Instruments>[pp. 45–47: calibration portions, bracketing standards, temperature and immersion; pp. 49–50 and 74–75: stable readings, rinsing, junction immersion and drift]{ref}

The readout pairs each added <amount of substance> with the measured <pH>. With negligible added <volume (thermodynamics)>, neighboring readings give the <finite difference> estimate
$$
\beta\approx\frac{c_T(v_{i+1}-v_i)}{V_0(\mathrm{pH}_{i+1}-\mathrm{pH}_i)},
$$
assigned to the interval's midpoint. High <buffer capacity> appears as a small change of <pH> for a measured reagent dose. Choose increments small enough to resolve changing <buffer capacity>, but large enough that the change of <pH> exceeds measurement noise. Repeat with a fresh portion and different increment sizes; remeasure a standard afterward to check drift. A <water> blank can reveal contamination, but its <pH> must not be subtracted from the sample <pH>. These controls are proposed tests of the measurement and model assumptions.

If additions appreciably increase <volume (thermodynamics)>, even the <analytical concentration> changes: $C(v)=C_0V_0/(V_0+v)$. Merely dividing the added <moles> by the current <volume (thermodynamics)> does not restore the constant-<volume (thermodynamics)> <derivative>. One alternative is explicitly to report a changing-volume convention. For $c(v)=c_Tv/(V_0+v)$, define
$$
\beta_V=\left|\frac{\mathrm dc}{\mathrm d\mathrm{pH}}\right|=\frac{c_TV_0}{(V_0+v)^2}\left|\frac{\mathrm dv}{\mathrm d\mathrm{pH}}\right|.
$$
This definition includes the reagent's changing <concentration> and differs from the constant-<volume (thermodynamics)> <buffer capacity> defined above.<Dynamic Buffer Capacity in Acid–Base Systems by Anna M. Michałowska-Kaczmarczyk and Tadeusz Michałowski>[§2, Definition of Dynamic Buffer Capacity, equations (1)–(3): reagent concentration in the combined volume and its derivative]{ref}

To compare with the constant-<volume (thermodynamics)> theory, fit the changing-<volume (thermodynamics)> <chemical equilibrium> model instead of treating the observed slope as $\beta$. In the same ideal <monoprotic acid> system, define $S_0=[\mathrm A^-]_0+[\mathrm{OH}^-]_0-[\mathrm H^+]_0$. For baseward additions, the <electric charge> balance at each reading is
$$
\frac{S_0V_0+c_Tv}{V_0+v}=\frac{C(v)K_a}{K_a+H}+c^\circ\left(\frac{K_w}{H}-H\right).
$$
Use a minus sign before $c_Tv$ for the corresponding acidward series. From the model parameters, evaluate the constant-<volume (thermodynamics)> <buffer capacity> at the chosen <analytical concentration> and <pH>. The fit also tests whether one <acid dissociation constant> describes the observations.

Limitations include incomplete mixing, slow <glass electrode> response, uncertain reagent <concentration>, and changes in <temperature> or <ionic strength>. Dissolved atmospheric <carbon dioxide> introduces an additional <chemical acid> system; comparable exposure and freshly prepared samples help distinguish it from the intended <monoprotic acid> response. Additional reacting <chemical species> and nonideal <activity coefficients> invalidate the simple formula. Especially when <pH> increments are tiny, measurement noise can dominate the inferred <derivative>; reporting the dose, <pH> interval, <volume (thermodynamics)> convention, and repeatability is part of reporting a meaningful <buffer capacity>.

= Neutralization
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki=Neutralization_(chemistry)}

A <neutralization> is a <chemical reaction> between a <chemical acid> and a <chemical base>. In the <buffer solution> example, <hydroxide> takes a <proton> from the <weak acid> to form <water> and the <conjugate base>. The <stoichiometry> updates the partner inventories before the approximate <chemical equilibrium> calculation. The name does not require the resulting <pH> to be $7$.

= Mixture
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Solution
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Mixture}
{wiki}

= Chemical solution
{synonym}

A <chemical solution> is a homogeneous <mixture> in which designated <solutes> are distributed in a <solvent>. The distinction between <solute> and <solvent> is part of the description; <water> is the <solvent> in the aqueous <pH> examples.

= Ionic strength
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}
{wiki}
{title2=$I_c=\frac12\sum_i c_i z_i^2$}

The concentration-based <ionic strength> is $I_c=\frac12\sum_i c_i z_i^2$, where $c_i$ is each dissolved <ion>'s <concentration> and $z_i$ its signed <electric charge> number. It has the same units as $c_i$; some formulas instead use the <dimensionless> ratio $I_c/c^\circ$. All dissolved <ions> contribute, including those that balance the <electric charge> of a <buffer solution>'s <conjugate base>. <Ionic strength> helps describe the interactions represented by <activity coefficients>. See https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf[Hippler and Metcalfe, activity coefficients and dimensionless ionic strength].

= Solute
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

A <solute> is a component of a <chemical solution> distinguished from its <solvent>. Its <concentration> describes how much is present relative to a specified quantity of <chemical solution> or <solvent>.

= Solvent
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}
{wiki}

= Dilution
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

<Dilution> lowers a <solute>'s <concentration> by adding <solvent>. When the <amount of substance> of that <solute> is conserved and $c$ denotes its amount per <volume (thermodynamics)> of <chemical solution>, $c_1V_1=c_2V_2$. This conservation statement does not by itself preserve the <pH>: a <chemical equilibrium> may change as the <chemical solution> is diluted.

= Nuclear magnetic resonance
{parent=Chemistry}

= NMR spectroscopy
{c}
{synonym}

<NMR spectroscopy> measures resonant responses of <atomic nuclei> to applied <electromagnetic radiation> in a <magnetic field>. In <residual-group monitoring of polyurethane formation>, distinguishable signal <integrals> supply a relative residual-group readout; overlap or very weak signals can limit that inference.

= Size-exclusion chromatography
{parent=Chemistry}

<Size-exclusion chromatography> separates dissolved <molecules> using porous columns. Elution depends on molecular size in the chosen <solvent>; calibration supplies an estimate of <molar mass>. A trace calibrated with another <polymer> does not automatically give absolute <molar masses> or a number-count <probability distribution>.

= Infrared spectroscopy
{parent=Chemistry}

= IR spectroscopy
{c}
{synonym}

<Infrared spectroscopy> measures a sample's response to <infrared> <electromagnetic radiation>. Absorption associated with stretching or bending motions of <chemical bonds> helps identify groups and their environments. Peak positions are commonly reported as reciprocal <wavelength>, in $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$; a lower value means lower <frequency>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/12-6-infrared-spectroscopy[McMurry: infrared spectroscopy and its wavelength convention]{ref}

= Stereochemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Stereochemical
{synonym}

<Stereochemistry> studies the spatial arrangement of <chemical atoms> in <molecules>. A <Fischer projection> records this information at successive <stereocenters>, whereas a <molecular formula> only counts <chemical atoms>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-2-representing-carbohydrate-stereochemistry-fischer-projections[McMurry: representing carbohydrate stereochemistry].

= Chirality
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

<Chirality (chemistry)> means that a <molecule> cannot be superposed on its mirror image. A <carbon> attached to four different groups is a common source of <chirality (chemistry)>; the definition concerns the whole <molecule>, rather than requiring this particular structural feature.

= Enantiomer
{parent=Chirality (chemistry)}
{wiki}

<Enantiomers> are a pair of nonsuperposable mirror-image <molecules>. The D and L configurations of <alanine> are an example; <glycine> has no such pair because its <amino acid alpha carbon> carries two identical <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, discussion of glycine and Fischer projections after Table 26.1]{ref}

= Stereocenter
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

At a <stereocenter>, exchanging two attached groups produces a different spatial configuration. The open-chain <ribose> and <glucose> examples have <carbon> <stereocenters> bearing four different groups; C2 of <deoxyribose> has two identical <hydrogen> substituents and is not a <stereocenter>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/stereo/RS.html#stereogenic[Moss, Basic Terminology of Stereochemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1996: stereogenic units].

= Fischer projection
{c}
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

In a <Fischer projection>, each cross represents a <carbon> position: horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer and vertical <chemical bonds> point away. For the open-chain <aldehyde> <sugars> illustrated here, place $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom. This is a convention for <stereochemistry>, not a measured molecular shape. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/stereo/FM.html#Fischer[Moss, IUPAC Recommendations 1996: Fischer projection].

In these <Fischer projections>, D specifies that the <hydroxy group> at the lowest <stereocenter> lies on the right: C4 for <ribose> and <deoxyribose>, C5 for <glucose>. D does not specify every other <stereocenter>, and it does not specify the direction of rotation of polarized <light>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-3-d-l-sugars[McMurry: the meaning of D and L].

= Functional group
{parent=Chemistry}

A <functional group> is an identifiable group of <chemical atoms> associated with characteristic <chemical reactions>. In <step-growth polymerization>, $A$ and $B$ name compatible reactive groups, not necessarily entire <monomers> or particular <chemical elements>.

= Hydroxy group
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{OH}$}

= Hydroxyl group
{synonym}

A <hydroxy group> is an $\mathrm{OH}$ group attached to the rest of a <molecule>. In the illustrated <sugars>, its <oxygen> is bonded to <carbon> and to <hydrogen>.

= Chemical substance
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Copper(II) sulfate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CuSO_4}$}

In the <catalysed peroxide comparison>, <copper(II) sulfate> supplies dissolved <ions> before addition of <sodium hydroxide> forms a solid by <precipitation (chemistry)>. The comparison tests that solid-forming treatment rather than assuming the dissolved salt is itself an effective <catalyst>.

= Amine
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$R\mathrm{NH}_2,\ R_2\mathrm{NH},\ R_3\mathrm N$}

A simple neutral <amine> has <nitrogen> bonded to one, two, or three groups attached through <carbon>, with <hydrogen> filling the remaining positions in the neutral three-bond form. These are the primary, secondary, and tertiary forms respectively. An <amide> has different connectivity to a <carbonyl group>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-1-naming-amines[McMurry: classification of amines]{ref}

= Amide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$R-\mathrm C(=\mathrm O)-\mathrm{NR'R''}$}

Here an <amide> means a <carbonyl group> whose <carbon> is bonded directly to <nitrogen>; each of $R$, $R'$ and $R''$ may be <hydrogen> or a group attached through <carbon>. A <peptide bond> contains this linkage. <Chemical resonance> distinguishes its <nitrogen> from that of an <amine>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-3-basicity-of-amines[McMurry: amide resonance]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/20-4-amines-and-amides[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 20.4: amide and peptide connectivity]{ref}

= Hydrogen peroxide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}_2$}
{wiki}

= Acyl group
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}}$}

For the <carboxylic acid>-derived examples here, an <acyl group> is the group remaining after formal removal of $\mathrm{OH}$ from $\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}OH}$. It includes the <oxygen> at the end of the <fatty acid>-derived chain, so it is more than just a <hydrocarbon> region. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/class/carba.html[IUPAC class names: carboxylic acids and derivatives].

= Sphingosine
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Zwitterion
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Zwitterionic
{synonym}

A <zwitterion> has opposite formal <electric charges> in different regions of a <molecule>, with zero net <electric charge>. <Phosphatidylcholine> provides a membrane-related example. This is stronger than merely having <chemical polarity> through fractional <partial atomic charges>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/Z06752[IUPAC Gold Book: zwitterions].

= Lipid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

<Lipids> are a chemically diverse family of <chemical substances>, conventionally associated with biological origin and solubility in <nonpolar> <solvents>. They include <triglycerides>, <phospholipids>, and <cholesterol>. The name does not specify one <molecular formula>, one repeating <monomer>, or one kind of <polymer>. Some <lipids> have both <hydrophilic> and <hydrophobic> regions rather than being uniformly <nonpolar>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/L03571[IUPAC Gold Book: lipids].

Structure explains the different jobs. A <triglyceride> has three <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> attached to <glycerol> and serves as an <energy> store. A typical <glycerophospholipid> instead combines two such chains with a <phosphate>-containing head, making it an <amphiphile> suited to a <lipid bilayer>. <Cholesterol> has a different structure and also contributes to <cell membrane> properties. Thus a <lipid> is a chemical building material, while a <lipid bilayer> is an organization of many <molecules>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.3: lipid structures and functions].

= Triglyceride
{parent=Lipid}

= Triacylglycerol
{synonym}

A <triglyceride> contains <glycerol> joined to three <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> through <ester> linkages. Compare the three chains with the two-chain, <phosphate>-headed <glycerophospholipid> illustrated below. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: fats and oils].

= Cholesterol
{parent=Lipid}

<Cholesterol> is a <lipid> with four fused rings and a small <hydrophilic> region. It helps regulate the fluidity of animal <cell membranes>; it is structurally distinct from a <phospholipid>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: cholesterol].

= Phospholipid
{parent=Lipid}
{wiki}

<Phospholipids> are <lipids> containing a <phosphate> group as part of their structure linked by <covalent bonds>. The familiar membrane-forming <phospholipids> are <amphiphilic>: a <hydrophilic> head region with <chemical polarity> coexists with a <hydrophobic>, largely <nonpolar> <hydrocarbon> region. These descriptions refer to different parts of the same <molecule>, not to two separate substances. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04559[IUPAC Gold Book: phospholipids] and https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00303[IUPAC Gold Book: amphiphilic].

A typical two-chain <glycerophospholipid> provides a useful example: a <glycerol> scaffold carries two <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> through <ester> linkages, plus a <phosphate>-containing head region. <Phosphatidylcholine> is one example. This scaffold describes an important subclass, rather than all <phospholipids>: <sphingomyelin> has a different backbone, and even <glycerophospholipids> need not all have two <acyl groups>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/lipid/lip1n2.html[IUPAC–IUB, Nomenclature of Lipids (1976), Lip-1.5 and Lip-2.1–2.6].

\Image[phospholipid-amphiphilic-schematic.png]
{title=Polar head and <hydrophobic> chains of an illustrative <glycerophospholipid>}
{description=Colored regions represent a <phosphate>-containing head, a <glycerol> scaffold, and two <fatty acid>-derived chains. The small paired-chain symbol is shorthand for this illustrative subclass. Shapes and connectors show organization, not <chemical atoms>, exact <chemical bonds>, chain lengths, or a measured <molecular geometry>. The head is not assigned a universal <electric charge>.}

Polar does not mean negatively charged overall. At near-neutral <pH>, <phosphatidylcholine> is <zwitterionic>: its <phosphate> region carries negative <electric charge> and its <choline> region positive <electric charge>, with zero net <electric charge> for the <molecule>. Counting those opposite charges gives $(-1)+(+1)=0$; it does not remove their local interactions with <water>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21726/[Lodish et al., Molecular Cell Biology: membrane lipids].

In a <lipid bilayer>, two <membrane leaflets> orient their <hydrophilic> heads toward the <water> on either side and their <hydrophobic> chains toward the shared interior. The <hydrophobic effect> helps explain this organization through the <free energy> of the <water> and <lipids> together. It is not a special repulsive <chemical bond> between chains and <water>. Which organization is favored also depends on <molecular geometry>, <temperature>, <concentration>, and the surrounding <chemical solution>; a <phospholipid> label alone does not guarantee a <lipid bilayer>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the lipid bilayer].

\Image[phospholipid-bilayer-organization.png]
{title=Two <membrane leaflets> shelter a <hydrophobic> interior between water-facing heads}
{description=An illustrative <phospholipid>-based <lipid bilayer> has <water> on both sides. Individual <phospholipids> can undergo <diffusion> within a fluid <membrane leaflet>. The heads and chains are schematic regions; spacing, arrows, and chain shapes are unscaled. This is a local cross-section, without assigning either side to a particular <biological cell> compartment.}

This organization supplies a flexible barrier for compartments. A fluid <lipid bilayer> allows <lipids> to undergo <diffusion> within each <membrane leaflet>; barrier function therefore coexists with motion of <molecules>, as in the <fluid mosaic model>. It is not completely impermeable: small <nonpolar> <molecules> such as <molecular oxygen> and <carbon dioxide> cross much more readily than <ions>. In a <cell membrane>, <ion channels> and other <membrane transport proteins> provide selective routes; the existing <membrane transport> article explains their driving forces. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/[Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport].

In 1925, E. Gorter and F. Grendel extracted <lipids> from <red blood cells> and compared the <surface area> of a one-<molecule>-thick film on <water> with the estimated total <surface area> of the <red blood cells>. Their table gave ratios near two, supporting a two-layer covering; they also proposed heads facing the aqueous surroundings on both sides. The inference depends on recovering the relevant <lipids>, estimating the <red blood cell> <surface area> correctly, and comparing compatible packing in the film and the <cell membrane>. It was indirect evidence, not an image of the <lipid bilayer>. The paper itself notes possible losses during evaporation. See https://doi.org/10.1084/jem.41.4.439[Gorter and Grendel, On Bimolecular Layers of Lipoids on the Chromocytes of the Blood, Journal of Experimental Medicine 41 (1925), 439–443].

= Glycerophospholipid
{parent=Phospholipid}
{wiki}

<Glycerophospholipids> are <phospholipids> built on a <glycerol>-<phosphate> scaffold. The illustrated two-<acyl group> form is common, but the subclass also includes other substitutions and one-chain derivatives. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/lipid/lip1n2.html[IUPAC–IUB lipid nomenclature, Lip-2.2 and Lip-2.6].

= Phosphatidylcholine
{parent=Glycerophospholipid}
{wiki}

= PC
{c}
{synonym}

<Phosphatidylcholine>, abbreviated <PC>, is a <glycerophospholipid> with a <phosphate>-<choline> head. Its <zwitterionic> head illustrates why a <hydrophilic> region need not have a nonzero net <electric charge>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21726/[Lodish et al.: membrane lipids].

= Sphingomyelin
{parent=Phospholipid}
{wiki}

<Sphingomyelin> is a <phospholipid> whose backbone is based on <sphingosine>, rather than <glycerol>. One long <hydrocarbon> region belongs to that backbone and another comes from an attached <fatty acid>. It is a counterexample to defining every <phospholipid> by the illustrated <glycerol> scaffold. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper, The Cell: the molecular composition of cells].

= Aldehyde
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

An <aldehyde> has a group $\mathrm R\mathrm C(=\mathrm O)\mathrm H$, written $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top of the illustrated open-chain <Fischer projections>; $R$ denotes the attached remainder of the <molecule>.

= Carbohydrate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

<Carbohydrates> are a family of <chemical substances> encompassing <monosaccharides>, combinations of <monosaccharides>, and their derivatives. <Glucose> is a small <carbohydrate>; <cellulose> is a long-chain <polysaccharide>. <Deoxyribose> illustrates why composition alone does not define the family: replacing a <hydroxy group> in <ribose> by <hydrogen> still leaves a <carbohydrate>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00820[IUPAC Gold Book: carbohydrates].

Many familiar <monosaccharides> have a <molecular formula> of the form $\mathrm C_n\mathrm H_{2n}\mathrm O_n$, equivalently $\mathrm C_n(\mathrm H_2\mathrm O)_n$. This explains the historical name, “hydrate of <carbon>”, but does not mean the <molecule> consists of <carbon> mixed with intact <water> <molecules>. Nor is it a universal formula: <deoxyribose> has $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. The family is defined through chemical structure and relationships, not that ratio. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00820[IUPAC's definition and origin of the name carbohydrate].

The useful surprise is the variety of roles obtainable from related building blocks. <Glucose> participates in <metabolism>, <ribose> forms part of <RNA>, and linked <glucose> units make structural <cellulose> in a plant <cell wall>. Understanding <carbohydrates> therefore connects cellular <energy>, molecular information, and material structure. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the chemical components of a cell].

= Sucrose
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}}$}

<Sucrose> dissolves in <water> as neutral <molecules>; it is a <nonelectrolyte> in this <solvent>.

= Sugar
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}

In chemical usage, <sugars> commonly means small <carbohydrates>, including single-unit <monosaccharides> and two-unit <disaccharides>. <Glucose>, <ribose>, and <deoxyribose> are <monosaccharides>. <Cellulose> is instead a <polysaccharide> assembled from many <glucose> units. The distinction counts chemically linked units; it does not classify every <carbohydrate> by sweetness or biological role. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.2: carbohydrates].

A useful first description of a <monosaccharide> asks how many <carbon> <chemical atoms> it contains and what its open-chain structure is. <Ribose> is an <aldopentose>, a five-<carbon> <aldehyde> <sugar>; <glucose> is an <aldohexose>, with six <carbon> <chemical atoms>. A <Fischer projection> then records the <stereochemistry>, information missing from the <molecular formula>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.1: carbohydrate classification].

= Ribose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$}

<Ribose> is a <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$. Its biologically familiar form is D-ribose, an <aldopentose> whose open-chain <Fischer projection> has $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top, $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom, and <hydroxy groups> on the right at C2, C3, and C4. The opposite horizontal substituents are <hydrogen>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-3-d-l-sugars[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.3: D-ribose and D-glucose configurations].

\Image[pentose-sugar-fischer-comparison.png]
{title=Open-chain <Fischer projections> of D-<ribose> and 2-deoxy-D-<ribose>}
{description=At each cross, horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer and vertical <chemical bonds> point away. Both drawings number the free <sugar> from its <aldehyde> carbon, C1. In <deoxyribose>, C2 has two <hydrogen> substituents and is not a <stereocenter>. These are open-chain reference structures, not a depiction of the predominant <chemical species> in <water>.}

Read the drawing by counting groups: $\mathrm{CHO}$ contributes $\mathrm C\mathrm H\mathrm O$, three $\mathrm{CHOH}$ groups contribute $\mathrm C_3\mathrm H_6\mathrm O_3$, and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ contributes $\mathrm C\mathrm H_3\mathrm O$. Their sum is $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$. The count checks composition; the right/right/right pattern additionally specifies the <stereochemistry> of D-<ribose>.

In <RNA>, <ribose> occurs as a ring within each <nucleotide>, joining a <nucleobase> to the <phosphate>-linked backbone. Its C2 position retains a <hydroxy group>, unlike <deoxyribose> in <DNA>. In a <nucleotide>, the <sugar> positions carry primes, so this is C2′. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al.: nucleotide sugars in RNA and DNA]. Free <ribose> in <water> is also predominantly cyclic, with several ring forms in <chemical equilibrium> and very little open-chain form; the <Fischer projection> is valuable for comparing configurations, not for estimating their abundances. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3819725/[Mason et al., The Conformation of a Ribose Derivative in Aqueous Solution: A Neutron Scattering and Molecular Dynamics Study (2013), introduction].

= Deoxyribose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$}

In the usual <DNA> context, <deoxyribose> means 2-deoxy-D-<ribose>, a five-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. Relative to D-<ribose>, the <hydroxy group> at C2 is replaced by <hydrogen>. The locant 2 matters: it identifies which position loses its <oxygen>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P10.html#P-102.5.3[Favre and Powell, IUPAC Blue Book, P-102.5.3: deoxy sugars] and https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/class/nucle.html[IUPAC: nucleic acids and their component sugars].

The paired <Fischer projections> in <ribose> make the change concrete. C2 has <hydrogen> on both sides, while C3 and C4 retain their <hydroxy groups> on the right. C2 is therefore not a <stereocenter>: exchanging its two identical <hydrogen> substituents cannot produce a distinct configuration. The unchanged C3 and C4 still carry <stereochemical> information.

Replacing $\mathrm{OH}$ by $\mathrm H$ removes one <oxygen> but leaves the total <hydrogen> count unchanged: the <hydrogen> belonging to the removed <hydroxy group> is replaced by a <hydrogen> bonded directly to <carbon>. Thus $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$ becomes $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. This comparison describes structures, rather than a complete <chemical reaction> or its mechanism.

In <DNA>, <deoxyribose> is incorporated as a ring in each <nucleotide>, with <phosphate> connections building the backbone. Its C2′ bears <hydrogen> where <RNA>'s <ribose> bears a <hydroxy group>. This small local difference lets the <sugar> structure identify the two kinds of <nucleic acid>; the <nucleobases> supply their sequence information. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al.: nucleotides as the subunits of DNA and RNA].

= Glucose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_6\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{O}_6$}

<Glucose> is a <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$. Its familiar biological form, D-glucose, is an <aldohexose>. In the open-chain <Fischer projection>, $\mathrm{CHO}$ is at the top and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom; the <hydroxy groups> at C2, C3, C4, and C5 point right, left, right, right. This full pattern distinguishes D-<glucose> from other six-<carbon> <aldehyde> <sugars>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-4-configurations-of-the-aldoses[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.4: configurations of the aldoses].

\Image[glucose-fischer-projection.png]
{title=D-<glucose>: six <carbon> positions and four open-chain <stereocenters>}
{description=Horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer; vertical <chemical bonds> point away. The <hydroxy group> pattern from C2 through C5 is right/left/right/right. $\mathrm{CHO}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ are condensed end groups. The diagram records an open-chain reference structure, not the predominant form in an aqueous <chemical solution>.}

The <molecular formula> can be checked directly: one $\mathrm{CHO}$ group, four $\mathrm{CHOH}$ groups, and one $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ group give six <carbon>, twelve <hydrogen>, and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. All four interior <carbon> positions are <stereocenters> in this open-chain structure. Neither their orientations nor the arrangement of <chemical bonds> can be recovered from the <molecular formula> alone.

In <water>, <glucose> exists mainly in six-membered rings in <chemical equilibrium> with a small amount of open-chain <glucose>. Ring closure connects the <hydroxy group> at C5 to the <aldehyde> carbon at C1; the ring contains five <carbon> <chemical atoms> and one <oxygen>, with C6 outside it. No <chemical atoms> are lost, so the <molecular formula> remains $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-5-cyclic-structures-of-monosaccharides-anomers[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.5: cyclic structures of monosaccharides].

<Glucose> is useful both as a substrate in cellular <metabolism> and as a building block of <polysaccharides>. <Cellulose> chains assembled from <glucose> units help support plant <cell walls>. The same small <sugar> thus connects chemical fuel to a structural material; its role depends on how it is transformed or linked. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper, The Cell: A Molecular Approach, the molecular composition of cells].

= Monosaccharide
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}

A <monosaccharide> is a single-unit <carbohydrate> that cannot be split into smaller <sugars> by <hydrolysis>. <Ribose>, <deoxyribose>, and <glucose> are examples. “Single-unit” does not mean a single <chemical atom> or a permanently open chain. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates[McMurry: simple carbohydrates].

= Aldopentose
{parent=Monosaccharide}

An <aldopentose> is a five-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with an <aldehyde> group in its open-chain form. <Ribose> is an example; <deoxyribose> is its 2-deoxy derivative. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates[McMurry: carbohydrate classification].

= Aldohexose
{parent=Monosaccharide}

An <aldohexose> is a six-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with an <aldehyde> group in its open-chain form. <Glucose> is an example. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates[McMurry: carbohydrate classification].

= Disaccharide
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}

A <disaccharide> contains two linked <monosaccharide> units. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Biology 2e: disaccharides].

= Polysaccharide
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}

A <polysaccharide> is a <polymer> built from many linked <monosaccharide> units. <Cellulose> provides a structural example. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Biology 2e: polysaccharides].

= Cellulose
{parent=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

<Cellulose> is a <polysaccharide> of <glucose> units whose chains form strong assemblies in plant <cell walls>. It illustrates a structural role for <carbohydrates>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26928/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the plant cell wall].

= Triose phosphate
{parent=Carbohydrate}

A <triose phosphate> is a three-<carbon> <sugar> carrying a <phosphate> group. <Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate> is one example. The <Calvin cycle> supplies a net <triose phosphate> for further <carbohydrate> synthesis. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: the Calvin cycle's carbohydrate product]{ref}.

= Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate
{parent=Triose phosphate}
{wiki}

= G3P
{c}
{synonym}

= Phosphate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Inorganic phosphate
{parent=Phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm P_i$}

<Inorganic phosphate>, abbreviated $\mathrm P_i$, denotes the free <phosphate> pool in biochemical <chemical equations>, including <ATP> <hydrolysis>. Its constituent <chemical species> depend on <pH> and <protonation>; $\mathrm P_i$ does not specify a single <electric charge> or proton count. See the https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/thermod2/[IUBMB recommendations on biochemical reactant pools].

= Chemical formula
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <chemical formula> uses symbols of <chemical elements> and numerical subscripts to record composition. In the <molecular formula> $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ of <water>, the subscript $2$ counts two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and the omitted subscript after $\mathrm O$ means one <oxygen> <chemical atom>. A <molecular formula> describes one <molecule>; a <chemical formula> need not describe a discrete <molecule>.

Read a preceding coefficient separately from the subscripts. One $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ contains two <hydrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>; $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ contains four <hydrogen> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> distributed between two <water> <molecules>. In $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$, the second subscript instead puts two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> into each <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Thus $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\ne\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$: the coefficient scales the whole <chemical formula>, while a subscript specifies its composition. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-4-chemical-formulas[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §2.4: chemical formulas].

\Image[chemical-formula-and-coefficient.png]
{title=A coefficient counts whole <molecules>; a subscript counts <chemical atoms> within each <molecule>}
{description=The three columns show one <water> <molecule>, two <water> <molecules>, and one <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Their <hydrogen> counts are $2,4,2$ and their <oxygen> counts are $1,2,2$. Connections are schematic $\mathrm{H-O-H}$ and $\mathrm{H-O-O-H}$, not models of actual <bond angles> or <bond lengths>.}

For crystalline $\mathrm{NaCl}$, the <chemical formula> specifies a $1:1$ ratio of <sodium> <ions> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ to <chlorine> <ions> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$. Its <formula unit> is the accounting entity; the ordinary crystal is an extended arrangement of <ions>, rather than a collection of discrete $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <molecules>. Specifying the entity matters when assigning an <amount of substance>. See <formula unit> and <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/2.6 Ionic and Molecular Compounds> (§2.6: ionic and molecular compounds).

A <molecular formula> gives an atom inventory, not the full arrangement of <chemical bonds> or the <molecular geometry>. For example, $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$ counts six <carbon>, twelve <hydrogen>, and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms> in a <glucose> <molecule>, but does not specify the pattern shown by its <Fischer projection>. Different structures can share a <molecular formula>. This compact inventory is useful for <chemical equation> accounting precisely because it leaves the structural detail to other representations.

The choice of letters has an early explanation in https://web2.lemoyne.edu/~giunta/berzelius.html[Jöns Jacob Berzelius, Essay on the Cause of Chemical Proportions (1813–1814), §III, transcribed by Carmen Giunta]: letters made chemical signs easier to write and print, and formulas compressed information about composition. His numerical notation differed from modern subscripts; the lasting idea was to make proportions readable in ordinary text.

= Molecular formula
{parent=Chemical formula}

A molecular formula gives the number of <chemical atoms> of each <chemical element> in a <molecule>. For <urea>, $\mathrm{CH}_4\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$ counts one <carbon>, four <hydrogen>, two <nitrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>. It records composition rather than the arrangement of <chemical bonds>.

= Formula unit
{parent=Chemical formula}
{wiki}

A <formula unit> is a specified compositional accounting entity for a <chemical substance> that need not consist of discrete <molecules>. For ordinary crystalline $\mathrm{NaCl}$, one <formula unit> counts one <sodium> <ion> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and one <chlorine> <ion> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$; it does not identify an isolated pair within the crystal. https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/IUPAC-GB3-2012-2ndPrinting-PDFsearchable.pdf#page=65[Cohen et al., IUPAC Green Book, 3rd ed., §2.10.1(iii)(b), p. 51] defines a <formula unit> as a specified group of <chemical atoms>, rather than a unit of measurement. An <amount of substance> of one <mole> of $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <formula units> therefore corresponds to one <mole> of each kind of <ion>.

= Organic acid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Carboxylic acid
{parent=Organic acid}
{wiki}

= Acetic acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$}

<Acetic acid> is a <weak acid> that partly transfers <protons> to <water>, supplying <hydronium> and negative <ions>. It is therefore a <weak electrolyte> in <water>.

= Acetate ion
{parent=Acetic acid}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}$}

The <conjugate base> of <acetic acid>. It is a <reaction product> in the <conductometric saponification assay>.

= Fatty acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}

A <fatty acid> is a <carboxylic acid> with a <hydrocarbon> chain. Its chain supplies much of the <hydrophobic> region when incorporated into a <triglyceride> or typical <glycerophospholipid>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: fatty acids].

= Amino acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}

An <amino acid> combines <carboxylic acid> and <amine> functionality in the same <molecule>; the name alone does not specify their separation. An <alpha-amino acid> has its amino <nitrogen> attached to the <carbon> immediately adjacent to the carboxyl <carbon>, the <amino acid alpha carbon>. For example, $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}COOH}$ is <glycine>, whereas $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}CH_2{-}COOH}$ has an extra intervening <carbon> and is not an <alpha-amino acid>. These <chemical formulas> describe connectivity, not the predominant <protonation> state in <water>.

Most common <proteinogenic amino acids> share the skeleton $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH}(R)\mathrm{{-}COOH}$, with $R$ the <amino acid side chain>. In <glycine>, $R$ is <hydrogen>; in <alanine>, $R=\mathrm{CH_3}$. <Proline> is a useful exception to the displayed primary-<amine> skeleton: its amino <nitrogen> is part of a ring and has a second <carbon> substituent. The shared backbone permits assembly into <polypeptides>, while different <amino acid side chains> give the resulting <proteins> diverse chemistry. <Nomenclature and Symbolism for Amino Acids and Peptides (Recommendations 1983) by IUPAC-IUB Joint Commission on Biochemical Nomenclature>[3AA-1, Table 1: glycine, alanine and proline structures; 3AA-2.2.5, backbone and side chain]{ref}

In a <chemical solution>, <protonation> changes the <electric charges> without changing this backbone. Free <glycine> near neutral <pH> is predominantly $\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH_2{-}COO^-}$, a <zwitterion>. Its positive and negative groups are joined within one <molecule>; zero net <electric charge> does not mean absence of local <electric charges>, nor does it mean the uncharged drawing $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}COOH}$ predominates. <Glycine protonation> explains how lowering or raising <pH> changes the balance. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, opening zwitterion and acid/base interconversion discussion]{ref}

Building a <protein> joins the carboxyl <carbon> of one <amino acid> to the amino <nitrogen> of another through a <peptide bond>, an <amide> linkage. The formal net condensation loses the elements of <water>; cellular <translation> uses activated substrates at a <ribosome>, rather than simply mixing free <amino acids> in <water>. Incorporated units are <amino acid residues>. The interior <amide> groups no longer have the same acid/base behavior as the two free terminal groups of <glycine>, so its free-<molecule> <glycine titration> cannot be copied independently at every chain position. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.4, opening peptide formation and amide resonance discussion]{ref}

<Chirality (chemistry)> distinguishes most common <proteinogenic amino acids> from <glycine>. The other nineteen members of the conventional twenty have a <stereocenter> at their <amino acid alpha carbon>; ordinary <translation> incorporates their L forms. <Glycine>'s two identical <hydrogen> substituents make it achiral. Thus being an <amino acid>, or even an <alpha-amino acid>, does not by itself imply <chirality (chemistry)>. <Nomenclature and Symbolism for Amino Acids and Peptides (Recommendations 1983) by IUPAC-IUB Joint Commission on Biochemical Nomenclature>[3AA-3.2, note: glycine itself is achiral; 3AA-3.3 and 3AA-3.5, protein-source L configuration]{ref}

= Methionine
{parent=Amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Met},\ \mathrm M$}

Methionine is an <amino acid> represented by Met or M in <protein> sequence notation. In the standard <genetic code>, AUG encodes it, as in the worked <gene expression> example. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[OpenStax Biology 2e, 15.1: the standard genetic code]{ref}

= Alanine
{parent=Amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ala},\ \mathrm A$}

Alanine is an <amino acid> represented by Ala or A in <protein> sequence notation. GCU is one of its <codons> in the standard <genetic code>, and supplies the second residue in the worked <gene expression> example. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[OpenStax Biology 2e, 15.1: alanine codons]{ref}

= Alpha-amino acid
{parent=Amino acid}

An <alpha-amino acid> has amino functionality on the <carbon> next to its carboxyl <carbon>. The skeleton in <amino acid> shows this adjacency explicitly. Numbering the carboxyl <carbon> as C1 makes the adjacent position C2, also called the alpha position. <Nomenclature and Symbolism for Amino Acids and Peptides (Recommendations 1983) by IUPAC-IUB Joint Commission on Biochemical Nomenclature>[3AA-1, Table 1 and its note on hypothetical uncharged formulas; 3AA-2.2.1, acyclic-amino-acid numbering]{ref}

= Proteinogenic amino acid
{parent=Alpha-amino acid}
{wiki}

A <proteinogenic amino acid> is incorporated into <proteins> during <translation>. The conventional twenty include <glycine>, <alanine>, <methionine> and <proline>; they are a subset of <amino acids>, not a definition of the whole family. <Nomenclature and Symbolism for Amino Acids and Peptides (Recommendations 1983) by IUPAC-IUB Joint Commission on Biochemical Nomenclature>[3AA-1, Table 1: alpha-amino acids incorporated into protein under mRNA direction]{ref}

= Proline
{parent=Proteinogenic amino acid}
{wiki}

<Proline> is a cyclic <alpha-amino acid> whose <amine> <nitrogen> is joined to two <carbon> positions within a five-membered ring. This explains the exception to $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH}(R)\mathrm{{-}COOH}$ in <amino acid>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, primary alpha-amino-acid and secondary proline structures]{ref}

= Glycine protonation
{parent=Alpha-amino acid}

The three principal aqueous <glycine> forms differ by loss of one <proton> at each step:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH_2{-}COOH}+\mathrm H_2\mathrm O&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH_2{-}COO^-}+\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+,\\
\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH_2{-}COO^-}+\mathrm H_2\mathrm O&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}COO^-}+\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+.
\end{aligned}
$$
Call these <glycine> forms $G^+$, $G^0$ and $G^-$, respectively: a <cation>, a <zwitterion> and an <anion>, with net <electric charges> $+e$, $0$ and $-e$, where $e$ is the <elementary charge>. $G^0$ specifically denotes the <zwitterion>, not an uncharged amino/carboxyl drawing. The first dissociation removes the carboxyl <proton>; the second removes a <proton> from the positively charged <nitrogen>. The <chemical equilibrium> changes are reversible, not three permanently separate kinds of <glycine>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, aqueous zwitterion protonation/deprotonation; §26.2, two successive amino-acid dissociations]{ref}

For all calculations and both diagrams here, explicitly stipulate the <pKa> values $\mathrm pK_{a1}=2.35$ and $\mathrm pK_{a2}=9.78$. These are illustrative model inputs, not measurements from the <glycine titration experiment> or a claim of universal constants. Actual <acid dissociation constants> depend on <temperature> and on the convention used for <thermodynamic activity>; apparent <concentration>-based values also depend on <ionic strength>. For comparison, the inspected textbook tabulates $2.34$ and $9.60$ for <glycine>, which would give a different <isoelectric point>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, Table 26.1, glycine row: pKa 2.34, 9.60 and pI 5.97]{ref}

\Image[glycine-protonation-speciation-model.png]
{title=<Glycine protonation> and calculated <chemical species> fractions}
{description=Original three-state ideal <glycine speciation model>, with stipulated <pKa> values $2.35$ and $9.78$. The structures show net <electric charges> $+1$, $0$ and $-1$ in units of the <elementary charge>. Curves are calculated fractions, not measured populations. The vertical markers locate the two <pKa> values and the model <isoelectric point> $6.065$.}

= Glycine speciation model
{parent=Glycine protonation}

In an ideal aqueous <chemical solution>, set $h=[\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+]/c^\circ=10^{-\mathrm{pH}}$, with $c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, and approximate <activity coefficients> by one. Put $K_1=10^{-2.35}$ and $K_2=10^{-9.78}$ for the stipulated <dimensionless> <acid dissociation constants>. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> gives the adjacent-state <ratios>
$$
\frac{[G^0]}{[G^+]}=\frac{K_1}{h},\qquad\frac{[G^-]}{[G^0]}=\frac{K_2}{h}.
$$
With <analytical concentration> $C_T=[G^+]+[G^0]+[G^-]$, normalization gives the three fractions and mean <electric charge> in units of the <elementary charge>:
$$
D=h^2+K_1h+K_1K_2,\qquad
\alpha_+=\frac{h^2}{D},\quad\alpha_0=\frac{K_1h}{D},\quad\alpha_-=\frac{K_1K_2}{D},\qquad
\bar z=\alpha_+-\alpha_-.
$$
All three fractions are nonnegative and sum to one. At <pH> $7$, the model gives about $99.832\%$ <zwitterion>, $0.00224\%$ <cation> and $0.1657\%$ <anion>; $\bar z\approx-0.00163$. Predominantly <zwitterionic> therefore need not mean exactly at the <isoelectric point>.

Setting $\bar z=0$ requires $h^2=K_1K_2$. Taking the <logarithm> yields
$$
\mathrm{pI}=\frac{\mathrm pK_{a1}+\mathrm pK_{a2}}2=\frac{2.35+9.78}2=6.065.
$$
At this model <isoelectric point>, $\alpha_+=\alpha_-\approx0.0001927$ and $\alpha_0\approx0.9996146$: about $0.0193\%$ of each charged form remains. At either <pKa>, the relevant adjacent fractions are equal, but the third fraction is not mathematically zero. This three-state model neglects the minor uncharged form $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}COOH}$; macroscopic <pKa> values alone do not determine its fraction separately from the <zwitterion>.

= Glycine titration
{parent=Glycine protonation}

Adding <sodium hydroxide> to acidified <glycine> successively removes its two titratable <protons>. Two broad <buffer solution> regions appear around the two <pKa> values, separated by a steep <pH> rise near the <isoelectric point>. This is useful evidence for two separated <acid-base chemistry> steps even though the changing <chemical species> are invisible. In the approximation that added <hydroxide> only changes <glycine protonation>, $b=1-\bar z$ is the added base <amount of substance> per initial <amount of substance> of <glycine>. Then $b\approx0.5$, $1$ and $1.5$ correspond to $\mathrm pK_{a1}$, $\mathrm{pI}$ and $\mathrm pK_{a2}$; endpoints are not exactly pure <chemical species>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.2, Figure 26.2: calculated two-leg titration and half-equivalent interpretation]{ref}

\Image[glycine-titration-mean-charge-model.png]
{title=Calculated <glycine titration> and mean <electric charge> models}
{description=Upper panel: solid curve includes <self-ionization of water> and <dilution> for $20\,\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.100\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <glycine> with one equivalent of <hydrochloric acid>, titrated with $0.100\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <sodium hydroxide>. Dashed curve retains only the <glycine> proton inventory. Lower panel: $\bar z$ against <pH> from the <glycine speciation model>. Both are stipulated ideal models, not laboratory data.}

For the solid-curve model, start with $n_G=0.00200\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <glycine> in $V_0=0.0200\,\mathrm L$ and exactly $n_G$ of fully dissociated <hydrochloric acid>; this fixes the <chloride> inventory, but does not force every <glycine> <molecule> to remain $G^+$. Let the <sodium hydroxide> <concentration> be $C_B=0.100\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and $b=n_{\mathrm{NaOH}}/n_G$. Assume <volume (thermodynamics)> is additive and no other <ions> are present; stipulate $K_w=10^{-14}$ for <self-ionization of water> at a <temperature> of $25\,\mathrm{{}^\circ C}$. Then
$$
V=V_0+\frac{bn_G}{C_B},\qquad C_T=\frac{n_G}{V},\qquad
[\mathrm{Na^+}]=bC_T,\qquad[\mathrm{Cl^-}]=C_T.
$$
Balancing all positive and negative <electric charges> requires
$$
c^\circ\left(h-\frac{K_w}{h}\right)+C_T\left(b+\bar z-1\right)=0.
$$
Solving this equation gives the solid curve. It remains finite at $b=0$ and beyond $b=2$, where ignoring free <hydronium> and <hydroxide> would fail. At the first <pKa>, the full model gives $b\approx0.436$, rather than $0.5$: appreciable free <hydronium> makes that half-equivalent shortcut inaccurate. At the second <pKa>, $b\approx1.502$. Known acid inventory, <dilution>, and <thermodynamic activity> assumptions are therefore part of any quantitative interpretation, not optional corrections inferred from the curve's shape.

= Glycine titration experiment
{parent=Glycine titration}

The Amrita Virtual Lab procedure uses $0.1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <glycine>, <hydrochloric acid> and <sodium hydroxide>, two <burettes>, a <pH> meter, beaker and stirrer. A <pipette> transfers $20\,\mathrm{mL}$ of <glycine> into a $100\,\mathrm{mL}$ beaker. Calibrate the meter with standard <buffer solutions> at <pH> $4$, $7$ and $10$, then record the starting <pH>. Add <hydrochloric acid> in $0.3\,\mathrm{mL}$ increments, recording <pH> after each addition until $1.6$. Rinse the <electrode> in distilled <water>, take a separate fresh $20\,\mathrm{mL}$ portion, and add <sodium hydroxide> in $0.3\,\mathrm{mL}$ increments until <pH> $12.5$. Its observed readout is paired added <volume (thermodynamics)> and <pH>, plotted as an <acid-base titration> curve; it does not measure each <glycine> form directly. <Titration Curves of Aminoacids, Amrita Virtual Lab/Procedure>[Materials Required, items 1–8; Working steps, items 1–9: separate acid and base titrations]{ref}

These are two titrations from the starting <glycine> <chemical solution>, rather than the single acidified-sample protocol assumed by the solid curve in <glycine titration>. Retain each portion's initial <volume (thermodynamics)> and the signed added acid/base <amount of substance>; do not concatenate raw <burette> readings and assume the result begins at $b=0$. For this procedure's $0.00200\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <glycine>, each $0.3\,\mathrm{mL}$ addition supplies $0.000030\,\mathrm{mol}$ of acid or base, a $0.015$ increment relative to the <glycine> inventory. This calculated resolution is not a reported measurement.

Useful additional controls are a fresh repeat portion, independently checked acid/base <concentrations>, a <water> blank, and a calibration-check <buffer solution> after the run. Stir without striking the <glass electrode>, keep its sensing region immersed and wait for stable readings; record <temperature> and background salts. Reduce addition size near a steep <pH> change. These are suggested controls for testing the model, beyond the published steps. A <water> blank can reveal contamination but must not be subtracted directly from <pH>, which is a <logarithm>.

The standards at <pH> $4$, $7$ and $10$ do not bracket either endpoint of this protocol. Extreme readings therefore require independently checked instrument performance, and alkaline samples are sensitive to atmospheric <carbon dioxide> uptake. <Electrode> drift, incomplete mixing, <volume (thermodynamics)> error, <dilution> and changing <ionic strength> can shift apparent <pKa> values. A titration constrains the acid/base model; it cannot establish <chirality (chemistry)> or separate every microscopic <protonation> form. The two original diagrams show calculated predictions to compare with measured points, not reconstructed experimental data.

= Citric acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_8\mathrm O_7$}

Its <chemical formula> and structure are recorded in https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:30769[ChEBI, citric acid].

= Citrate
{parent=Citric acid}

Citrate denotes an <ion> derived from <citric acid>. The commonly specified form has <electric charge> equal to that of three <electrons> and <chemical formula> $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_5\mathrm O_7^{3-}$; it results from removing three <hydrogen> <ions> from <citric acid>. Forms with additional <hydrogen> <ions> attached through <protonation> also occur. https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:16947[ChEBI, citrate(3−)]{ref}

= Hydrogen deuteride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{HD}$}

The diatomic molecule containing one ordinary hydrogen nucleus and one deuterium nucleus is an isotopic form of <molecular hydrogen>. It has a weak electric dipole and can supply low-temperature <molecular line cooling> in primordial gas when its abundance is sufficient.

= Chemical element
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <chemical element> is an identity shared by all <chemical atoms> whose <atomic nuclei> contain the same number of <protons>. That number is its <atomic number>, $Z$: six means <carbon>, eleven means <sodium>. The identity survives differences in <neutron> count, which distinguish <isotopes>, and differences in <electron> count, which distinguish neutral <chemical atoms> from <ions>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01022[IUPAC Gold Book, chemical element].

The same name can also denote a pure <chemical substance> containing only that <chemical element>. These two uses answer different questions. <Molecular oxygen>, $\mathrm O_2$, is a <chemical substance> made entirely of <oxygen>; <water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, is a <chemical compound> containing both <hydrogen> and <oxygen>. A <water> <molecule> contains three <chemical atoms> but only two <chemical elements>. Joining <chemical atoms> through <chemical bonds> changes their arrangement without changing their <atomic numbers>.

This distinction makes <stoichiometry> possible: ordinary <chemical reactions> rearrange <chemical atoms>, so one can count each <chemical element> before and after a transformation. Changing the <proton> count instead changes the <chemical element> and requires a <nuclear reaction>. The <periodic table> organizes these identities into a useful map, while <atomic number> bookkeeping keeps identity, <isotope>, and <electric charge> separate.

= Tin
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Sn}$}

= Tungsten
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm W$}

= Mercury
{disambiguate=element}
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Hg}$}

= Platinum
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Pt}$}

= Zinc
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Zn}$}

= Copper
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cu}$}

= Periodic table
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}

The <periodic table> arranges <chemical elements> in increasing <atomic number>, across successive <periodic table periods>. Its columns are <periodic groups>, numbered 1–18. The layout places recurring outer <electron configurations> above one another, making similarities in <chemical bonds> and <chemical reactions> easier to recognize. Ordering is by <proton> count, not by <mass>; the <mass number> of a particular <isotope> does not determine its box. See the https://iupac.org/what-we-do/periodic-table-of-elements/[official IUPAC periodic table and numbering conventions].

For the first eighteen neutral <chemical atoms> in their <ground states>, a compact description counts <electrons> in successive <electron shells>. Thus <carbon> has $(2,4)$, meaning two <electrons> in the first <electron shell> and four in the second; the sum $2+4=6$ agrees with its <atomic number>. <Sodium> has $(2,8,1)$, whose sum is $11$. These are occupancy counts, not circular trajectories of <electrons>.

\Image[first-three-periods.png]
{title=The first three <periodic table periods>: <atomic numbers> and neutral <ground state> <electron shell> occupancies}
{description=Colours distinguish <periodic group> columns only, without encoding numerical property trends. The spaces in columns 3–12 preserve the eighteen-column <periodic table> layout: no <chemical elements> occupy those positions in these three <periodic table periods>. Each cell gives a <chemical element>'s symbol, <atomic number>, and <electron shell> counts.}

Reading each row from left to right gives:
* <Periodic table period> 1: <hydrogen> $(1)$ and <helium> $(2)$.
* <Periodic table period> 2: <lithium> $(2,1)$, <beryllium> $(2,2)$, <boron> $(2,3)$, <carbon> $(2,4)$, <nitrogen> $(2,5)$, <oxygen> $(2,6)$, <fluorine> $(2,7)$, and <neon> $(2,8)$.
* <Periodic table period> 3: <sodium> $(2,8,1)$, <magnesium> $(2,8,2)$, <aluminium> $(2,8,3)$, <silicon> $(2,8,4)$, <phosphorus> $(2,8,5)$, <sulfur> $(2,8,6)$, <chlorine> $(2,8,7)$, and <argon> $(2,8,8)$.

The counting outline is short: the occupied <electron subshells> in this range are $1s$, $2s$, $2p$, $3s$, and $3p$, filled in that order for these neutral <ground states>. Each $s$ <electron subshell> holds up to two <electrons> and each $p$ <electron subshell> up to six, giving $2$, $2+6=8$, and $2+6=8$ along these three rows. This $2,8,8$ summary applies only through <argon>; it is not a general rule that each <electron shell> fills completely before the next begins, nor a claim that the third <electron shell> can hold only eight <electrons>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, electronic structure and electron configurations].

The recurring outer counts make the arrangement useful. <Lithium> and <sodium> each have one <valence electron>, whereas <fluorine> and <chlorine> each have seven <valence electrons>. Such similarities help anticipate <chemical reactions>, without making all properties identical throughout a <periodic group>. <Helium> is an instructive exception to an eight-<electron> shortcut: its two <electrons> complete the first <electron shell>, so it belongs above <neon> and <argon> in <periodic group> 18. <Hydrogen> shares column 1 with <lithium> and <sodium> but has its own distinct chemistry. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-5-the-periodic-table[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, periodic-table organization].

= Group
{disambiguate=periodic table}
{parent=Periodic table}
{wiki}

= Periodic group
{synonym}

= Periodic table group
{synonym}

A <periodic group> is a vertical column of the <periodic table>. For example, <fluorine> and <chlorine> are in <periodic group> 17, with seven <valence electrons> in each neutral <ground state> <chemical atom>. A shared outer <electron configuration> supports similarities in <chemical reactions>, rather than equality of every property.

= Period
{disambiguate=periodic table}
{parent=Periodic table}
{wiki}

= Periodic table period
{synonym}

A <periodic table period> is a horizontal row of the <periodic table>, read in increasing <atomic number>. <Periodic table period> 2 runs from <lithium>, $Z=3$, to <neon>, $Z=10$: $10-3+1=8$ <chemical elements>. In these neutral <ground states>, the second <electron shell> gains <electrons> while the first retains two.

= Boron
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm B$, $Z=5$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> five.

= Fluorine
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm F$, $Z=9$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> nine.

= Aluminium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Al}$, $Z=13$}
{wiki}

= Aluminum
{synonym}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> thirteen.

= Phosphorus
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm P$, $Z=15$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> fifteen.

= Sulfur
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm S$, $Z=16$}
{wiki}

= Sulphur
{synonym}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> sixteen.

= Chlorine
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cl}$, $Z=17$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> seventeen.

= Argon
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ar}$, $Z=18$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> eighteen.

= Sodium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Na}$}

Sodium is the <chemical element> of <atomic number> eleven. Carbon-fusion channels in <carbon burning> include ${}^{12}\mathrm C+{}^{12}\mathrm C\longrightarrow{}^{23}\mathrm{Na}+p$, producing sodium-23 and a <proton>. Its abundance in expelled stellar material depends on later reactions and mixing, rather than on this one production channel alone.

= Calcium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ca}$}
{wiki}

= Potassium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{K}$}
{wiki}

= Atomic number
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$Z$}
{wiki}

The <atomic number> $Z$ is the number of <protons> in an <atomic nucleus>, and fixes the <chemical element>. It is a count, with no units. An <atomic nucleus> with six <protons> is always <carbon>, regardless of how many <neutrons> accompany them or how many <electrons> surround it. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00499[IUPAC Gold Book, atomic number].

Three independent bookkeeping questions become easy once their symbols are kept separate. Let $N$ count <neutrons>, $n_e$ count <electrons>, $A$ be the <mass number>, and $Q$ be the net <electric charge>. A <proton> carries <electric charge> $+e$ and an <electron> carries $-e$, where $e$ is the positive <elementary charge>; a <neutron> carries zero net <electric charge>. Hence
$$
A=Z+N,\qquad N=A-Z,\qquad Q=(Z-n_e)e,\qquad n_e=Z-Q/e.
$$
The reasoning is just counting: add <protons> and <neutrons> for $A$, and subtract negative <electron> contributions from positive <proton> contributions for $Q$. A neutral <chemical atom> has $Q=0$, so $n_e=Z$. For a positively charged <ion>, fewer <electrons> remain. Notation ${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$ gives the <mass number> at upper left and <atomic number> at lower left, with $\mathrm X$ standing for the <chemical element>; a right superscript such as $+$ specifies the <electric charge> in units of $e$. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-3-atomic-structure-and-symbolism[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, atomic structure and symbolism].

For neutral <carbon-12>, ${}^{12}_{6}\mathrm C$, the <atomic number> gives six <protons>, the subtraction $N=12-6=6$ gives six <neutrons>, and neutrality gives six <electrons>. Neutral <carbon-13>, ${}^{13}_{6}\mathrm C$, instead has $N=13-6=7$ <neutrons>, with the same six <protons> and six <electrons>. Thus these two <isotopes> occupy the same <carbon> box in the <periodic table>.

For neutral <sodium> of <mass number> $23$, ${}^{23}_{11}\mathrm{Na}$, the counts are eleven <protons>, $23-11=12$ <neutrons>, and eleven <electrons>. Removing one <electron> gives the <ion> ${}^{23}_{11}\mathrm{Na}^{+}$: $Q/e=1$, so $n_e=11-1=10$. Both its <atomic number> and its twelve <neutrons> stay unchanged. This is <ionization>, not conversion into the <chemical element> <neon>, even though a neutral <neon> <chemical atom> also has ten <electrons>.

\Image[atomic-number-isotopes-and-ions.png]
{title=<Atomic number> fixes <chemical element> identity: changing <isotopes> changes <neutrons>, while forming a <sodium> <ion> changes <electrons>}
{description=The dots are counting tokens, with <protons> and <neutrons> in an <atomic nucleus> inventory and <electrons> in a separate inventory. Their layout does not depict physical positions, sizes, or orbits. Neutral <carbon-12> and <carbon-13> differ by one <neutron>; neutral <sodium> of <mass number> 23 and its singly positive <ion> differ by one <electron>.}

This bookkeeping is useful whenever a symbol must be translated into a physical inventory. <Mass number> answers how many <protons> plus <neutrons> there are; <electric charge> answers the imbalance between <protons> and <electrons>; <atomic number> alone answers which <chemical element> it is. Keeping those questions distinct prevents an <isotope> or an <ion> from being mistaken for a different <chemical element>.

= Beryllium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Be}$}
{wiki}

Beryllium is the <chemical element> with atomic number four. Its isotope <beryllium-7> provides an important route to primordial <lithium-7>.

= Beryllium-7
{parent=Beryllium}
{title2=$^7\mathrm{Be}$}

Beryllium-7 contains four <protons> and three <neutrons>. It forms in reactions including $^3\mathrm{He}+{}^4\mathrm{He}\to{}^7\mathrm{Be}+\gamma$ and becomes <lithium-7> through <electron capture>. In the early universe its ionization state affects when capture occurs, so the final primordial lithium inventory includes the earlier beryllium inventory rather than just lithium present when nuclear burning ends.

= Lithium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Li}$}
{wiki}

Lithium is the <chemical element> with atomic number three. Its stable isotopes include lithium-6 and <lithium-7>. Lithium observed in old stellar atmospheres is an important but processing-sensitive test of <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Lithium-7
{parent=Lithium}
{title2=$^7\mathrm{Li}$}

Lithium-7 has three <protons> and four <neutrons>. In <Big Bang nucleosynthesis> it can form directly or through <beryllium-7>, which later undergoes <electron capture>. Competition between direct lithium production, lithium destruction and beryllium production produces a valley in the primordial lithium abundance versus <baryon-to-photon ratio>. Its measured stellar surface abundance need not equal its birth abundance because stellar transport and nuclear burning can remove lithium.

= Nuclide
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$}
{wiki}

A nuclear species specified by <atomic number>, <mass number>, and nuclear <energy> state. <Isotopes> compare <nuclides> with the same <atomic number>; changing only the nuclear <energy> state does not create a different <isotope>.

= Isotope
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$}
{wiki}

<Isotopes> of a <chemical element> have the same <atomic number> $Z$, hence the same number of <protons>, but different <neutron numbers> $N$ and therefore different <mass numbers> $A=Z+N$. For example, every <neon> <atomic nucleus> contains ten <protons>: <neon-20>, <neon-21>, and <neon-22> contain ten, eleven, and twelve <neutrons>, respectively. They occupy one <chemical element> box in the <periodic table>, rather than three boxes.

\Image[isotope-nucleus-neon-neutron-counts.png]
{title=<Neon> <isotopes>: the same ten <protons> with different <neutron numbers>}
{description=Every dot counts one <nucleon>. The arrays inventory <atomic nuclei>; they do not model the positions or sizes of <protons> and <neutrons>. The <mass numbers> 20, 21, and 22 are exact counts.}

Three labels answer different questions. The <chemical element> fixes $Z$; the <isotope> specifies which <neutron number> accompanies that $Z$; the <ion> label specifies the net <electric charge>. Neutral ${}^{22}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$ and the <ion> ${}^{22}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}^{+}$ both contain ten <protons> and twelve <neutrons>; they contain ten and nine <electrons>, respectively. Ordinary <ionization> changes the <electron> count, leaving the <isotope> unchanged. Changing the <neutron number> can change <nuclear binding energy> and the possibility of <radioactive decay>. <Isotopic abundances> can help distinguish material sources, while <radioactive decay> can additionally supply chronological information.

A <mass spectrum> makes the distinction experimentally visible: a single <chemical element> can give separate <ion> peaks at different <mass-to-charge ratios>. Its bulk <atomic weight> is a <weighted mean> governed by <isotopic abundances>, rather than the location of one compulsory peak. The historical <mass spectrograph> experiment and the synthetic <neon> <mass spectrum> below illustrate these complementary observations.

= Mass number
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=$A=Z+N$}
{wiki}

The <mass number> $A$ is the total number of <nucleons>, namely <protons> plus <neutrons>, in an <atomic nucleus>:
$$
A=Z+N,\qquad N=A-Z.
$$
It is an exact positive <integer> with no units, rather than a measured <mass>. <Carbon-12> has $A=12$ because its <atomic nucleus> contains six <protons> and six <neutrons>. No improvement in a <mass> measurement can turn that count into $11.999$ or $12.001$.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/mass number>{ref}

The left superscript in ${}^{22}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$ denotes <mass number> $22$, not an <atomic mass> of exactly $22\,\mathrm u$. For <neon-22>, the displayed reference <atomic mass> is about $21.991385114\,\mathrm u$. Even a <weighted mean> of <mass numbers>, such as $20.1877$ for the illustrative <neon> population below, is a population average, not the <mass number> of an individual <atomic nucleus>.<Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions for Neon>{ref}

= Neutron number
{parent=Mass number}
{title2=$N=A-Z$}
{wiki}

The number of <neutrons> in an <atomic nucleus>. Two <isotopes> of the same <chemical element> differ in <neutron number> while sharing an <atomic number>.

= Atomic mass
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=$m_{\rm a}$}
{wiki}

The <rest mass> of a <chemical atom> in its <ground state>, a <physical quantity> expressed in <SI> <mass> units or the <unified atomic mass unit>. Here $m_i$ means the <atomic mass> of a neutral <chemical atom> of <isotope> $i$, not the <mass> of its bare <atomic nucleus> and not a population average.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/atomic mass>{ref}

<Atomic mass> includes <electrons> and depends on <nuclear binding energy>; it is not just <mass number> times a universal <nucleon> <mass>. The <atomic mass> of neutral <carbon-12> in its <ground state> is exactly $12\,\mathrm u$ by definition of the <unified atomic mass unit>. That exactness establishes the reference scale, rather than making the <atomic masses> of all <isotopes> exact <integers> in the same unit.

= Dalton
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Atomic mass}
{title2=$\mathrm{Da}=\mathrm u$}
{wiki}

= Unified atomic mass unit
{synonym}

The <unified atomic mass unit> and <dalton (unit)> are names for the same <mass> unit:
$$
1\,\mathrm u=1\,\mathrm{Da}=\frac{m_{\rm a}({}^{12}\mathrm C)}{12},
$$
where the <carbon-12> <chemical atom> is free, at rest, and in its nuclear and electronic <ground state>. Its conversion to <SI> <mass> units is determined experimentally. See <The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure> (Table 8, footnote (f), printed p. 146: dalton and unified atomic mass unit).

= Nuclear mass defect
{parent=Atomic mass}
{title2=$\Delta m=Zm_p+Nm_n-M_{\rm nuc}$}

The <mass> deficit of a bound <atomic nucleus> relative to its separated <protons> and <neutrons>. If their <rest masses> are $m_p$ and $m_n$, the <atomic nucleus> has <rest mass> $M_{\rm nuc}$, and the positive <nuclear binding energy> is $B$, then
$$
\Delta m=Zm_p+Nm_n-M_{\rm nuc}=\frac{B}{c^2},
$$
with $c$ the <speed of light>. Releasing <energy> when the <nucleons> bind reduces the <rest mass> of the bound system without changing the exact count $A=Z+N$. A neutral <atomic mass> also includes $Z$ <electron> <rest masses>, reduced by the <energy> required to separate those bound <electrons>. Consequently $A-m_{\rm a}/\mathrm u$ is not the definition of the <nuclear mass defect>: the reference unit and constituent <rest masses> must also be accounted for.

= Relative atomic mass
{parent=Atomic mass}
{title2=$A_{\rm r}=\bar m/\mathrm u$}
{wiki}

= Atomic weight
{synonym}

For a <chemical element> in a specified population, its <relative atomic mass> or <atomic weight> is the <dimensionless> ratio $A_{\rm r}=\bar m/\mathrm u$, with $\bar m$ the mean neutral <atomic mass> weighted by <isotopic abundance>. For the illustrative <neon> population, $A_{\rm r}\approx20.1800$, whereas $\bar m\approx20.1800\,\mathrm u$ has <mass> units.<Column Descriptions for Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions>{ref}

= Standard atomic weight
{parent=Relative atomic mass}
{title2=$A_{\rm r}^{\circ}$}
{wiki}

A recommended <atomic weight> applicable to normal materials, rather than the result for every possible enriched sample. The tabulated <neon> value $20.1797(6)$ and the arithmetic result above need not agree in every last displayed digit. Some <chemical elements> have intervals of <standard atomic weight> because their <isotopic abundances> vary across normal materials.<Standard Atomic Weights 2024>{ref}

= Isotopic abundance
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=$f_i$}

An <isotopic abundance> is an atom fraction within a specified population of one <chemical element>.<Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book), second edition/isotopic abundance>{ref} If $n_i$ counts its <chemical atoms> of <isotope> $i$, use
$$
f_i=\frac{n_i}{\sum_j n_j},\qquad f_i\geq0,\qquad\sum_i f_i=1.
$$
The denominator includes every <isotope> of that <chemical element> included in the stated population, not every <chemical atom> of every <chemical element> in the sample. Thus an <oxygen> atom fraction among all <chemical elements> is a different quantity.

For an illustrative neutral <neon> population, take the displayed central <isotopic abundances> $f_{20}=0.9048$, $f_{21}=0.0027$, and $f_{22}=0.0925$ from the reference table, so $0.9048+0.0027+0.0925=1$. Its mean <atomic mass> is the <weighted arithmetic mean>
$$
\begin{aligned}
\bar m&=\sum_i f_i m_i\\
&=\bigl(0.9048\times19.9924401762+0.0027\times20.993846685+0.0925\times21.991385114\bigr)\,\mathrm u\\
&=20.18004638052026\,\mathrm u\approx20.1800\,\mathrm u.
\end{aligned}
$$
These are rounded reference inputs with uncertainties, not exact universal <isotopic abundances>; the long decimal shows their arithmetic result, not measurement precision.<Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions for Neon>{ref}

The population convention matters. If instead $w_i$ is the fraction of total neutral-atom <mass> supplied by <isotope> $i$, then $w_i=f_i m_i/\bar m$ and
$$
\bar m=\left(\sum_i\frac{w_i}{m_i}\right)^{-1}.
$$
This is a <weighted harmonic mean> in the <mass> fractions. Using $\sum_i w_i m_i$ with those <mass> fractions would answer a different question. The conversion follows directly by counting <chemical atoms> and adding their <masses>.

= Uranium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{U}$, $Z=92$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 92. Its long-lived radioactive isotopes illustrate actinide production by the <rapid neutron-capture process>.

= Lead
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Pb}$, $Z=82$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 82. Lead isotopes are important heavy products of the <slow neutron-capture process> in suitable neutron-exposure conditions.

= Barium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ba}$, $Z=56$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 56. Enrichment in barium can reveal transferred <slow neutron-capture process> products, as in a <barium star>.

= Silicon
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Si}$, $Z=14$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 14. <Oxygen burning> produces silicon-group nuclei; subsequent <silicon burning> is a reaction network approaching an iron-group mixture.

= Magnesium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Mg}$, $Z=12$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 12. Magnesium isotopes occur among products of <carbon burning> and alpha-capture neutron-source reactions.

= Neon
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ne}$, $Z=10$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 10. <Neon burning> uses photodisintegration and alpha capture, while neon-22 can provide neutrons through $^{22}\mathrm{Ne}(\alpha,n){}^{25}\mathrm{Mg}$.

= Neon-20
{parent=Neon}
{title2=${}^{20}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$}

The <neon> <isotope> with ten <protons> and ten <neutrons>.

= Neon-21
{parent=Neon}
{title2=${}^{21}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$}

The <neon> <isotope> with ten <protons> and eleven <neutrons>.

= Neon-22
{parent=Neon}
{title2=${}^{22}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$}

The <neon> <isotope> with ten <protons> and twelve <neutrons>.

= Cobalt
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Co}$, $Z=27$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 27. Cobalt-56 is the intermediate radioactive nucleus in the <nickel-56 decay chain>.

= Nickel
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ni}$, $Z=28$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 28. Radioactive nickel-56 is produced in explosive burning; the <nickel-56 decay chain> contributes energy to supernova emission.

= Nickel-56 decay chain
{parent=Nickel}
{title2=$^{56}\mathrm{Ni}\longrightarrow{}^{56}\mathrm{Co}\longrightarrow{}^{56}\mathrm{Fe}$}

The radioactive chain converts nickel-56 through cobalt-56 into iron-56, releasing energy that can power supernova light curves. <Electron capture> and, where allowed, <beta-plus decay> convert protons into neutrons; gamma rays and charged decay products deposit some of the released energy. Ionization and escape of decay radiation affect the observed heating.

= Manganese
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Mn}$, $Z=25$}

= Iron
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Fe}$, $Z=26$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 26. Iron-group <nuclear binding energy> and <silicon burning> are central to the termination of ordinary energy-producing stellar fusion.

= Oxygen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm O$}
{wiki}

= Oxygen-16
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=$^{16}\mathrm O$}

= Molecular oxygen
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=$\mathrm O_2$}

The diatomic form of <oxygen>. Modern terrestrial abundance is maintained largely by oxygenic <photosynthesis>, balanced by chemical and biological sinks.

= Nitrogen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm N$}
{wiki}

= Carbon
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Carbon-12
{parent=Carbon}
{title2=$^{12}\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Carbon-13
{parent=Carbon}
{title2=$^{13}\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

The <carbon> <isotope> with six <protons> and seven <neutrons>. A neutral <carbon-13> <chemical atom> has six <electrons>.

= Helium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{He}$}
{wiki}

= Helium-3
{parent=Helium}
{title2=$^3\mathrm{He}$}
{wiki}

This stable <helium> isotope contains two <protons> and one <neutron>. In solar proton-proton burning it is an intermediate: two such nuclei can form <helium-4> and return two <protons>, or one can capture <helium-4> to enter the second chain branch. The balance between production and destruction determines the <helium-3 equilibrium abundance> and its <helium-3 relaxation time>.

= Helium-4
{parent=Helium}
{title2=$^4\mathrm{He}$}

Helium-4 is the stable helium isotope with two protons and two neutrons; its nucleus is an alpha particle. Its strong binding makes it the principal neutron-containing product of primordial <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Hydrogen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

= Metallic hydrogen
{parent=Hydrogen}
{wiki}

Metallic hydrogen is a high-pressure conducting state of <hydrogen>. Its fluid form in the deep interiors of <gas giants> supplies a conducting medium for a <planetary dynamo>, unlike the iron-alloy conducting cores of <terrestrial planets>.

= Deuterium
{parent=Hydrogen}
{title2=$^2\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

This stable <hydrogen> isotope has one <proton> and one <neutron>. In the <Proton–proton chain> it is produced by the slow weak proton-pair reaction and is rapidly consumed by <proton> capture to make <helium-3>. Its short nuclear lifetime inside a stellar core allows a <deuterium quasi-equilibrium in proton-proton burning> approximation.

= Hydrogen anion
{parent=Hydrogen}
{title2=$\mathrm H^-$}
{wiki}

= Negative hydrogen ion
{synonym}

A negative hydrogen ion is a <hydrogen> nucleus with two bound electrons. Its weakly bound extra electron permits absorption at visible and infrared wavelengths in cool stellar atmospheres.

= Molecular nitrogen
{title2=$\mathrm{N}_2$}
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Molecular hydrogen
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2$}
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Chemical compound
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Sodium hydride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaH}$}

An <ionic compound> of <sodium> <cations> and <hydrogen anions>. Its <oxidation state> assignments are $+1$ for <sodium> and $-1$ for <hydrogen>.

= Sulfuric acid
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm H_2\mathrm{SO}_4$}

A <chemical acid> used to supply the acidic conditions of a <permanganate-iron titration>.

= Manganese dioxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{MnO}_2$}

The <manganese> <oxidation state> is $+4$ when each <oxygen> is assigned $-2$.

= Fluorescein
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Tetradecyltrimethylammonium bromide
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Sodium hydroxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaOH}$}

= Tert-amyl bromide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_5\mathrm{H}_{11}\mathrm{Br}$}

= Tertiary amylbromide
{synonym}

= 2-Bromo-2-methylbutane
{synonym}

The <chemical compound> used in the <conductometric determination of activation energy>. Its <chemical formula> and alternative name <2-bromo-2-methylbutane> are listed by the <NIST Chemistry WebBook, SRD 69/Butane, 2-bromo-2-methyl->{ref}.

= Benzene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_6$}

<Benzene> has a planar ring of six <carbon> <chemical atoms>, each bearing one <hydrogen>. Its $\pi$ <electron density> is distributed over the ring; the alternating <covalent bonds> in one <resonance structure> do not describe alternating actual <bond lengths>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/15-2-structure-and-stability-of-benzene[McMurry: benzene structure]{ref}

= Phenol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_5\mathrm{OH}$}

<Phenol> is <benzene> with one ring <hydrogen> replaced by a <hydroxy group>. That O–H group supplies the <hydrogen bond donor> in the experiment under <hydrogen bond acceptor>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/17-1-naming-alcohols-and-phenols[McMurry: phenol naming and structures]{ref}

= Sodium chloride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaCl}$}

<Sodium chloride> contains <sodium> <cations> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and <chlorine> <anions> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ in a $1:1$ ratio; its <formula unit> has zero net <electric charge>.

= Glycerol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_3\mathrm{H}_8\mathrm{O}_3$}

<Glycerol> has three <carbon> <chemical atoms> and three $\mathrm{OH}$ groups. It supplies the scaffold of <triglycerides> and <glycerophospholipids>, but is not itself a long <hydrocarbon> chain. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: glycerol].

= Hydrocarbon
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

A <hydrocarbon> contains only <carbon> and <hydrogen>. A <fatty acid> also contains <oxygen>; calling its long chain a <hydrocarbon> region does not make the whole <fatty acid> a <hydrocarbon>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02889[IUPAC Gold Book: hydrocarbons].

= Ethane
{parent=Hydrocarbon}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_2H_6}$}

= Ester
{parent=Chemical compound}

The <carboxylic acid>-derived <ester> linkages in a <triglyceride> or typical <glycerophospholipid> have the form $\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}O{-}R'}$. Here the <acyl group> is attached through <oxygen> to the <glycerol>-derived part. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P6.html[IUPAC Blue Book, P-65.6: esters].

= Ethyl acetoacetate
{parent=Ester}
{wiki}

= Ethyl acetate
{parent=Ester}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COOC_2H_5}$}

An <ester> consumed in the <conductometric saponification assay>, where <hydrolysis> under basic conditions produces <ethanol> and an <acetate ion>.

= Methanol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Choline
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Disulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Thiol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{R-SH}$}
{wiki}

A <thiol> is a <chemical compound> of the form $\mathrm{R-SH}$, with an organic group $R$ attached to <sulfur>. The <conjugate base> is $\mathrm{R-S^-}$; <cysteine> and <mercaptoethanol> supply examples of <thiol> groups. Two <thiol> groups can undergo <oxidation> to form a <disulfide bond>. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/T06359[IUPAC Gold Book: thiols]{ref}

= Ellman's reagent
{c}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= DTNB
{c}
{synonym}

<Ellman's reagent> is 5,5'-dithiobis(2-nitrobenzoic acid), abbreviated <DTNB>, a <disulfide> used to quantify accessible <thiols>. Reaction releases the colored TNB species measured by <absorbance> at <wavelength> $412\,\mathrm{nm}$. The <disulfide bond> experiment above explains why a <thiol> measurement can become an indirect bridge count only after controlled <reduction>. https://assets.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/LSG/manuals/MAN0011216_Ellmans_Reag_UG.pdf[Pierce Biotechnology: identity and use of Ellman's reagent]{ref}

= Diol
{parent=Chemical compound}

A <diol> has two <hydroxy groups>. Its two groups can serve as joining ends in <polyaddition>; the rest of its <molecule> can already be a long chain.

= Isocyanate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{R{-}N=C=O}$}

An <isocyanate> bears an $\mathrm{R{-}N=C=O}$ group. A <molecule> bearing two such groups can react with a <diol> to form <polyurethane> connections.

= Urea
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$(\mathrm{NH}_2)_2\mathrm{CO}$}
{wiki}

Urea is the <chemical compound> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm{CH}_4\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$ and structure $(\mathrm{NH}_2)_2\mathrm{CO}$. See the https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C57136&Mask=20[NIST Chemistry WebBook, urea: formula and structure]. Concentrated urea is used to promote <protein denaturation> in the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>.

= 2-Mercaptoethanol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{HOCH}_2\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{SH}$}
{wiki}

= Mercaptoethanol
{synonym}

2-Mercaptoethanol is the <chemical compound> $\mathrm{HOCH}_2\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{SH}$; see the https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C60242&Units=SI[NIST Chemistry WebBook, 2-mercaptoethanol: structure and names]. In the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>, it breaks <protein disulfide bonds>, while the <peptide bonds> of the <protein backbone> remain intact. This separates a change in crosslinks from cutting the <polypeptide chain>.

= Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

<Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate> is a <cofactor> used in <metabolism>. Its oxidized and reduced forms, <NADP+> and <NADPH>, carry <electrons> between coupled <chemical reactions>. In <oxygenic photosynthesis>, <light-dependent reactions> produce <NADPH> for the <Calvin cycle>, which returns <NADP+> for reuse. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: recycling NADP+ and NADPH]{ref}.

= NADP+
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm{NADP}^{+}$}

= Oxidized NADP
{synonym}

<NADP+> is the oxidized form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate>. It can accept <electrons> to form <NADPH>; the <light-dependent reactions> supply this conversion during <oxygenic photosynthesis>.

= NADPH
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm{NADPH}$}

<NADPH> is the reduced form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate>. It carries <electrons> used in synthesis; in the <Calvin cycle> it becomes the oxidized form <NADP+>, available for reuse by <light-dependent reactions>. <NADPH> and <ATP> serve different roles even though both connect <light> capture to <carbon fixation>.

= Chloromethane
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH}_3\mathrm{Cl}$}
{wiki}

= Methyl chloride
{synonym}

An organochlorine gas with biological as well as possible nonbiological sources. Its specialized biological production is a <secondary metabolic byproduct> example.

= Dimethyl sulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$(\mathrm{CH}_3)_2\mathrm S$}
{wiki}

= DMS
{c}
{synonym}

An organic sulfur gas emitted through biological processing, including marine production associated with sulfur-containing metabolites. It is a <secondary metabolic byproduct> example and a possible <atmospheric biosignature gas> whose detectability depends on production and photochemical destruction.

= Ozone
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm O_3$}
{wiki}

A triatomic form of <oxygen>, produced photochemically from <molecular oxygen>. The <ozone layer> absorbs ultraviolet radiation; ozone can trace atmospheric oxygen without being directly emitted by metabolism.

= Nitrous oxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$}
{wiki}

A nitrogen oxide produced substantially by microbial nitrogen cycling on modern <Earth>. Its atmospheric abundance and lifetime make it a candidate <atmospheric biosignature gas>, with context-dependent abiotic alternatives.

= Phosphine
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PH}_3$}
{wiki}

Phosphine is $\mathrm{PH}_3$. Its abundance in giant-planet observable atmospheres can depend on transport from deeper layers faster than local chemical destruction.

= Vanadium(II) oxide
{title2=$\mathrm{VO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Vanadium monoxide
{synonym}

= Titanium monoxide
{title2=$\mathrm{TiO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Acetylene
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_2\mathrm{H}_2$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Hydrogen cyanide
{title2=$\mathrm{HCN}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Carbon dioxide
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}_2$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= CO2
{c}
{synonym}

= Carbon monoxide
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Ammonia
{title2=$\mathrm{NH}_3$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Methane
{title2=$\mathrm{CH}_4$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Methanogenesis
{parent=Methane}
{wiki}

Methanogenesis is biological production of <methane> by anaerobic microorganisms. Waterlogged soil and lake sediments can favour it. Warming or thaw changes substrate availability, but production is distinct from atmospheric emission because oxidation and transport intervene.

= Water
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Self-ionization of water
{parent=Water}
{wiki}

Two <water> <molecules> can exchange a <proton>, producing <hydronium> and <hydroxide> <ions>: $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\rightleftharpoons\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^++\mathrm{OH}^-$. Even pure <water> therefore supplies <ions>; extreme <dilution> eventually invalidates the <strong acid> assumption that it supplies all the <hydronium>.

= Ice
{parent=Water}
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}(s)$}
{wiki}

= Snowflake
{parent=Ice}
{wiki}

A snowflake is an <ice> crystal or an aggregate of <ice> crystals grown in atmospheric conditions. Its morphology depends on vapour supersaturation, transport, <temperature> and crystal anisotropy. Not every snowflake is dendritic.

= Dendritic growth of a snowflake
{parent=Snowflake}

An exposed tip receives vapour more efficiently than a sheltered surface and can amplify its lead, producing <crystal dendrites> and side branches. The planar thermal <solidification> mechanism is an analogy, not a complete vapour-growth model. The <hexagonal ice crystal anisotropy> selects six preferred directions; an isotropic linear instability alone does not select six arms.

= Steady state
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <steady state (chemistry)> has time-independent concentrations because formation and removal rates balance for the species considered. Nonzero reaction fluxes can continue, so a steady state need not be <chemical equilibrium>. A <quasi-steady-state approximation> imposes this balance approximately for a fast intermediate while slower species evolve.

= Physical chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Chemical polarity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

<Chemical polarity> concerns unequal distribution of <electron density> in a <chemical bond> or <molecule>. A <polar molecule> has a permanent <electric dipole moment>; the effects of individual bonds must be combined with their spatial arrangement. In a symmetric linear <carbon dioxide> <molecule>, opposing bond-dipole contributions cancel. In <water>, its <bent molecular geometry> prevents that cancellation, giving a permanent <electric dipole moment>.https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Chemistry 2e, 7.6: geometry and molecular polarity]{ref}

= Polar molecule
{parent=Chemical polarity}

A <polar molecule> has a nonzero permanent <electric dipole moment> because of its internal <electric charge> distribution. It can still have zero total <electric charge>. <Water> is polar; a <nonpolar molecule> has no permanent <electric dipole moment>.

= Nonpolar molecule
{parent=Chemical polarity}

= Nonpolar
{synonym}

A <nonpolar molecule> has no permanent <electric dipole moment>. This distinguishes its charge distribution from the separated partial charges of a polar <molecule>.

= Hydrophilicity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hydrophilic
{synonym}

<Hydrophilicity> describes affinity for <water>. Regions with <chemical polarity> or <electric charge> can interact favorably with <water>, including through <hydrogen bonds> where suitable groups are present. <Hydrophilic> regions of membrane-forming <phospholipids> face the <water> on either side of a <lipid bilayer>. Such affinity does not mean forming <covalent bonds> with every neighboring substance, nor does it require a net negative <electric charge>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al.: the chemical components of a cell].

= Amphiphile
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Amphiphilic
{synonym}

= Amphipathic
{synonym}

An <amphiphile> is a <molecule> containing distinct <hydrophilic> and <hydrophobic> regions. <Amphiphilic> and <amphipathic> describe this combination. Membrane-forming <phospholipids> are examples; their two regions give different parts of one <molecule> different affinities for <water>. The word describes a structure, not a guarantee of a particular aggregate shape. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00303[IUPAC Gold Book: amphiphilic].

= Hydrophobicity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hydrophobic
{synonym}

<Hydrophobicity> describes unfavorable mixing with <water>, as illustrated by long <hydrocarbon> regions. The <hydrophobic> regions of membrane-forming <phospholipids> face the interior of a <lipid bilayer>. This organization is explained by the <hydrophobic effect>, rather than by imagining that chains actively repel <water>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02907[IUPAC Gold Book: hydrophobic interaction].

= Hydrophobic effect
{parent=Hydrophobicity}
{wiki}

The <hydrophobic effect> is the solvent-mediated tendency of <nonpolar> regions to associate in <water>. Exposing a <hydrocarbon> region changes how surrounding <water> <molecules> can arrange and interact; reducing that exposure can lower the total <free energy>. Both <water> and <lipids> enter the accounting. The effect is not an additional <covalent bond> between chains. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02907[IUPAC Gold Book: hydrophobic interaction].

At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, compare two candidate organizations by the change in <Gibbs free energy>, $\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S$, where $H$ is <enthalpy>, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, and $S$ is <entropy>. An organization with lower $G$ is favored in this comparison. Reducing exposed <hydrocarbon> area is one contribution; changes in chain packing and the <entropy> of the <lipids> also matter. This explains why <hydrophobicity> alone cannot predict every aggregate shape. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/16.4 Free Energy>.

= Absorbance
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{title2=$A$}
{wiki}

<Absorbance> is the <dimensionless> quantity $A=\log_{10}(P_0/P)$, where $P_0$ and $P$ are reference and transmitted radiant <powers> at the chosen <wavelength>. For example, $A=1$ means one tenth of the reference <power> is transmitted. Under the <Beer-Lambert law>, <absorbance> can determine an absorbing species' <concentration>; attenuation by cloudiness or unrelated colored <solutes> needs separate controls. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/B00626[IUPAC Gold Book: absorbance and its concentration relation]{ref}

= Beer-Lambert law
{c}
{parent=Absorbance}
{title2=$A=\varepsilon lc$}
{wiki=Beer–Lambert_law}

The <Beer-Lambert law> relates <absorbance> $A$ to an absorbing species' <concentration> $c$ and path length $l$ by $A=\varepsilon lc$ in a homogeneous medium using sufficiently narrow-band light. The coefficient $\varepsilon$ depends on the species, <wavelength> and <chemical solution> conditions. This makes <Ellman's reagent> useful for quantitative <thiol> assays once its released colored species has been calibrated. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/B00626[IUPAC Gold Book: Beer-Lambert law and validity conditions]{ref}

= Thermochemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hess's law
{c}
{parent=Thermochemistry}
{wiki}

<Hess's law> states that compatible <chemical reaction> <enthalpies> add when their equations add. Reversing a <chemical reaction> reverses its <enthalpy> change; multiplying the entire <chemical reaction> equation by a factor multiplies that change by the same factor. The reason is that <enthalpy> is a <state function>: intermediate states cancel from the differences. A cycle supplies one independent balance relation; it cannot determine two unknown inputs without further information.

= Standard enthalpy of formation
{parent=Thermochemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta H_{\rm f}^{\circ}$}

The <standard enthalpy of formation> is the <enthalpy> change for forming one <mole> of a specified <chemical substance> from its <chemical elements> in their reference states at the stated <temperature>. For <sodium chloride> at $298.15\,\mathrm K$, those reactant states are <solid> <sodium> and diatomic gaseous <chlorine>. <Standard state> specifies conditions; it does not by itself select a <temperature>.

= Enthalpy of atomization
{parent=Thermochemistry}
{title2=$\Delta H_{\rm at}^{\circ}$}

An <enthalpy of atomization> is the <enthalpy> change for making one <mole> of separated gaseous <chemical atoms> from a specified starting <chemical substance>. For <sodium>, the relevant step is <sublimation> to monatomic <gas>; for diatomic <chlorine>, it is half the compatible molar <bond-dissociation energy> expressed as an <enthalpy>.

= Enthalpy change of solution
{parent=Thermochemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta H_{\rm sol}$}

= Enthalpy of solution
{synonym}

An <enthalpy of solution> is an <enthalpy> difference for dissolution into a specified <solvent> at stated <temperature> and final <concentration>. Keep a measured finite-dilution value distinct from an infinite-dilution reference value.

= Mass spectrometry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

<Mass spectrometry> separates and detects <ions> according to <mass-to-charge ratio>. It can distinguish <isotopes> which belong to the same <chemical element> but have different <atomic masses>. The instrument measures <ions>: interpreting the result as a neutral <atomic mass> requires accounting for <electron> removal and its <ionization energy>. For a singly positive <ion> in its <ground state>, $m_+=m_{\rm a}-m_e+I/c^2$, where $m_e$ is the <electron> <rest mass>, $I$ is the <ionization energy>, and $c$ is the <speed of light>.

For a simple illustrative analyzer, positive <ions> of <mass> $m$ and <electric charge> $q=ze$ gain <kinetic energy> $qV=mv^2/2$ across an <electric potential difference> $V$ before entering a transverse uniform <magnetic field> $B$. The <Lorentz force> supplies <centripetal acceleration>, so
$$
qvB=\frac{mv^2}{r},\qquad\frac{m}{q}=\frac{B^2r^2}{2V}.
$$
At fixed $V$ and $B$, the measured bending radius $r$ separates <mass-to-charge ratios>, not <masses> independently of <electric charge>. This ideal model assumes negligible initial <kinetic energy> and speeds small compared with the <speed of light>; the historical focusing <mass spectrograph> used the geometry described below.

= Mass spectrograph
{parent=Mass spectrometry}

A <mass spectrometry> instrument with a photographic readout. In the 1919 apparatus described in <Isotopes by Francis William Aston/Chapter V: The Mass-Spectrograph>, a <gas> discharge produced positive <ions>. Two narrow slits formed a ribbon beam in a <vacuum>; plates applied an <electric field> to spread it, an aperture selected part of it, and a <magnetic field> bent it oppositely. Focusing reduced the spread caused by differing <ion> <speeds>, concentrating a given <mass-to-charge ratio> into a line on a photographic plate.

The observed quantity was line displacement from a fiducial spot. Known reference lines calibrated displacement against <mass-to-charge ratio>; repeated exposures with changed <magnetic field> tested the calibration. Evacuation limited collisions. Comparing line shapes with narrow and wide apertures helped distinguish geometric broadening from photographic spreading. Finite slit widths, plate response, and overlapping molecular or multiply charged <ions> limited interpretation. See <Isotopes by Francis William Aston/Chapter V: The Mass-Spectrograph> (§§33–40: apparatus and photographic procedure; §§43 and 45–46: calibration, aperture comparisons, and accuracy).

In <Mass spectra and isotopes by Francis William Aston> (Nobel lecture, 12 December 1922), the reported <neon> lines at 20 and 22 have companions at 10 and 11 from doubly charged <ions>. That paired pattern and comparison with reference lines support different <atomic masses> within one <chemical element>, rather than an <atomic mass> concentrated at the bulk average. The lecture also describes an earlier failed separation by distillation and partial separation by <diffusion>, showing why the focused <mass spectrum> supplied stronger evidence. These numbers used a historical <oxygen>-based reference scale. The lecture's whole-number rule was an empirical approximation at its stated accuracy, not the modern exact definition $A=Z+N$.<Mass spectra and isotopes by Francis William Aston>[pp. 9–13: separation attempts, focusing, reference lines, charge orders, and neon; pp. 15–17: whole-number rule and historical nuclear interpretation]{ref}

= Mass-to-charge ratio
{parent=Mass spectrometry}
{title2=$m/q$}
{wiki}

The ratio of an <ion>'s <mass> to its <electric charge>; in <SI> it has units $\mathrm{kg/C}$. A convenient numerical <mass spectrum> coordinate is $x=(m/\mathrm u)/z$, with $q=ze$ and positive integer $z$ here. This $x$ is <dimensionless>. Doubling $z$ at fixed <mass> halves $x$, so the <electric charge> state must be identified before assigning an <isotope>.

= Mass spectrum
{parent=Mass spectrometry}
{wiki}

= Mass spectra
{synonym}

A <mass spectrum> displays detected signal against <mass-to-charge ratio>. Peak positions identify candidate <ions>; peak areas give <isotopic abundances> only after the response and overlapping contributions have been accounted for.

\Image[neon-synthetic-mass-spectrum-weighted-mean.png]
{title=Synthetic <neon> <mass spectra> and the <weighted mean> of neutral <atomic masses>}
{description=This is a model, not a measured <mass spectrum> or a reconstruction of Aston's plate. All <ions> have $z=+1$; neutral <atomic masses> approximate <ion> <masses>. Chosen <Gaussian functions> have unit area and widths $\sigma=0.04$ and $0.70$ in the <dimensionless> coordinate $x$. Equal responses make component areas equal the displayed reference <isotopic abundances>. The inset reveals the small <neon-21> peak. The bottom diagram places the <weighted mean> between the component <atomic masses>; it is not an additional <isotope>.}

Broadening can hide a weak component without changing its area in this ideal model. An actual <mass spectrum> can also contain molecular <ions>, different <electric charge> states, background, and unequal detection responses. A missing visible peak therefore cannot by itself prove the absence of an <isotope>. Conversely, a fractional <atomic weight> is a property of a population: there is no requirement for an <ion> peak at the <weighted mean>.

= Mass resolution
{parent=Mass spectrometry}
{title2=$R=x/\Delta x$}
{wiki}

= Mass resolving power
{synonym}

<Mass resolving power> compares a peak coordinate $x$ with a stated width or separation criterion $\Delta x$. With $\Delta x$ taken as <full width at half maximum>, a <Gaussian function> of <standard deviation> $\sigma$ has
$$
\Delta x=2\sqrt{2\log2}\,\sigma,\qquad R=\frac{x}{\Delta x}.
$$
At the <neon-20> coordinate in the synthetic <mass spectrum>, $\sigma=0.04$ gives $\Delta x\approx0.0942$ and $R\approx212$, while $\sigma=0.70$ gives $\Delta x\approx1.648$ and $R\approx12.1$. The width criterion and relative peak areas both matter when judging whether a small neighboring <isotope> can be distinguished. <Mass resolution> also differs from the accuracy of a calibrated peak position.

